/* The Expositor's Translation -- inline annotations.
 *
 * GENERATED-BY-HAND (small enough not to need a build tool yet -- revisit if
 * this grows past a book or two). Keyed "Book C:V" against data/kjv.js. The
 * base verse wording must match data/kjv.js exactly -- these strings only
 * ADD annotation, never alter the scripture text itself.
 *
 * This is the whole of the translation's added content: short definitional
 * glosses AND longer devotional notes both live here, inline, right after
 * the specific word or phrase they explain -- never in a separate popup
 * panel. Rendered black-letter (the KJV text) / red-letter (the annotation),
 * the same split the source study Bible uses.
 *
 * Every note is deliberately varied in shape -- question, plain statement,
 * an aside that trails off, a fact with no closing line at all. Falling into
 * one rhetorical template ("not X, but Y...") across many notes reads as
 * formula even when the content differs, so no two notes in a row should
 * lean on the same construction.
 *
 * A verse with no entry here renders the plain KJV text -- exactly like the
 * King James Version translation does.
 */
const EXP_INLINE = {
  "Matthew 1:1": "The book (record) of the genealogy (family line) of Jesus Christ (Saviour, the Anointed One), the son of David, the son of Abraham (three names, three promises: David's throne, Abraham's blessing, and everything Isaiah and the prophets went on to say about a Seed still to come -- Matthew stacks all three into one sentence before the story even starts).",
  "Matthew 1:5": "And Salmon begat Booz of Rachab (Rachab is Rahab -- the Canaanite woman who hid Israel's spies in Jericho, a prostitute by trade, named here rather than left out the way genealogies usually leave the mothers out); and Booz begat Obed of Ruth; and Obed begat Jesse;",
  "Matthew 1:6": "And Jesse begat David the king; and David the king begat Solomon of her that had been the wife of Urias (why phrase it that way instead of simply writing her name? Matthew's wording is the one that reminds the reader whose wife she used to be, and leaves David's worst chapter sitting in the family record rather than editing it out);",
  "Matthew 1:16": "And Jacob begat Joseph the husband of Mary, of whom was born Jesus (Saviour), who is called Christ (a title, not a surname -- the Anointed One, the promised King the whole list of names above has been building toward).",
  "Matthew 1:17": "So all the generations from Abraham to David are fourteen generations; and from David until the carrying away into Babylon are fourteen generations; and from the carrying away into Babylon unto Christ are fourteen generations (Matthew has trimmed the full family record to make these three sets match -- ancient genealogies did this regularly, arranging names for memory rather than a complete census, and the pattern he wants remembered is the count itself, ticking down to Christ).",
  "Matthew 1:18": "Now the birth of Jesus Christ was on this wise: When as his mother Mary was espoused (engaged, but not yet married) to Joseph, before they came together, she was found with child of the Holy Ghost (Luke tells this from Mary's side of the news; Matthew tells it from Joseph's, which is why the very next verse is about what Joseph decided to do with it).",
  "Matthew 1:19": "Then Joseph her husband, being a just man, and not willing to make her a publick example, was minded to put her away privily (to end the engagement quietly, sparing her public shame -- the law gave him grounds for a far harsher, public accusation, and he settles on the quieter option on his own, before any angel tells him to choose mercy).",
  "Matthew 1:21": "And she shall bring forth a son, and thou shalt call his name JESUS (Saviour): for he shall save his people from their sins (the angel states this as already decided, months before Jesus preaches a single sermon or performs a single miracle -- the name carries the outcome ahead of the rest of the story).",
  "Matthew 1:23": "Behold, a virgin shall be with child, and shall bring forth a son, and they shall call his name Emmanuel, which being interpreted is, God with us (sacrifice, priesthood, and law had all worked, for centuries, to close the distance between a holy God and sinful people one careful step at a time; this name is announced as that distance already closed).",
  "Matthew 1:24": "Then Joseph being raised from sleep did as the angel of the Lord had bidden him, and took unto him his wife (went ahead at once with the marriage):",
  "Matthew 1:25": "And knew her not (had no sexual relation with her) till she had brought forth her firstborn son: and he called his name JESUS.",
  "Matthew 2:6": "And thou Bethlehem, in the land of Juda, art not the least among the princes of Juda: for out of thee shall come a Governor, that shall rule my people Israel (Micah wrote this seven hundred years earlier, naming the town before it had produced anything grander than a shepherd boy named David; the chief priests quote it back word for word to the very king trying to kill its fulfillment, then do nothing else with what they know).",
  "Matthew 2:7": "Then Herod, when he had privily (privately) called the wise men, enquired of them diligently what time the star appeared.",
  "Matthew 2:11": "And when they were come into the house (the manger is already behind them by this point -- the family has settled into a house, and the two-year window Herod works from in verse sixteen is the clue for how much later this visit actually happened), they saw the young child with Mary his mother, and fell down, and worshipped him: and when they had opened their treasures, they presented unto him gifts; gold, and frankincense, and myrrh (a king's tribute, incense fit for worship, and the spice used to prepare a body for burial -- the third gift is already looking ahead to Good Friday).",
  "Matthew 2:15": "And was there until the death of Herod: that it might be fulfilled which was spoken of the Lord by the prophet, saying, Out of Egypt have I called my son (Hosea wrote this about the nation, remembering the Exodus; Matthew applies the same line to one Child, because the story Israel was called to live and never finished, this Son was on His way to finishing in their place).",
  "Matthew 2:16": "Then Herod, when he saw that he was mocked of the wise men (they never doubled back through Jerusalem to report, the way he had ordered), was exceeding wroth, and sent forth, and slew all the children that were in Bethlehem, and in all the coasts thereof, from two years old and under, according to the time which he had diligently enquired of the wise men (this detail is how the timeline gets dated at all -- Herod is working off a window of up to two years, so the star, the journey, and the visit were spread out, not crowded into one December with the shepherds).",
  "Matthew 2:18": "In Rama was there a voice heard, lamentation, and weeping, and great mourning, Rachel weeping for her children, and would not be comforted, because they are not (Jeremiah first wrote this of mothers watching sons marched into exile centuries earlier; Matthew hears the same grief coming out of Bethlehem and does not soften it, explain it, or hurry past it -- the weeping is simply left standing in the text).",
  "Matthew 2:19": "But when Herod was dead, behold, an angel of the Lord appeareth in a dream to Joseph in Egypt (the third dream given to Joseph in this account, and the third time Matthew records no reply from him at all),",
  "Matthew 2:22": "But when he heard that Archelaus did reign in Judea in the room of his father Herod, he was afraid to go thither: notwithstanding, being warned of God in a dream, he turned aside into the parts of Galilee (a fourth dream, a fourth move made without a single recorded word -- everything these two chapters know of Joseph's faith comes from what he did, never from anything he said):",
  "Matthew 2:23": "And he came and dwelt in a city called Nazareth: that it might be fulfilled which was spoken by the prophets, He shall be called a Nazarene (Nazareth was a punchline more than a landmark -- decades later, Nathanael's first reaction to the name was to ask whether anything good could come out of it. Matthew is saying that reputation was part of the plan, not an accident of geography).",
  "Matthew 3:1": "In those days came John the Baptist, preaching in the wilderness of Judea (a stretch of dry country near Jericho, not a random backdrop -- the same wilderness Israel had wandered in centuries before),",
  "Matthew 3:3": "For this is he that was spoken of by the prophet Esaias (Isaiah, in its Greek spelling), saying, The voice of one crying in the wilderness, Prepare ye the way of the Lord, make his paths straight.",
  "Matthew 3:7": "But when he saw many of the Pharisees and Sadducees (two rival Jewish parties that agreed on almost nothing except showing up together here) come to his baptism, he said unto them, O generation of vipers, who hath warned you to flee from the wrath to come?",
  "Matthew 3:8": "Bring forth therefore fruits meet for repentance (the outward evidence of an inward change, not a payment made to secure one):",
  "Matthew 3:9": "And think not to say within yourselves, We have Abraham to our father: for I say unto you, that God is able of these stones to raise up children unto Abraham (He tells them their bloodline is not a fallback plan -- God could make Abraham's children out of rocks if He needed to).",
  "Matthew 3:10": "And now also the axe is laid unto the root of the trees: therefore every tree which bringeth not forth good fruit is hewn down, and cast into the fire (the tree pictures the nation corporately, the same picture Paul returns to in Romans eleven -- Israel cut down for unbelief, not any one Israelite).",
  "Matthew 3:12": "Whose fan is in his hand, and he will throughly purge his floor, and gather his wheat into the garner (the old picture of tossing grain into the wind so the husk blows off and the kernel drops back down); but he will burn up the chaff with unquenchable fire (chaff was never grain to begin with -- it is what is left over once the real kernel is gone, which makes this a picture of what had no substance, not a warning about grain that later failed).",
  "Matthew 3:13": "Then cometh Jesus from Galilee to Jordan unto John, to be baptized of him (a thirty-year story, and this is the verse where the actual ministry finally starts).",
  "Matthew 3:15": "And Jesus answering said unto him, Suffer it to be so now: for thus it becometh us to fulfil all righteousness (Paul later calls baptism a picture of death, burial, and resurrection -- Jesus goes under this water for a repentance He personally has no need of, three years before He goes under something far heavier for sins that were never His either). Then he suffered him.",
  "Matthew 3:16": "And Jesus, when he was baptized, went up straightway out of the water: and, lo, the heavens were opened unto him, and he saw the Spirit of God descending like a dove, and lighting upon him (Luke adds that this happened while Jesus was praying -- the anointing follows the prayer, not the other way around):",
  "Matthew 3:17": "And lo a voice from heaven, saying, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased (three Persons in one moment: the Son coming up out of the water, the Spirit coming down as a dove, and now the Father speaking from above. Scripture rarely argues the Trinity outright -- here it simply lets it happen).",
  "Matthew 4:1": "Then was Jesus led up of the Spirit into the wilderness to be tempted of the devil (the Last Adam, facing the same kind of test the first Adam failed -- except Adam had a garden with everything he could want, and this test happens in a desert with nothing).",
  "Matthew 4:2": "And when he had fasted forty days and forty nights, he was afterward an hungred (the temptation waits for the exact moment His strength is lowest -- not the beginning of the fast, the end of it).",
  "Matthew 4:4": "But he answered and said, It is written, Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceedeth out of the mouth of God (a real, physical, forty-day hunger, answered with a sentence from Deuteronomy instead of a miracle -- the shortcut is refused before it's even taken).",
  "Matthew 4:6": "And saith unto him, If thou be the Son of God, cast thyself down: for it is written, He shall give his angels charge concerning thee (Satan quotes Psalm 91 accurately and still uses it wrongly -- the verse promises protection along the path of obedience, not a stunt staged to test whether the promise holds up).",
  "Matthew 4:7": "Jesus said unto him, It is written again, Thou shalt not tempt the Lord thy God (one line of Scripture answering another -- Jesus does not out-argue the devil here, He out-quotes him).",
  "Matthew 4:9": "And saith unto him, All these things will I give thee, if thou wilt fall down and worship me (the same kingdoms the Father had already promised the Son, offered again on a shortcut -- minus a cross, and minus the Father).",
  "Matthew 4:10": "Then saith Jesus unto him, Get thee hence, Satan (the first time in this Gospel Jesus addresses Satan directly): for it is written, Thou shalt worship the Lord thy God, and him only shalt thou serve.",
  "Matthew 4:11": "Then the devil leaveth him, and, behold, angels came and ministered unto him (Luke adds that the devil left only \"for a season\" -- this is the end of round one, not the last temptation Jesus would face).",
  "Matthew 4:17": "From that time Jesus began to preach, and to say, Repent: for the kingdom of heaven is at hand (the same message John had already been preaching -- Jesus does not open His ministry with a different one).",
  "Matthew 4:19": "And he saith unto them, Follow me, and I will make you fishers of men (He recruits men still holding their nets, mid-shift, with no resume asked for and no notice given).",
  "Matthew 4:22": "And they immediately left the ship and their father, and followed him (Zebedee is left standing in the boat -- Scripture never records what he thought about it).",
  "Matthew 4:24": "And his fame went throughout all Syria: and they brought unto him all sick people that were taken with divers diseases and torments, and those which were possessed with devils, and those which were lunatick, and those that had the palsy; and he healed them (the chapter's first summary of His public life closes on this line -- nobody in the list is described being turned away).",
  "Matthew 5:1": "And seeing the multitudes, he went up into a mountain: and when he was set (sitting down was the signal, for a rabbi, that real instruction was beginning), his disciples came unto him:",
  "Matthew 5:3": "Blessed are the poor in spirit: for theirs is the kingdom of heaven (not the financially poor -- those who know they have nothing to bring God, which is where every line after this one actually starts).",
  "Matthew 5:9": "Blessed are the peacemakers: for they shall be called the children of God (a peacemaker is not a peacekeeper -- keeping the peace avoids a conflict, making it ends one, usually at some cost to the one making it).",
  "Matthew 5:13": "Ye are the salt of the earth: but if the salt have lost his savour, wherewith shall it be salted? (salt from that region was mined mixed with gypsum and other minerals; the true salt could leach out over time and leave a white residue that looked like salt and did none of what salt does) it is thenceforth good for nothing, but to be cast out, and to be trodden under foot of men.",
  "Matthew 5:16": "Let your light so shine before men, that they may see your good works, and glorify your Father which is in heaven (the works are meant to be seen -- the glory is just never supposed to land on the one doing them).",
  "Matthew 5:17": "Think not that I am come to destroy the law, or the prophets: I am not come to destroy, but to fulfil (a builder finishing the house is not tearing up the blueprint -- he is the reason it was drawn in the first place).",
  "Matthew 5:19": "Whosoever therefore shall break one of these least commandments, and shall teach men so, he shall be called the least in the kingdom of heaven: but whosoever shall do and teach them, the same shall be called great in the kingdom of heaven (He is describing rank inside the Kingdom, not admission to it -- calling someone 'least' still leaves them in the room).",
  "Matthew 5:20": "For I say unto you, That except your righteousness shall exceed the righteousness of the scribes and Pharisees, ye shall in no case enter into the kingdom of heaven (the righteousness in view is not extra rule-keeping stacked on top of theirs -- it is a different kind entirely, the kind only the new birth supplies).",
  "Matthew 5:29": "And if thy right eye offend thee, pluck it out, and cast it from thee: for it is profitable for thee that one of thy members should perish, and not that thy whole body should be cast into hell (He is not prescribing surgery -- a blind man can still lust; the organ was never the real offender).",
  "Matthew 5:30": "And if thy right hand offend thee, cut it off, and cast it from thee: for it is profitable for thee that one of thy members should perish, and not that thy whole body should be cast into hell (the hand stands in for whatever habit keeps producing the sin -- cutting it off is the picture; the Cross is the actual method).",
  "Matthew 5:44": "But I say unto you, Love your enemies, bless them that curse you, do good to them that hate you, and pray for them which despitefully use you, and persecute you (the group Jesus names here -- people who curse, hate, and mistreat you -- is exactly who He's asking to be loved, not a gentler group substituted in);",
  "Matthew 5:45": "That ye may be the children of your Father which is in heaven: for he maketh his sun to rise on the evil and on the good, and sendeth rain on the just and on the unjust (God's ordinary kindness in the weather checks nobody's record first).",
  "Matthew 5:48": "Be ye therefore perfect, even as your Father which is in heaven is perfect (the word is teleios -- complete, full-grown, brought to its intended finish, the same word used elsewhere for a mature harvest, not a claim about sinless performance).",
  "Matthew 6:1": "Take heed that ye do not your alms before men, to be seen of them: otherwise ye have no reward of your Father which is in heaven (the audience is what's being corrected, not the action -- giving in front of people is not the problem; giving for people is).",
  "Matthew 6:9": "After this manner therefore pray ye: Our Father which art in heaven, Hallowed be thy name (the first word of the model prayer is a relationship, not a request -- everything that follows is asked by a child, not negotiated by a petitioner).",
  "Matthew 6:15": "But if ye forgive not men their trespasses, neither will your Father forgive your trespasses (the forgiveness in view is the daily, relational kind between Father and child -- a heart that will not forgive has shut the very channel it is asking Him to open).",
  "Matthew 6:19": "Lay not up for yourselves treasures upon earth, where moth and rust doth corrupt, and where thieves break through and steal (three separate ways to lose something -- decay, theft, and time -- covering every kind of security money was never actually able to provide):",
  "Matthew 6:21": "For where your treasure is, there will your heart be also (the verse runs backward from how it usually gets quoted -- the heart doesn't decide the treasure, the treasure trains the heart).",
  "Matthew 6:24": "No man can serve two masters: for either he will hate the one, and love the other; or else he will hold to the one, and despise the other. Ye cannot serve God and mammon (an Aramaic word for wealth, personified here as a rival master rather than a neutral tool).",
  "Matthew 6:33": "But seek ye first the kingdom of God, and his righteousness; and all these things shall be added unto you (the sentence promises addition, not subtraction -- nothing on the list before this verse gets taken away by seeking the kingdom first; it gets handled while attention is spent somewhere better).",
  "Matthew 7:1": "Judge not, that ye be not judged (not a ban on discernment -- this same chapter goes on to say a false prophet is spotted by his fruit. What's forbidden is standing in as judge, not the ability to see clearly).",
  "Matthew 7:7": "Ask, and it shall be given you; seek, and ye shall find; knock, and it shall be opened unto you (three verbs, each more active than the last -- asking is the easy one to stop at, and Jesus doesn't stop there).",
  "Matthew 7:12": "Therefore all things whatsoever ye would that men should do to you, do ye even so to them: for this is the law and the prophets (the Golden Rule closes out the section on judging and asking with one working test -- when in doubt, run the action through what you'd want done to you first).",
  "Matthew 7:13": "Enter ye in at the strait gate: for wide is the gate, and broad is the way, that leadeth to destruction, and many there be which go in thereat (the gate is named elsewhere as a person, not a performance -- narrow because there is only one, not because it is hard to find).",
  "Matthew 7:19": "Every tree that bringeth not forth good fruit is hewn down, and cast into the fire (the test he just gave for spotting false prophets ends here -- whatever doesn't produce Christ-centered fruit doesn't survive the scrutiny).",
  "Matthew 7:21": "Not every one that saith unto me, Lord, Lord, shall enter into the kingdom of heaven; but he that doeth the will of my Father which is in heaven (the next two verses define what doing the Father's will actually means -- knowing Christ, not compiling a résumé of ministry activity).",
  "Matthew 7:23": "And then will I profess unto them, I never knew you: depart from me, ye that work iniquity (the charge is not too little activity -- these people list miracles in the previous verse -- the charge is that he never knew them; relationship was missing, not résumé).",
  "Matthew 7:24": "Therefore whosoever heareth these sayings of mine, and doeth them, I will liken him unto a wise man, which built his house upon a rock (the difference between the wise builder and the foolish one in the next three verses was never the storm -- both get hit by the same one. The difference was underneath, decided before the rain ever started, and invisible until the flood came looking for it):",
  "Matthew 8:3": "And Jesus put forth his hand, and touched him, saying, I will; be thou clean (in the sentence's own order, the cleansing lands on the words \"I will,\" not on the touch that follows -- the hand only confirms what the word already did). And immediately his leprosy was cleansed.",
  "Matthew 8:8": "The centurion answered and said, Lord, I am not worthy that thou shouldest come under my roof: but speak the word only, and my servant shall be healed (a professional soldier reasoning from what he knows -- a command doesn't have to travel in person to take effect).",
  "Matthew 8:9": "For I am a man under authority, having soldiers under me: and I say to this man, Go, and he goeth; and to another, Come, and he cometh; and to my servant, Do this, and he doeth it (he treats Jesus's word the same way he treats his own -- as something that simply gets obeyed).",
  "Matthew 8:10": "When Jesus heard it, he marvelled, and said to them that followed, Verily I say unto you, I have not found so great faith, no, not in Israel (Scripture records Jesus marvelling only twice -- here, at a Gentile soldier's faith, and once more at Nazareth's unbelief. This is the better of the two).",
  "Matthew 8:12": "But the children of the kingdom shall be cast out into outer darkness: there shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth (the children of the kingdom named here are Israel by birthright -- birthright, this verse shows, was never the same thing as belonging).",
  "Matthew 8:17": "That it might be fulfilled which was spoken by Esaias the prophet, saying, Himself took our infirmities, and bare our sicknesses (Isaiah's line about the Suffering Servant, applied here to a single evening of healings rather than to the cross -- Matthew reads the same verse as covering both).",
  "Matthew 8:20": "And Jesus saith unto him, The foxes have holes, and the birds of the air have nests; but the Son of man hath not where to lay his head (every other creature in that sentence has somewhere to belong. The Son of Man, on His own earth, does not).",
  "Matthew 8:22": "But Jesus said unto him, Follow me; and let the dead bury their dead (a hard word to a man asking for time -- the request in that culture usually meant waiting years for an aging parent to die, not stepping away from a funeral already underway).",
  "Matthew 8:26": "And he saith unto them, Why are ye fearful, O ye of little faith? Then he arose, and rebuked the winds and the sea; and there was a great calm (the rebuke comes before the calm -- Jesus corrects the fear and the weather in the same breath, treating both as the same kind of problem).",
  "Matthew 8:27": "But the men marvelled, saying, What manner of man is this, that even the winds and the sea obey him! (the question the disciples ask here is the one the whole Gospel is spent answering).",
  "Matthew 8:29": "And, behold, they cried out, saying, What have we to do with thee, Jesus, thou Son of God? art thou come hither to torment us before the time? (the demons identify Him correctly on sight and know exactly what is coming -- theirs is a clearer confession than most of the crowds following Him ever manage).",
  "Matthew 9:6": "But that ye may know that the Son of man hath power on earth to forgive sins, (then saith he to the sick of the palsy,) Arise, take up thy bed, and go unto thine house (Jesus answers the accusation of blasphemy by doing the one thing only God can do, in front of the very men questioning His right to do it).",
  "Matthew 9:9": "And as Jesus passed forth from thence, he saw a man, named Matthew, sitting at the receipt of custom (a tax booth -- collecting Roman taxes from his own countrymen, which made him one of the most hated men in whatever town he worked): and he saith unto him, Follow me. And he arose, and followed him.",
  "Matthew 9:12": "But when Jesus heard that, he said unto them, They that be whole need not a physician, but they that are sick (He does not argue that the accusation is wrong -- He agrees with it and states plainly why He's there).",
  "Matthew 9:13": "But go ye and learn what that meaneth, I will have mercy, and not sacrifice: for I am not come to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance (Hosea's line, aimed at people who kept every ritual and still missed the point of it).",
  "Matthew 9:15": "And Jesus said unto them, Can the children of the bridechamber mourn, as long as the bridegroom is with them? but the days will come, when the bridegroom shall be taken from them, and then shall they fast (the first time Jesus calls Himself the bridegroom -- a title the rest of the New Testament keeps building on, all the way to the wedding at the end of Revelation).",
  "Matthew 9:20": "And, behold, a woman, which was diseased with an issue of blood twelve years, came behind him, and touched the hem of his garment (under the law her condition made her ceremonially unclean, and touching her should have made Him unclean too -- she reaches for Him anyway, and He never draws back):",
  "Matthew 9:22": "But Jesus turned him about, and when he saw her, he said, Daughter, be of good comfort; thy faith hath made thee whole. And the woman was made whole from that hour (He calls her daughter before He explains anything -- the relationship comes before the theology).",
  "Matthew 9:25": "But when the people were put forth, he went in, and took her by the hand, and the maid arose (life touches death in this sentence, and death loses).",
  "Matthew 9:29": "Then touched he their eyes, saying, According to your faith be it unto you (not a measurement of how much faith they were carrying -- simply a statement that their trust met Him at the exact point of their need).",
  "Matthew 9:36": "But when he saw the multitudes, he was moved with compassion on them, because they fainted, and were scattered abroad, as sheep having no shepherd (compassion in the Gospels is never only a feeling Jesus has -- it's always the thing that happens right before He does something about it).",
  "Matthew 9:37": "Then saith he unto his disciples, The harvest truly is plenteous, but the labourers are few (an abundance problem, framed here as a shortage of workers rather than a shortage of harvest);",

  "Matthew 10:4": "Simon the Canaanite, and Judas Iscariot, who also betrayed him (a former zealot -- someone who would have killed a Roman collaborator on principle -- and Matthew, an actual tax collector working for Rome, are on the same list of twelve).",
  "Matthew 10:8": "Heal the sick, cleanse the lepers, raise the dead, cast out devils: freely ye have received, freely give (the instruction covers everything they are about to do -- it came to them with no bill attached, so it goes out the same way).",
  "Matthew 10:16": "Behold, I send you forth as sheep in the midst of wolves: be ye therefore wise as serpents, and harmless as doves (an unusual pairing -- shrewdness and gentleness are not often asked of the same person at the same time).",
  "Matthew 10:29": "Are not two sparrows sold for a farthing? and one of them shall not fall on the ground without your Father (the cheapest thing sold in the market, and still on His radar).",
  "Matthew 10:31": "Fear ye not therefore, ye are of more value than many sparrows (the argument runs from the least valuable bird in the market to the most overlooked detail on a person's head, and lands on you).",
  "Matthew 10:39": "He that findeth his life shall lose it: and he that loseth his life for my sake shall find it (the sentence is built to sound like a trick, but holding on and letting go simply lead to opposite places).",
  "Matthew 10:40": "He that receiveth you receiveth me, and he that receiveth me receiveth him that sent me (a chain of welcome running all the way back to the Father -- how someone treats the messenger counts as how they treated the One who sent him).",
  "Matthew 10:42": "And whosoever shall give to drink unto one of these little ones a cup of cold water only in the name of a disciple, verily I say unto you, he shall in no wise lose his reward (the smallest kindness on the whole list -- a cup of water, not even warmed -- and it's the one Jesus singles out to guarantee).",

  "Matthew 11:3": "And said unto him, Art thou he that should come, or do we look for another? (John baptized Jesus, heard the voice from heaven at the Jordan, and is now, from a prison cell, asking whether he got it right -- even the one who introduced Him has a moment of doubt).",
  "Matthew 11:6": "And blessed is he, whosoever shall not be offended in me (an odd word to attach to the Messiah -- but Jesus never arrived the way anyone expected, and this line quietly admits it).",
  "Matthew 11:11": "Verily I say unto you, Among them that are born of women there hath not risen a greater than John the Baptist: notwithstanding he that is least in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he (not a verdict on John's character -- a statement about what the kingdom is about to become. Even the greatest man born under the old arrangement stands outside what is coming).",
  "Matthew 11:19": "The Son of man came eating and drinking, and they say, Behold a man gluttonous, and a winebibber, a friend of publicans and sinners. But wisdom is justified of her children (John fasted and was accused of having a demon; Jesus ate at people's tables and was accused of being one of them. Nothing either man did was ever going to satisfy the same crowd).",
  "Matthew 11:25": "At that time Jesus answered and said, I thank thee, O Father, Lord of heaven and earth, because thou hast hid these things from the wise and prudent, and hast revealed them unto babes (the credentialed experts in this story miss it entirely, and the ones who get it are the ones with nothing to bring but open hands).",
  "Matthew 11:27": "All things are delivered unto me of my Father: and no man knoweth the Son, but the Father; neither knoweth any man the Father, save the Son, and he to whomsoever the Son will reveal him (no direct route to the Father bypasses the Son -- knowing God, in this verse, is not a matter of study but of being shown).",
  "Matthew 11:28": "Come unto me, all ye that labour and are heavy laden, and I will give you rest (not come and work harder -- come and stop. The invitation is addressed to people already exhausted by trying).",
  "Matthew 11:29": "Take my yoke upon you, and learn of me; for I am meek and lowly in heart: and ye shall find rest unto your souls (a yoke is built for two -- and this is the only place in the Gospels where Jesus describes His own heart in His own words).",
  "Matthew 11:30": "For my yoke is easy, and my burden is light (easy does not mean nothing -- it means shared. A yoke built for two carries weight differently than a yoke built for one).",

  "Matthew 12:3": "But he said unto them, Have ye not read what David did, when he was an hungred, and they that were with him (David, fleeing for his life, breaks a rule reserved for priests, and Scripture records it without a word of correction);",
  "Matthew 12:6": "But I say unto you, That in this place is one greater than the temple (the building everyone in that room had been taught to revere above everything else, and Jesus points at Himself standing next to it).",
  "Matthew 12:8": "For the Son of man is Lord even of the sabbath day (not claiming an exception to the rule -- claiming to have written it).",
  "Matthew 12:18": "Behold my servant, whom I have chosen; my beloved, in whom my soul is well pleased: I will put my spirit upon him, and he shall shew judgment to the Gentiles (Isaiah wrote this seven centuries early, and Matthew quotes it word for word -- the same voice that spoke at the Jordan had already spoken through a prophet long before the river existed).",
  "Matthew 12:20": "A bruised reed shall he not break, and smoking flax shall he not quench, till he send forth judgment unto victory (a reed already bent and a wick already going out -- the two most fragile, least impressive images available, used here for how the Messiah treats what is barely hanging on).",
  "Matthew 12:29": "Or else how can one enter into a strong man's house, and spoil his goods, except he first bind the strong man? and then he will spoil his house (Jesus describes His own mission in one sentence, before He's done it -- into the stronghold, binding the one guarding it, and taking back what was stolen).",
  "Matthew 12:40": "For as Jonas was three days and three nights in the whale's belly; so shall the Son of man be three days and three nights in the heart of the earth (the only sign He offers a generation demanding one -- not a miracle to watch, but a death and a return from it).",
  "Matthew 12:42": "The queen of the south shall rise up in the judgment with this generation, and shall condemn it: for she came from the uttermost parts of the earth to hear the wisdom of Solomon; and, behold, a greater than Solomon is here (she crossed a continent for wisdom that only pointed toward Him; the people standing in front of Him would not cross the room).",
  "Matthew 12:50": "For whosoever shall do the will of my Father which is in heaven, the same is my brother, and sister, and mother (family, in this sentence, is redrawn around a shared Father rather than a shared birth).",

  "Matthew 13:8": "But other fell into good ground, and brought forth fruit, some an hundredfold, some sixtyfold, some thirtyfold (the parable does not rank the sower's effort by soil type -- the seed and the sowing are identical every time; the harvest is simply what different ground does with the same seed).",
  "Matthew 13:11": "He answered and said unto them, Because it is given unto you to know the mysteries of the kingdom of heaven, but to them it is not given (not a reward for cleverness -- the disciples did not earn the explanation, it was given to them).",
  "Matthew 13:16": "But blessed are your eyes, for they see: and your ears, for they hear.",
  "Matthew 13:17": "For verily I say unto you, That many prophets and righteous men have desired to see those things which ye see, and have not seen them; and to hear those things which ye hear, and have not heard them (David, Isaiah, every prophet who wrote toward a coming King -- all of them wrote toward a day none of them got to stand in. The disciples are standing in it).",
  "Matthew 13:24": "Another parable put he forth unto them, saying, The kingdom of heaven is likened unto a man which sowed good seed in his field:",
  "Matthew 13:30": "Let both grow together until the harvest: and in the time of harvest I will say to the reapers, Gather ye together first the tares, and bind them in bundles to burn them: but gather the wheat into my barn (the servants want to start pulling weeds immediately; the owner tells them to wait -- sorting is not a job he hands off early).",
  "Matthew 13:31": "Another parable put he forth unto them, saying, The kingdom of heaven is like to a grain of mustard seed, which a man took, and sowed in his field:",
  "Matthew 13:32": "Which indeed is the least of all seeds: but when it is grown, it is the greatest among herbs, and becometh a tree, so that the birds of the air come and lodge in the branches thereof (the parable measures the kingdom by the distance between its start and its finish -- the smallest seed available, grown into something birds can nest in).",
  "Matthew 13:44": "Again, the kingdom of heaven is like unto treasure hid in a field; the which when a man hath found, he hideth, and for joy thereof goeth and selleth all that he hath, and buyeth that field (he does not sell everything out of duty -- the text is specific that it is for joy. Nobody grieves what they give up for something worth more).",
  "Matthew 13:45": "Again, the kingdom of heaven is like unto a merchant man, seeking goodly pearls:",
  "Matthew 13:46": "Who, when he had found one pearl of great price, went and sold all that he had, and bought it (this merchant is a professional -- he already owns pearls, and knows exactly what he is looking at when he finds the one that ends the search).",
  "Matthew 13:52": "Then said he unto them, Therefore every scribe which is instructed unto the kingdom of heaven is like unto a man that is an householder, which bringeth forth out of his treasure things new and old (Jesus has spent this whole chapter teaching in parables nobody had used quite this way before -- and He calls that same gift \"old,\" as if it had been sitting in the treasury the entire time, waiting to be brought out).",
  "Matthew 13:54": "And when he was come into his own country, he taught them in their synagogue, insomuch that they were astonished, and said, Whence hath this man this wisdom, and these mighty works? (the people who watched Him grow up are the ones who cannot make sense of Him -- familiarity is doing exactly what it always does).",
  "Matthew 13:57": "And they were offended in him. But Jesus said unto them, A prophet is not without honour, save in his own country, and in his own house (the one place on earth Jesus was underestimated was the town where everyone already thought they knew Him).",

  "Matthew 14:17": "And they say unto him, We have here but five loaves, and two fishes (not nothing, but nowhere near enough for the crowd in front of them -- and Jesus never asks for more than what is already in their hands).",
  "Matthew 14:19": "And he commanded the multitude to sit down on the grass, and took the five loaves, and the two fishes, and looking up to heaven, he blessed, and brake, and gave the loaves to his disciples, and the disciples to the multitude (took, blessed, broke, gave -- the same four verbs, in the same order, He uses again at a much smaller table not long before the cross).",
  "Matthew 14:27": "But straightway Jesus spake unto them, saying, Be of good cheer; it is I; be not afraid (He identifies Himself before He explains anything -- the fear is answered by who is walking toward them, not by a description of the situation).",
  "Matthew 14:29": "And he said, Come. And when Peter was come down out of the ship, he walked on the water, to go to Jesus (one word, and Peter becomes the only person in the account who ever did this -- not because he was different from the other eleven, but because he was the one who got out of the boat).",
  "Matthew 14:30": "But when he saw the wind boisterous, he was afraid; and beginning to sink, he cried, saying, Lord, save me (the shortest prayer in the Gospels, and one of the most answered).",
  "Matthew 14:31": "And immediately Jesus stretched forth his hand, and caught him, and said unto him, O thou of little faith, wherefore didst thou doubt? (the hand comes before the question -- Jesus rescues him first and asks about the doubt second).",
  "Matthew 14:36": "And besought him that they might only touch the hem of his garment: and as many as touched were made perfectly whole (they were asking for the smallest possible contact, and it was enough).",

  "Matthew 15:11": "Not that which goeth into the mouth defileth a man; but that which cometh out of the mouth, this defileth a man (the Pharisees were tracking unwashed hands; Jesus points somewhere they were not looking -- the heart behind the words).",
  "Matthew 15:22": "And, behold, a woman of Canaan came out of the same coasts, and cried unto him, saying, Have mercy on me, O Lord, thou Son of David; my daughter is grievously vexed with a devil (a Gentile woman uses a Jewish title for the Messiah -- she has no claim to make on it and makes her case anyway).",
  "Matthew 15:25": "Then came she and worshipped him, saying, Lord, help me (three words, no argument attached, after the longer appeal in the verse before got no answer).",
  "Matthew 15:27": "And she said, Truth, Lord: yet the dogs eat of the crumbs which fall from their masters' table (she does not dispute the label -- she simply points out that even the label leaves room for her).",
  "Matthew 15:28": "Then Jesus answered and said unto her, O woman, great is thy faith: be it unto thee even as thou wilt. And her daughter was made whole from that very hour (only one other person in this Gospel is told to his face that his faith is great, and he was a Roman soldier -- both times, the compliment lands on an outsider).",
  "Matthew 15:32": "Then Jesus called his disciples unto him, and said, I have compassion on the multitude, because they continue with me now three days, and have nothing to eat: and I will not send them away fasting, lest they faint in the way (He notices the hunger before anyone asks Him to).",
  "Matthew 15:36": "And he took the seven loaves and the fishes, and gave thanks, and brake them, and gave to his disciples, and the disciples to the multitude (the second time this Gospel records the same sequence -- and the disciples still have nothing of their own to give; every basket runs back through Him).",

  "Matthew 16:13": "When Jesus came into the coasts of Cesarea Philippi, he asked his disciples, saying, Whom do men say that I the Son of man am? (Caesarea Philippi was built around a shrine to the god Pan, at the base of a cliff crowded with pagan idols -- Jesus asks who people say He is standing in the one place in Israel most stocked with rival answers).",
  "Matthew 16:15": "He saith unto them, But whom say ye that I am? (the question moves from a survey of public opinion to something none of them can answer secondhand).",
  "Matthew 16:16": "And Simon Peter answered and said, Thou art the Christ, the Son of the living God (two titles in one sentence -- the promised King, and God Himself, said of the same man in the same breath).",
  "Matthew 16:17": "And Jesus answered and said unto him, Blessed art thou, Simon Bar-jona: for flesh and blood hath not revealed it unto thee, but my Father which is in heaven (Peter did not work this out -- he is told, on the spot, that he could not have).",
  "Matthew 16:18": "And I say also unto thee, That thou art Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church; and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it (a wordplay sits underneath the English -- Peter is petros, a stone; the rock, petra, is the confession he just made, not the man making it).",
  "Matthew 16:21": "From that time forth began Jesus to shew unto his disciples, how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised again the third day (the first time He states it plainly -- not a possibility being weighed, but a fixed itinerary, spoken right after the disciples finally get His identity right).",
  "Matthew 16:23": "But he turned, and said unto Peter, Get thee behind me, Satan: thou art an offence unto me: for thou savourest not the things that be of God, but those that be of men (the same man just called blessed for a Father-given revelation is, one verse later, rebuked in nearly the words used on the devil in the wilderness -- getting the identity right did not mean getting the mission right).",
  "Matthew 16:24": "Then said Jesus unto his disciples, If any man will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me (spoken years before Rome ever nailed Him to one -- His listeners already knew exactly what a cross meant, and He uses the word anyway).",
  "Matthew 16:25": "For whosoever will save his life shall lose it: and whosoever will lose his life for my sake shall find it (the same word, life, doing two different jobs in one sentence -- the one held onto and the one given away).",
  "Matthew 16:26": "For what is a man profited, if he shall gain the whole world, and lose his own soul? or what shall a man give in exchange for his soul? (the whole world is the largest number Jesus could have named, and He still puts it on the losing side of the scale).",

  "Matthew 17:2": "And was transfigured before them: and his face did shine as the sun, and his raiment was white as the light (not light landing on Him from outside -- the text has it breaking through from within, out of the ordinary appearance He had worn for thirty years).",
  "Matthew 17:3": "And, behold, there appeared unto them Moses and Elias talking with him (the giver of the Law and the greatest of the prophets, both standing in front of the One both of them had written toward).",
  "Matthew 17:5": "While he yet spake, behold, a bright cloud overshadowed them: and behold a voice out of the cloud, which said, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased; hear ye him (Peter had just finished offering to build three shelters, treating Moses, Elijah, and Jesus as equals -- the voice from the cloud settles the matter before Peter finishes the thought: hear Him).",
  "Matthew 17:8": "And when they had lifted up their eyes, they saw no man, save Jesus only (Moses is gone, Elijah is gone, the cloud and the voice are gone -- what is left is what was always meant to be left).",
  "Matthew 17:20": "And Jesus said unto them, Because of your unbelief: for verily I say unto you, If ye have faith as a grain of mustard seed, ye shall say unto this mountain, Remove hence to yonder place; and it shall remove; and nothing shall be impossible unto you (He does not ask for a mountain-sized faith to move a mountain -- the smallest seed in the garden is enough).",
  "Matthew 17:27": "Notwithstanding, lest we should offend them, go thou to the sea, and cast an hook, and take up the fish that first cometh up; and when thou hast opened his mouth, thou shalt find a piece of money: that take, and give unto them for me and thee (a tax He does not legally owe, paid anyway, pulled out of a fish's mouth -- the most roundabout way available to avoid an argument not worth having).",

  "Matthew 18:2": "And Jesus called a little child unto him, and set him in the midst of them (the disciples had just been arguing about who ranks highest -- Jesus answers by putting the one person in the room with no rank at all in the center of it).",
  "Matthew 18:4": "Whosoever therefore shall humble himself as this little child, the same is greatest in the kingdom of heaven (greatness gets redefined by the same object lesson twice in five verses -- once for entry, now for rank).",
  "Matthew 18:5": "And whoso shall receive one such little child in my name receiveth me (the standard for welcoming Christ turns out to be how a person treats someone with nothing to offer in return).",
  "Matthew 18:10": "Take heed that ye despise not one of these little ones; for I say unto you, That in heaven their angels do always behold the face of my Father which is in heaven (whoever this world ranks as least significant, heaven has assigned a direct line to the Father's own face).",
  "Matthew 18:11": "For the Son of man is come to save that which was lost (a one-sentence summary of the whole mission, dropped into the middle of a chapter about children and forgiveness).",
  "Matthew 18:19": "Again I say unto you, That if two of you shall agree on earth as touching any thing that they shall ask, it shall be done for them of my Father which is in heaven.",
  "Matthew 18:20": "For where two or three are gathered together in my name, there am I in the midst of them (the smallest possible gathering still qualifies -- two or three, not a crowd, and He names Himself present in it).",
  "Matthew 18:21": "Then came Peter to him, and said, Lord, how oft shall my brother sin against me, and I forgive him? till seven times? (Peter offers a number he clearly thinks is generous -- the rabbis of his day taught that three times was enough).",
  "Matthew 18:22": "Jesus saith unto him, I say not unto thee, Until seven times: but, Until seventy times seven (not a new number to count to -- a number large enough that nobody keeping score could keep up with it).",
  "Matthew 18:24": "And when he had begun to reckon, one was brought unto him, which owed him ten thousand talents (a debt so large the original hearers would have laughed -- no servant could rack up a sum like that in a lifetime, which is the point).",
  "Matthew 18:27": "Then the lord of that servant was moved with compassion, and loosed him, and forgave him the debt (no payment plan, no partial mercy -- the entire impossible number, cancelled in one sentence).",

  "Matthew 19:5": "For this cause shall a man leave father and mother, and shall cleave to his wife: and they twain shall be one flesh?",
  "Matthew 19:6": "Wherefore they are no more twain, but one flesh. What therefore God hath joined together, let not man put asunder (Jesus does not cite tradition or a rabbinic ruling to answer the Pharisees -- He goes back to Genesis, before there was a law to argue about).",
  "Matthew 19:14": "But Jesus said, Suffer little children, and forbid them not, to come unto me: for of such is the kingdom of heaven (the disciples are guarding His time; He overrules them in favor of the people with the least to offer Him).",
  "Matthew 19:21": "Jesus said unto him, If thou wilt be perfect, go and sell that thou hast, and give to the poor, and thou shalt have treasure in heaven: and come and follow me (the man asked what he still lacked -- Jesus names the one thing he already has too much of).",
  "Matthew 19:22": "But when the young man heard that saying, he went away sorrowful: for he had great possessions (he came running, according to Mark, and leaves slowly -- the only person in the Gospels recorded turning down a direct invitation from Jesus).",
  "Matthew 19:26": "But Jesus beheld them, and said unto them, With men this is impossible; but with God all things are possible (the disciples had just heard that no one can qualify -- His answer is not a loophole, it is a different basis for entry altogether).",
  "Matthew 19:29": "And every one that hath forsaken houses, or brethren, or sisters, or father, or mother, or wife, or children, or lands, for my name's sake, shall receive an hundredfold, and shall inherit everlasting life (whatever the rich young ruler could not let go of, this verse names the trade Jesus was actually offering him).",

  "Matthew 20:1": "For the kingdom of heaven is like unto a man that is an householder, which went out early in the morning to hire labourers into his vineyard (Jesus is answering Peter's question from the end of the last chapter -- what do we get for giving everything up? -- and the answer arrives as a parable about pay, not a straight number).",
  "Matthew 20:14": "Take that thine is, and go thy way: I will give unto this last, even as unto thee (the man hired at dawn is not shorted a single coin -- he only feels shorted once he sees what grace looks like toward someone else).",
  "Matthew 20:15": "Is it not lawful for me to do what I will with mine own? Is thine eye evil, because I am good? (the complaint in this parable was never about fairness -- everyone got exactly what was promised. It was someone else's generosity feeling like a personal loss).",
  "Matthew 20:19": "And shall deliver him to the Gentiles to mock, and to scourge, and to crucify him: and the third day he shall rise again (each detail named in advance -- the mocking, the whipping, the method of execution, and the day it reverses).",
  "Matthew 20:22": "But Jesus answered and said, Ye know not what ye ask. Are ye able to drink of the cup that I shall drink of, and to be baptized with the baptism that I am baptized with? They say unto him, We are able (they answer yes to a question about suffering while picturing thrones -- the request and the answer to it are not describing the same kingdom yet).",
  "Matthew 20:28": "Even as the Son of man came not to be ministered unto, but to minister, and to give his life a ransom for many (a ransom is paid to free someone who cannot free themselves -- this Gospel names exactly what the payment would be, chapters before it happens).",
  "Matthew 20:34": "So Jesus had compassion on them, and touched their eyes: and immediately their eyes received sight, and they followed him (compassion, touch, sight, and then a decision to follow -- the miracle and the response happen in the same verse, in that order).",

  "Matthew 21:5": "Tell ye the daughter of Sion, Behold, thy King cometh unto thee, meek, and sitting upon an ass, and a colt the foal of an ass (Zechariah wrote this five centuries before a king ever entered Jerusalem this way -- on a borrowed animal, not a warhorse, exactly as the prophecy specified).",
  "Matthew 21:9": "And the multitudes that went before, and that followed, cried, saying, Hosanna to the Son of David: Blessed is he that cometh in the name of the Lord; Hosanna in the highest (Hosanna means \"save now\" -- the crowd is quoting a psalm and making a request in the same breath, days before anyone understands what saving them would actually cost).",
  "Matthew 21:13": "And said unto them, It is written, My house shall be called the house of prayer; but ye have made it a den of thieves (He quotes the very verse that names what the building was for, against what it had actually become).",
  "Matthew 21:14": "And the blind and the lame came to him in the temple; and he healed them (the same courtyard He had just overturned tables in becomes, one verse later, a place people walk into broken and walk out whole).",
  "Matthew 21:16": "And said unto him, Hearest thou what these say? And Jesus saith unto them, Yea; have ye never read, Out of the mouth of babes and sucklings thou hast perfected praise? (the religious leaders want the children quieted; Jesus answers with a psalm that says children's praise was never the weak link).",
  "Matthew 21:21": "Jesus answered and said unto them, Verily I say unto you, If ye have faith, and doubt not, ye shall not only do this which is done to the fig tree, but also if ye shall say unto this mountain, Be thou removed, and be thou cast into the sea; it shall be done.",
  "Matthew 21:22": "And all things, whatsoever ye shall ask in prayer, believing, ye shall receive (the promise sits right next to a hard example -- a tree withered in a day -- so the disciples see what this kind of praying looks like before He ever hands them the principle).",
  "Matthew 21:31": "Whether of them twain did the will of his father? They say unto him, The first. Jesus saith unto them, Verily I say unto you, That the publicans and the harlots go into the kingdom of God before you (the religious leaders answer their own trap correctly, and Jesus tells them the answer describes the people they look down on, not them).",
  "Matthew 21:32": "For John came unto you in the way of righteousness, and ye believed him not: but the publicans and the harlots believed him: and ye, when ye had seen it, repented not afterward, that ye might believe him (the ones with a reputation to protect believed nothing; the ones with nothing left to lose believed everything).",
  "Matthew 21:37": "But last of all he sent unto them his son, saying, They will reverence my son (the parable moves through servant after servant before naming who it was really about all along).",
  "Matthew 21:42": "Jesus saith unto them, Did ye never read in the scriptures, The stone which the builders rejected, the same is become the head of the corner: this is the Lord's doing, and it is marvellous in our eyes? (the very builders trained to recognize a cornerstone are the ones who threw this one out -- the psalm was written a thousand years before it happened to Him).",

  "Matthew 22:21": "They say unto him, Cesar's. Then saith he unto them, Render therefore unto Cesar the things which are Cesar's; and unto God the things that are God's (they set the trap expecting Him to pick a side -- Rome or God -- and He answers with a category neither side saw coming: both, in their own place).",
  "Matthew 22:29": "Jesus answered and said unto them, Ye do err, not knowing the scriptures, nor the power of God (the Sadducees built an elaborate hypothetical to disprove the resurrection, and Jesus locates the error before He ever gets to their question -- they do not know what they think they know).",
  "Matthew 22:32": "I am the God of Abraham, and the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob? God is not the God of the dead, but of the living (Jesus builds His entire case for the resurrection on a single present-tense verb, spoken to Moses centuries after Abraham had died).",
  "Matthew 22:37": "Jesus said unto him, Thou shalt love the Lord thy God with all thy heart, and with all thy soul, and with all thy mind.",
  "Matthew 22:39": "And the second is like unto it, Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself (asked for one commandment, He gives two -- and says the second is cut from the same cloth as the first, not a lesser add-on).",
  "Matthew 22:40": "On these two commandments hang all the law and the prophets (not two rules among hundreds -- the two everything else was written to explain).",
  "Matthew 22:42": "Saying, What think ye of Christ? whose son is he? They say unto him, The Son of David (the correct answer, straight out of the textbook -- and the question that follows shows how much more there is to it than the textbook answer).",
  "Matthew 22:44": "The Lord said unto my Lord, Sit thou on my right hand, till I make thine enemies thy footstool? (David calls his own descendant \"Lord,\" which only makes sense if the descendant existed, in some sense, before David did).",
  "Matthew 22:45": "If David then call him Lord, how is he his son? (a single verse holds both truths at once -- David's son by birth, David's Lord by nature -- and no one in the crowd has an answer for it).",

  "Matthew 23:8": "But be not ye called Rabbi: for one is your Master, even Christ; and all ye are brethren (the disciples are told not to collect the titles the Pharisees had just been shown collecting -- rank among believers gets flattened by the same verse that names who the real Teacher is).",
  "Matthew 23:11": "But he that is greatest among you shall be your servant (a definition of greatness that runs backward from every other one His audience had ever heard).",
  "Matthew 23:12": "And whosoever shall exalt himself shall be abased; and he that shall humble himself shall be exalted (the chapter just spent thirty-plus verses describing men doing the first half of this sentence in public -- Jesus states the second half as plain fact, not threat).",
  "Matthew 23:23": "Woe unto you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! for ye pay tithe of mint and anise and cummin, and have omitted the weightier matters of the law, judgment, mercy, and faith: these ought ye to have done, and not to leave the other undone (He names the exact kitchen herbs they measured out down to the leaf, and the three larger things -- judgment, mercy, faith -- they somehow missed entirely).",
  "Matthew 23:37": "O Jerusalem, Jerusalem, thou that killest the prophets, and stonest them which are sent unto thee, how often would I have gathered thy children together, even as a hen gathereth her chickens under her wings, and ye would not! (after a whole chapter of confrontation, it ends with Him grieving over the same city, reaching for the gentlest image available -- a hen covering her chicks -- for a people who would not let Him).",

  "Matthew 24:35": "Heaven and earth shall pass away, but my words shall not pass away (everything visible from where He is sitting -- the mountain, the temple He has just predicted will fall -- is given a shorter shelf life than a sentence He spoke out loud).",
  "Matthew 24:36": "But of that day and hour knoweth no man, no, not the angels of heaven, but my Father only (He has just described the event in detail -- lightning, gathered nations, trumpets -- and still says plainly that the timing is not His to disclose).",

  "Matthew 25:21": "His lord said unto him, Well done, thou good and faithful servant: thou hast been faithful over a few things, I will make thee ruler over many things: enter thou into the joy of thy lord (the commendation is for faithfulness, not for the size of the result -- the servant who started with five talents and the one who started with two hear the same sentence, word for word).",
  "Matthew 25:23": "His lord said unto him, Well done, good and faithful servant; thou hast been faithful over a few things, I will make thee ruler over many things: enter thou into the joy of thy lord (the measure was never the amount he started with).",
  "Matthew 25:34": "Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, ye blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world (an inheritance already prepared before the people receiving it existed to earn it).",
  "Matthew 25:35": "For I was an hungred, and ye gave me meat: I was thirsty, and ye gave me drink: I was a stranger, and ye took me in (Christ identifies Himself with the hungry, the thirsty, and the displaced before anyone in the story knows they were talking to Him).",
  "Matthew 25:36": "Naked, and ye clothed me: I was sick, and ye visited me: I was in prison, and ye came unto me.",
  "Matthew 25:40": "And the King shall answer and say unto them, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as ye have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, ye have done it unto me (the smallest kindness done to the least noticed person in the room turns out to have had the largest possible recipient).",

  "Matthew 26:12": "For in that she hath poured this ointment on my body, she did it for my burial (Mary anoints Him for a burial He has not had yet -- the one person in the Gospels who seems to have actually believed the resurrection was coming before it happened).",
  "Matthew 26:13": "Verily I say unto you, Wheresoever this gospel shall be preached in the whole world, there shall also this, that this woman hath done, be told for a memorial of her (a promise made two thousand years ago about how far this story would travel, kept in this very sentence being read right now).",
  "Matthew 26:26": "And as they were eating, Jesus took bread, and blessed it, and brake it, and gave it to the disciples, and said, Take, eat; this is my body (the bread is broken before anyone at the table understands what is about to happen to the Body it stands for).",
  "Matthew 26:27": "And he took the cup, and gave thanks, and gave it to them, saying, Drink ye all of it;",
  "Matthew 26:28": "For this is my blood of the new testament, which is shed for many for the remission of sins (a covenant announced at a dinner table hours before it was paid for).",
  "Matthew 26:29": "But I say unto you, I will not drink henceforth of this fruit of the vine, until that day when I drink it new with you in my Father's kingdom (He ends the meal by pointing past the cross to a table still to come).",
  "Matthew 26:39": "And he went a little further, and fell on his face, and prayed, saying, O my Father, if it be possible, let this cup pass from me: nevertheless not as I will, but as thou wilt (the most honest prayer in the Gospels, prayed by the one person with the standing to refuse who did not).",
  "Matthew 26:41": "Watch and pray, that ye enter not into temptation: the spirit indeed is willing, but the flesh is weak (said to three men who fell asleep instead of praying -- the diagnosis fits them, and the warning comes anyway).",
  "Matthew 26:42": "He went away again the second time, and prayed, saying, O my Father, if this cup may not pass away from me, except I drink it, thy will be done (the same request, prayed a second time, ending in the same surrender).",
  "Matthew 26:50": "And Jesus said unto him, Friend, wherefore art thou come? Then came they, and laid hands on Jesus, and took him (He calls the man kissing Him to betray Him \"friend\" -- not sarcasm, the last word Judas ever hears from Him).",
  "Matthew 26:52": "Then said Jesus unto him, Put up again thy sword into his place: for all they that take the sword shall perish with the sword (Peter draws a weapon to defend the one man in the garden who does not need defending).",
  "Matthew 26:53": "Thinkest thou that I cannot now pray to my Father, and he shall presently give me more than twelve legions of angels? (He names the exact rescue available to Him one sentence before declining to ask for it).",
  "Matthew 26:64": "Jesus saith unto him, Thou hast said: nevertheless I say unto you, Hereafter shall ye see the Son of man sitting on the right hand of power, and coming in the clouds of heaven (asked directly, under oath, on trial for His life, He answers with the one claim guaranteed to convict Him -- and makes it anyway).",
  "Matthew 26:75": "And Peter remembered the word of Jesus, which said unto him, Before the cock crow, thou shalt deny me thrice. And he went out, and wept bitterly (the same man who swore, hours earlier, that he would die before denying Christ -- the text does not excuse him, only records the weeping).",

  "Matthew 27:37": "And set up over his head his accusation written, THIS IS JESUS THE KING OF THE JEWS (posted in mockery, in three languages so no one passing could miss it -- and every word of the sign was true).",
  "Matthew 27:42": "He saved others; himself he cannot save. If he be the King of Israel, let him now come down from the cross, and we will believe him (the mockers have the verb wrong -- not cannot, but will not; coming down was the one thing available to Him that He refused to do).",
  "Matthew 27:45": "Now from the sixth hour there was darkness over all the land unto the ninth hour (midday to mid-afternoon, the sun blotted out over the one event history would end up dating everything else around).",
  "Matthew 27:46": "And about the ninth hour Jesus cried with a loud voice, saying, Eli, Eli, lama sabachthani? that is to say, My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me? (the one prayer in the Gospels where the answer is silence, spoken by the only person who had never once been left unanswered before).",
  "Matthew 27:50": "Jesus, when he had cried again with a loud voice, yielded up the ghost (a loud voice, not a fading one -- nothing about the way He died reads like a man whose strength simply ran out).",
  "Matthew 27:51": "And, behold, the veil of the temple was rent in twain from the top to the bottom; and the earth did quake, and the rocks rent; (a curtain sixty feet tall, torn from the top down -- the one direction no man standing on the ground could have reached).",
  "Matthew 27:54": "Now when the centurion, and they that were with him, watching Jesus, saw the earthquake, and those things that were done, they feared greatly, saying, Truly this was the Son of God (a Roman soldier says out loud, standing at the foot of the cross, what the Sanhedrin refused to say in the courtroom).",
  "Matthew 27:60": "And laid it in his own new tomb, which he had hewn out in the rock: and he rolled a great stone to the door of the sepulchre, and departed (a rich man's unused grave, given to a body that would only need it for three days).",

  "Matthew 28:5": "And the angel answered and said unto the women, Fear not ye: for I know that ye seek Jesus, which was crucified (the angel names the crucifixion outright, without flinching from the word -- the same event the whole book has been moving toward, spoken now from the other side of it).",
  "Matthew 28:6": "He is not here: for he is risen, as he said. Come, see the place where the Lord lay (four words undo everything the previous chapter accomplished -- he is not here. The invitation is not to believe it secondhand, but to go look).",
  "Matthew 28:9": "And as they went to tell his disciples, behold, Jesus met them, saying, All hail. And they came and held him by the feet, and worshipped him (they do not just see Him -- they hold Him, which settles the one question a vision or a ghost could never answer).",
  "Matthew 28:17": "And when they saw him, they worshipped him: but some doubted (the text does not hide the doubt to make the story sound cleaner -- both reactions are recorded in the same sentence, standing in the same room).",
  "Matthew 28:18": "And Jesus came and spake unto them, saying, All power is given unto me in heaven and in earth (a claim this size, spoken by a man who had been executed as a criminal seven weeks earlier).",
  "Matthew 28:20": "Teaching them to observe all things whatsoever I have commanded you: and, lo, I am with you alway, even unto the end of the world. Amen (the last words of this Gospel are a promise of presence, not absence -- the book closes with Him still in the room).",

  "Mark 1:1": "The beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God (the very first sentence of this Gospel names both His mission and His identity, before a single event of the story has happened);",
  "Mark 1:3": "The voice of one crying in the wilderness, Prepare ye the way of the Lord, make his paths straight (a prophecy written centuries earlier is read out loud, and the man it describes is standing in the crowd listening to it).",
  "Mark 1:8": "I indeed have baptized you with water: but he shall baptize you with the Holy Ghost (a baptism no man could perform, reserved for the One John says is still to come).",
  "Mark 1:11": "And there came a voice from heaven, saying, Thou art my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased (before He preaches a sermon or heals a single person, the Father's approval is already settled).",
  "Mark 1:17": "And Jesus said unto them, Come ye after me, and I will make you to become fishers of men (no resume requested -- a trade reassigned in the same sentence it was named in).",
  "Mark 1:24": "Saying, Let us alone; what have we to do with thee, thou Jesus of Nazareth? art thou come to destroy us? I know thee who thou art, the Holy One of God (the unclean spirit names Him correctly in the first chapter -- the religious leaders never manage it in sixteen).",
  "Mark 1:31": "And he came and took her by the hand, and lifted her up; and immediately the fever left her, and she ministered unto them (healed and serving within the same verse -- the pattern repeats throughout this Gospel).",
  "Mark 1:41": "And Jesus, moved with compassion, put forth his hand, and touched him, and saith unto him, I will; be thou clean (the shortest possible answer to a leper's only doubt, which was never about His power).",
  "Mark 1:45": "But he went out, and began to publish it much, and to blaze abroad the matter, insomuch that Jesus could no more openly enter into the city, but was without in desert places: and they came to him from every quarter (compassion, given away, costs Him His freedom of movement -- and He heals the man anyway).",

  "Mark 2:5": "When Jesus saw their faith, he said unto the sick of the palsy, Son, thy sins be forgiven thee (He addresses the problem underneath the one everyone came to watch Him fix).",
  "Mark 2:9": "Whether is it easier to say to the sick of the palsy, Thy sins be forgiven thee; or to say, Arise, and take up thy bed, and walk (He offers proof of the claim no one in the room could otherwise verify)?",
  "Mark 2:14": "And as he passed by, he saw Levi the son of Alpheus sitting at the receipt of custom, and said unto him, Follow me. And he arose and followed him (a tax collector's entire life turns on one clause).",
  "Mark 2:17": "When Jesus heard it, he saith unto them, They that are whole have no need of the physician, but they that are sick: I came not to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance (His mission statement, spoken at a dinner table surrounded by the very people it names).",
  "Mark 2:19": "And Jesus said unto them, Can the children of the bridechamber fast, while the bridegroom is with them? as long as they have the bridegroom with them, they cannot fast (He calls Himself the bridegroom two chapters before anyone has thought to ask who He is).",
  "Mark 2:20": "But the days will come, when the bridegroom shall be taken away from them, and then shall they fast in those days (a quiet reference to His own death, tucked inside a question about fasting).",
  "Mark 2:27": "And he said unto them, The sabbath was made for man, and not man for the sabbath (He redefines an institution He Himself established):",
  "Mark 2:28": "Therefore the Son of man is Lord also of the sabbath (and then claims ownership of it in the very next breath).",

  "Mark 3:5": "And when he had looked round about on them with anger, being grieved for the hardness of their hearts, he saith unto the man, Stretch forth thine hand. And he stretched it out: and his hand was restored whole as the other (anger and compassion sit in the same verse, aimed at two different targets in the same room).",
  "Mark 3:11": "And unclean spirits, when they saw him, fell down before him, and cried, saying, Thou art the Son of God (demons state plainly, in public, what the religious leaders standing beside them refuse to say).",
  "Mark 3:14": "And he ordained twelve, that they should be with him, and that he might send them forth to preach (the first qualification listed for the job is presence, not skill),",
  "Mark 3:21": "And when his friends heard of it, they went out to lay hold on him: for they said, He is beside himself (His own family's verdict on Him, delivered long before the religious leaders reach theirs).",
  "Mark 3:27": "No man can enter into a strong man's house, and spoil his goods, except he will first bind the strong man; and then he will spoil his house (a description of what the cross would do to Satan's house, spoken well before the cross itself).",
  "Mark 3:28": "Verily I say unto you, All sins shall be forgiven unto the sons of men, and blasphemies wherewith soever they shall blaspheme (a promise this wide, spoken to a room that includes the men currently plotting against Him):",
  "Mark 3:35": "For whosoever shall do the will of God, the same is my brother, and my sister, and mother (family redefined by something other than blood, without ever demeaning the blood relation it stands beside).",

  "Mark 4:3": "Hearken; Behold, there went out a sower to sow (an ordinary picture from an ordinary field, chosen because everyone listening already understood it):",
  "Mark 4:8": "And other fell on good ground, and did yield fruit that sprang up and increased; and brought forth, some thirty, and some sixty, and some an hundred (the same seed, the same sower -- the difference is entirely in what the seed lands on).",
  "Mark 4:9": "And he said unto them, He that hath ears to hear, let him hear (an invitation, not a test -- those who want to hear will keep listening until it makes sense).",
  "Mark 4:20": "And these are they which are sown on good ground; such as hear the word, and receive it, and bring forth fruit, some thirtyfold, some sixty, and some an hundred (hearing and receiving both happen before any fruit ever shows).",
  "Mark 4:26": "And he said, So is the kingdom of God, as if a man should cast seed into the ground;",
  "Mark 4:27": "And should sleep, and rise night and day, and the seed should spring and grow up, he knoweth not how (the kingdom advances by a process the man who planted it can't explain and doesn't have to).",
  "Mark 4:35": "And the same day, when the even was come, he saith unto them, Let us pass over unto the other side (a destination stated as settled fact, before the storm arrives to test whether it was true).",
  "Mark 4:38": "And he was in the hinder part of the ship, asleep on a pillow: and they awake him, and say unto him, Master, carest thou not that we perish (asked of the one man in the boat who was asleep precisely because He trusted the promise they were doubting)?",
  "Mark 4:39": "And he arose, and rebuked the wind, and said unto the sea, Peace, be still. And the wind ceased, and there was a great calm (two words end a storm that had already filled the boat with water).",
  "Mark 4:41": "And they feared exceedingly, and said one to another, What manner of man is this, that even the wind and the sea obey him (the question this whole Gospel spends fifteen more chapters answering)?",

  "Mark 5:6": "But when he saw Jesus afar off, he ran and worshipped him (a man possessed by an entire legion of unclean spirits still recognizes, and bows to, exactly who is standing in front of him),",
  "Mark 5:8": "For he said unto him, Come out of the man, thou unclean spirit (a command, not a request -- the spirit obeys it before the man even learns His name).",
  "Mark 5:19": "Howbeit Jesus suffered him not, but saith unto him, Go home to thy friends, and tell them how great things the Lord hath done for thee, and hath had compassion on thee (the man begs to come along, and is sent to go tell instead).",
  "Mark 5:23": "And besought him greatly, saying, My little daughter lieth at the point of death: I pray thee, come and lay thy hands on her, that she may be healed; and she shall live (a father's whole request fits inside one breath).",
  "Mark 5:28": "For she said, If I may touch but his clothes, I shall be whole (her theology was thin -- reaching for a hem, not a hand -- but her faith wasn't).",
  "Mark 5:34": "And he said unto her, Daughter, thy faith hath made thee whole; go in peace, and be whole of thy plague (twenty-five verses ago she was only ever called a certain woman -- now she has a name in His family).",
  "Mark 5:36": "As soon as Jesus heard the word that was spoken, he saith unto the ruler of the synagogue, Be not afraid, only believe (spoken the instant the news turns from bad to worse, not before).",
  "Mark 5:39": "And when he was come in, he saith unto them, Why make ye this ado, and weep? the damsel is not dead, but sleepeth (He redescribes death before He reverses it).",
  "Mark 5:41": "And he took the damsel by the hand, and said unto her, Talitha cumi; which is, being interpreted, Damsel, I say unto thee, arise (a strong grip and two words in her own tongue end a family's worst day).",

  "Mark 6:3": "Is not this the carpenter, the son of Mary, the brother of James, and Joses, and of Juda, and Simon? and are not his sisters here with us? And they were offended at him (the people who watched Him grow up are the ones least able to see who He is).",
  "Mark 6:5": "And he could there do no mighty work, save that he laid his hands upon a few sick folk, and healed them (the only place in the Gospels where His power is checked -- and it isn't power that stops Him).",
  "Mark 6:6": "And he marvelled because of their unbelief. And he went round about the villages, teaching (this is the only other time in the Gospels He is recorded marvelling -- the first was at a centurion's faith, this one runs the opposite direction).",
  "Mark 6:34": "And Jesus, when he came out, saw much people, and was moved with compassion toward them, because they were as sheep not having a shepherd: and he began to teach them many things (the reason He kept teaching instead of resting, stated in the same sentence).",
  "Mark 6:37": "He answered and said unto them, Give ye them to eat (the impossible problem is handed back to the twelve before He does anything about it Himself). And they say unto him, Shall we go and buy two hundred pennyworth of bread, and give them to eat?",
  "Mark 6:41": "And when he had taken the five loaves and the two fishes, he looked up to heaven, and blessed, and brake the loaves, and gave them to his disciples to set before them; and the two fishes divided he among them all (the multiplication happens somewhere between the breaking and the giving, in hands that were not His own).",
  "Mark 6:48": "And he saw them toiling in rowing; for the wind was contrary unto them: and about the fourth watch of the night he cometh unto them, walking upon the sea, and would have passed by them (He arrives by a means nobody in that boat had thought to expect Him by).",
  "Mark 6:50": "For they all saw him, and were troubled. And immediately he talked with them, and saith unto them, Be of good cheer: it is I; be not afraid (He identifies Himself before He calms anything else about the situation).",
  "Mark 6:56": "And whithersoever he entered, into villages, or cities, or country, they laid the sick in the streets, and besought him that they might touch if it were but the border of his garment: and as many as touched him were made whole (a whole region's worth of longing, and not one recorded refusal).",

  "Mark 7:6": "He answered and said unto them, Well hath Esaias prophesied of you hypocrites, as it is written, This people honoureth me with their lips, but their heart is far from me (a verdict written eight centuries earlier, read out loud to the men it was still describing).",
  "Mark 7:15": "There is nothing from without a man, that entering into him can defile him: but the things which come out of him, those are they that defile the man (He relocates the problem of sin from the plate to the heart).",
  "Mark 7:24": "And from thence he arose, and went into the borders of Tyre and Sidon, and entered into an house, and would have no man know it: but he could not be hid (even His attempt at privacy fails, simply because of who He is).",
  "Mark 7:28": "And she answered and said unto him, Yes, Lord: yet the dogs under the table eat of the children's crumbs (she doesn't argue with the comparison -- she just asks for what a comparison like that would still leave on the floor).",
  "Mark 7:29": "And he said unto her, For this saying go thy way; the devil is gone out of thy daughter (one sentence of desperate faith is enough to answer the entire request).",
  "Mark 7:34": "And looking up to heaven, he sighed, and saith unto him, Ephphatha, that is, Be opened (a word spoken directly to the man, in a language he could understand, undoing two disabilities in a single motion).",
  "Mark 7:37": "And were beyond measure astonished, saying, He hath done all things well: he maketh both the deaf to hear, and the dumb to speak (praised for two abilities that, days earlier, this same man had neither of).",

  "Mark 8:2": "I have compassion on the multitude, because they have now been with me three days, and have nothing to eat (He notices a hunger the crowd itself never voiced a complaint about):",
  "Mark 8:12": "And he sighed deeply in his spirit, and saith, Why doth this generation seek after a sign? verily I say unto you, There shall no sign be given unto this generation (it was never evidence they were short on).",
  "Mark 8:29": "And he saith unto them, But whom say ye that I am? And Peter answereth and saith unto him, Thou art the Christ (the Great Confession, given with no hedge attached to it).",
  "Mark 8:31": "And he began to teach them, that the Son of man must suffer many things, and be rejected of the elders, and of the chief priests, and scribes, and be killed, and after three days rise again (He states His own death and resurrection as settled fact, not as a fear to be managed).",
  "Mark 8:33": "But when he had turned about and looked on his disciples, he rebuked Peter, saying, Get thee behind me, Satan: for thou savourest not the things that be of God, but the things that be of men (the sharpest rebuke in this Gospel, aimed at a friend who had just tried to talk Him out of the cross).",
  "Mark 8:34": "And when he had called the people unto him with his disciples also, he said unto them, Whosoever will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me (the terms of following Him were never hidden or softened for the crowd).",
  "Mark 8:35": "For whosoever will save his life shall lose it; but whosoever shall lose his life for my sake and the gospel's, the same shall save it (a trade that looks like loss on both ends until the moment it doesn't).",
  "Mark 8:36": "For what shall it profit a man, if he shall gain the whole world, and lose his own soul (the entire world set on one side of the scale, and it still doesn't balance)?",

  "Mark 9:3": "And his raiment became shining, exceeding white as snow; so as no fuller on earth can white them (a glory no craft or bleach on earth could reproduce, breaking through from underneath the fabric He was already wearing).",
  "Mark 9:7": "And there was a cloud that overshadowed them: and a voice came out of the cloud, saying, This is my beloved Son: hear him (the same voice heard once already at the Jordan, saying almost the same sentence, for the same reason).",
  "Mark 9:23": "Jesus said unto him, If thou canst believe, all things are possible to him that believeth (the man asked if Jesus could -- Jesus turns the question back to what the man believes).",
  "Mark 9:24": "And straightway the father of the child cried out, and said with tears, Lord, I believe; help thou mine unbelief (the most honest prayer in the Gospels might be these two halves of the same sentence).",
  "Mark 9:31": "For he taught his disciples, and said unto them, The Son of man is delivered into the hands of men, and they shall kill him; and after that he is killed, he shall rise the third day (stated plainly in the middle of an ordinary walk through Galilee, almost in passing).",
  "Mark 9:35": "And he sat down, and called the twelve, and saith unto them, If any man desire to be first, the same shall be last of all, and servant of all (this reverses the exact ranking his own disciples had just been arguing about on the road).",
  "Mark 9:37": "Whosoever shall receive one of such children in my name, receiveth me: and whosoever shall receive me, receiveth not me, but him that sent me (a child standing in His arms becomes the whole of His argument).",
  "Mark 9:41": "For whosoever shall give you a cup of water to drink in my name, because ye belong to Christ, verily I say unto you, he shall not lose his reward (the smallest possible kindness, and He promises to remember it).",
  "Mark 9:50": "Salt is good: but if the salt have lost his saltness, wherewith will ye season it? Have salt in yourselves, and have peace one with another (the chapter that began with an argument over who was greatest ends with an instruction to keep peace with each other).",

  "Mark 10:14": "But when Jesus saw it, he was much displeased, and said unto them, Suffer the little children to come unto me, and forbid them not: for of such is the kingdom of God (the disciples were guarding His time -- He was giving it away, on purpose, to the ones least able to demand it).",
  "Mark 10:15": "Verily I say unto you, Whosoever shall not receive the kingdom of God as a little child, he shall not enter therein (a child does not negotiate for what is given -- receiving the Kingdom like that is not a performance, it is an open hand).",
  "Mark 10:21": "Then Jesus beholding him loved him, and said unto him, One thing thou lackest: go thy way, sell whatsoever thou hast, and give to the poor, and thou shalt have treasure in heaven: and come, take up the cross, and follow me (the man's real problem is named in a single sentence, and the invitation stands right alongside the diagnosis).",
  "Mark 10:23": "And Jesus looked round about, and saith unto his disciples, How hardly shall they that have riches enter into the kingdom of God (the danger named is not the money itself -- it's what money is uniquely good at convincing a person he doesn't need, including God)!",
  "Mark 10:24": "And the disciples were astonished at his words. But Jesus answereth again, and saith unto them, Children, how hard is it for them that trust in riches to enter into the kingdom of God (He narrows His own statement here -- the problem was never having riches, it's trusting in them; even the word 'Children' echoes what He had just said about receiving the Kingdom)!",
  "Mark 10:25": "It is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle, than for a rich man to enter into the kingdom of God (the image is deliberately absurd -- He reaches for the largest animal and the smallest opening He can think of).",
  "Mark 10:27": "And Jesus looking upon them saith, With men it is impossible, but not with God: for with God all things are possible (the disciples' arithmetic gets corrected in one line).",
  "Mark 10:31": "But many that are first shall be last; and the last first (an entire social order, turned over in eight words).",
  "Mark 10:38": "But Jesus said unto them, Ye know not what ye ask: can ye drink of the cup that I drink of? and be baptized with the baptism that I am baptized with (two men ask for glory and are answered, gently, with a description of the cross)?",
  "Mark 10:45": "For even the Son of man came not to be ministered unto, but to minister, and to give his life a ransom for many (His whole mission, compressed into a single sentence).",
  "Mark 10:47": "And when he heard that it was Jesus of Nazareth, he began to cry out, and say, Jesus, thou Son of David, have mercy on me (a blind man calls Him by the one title the sighted religious leaders of Israel never use).",
  "Mark 10:48": "And many charged him that he should hold his peace: but he cried the more a great deal, Thou Son of David, have mercy on me (a whole crowd tells him to be quiet, and he only gets louder).",
  "Mark 10:52": "And Jesus said unto him, Go thy way; thy faith hath made thee whole. And immediately he received his sight, and followed Jesus in the way (healed, and immediately following -- the same pattern this Gospel keeps returning to).",

  "Mark 11:9": "And they that went before, and they that followed, cried, saying, Hosanna; Blessed is he that cometh in the name of the Lord (a crowd quotes a thousand-year-old psalm at the very Person the psalm was written about, without fully knowing what they're saying):",
  "Mark 11:17": "And he taught, saying unto them, Is it not written, My house shall be called of all nations the house of prayer? but ye have made it a den of thieves (the one moment in the Gospels His anger turns physical, aimed at what had been done inside His Father's house).",
  "Mark 11:22": "And Jesus answering saith unto them, Have faith in God (three words answer a question His disciples raised about a withered tree).",
  "Mark 11:24": "Therefore I say unto you, What things soever ye desire, when ye pray, believe that ye receive them, and ye shall have them (a promise this large, spoken right after a fig tree just proved what His word alone can do).",
  "Mark 11:33": "And they answered and said unto Jesus, We cannot tell. And Jesus answering saith unto them, Neither do I tell you by what authority I do these things (they already know the answer -- refusing to say it out loud is what costs them His).",

  "Mark 12:6": "Having yet therefore one son, his wellbeloved, he sent him also last unto them, saying, They will reverence my son (the parable's ending is telegraphed by that one word -- last).",
  "Mark 12:10": "And have ye not read this scripture; The stone which the builders rejected is become the head of the corner (He quotes a psalm written a thousand years before the rejection He's describing had even happened):",
  "Mark 12:17": "And Jesus answering said unto them, Render to Cesar the things that are Cesar's, and to God the things that are God's. And they marvelled at him (the trap had two jaws, and both close on nothing).",
  "Mark 12:24": "And Jesus answering said unto them, Do ye not therefore err, because ye know not the scriptures, neither the power of God (He locates their mistake before He ever answers the question they came to ask)?",
  "Mark 12:26": "And as touching the dead, that they rise: have ye not read in the book of Moses, how in the bush God spake unto him, saying, I am the God of Abraham, and the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob (a present-tense verb, spoken to Moses centuries after Abraham had already died)?",
  "Mark 12:30": "And thou shalt love the Lord thy God with all thy heart, and with all thy soul, and with all thy mind, and with all thy strength: this is the first commandment.",
  "Mark 12:34": "And when Jesus saw that he answered discreetly, he said unto him, Thou art not far from the kingdom of God. And no man after that durst ask him any question (close gets named for exactly what it is -- close, not there).",
  "Mark 12:37": "David therefore himself calleth him Lord; and whence is he then his son? And the common people heard him gladly (the ordinary crowd catches what the trained experts standing right beside them miss entirely).",
  "Mark 12:40": "Which devour widows' houses, and for a pretence make long prayers: these shall receive greater damnation (the harshest words in this chapter are aimed at men preying on the very widows their long prayers were supposed to comfort).",
  "Mark 12:44": "For all they did cast in of their abundance; but she of her want did cast in all that she had, even all her living (the smallest coin dropped into the treasury that day turns out to be the largest gift given in it).",

  "Mark 13:2": "And Jesus answering said unto him, Seest thou these great buildings? there shall not be left one stone upon another, that shall not be thrown down (a prediction about the most permanent-looking structure in Jerusalem).",
  "Mark 13:11": "But when they shall lead you, and deliver you up, take no thought beforehand what ye shall speak, neither do ye premeditate: but whatsoever shall be given you in that hour, that speak ye: for it is not ye that speak, but the Holy Ghost (help is promised for the exact moment it's needed, not a moment before).",
  "Mark 13:26": "And then shall they see the Son of man coming in the clouds with great power and glory (what began in a manger ends with a description this size).",
  "Mark 13:31": "Heaven and earth shall pass away: but my words shall not pass away (He claims His own sentence will outlast the universe He spoke it into).",
  "Mark 13:32": "But of that day and that hour knoweth no man, no, not the angels which are in heaven, neither the Son, but the Father (He has just described the event down to the darkened sun and falling stars, and still says plainly the timing isn't His to disclose).",
  "Mark 13:37": "And what I say unto you I say unto all, Watch (His last word on the whole subject is one syllable long).",

  "Mark 14:3": "And being in Bethany in the house of Simon the leper, as he sat at meat, there came a woman having an alabaster box of ointment of spikenard very precious; and she brake the box, and poured it on his head (she breaks something costly open over Him while the men in the room are still calculating its price).",
  "Mark 14:8": "She hath done what she could: she is come aforehand to anoint my body to the burying (she prepares Him for a death none of the men in that room believe is actually coming).",
  "Mark 14:9": "Verily I say unto you, Wheresoever this gospel shall be preached throughout the whole world, this also that she hath done shall be spoken of for a memorial of her (a promise made two thousand years ago about how far this story would travel, kept in this very sentence being read right now).",
  "Mark 14:22": "And as they did eat, Jesus took bread, and blessed, and brake it, and gave to them, and said, Take, eat: this is my body (bread is broken and named before the body it stood for ever was).",
  "Mark 14:24": "And he said unto them, This is my blood of the new testament, which is shed for many (a covenant announced at a supper table, hours ahead of the event that would pay for it).",
  "Mark 14:34": "And saith unto them, My soul is exceeding sorrowful unto death: tarry ye here, and watch (the most openly human sentence He speaks in any of the four Gospels).",
  "Mark 14:36": "And he said, Abba, Father, all things are possible unto thee; take away this cup from me: nevertheless not what I will, but what thou wilt (the prayer that decided everything, prayed by the one Man with the standing to refuse who did not).",
  "Mark 14:38": "Watch ye and pray, lest ye enter into temptation. The spirit truly is ready, but the flesh is weak (said about the three men He found asleep an arm's length away, and about every reader of this page since).",
  "Mark 14:41": "And he cometh the third time, and saith unto them, Sleep on now, and take your rest: it is enough, the hour is come; behold, the Son of man is betrayed into the hands of sinners (He announces His own arrest before the soldiers arrive to make it official).",
  "Mark 14:50": "And they all forsook him, and fled (the moment stripped of every one of its heroes, just before it produces its Savior).",
  "Mark 14:62": "And Jesus said, I am: and ye shall see the Son of man sitting on the right hand of power, and coming in the clouds of heaven (two words, spoken under oath, guarantee His own conviction).",
  "Mark 14:72": "And the second time the cock crew. And Peter called to mind the word that Jesus said unto him, Before the cock crow twice, thou shalt deny me thrice. And when he thought thereon, he wept (the same man who swore hours earlier that he would never do this -- the text doesn't excuse him, only records the weeping).",

  "Mark 15:5": "But Jesus yet answered nothing; so that Pilate marvelled (silence baffles a man who was used to defendants fighting for their lives).",
  "Mark 15:15": "And so Pilate, willing to content the people, released Barabbas unto them, and delivered Jesus, when he had scourged him, to be crucified (an actual insurrectionist goes free in the place of the one man falsely accused of being one).",
  "Mark 15:18": "And began to salute him, Hail, King of the Jews (said in mockery, by men who had no idea they were telling the truth)!",
  "Mark 15:24": "And when they had crucified him, they parted his garments, casting lots upon them, what every man should take (soldiers gambling at the foot of the cross fulfill a psalm none of them had ever read).",
  "Mark 15:26": "And the superscription of his accusation was written over, THE KING OF THE JEWS (the charge nailed above His head to mock Him turns out to be the one accurate statement made at the entire trial).",
  "Mark 15:34": "And at the ninth hour Jesus cried with a loud voice, saying, Eloi, Eloi, lama sabachthani? which is, being interpreted, My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me (the one prayer in the Gospels where the answer is silence, prayed by the only Man who had never once gone unanswered before)?",
  "Mark 15:37": "And Jesus cried with a loud voice, and gave up the ghost (a loud voice, not a fading one -- nothing about the way He dies reads like a man whose strength simply ran out).",
  "Mark 15:38": "And the veil of the temple was rent in twain from the top to the bottom (a curtain sixty feet tall, torn from the direction no man standing on the ground could have reached).",
  "Mark 15:39": "And when the centurion, which stood over against him, saw that he so cried out, and gave up the ghost, he said, Truly this man was the Son of God (a Roman soldier's verdict, delivered at the foot of the cross, that the Sanhedrin never once reached in the courtroom).",
  "Mark 15:46": "And he bought fine linen, and took him down, and wrapped him in the linen, and laid him in a sepulchre which was hewn out of a rock, and rolled a stone unto the door of the sepulchre (a borrowed tomb, sealed shut by a stone that would need to move again in three days).",

  "Mark 16:4": "And when they looked, they saw that the stone was rolled away: for it was very great (the size is noted on purpose -- this was not the kind of stone that moves by accident).",
  "Mark 16:6": "And he saith unto them, Be not affrighted: Ye seek Jesus of Nazareth, which was crucified: he is risen; he is not here: behold the place where they laid him (four words undo everything the previous chapter accomplished -- he is not here).",
  "Mark 16:7": "But go your way, tell his disciples and Peter that he goeth before you into Galilee: there shall ye see him, as he said unto you (Peter is named separately, by name, one week after denying he even knew this Man).",
  "Mark 16:11": "And they, when they had heard that he was alive, and had been seen of her, believed not (the first witnesses to the resurrection are doubted first by their own friends).",
  "Mark 16:14": "Afterward he appeared unto the eleven as they sat at meat, and upbraided them with their unbelief and hardness of heart, because they believed not them which had seen him after he was risen (even the men who had walked with Him for three years had to be talked out of their own disbelief).",
  "Mark 16:15": "And he said unto them, Go ye into all the world, and preach the gospel to every creature (the scope of the assignment leaves no one, anywhere, off the list).",
  "Mark 16:16": "He that believeth and is baptized shall be saved; but he that believeth not shall be damned (the same two words from the very first chapter -- repent, believe -- now stand at the close of the book as well).",
  "Mark 16:20": "And they went forth, and preached every where, the Lord working with them, and confirming the word with signs following. Amen (the book that opened with one Man's ministry closes with an entire Church continuing it).",

  "Luke 1:1": "Forasmuch as many have taken in hand to set forth in order a declaration of those things which are most surely believed among us, (Luke opens by naming his method -- an orderly account of things already established, not a first attempt at invention)",
  "Luke 1:3": "It seemed good to me also, having had perfect understanding of all things from the very first, to write unto thee in order, most excellent Theophilus, (a Gospel written for one named reader, and then read by everyone else who ever picked it up since)",
  "Luke 1:15": "For he shall be great in the sight of the Lord, and shall drink neither wine nor strong drink; and he shall be filled with the Holy Ghost, even from his mother's womb (a life marked out for a mission before it had drawn its first breath).",
  "Luke 1:17": "And he shall go before him in the spirit and power of Elias, to turn the hearts of the fathers to the children, and the disobedient to the wisdom of the just; to make ready a people prepared for the Lord (John's entire assignment fits inside one sentence -- get a people ready for someone else).",
  "Luke 1:27": "To a virgin espoused to a man whose name was Joseph, of the house of David; and the virgin's name was Mary (the word virgin is used twice in a single verse, as if anticipating that someone, someday, would try to explain it away).",
  "Luke 1:28": "And the angel came in unto her, and said, Hail, thou that art highly favoured, the Lord is with thee: blessed art thou among women (an unremarkable town gets the largest greeting an angel ever spoke).",
  "Luke 1:31": "And, behold, thou shalt conceive in thy womb, and bring forth a son, and shalt call his name JESUS (the name comes before the child does, because the name was always the point).",
  "Luke 1:35": "And the angel answered and said unto her, The Holy Ghost shall come upon thee, and the power of the Highest shall overshadow thee: therefore also that holy thing which shall be born of thee shall be called the Son of God (the same Spirit that once moved over the face of the waters now moves over a single womb in Nazareth).",
  "Luke 1:37": "For with God nothing shall be impossible (six words answer every objection Mary could have raised).",
  "Luke 1:41": "And it came to pass, that, when Elisabeth heard the salutation of Mary, the babe leaped in her womb; and Elisabeth was filled with the Holy Ghost (an unborn child responds to a presence the grown men in this story haven't caught wind of yet):",
  "Luke 1:42": "And she spake out with a loud voice, and said, Blessed art thou among women, and blessed is the fruit of thy womb (an old woman recognizes in an instant what the religious leadership in Jerusalem will spend three decades refusing to see).",
  "Luke 1:47": "And my spirit hath rejoiced in God my Saviour (she carries the Saviour and still calls Him her own -- Mary needed saving too).",
  "Luke 1:68": "Blessed be the Lord God of Israel; for he hath visited and redeemed his people, (a man silenced for nine months opens his mouth again and the first thing out of it is a sermon)",
  "Luke 1:78": "Through the tender mercy of our God; whereby the dayspring from on high hath visited us, (a sunrise that arrives on foot, born in the kind of town no one important ever came from)",

  "Luke 2:1": "And it came to pass in those days, that there went out a decree from Cesar Augustus, that all the world should be taxed (an empire-wide census, issued for tax purposes, becomes the reason the Messiah is born in the exact town the prophets named).",
  "Luke 2:7": "And she brought forth her firstborn son, and wrapped him in swaddling clothes, and laid him in a manger; because there was no room for them in the inn (the Creator's first bed on earth is a feeding trough, because there was nowhere else to lay Him).",
  "Luke 2:8": "And there were in the same country shepherds abiding in the field, keeping watch over their flock by night (the announcement goes first to men with the lowest standing in Israel, not the highest).",
  "Luke 2:10": "And the angel said unto them, Fear not: for, behold, I bring you good tidings of great joy, which shall be to all people (the joy is not sent to one nation only -- the angel says all people, and means it).",
  "Luke 2:11": "For unto you is born this day in the city of David a Saviour, which is Christ the Lord (the announcement skips every future tense -- Saviour is not what this child will grow into, it is what he already is at birth).",
  "Luke 2:12": "And this shall be a sign unto you; Ye shall find the babe wrapped in swaddling clothes, lying in a manger (the sign that confirms the King's arrival is a description of His poverty, not His palace).",
  "Luke 2:14": "Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace, good will toward men (sinless angels sing over the birth of a Redeemer they themselves never needed).",
  "Luke 2:19": "But Mary kept all these things, and pondered them in her heart (she says nothing recorded here -- she just keeps turning it all over, alone).",
  "Luke 2:21": "And when eight days were accomplished for the circumcising of the child, his name was called JESUS, which was so named of the angel before he was conceived in the womb (the name was settled before the pregnancy was).",
  "Luke 2:25": "And, behold, there was a man in Jerusalem, whose name was Simeon; and the same man was just and devout, waiting for the consolation of Israel: and the Holy Ghost was upon him (an old man spends years waiting on a promise he has no reason left to doubt).",
  "Luke 2:30": "For mine eyes have seen thy salvation, (Simeon doesn't need Mary to explain who the baby is -- he just knows)",
  "Luke 2:34": "And Simeon blessed them, and said unto Mary his mother, Behold, this child is set for the fall and rising again of many in Israel; and for a sign which shall be spoken against; (the same infant will divide the nation he was born into -- Simeon says so while he is still holding Him)",
  "Luke 2:35": "(Yea, a sword shall pierce through thy own soul also,) that the thoughts of many hearts may be revealed (Mary is warned, at His dedication, of the grief His Cross will one day cost her).",
  "Luke 2:40": "And the child grew, and waxed strong in spirit, filled with wisdom: and the grace of God was upon him (thirty years pass between this verse and the start of His ministry, and Luke compresses nearly all of them into a single sentence).",
  "Luke 2:46": "And it came to pass, that after three days they found him in the temple, sitting in the midst of the doctors, both hearing them, and asking them questions (a twelve-year-old sits among the most trained scholars in Israel, and the questions run in both directions).",
  "Luke 2:49": "And he said unto them, How is it that ye sought me? wist ye not that I must be about my Father's business? (His first recorded sentence names God as His Father, decades before anyone else understood what that meant).",
  "Luke 2:52": "And Jesus increased in wisdom and stature, and in favour with God and man (the only description Scripture gives of eighteen ordinary years, and it takes a single verse to cover all of them).",

  "Luke 3:3": "And he came into all the country about Jordan, preaching the baptism of repentance for the remission of sins; (a voice by a river becomes the last prophet Israel would hear from in four hundred years)",
  "Luke 3:4": "As it is written in the book of the words of Esaias the prophet, saying, The voice of one crying in the wilderness, Prepare ye the way of the Lord, make his paths straight (Isaiah wrote this sentence roughly seven centuries before there was a voice or a wilderness to fulfill it in).",
  "Luke 3:6": "And all flesh shall see the salvation of God (not one nation only -- all flesh).",
  "Luke 3:7": "Then said he to the multitude that came forth to be baptized of him, O generation of vipers, who hath warned you to flee from the wrath to come? (the harshest words in the chapter are aimed at the crowd that showed up wanting to be baptized, not the crowd that stayed home)",
  "Luke 3:8": "Bring forth therefore fruits worthy of repentance, and begin not to say within yourselves, We have Abraham to our father: for I say unto you, That God is able of these stones to raise up children unto Abraham (the fruit he is asking for is evidence, not currency -- something repentance produces, not something that earns it).",
  "Luke 3:9": "And now also the axe is laid unto the root of the trees: every tree therefore which bringeth not forth good fruit is hewn down, and cast into the fire (the axe is already at the root, not swinging yet -- a warning timed to still be useful).",
  "Luke 3:16": "John answered, saying unto them all, I indeed baptize you with water; but one mightier than I cometh, the latchet of whose shoes I am not worthy to unloose: he shall baptize you with the Holy Ghost and with fire (John measures the difference between the two baptisms by what each man is worthy to do):",
  "Luke 3:21": "Now when all the people were baptized, it came to pass, that Jesus also being baptized, and praying, the heaven was opened (the only sinless man in the crowd lines up to be baptized alongside everyone else),",
  "Luke 3:22": "And the Holy Ghost descended in a bodily shape like a dove upon him, and a voice came from heaven, which said, Thou art my beloved Son; in thee I am well pleased (Father, Son, and Spirit are all visible or audible in the same moment, at the edge of the same river).",

  "Luke 4:1": "And Jesus being full of the Holy Ghost returned from Jordan, and was led by the Spirit into the wilderness, (He walks from His baptism straight into forty days of testing -- the same Spirit that filled Him is the Spirit that leads Him there)",
  "Luke 4:2": "Being forty days tempted of the devil. And in those days he did eat nothing: and when they were ended, he afterward hungered (real hunger, in a real body, after a real fast -- His temptation cost Him something).",
  "Luke 4:4": "And Jesus answered him, saying, It is written, That man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word of God (three temptations, three answers, and every one of them a quotation rather than an argument of His own).",
  "Luke 4:8": "And Jesus answered and said unto him, Get thee behind me, Satan: for it is written, Thou shalt worship the Lord thy God, and him only shalt thou serve (offered the whole world without the Cross, He turns it down without a debate).",
  "Luke 4:12": "And Jesus answering said unto him, It is said, Thou shalt not tempt the Lord thy God (Satan can quote a psalm accurately and still use it to lie).",
  "Luke 4:13": "And when the devil had ended all the temptation, he departed from him for a season (the word season means this was not the last time -- only the first).",
  "Luke 4:18": "The Spirit of the Lord is upon me, because he hath anointed me to preach the gospel to the poor; he hath sent me to heal the brokenhearted, to preach deliverance to the captives, and recovering of sight to the blind, to set at liberty them that are bruised, (He reads a description of His own mission from a scroll written centuries before He arrived to fulfill it)",
  "Luke 4:21": "And he began to say unto them, This day is this scripture fulfilled in your ears (one sentence, spoken in His hometown synagogue, that amounts to Him telling His neighbors exactly who He is).",
  "Luke 4:22": "And all bare him witness, and wondered at the gracious words which proceeded out of his mouth. And they said, Is not this Joseph's son (they marvel at what He says and cannot reconcile it with who they think He is)?",
  "Luke 4:24": "And he said, Verily I say unto you, No prophet is accepted in his own country (the town that watched Him grow up is the one town that will not receive Him).",
  "Luke 4:29": "And rose up, and thrust him out of the city, and led him unto the brow of the hill whereon their city was built, that they might cast him down headlong (His own village is the first place in this Gospel that tries to kill Him).",
  "Luke 4:32": "And they were astonished at his doctrine: for his word was with power (people can tell the difference between someone quoting an authority and someone who has one).",
  "Luke 4:36": "And they were all amazed, and spake among themselves, saying, What a word is this! for with authority and power he commandeth the unclean spirits, and they come out (a single word, spoken once, and the spirits simply obey it).",
  "Luke 4:39": "And he stood over her, and rebuked the fever; and it left her: and immediately she arose and ministered unto them (healed so completely that she is up serving a meal in the same verse).",
  "Luke 4:41": "And devils also came out of many, crying out, and saying, Thou art Christ the Son of God. And he rebuking them suffered them not to speak: for they knew that he was Christ (the demons state His identity more plainly than the religious leaders of Israel ever will).",
  "Luke 4:43": "And he said unto them, I must preach the kingdom of God to other cities also: for therefore am I sent (the crowd wants to keep Him in one town -- His assignment was never limited to one).",

  "Luke 5:5": "And Simon answering said unto him, Master, we have toiled all the night, and have taken nothing: nevertheless at thy word I will let down the net (a professional fisherman obeys a carpenter's instruction about fish, against his own experience).",
  "Luke 5:8": "When Simon Peter saw it, he fell down at Jesus' knees, saying, Depart from me; for I am a sinful man, O Lord (the miracle that should draw him closer is the very thing that makes him beg for distance).",
  "Luke 5:11": "And when they had brought their ships to land, they forsook all, and followed him (they walk away from the best catch of their careers to follow a stranger).",
  "Luke 5:13": "And he put forth his hand, and touched him, saying, I will: be thou clean. And immediately the leprosy departed from him (He touches the one man in the city everyone else was required by law to avoid).",
  "Luke 5:20": "And when he saw their faith, he said unto him, Man, thy sins are forgiven thee (the paralyzed man came for his legs and is answered first about something no one asked Him to fix).",
  "Luke 5:24": "But that ye may know that the Son of man hath power upon earth to forgive sins, (he said unto the sick of the palsy,) I say unto thee, Arise, and take up thy couch, and go into thine house (the visible healing is offered as proof of the invisible one).",
  "Luke 5:26": "And they were all amazed, and they glorified God, and were filled with fear, saying, We have seen strange things to day (even the word they reach for -- strange -- admits they have no category for what just happened).",
  "Luke 5:27": "And after these things he went forth, and saw a publican, named Levi, sitting at the receipt of custom: and he said unto him, Follow me (He calls a man whose profession made him a traitor in the eyes of his own nation).",
  "Luke 5:31": "And Jesus answering said unto them, They that are whole need not a physician; but they that are sick (He answers an accusation about His company by simply stating why He keeps it).",
  "Luke 5:38": "But new wine must be put into new bottles; and both are preserved (grace was never meant to be poured into the old container).",

  "Luke 6:12": "And it came to pass in those days, that he went out into a mountain to pray, and continued all night in prayer to God (before choosing the twelve men who would carry His mission, He spends an entire night doing nothing but this).",
  "Luke 6:13": "And when it was day, he called unto him his disciples: and of them he chose twelve, whom also he named apostles (a whole night of prayer produces a list of twelve very ordinary names);",
  "Luke 6:20": "And he lifted up his eyes on his disciples, and said, Blessed be ye poor: for yours is the kingdom of God (He looks straight at the ones with nothing and calls them blessed).",
  "Luke 6:22": "Blessed are ye, when men shall hate you, and when they shall separate you from their company, and shall reproach you, and cast out your name as evil, for the Son of man's sake (being hated for His name is placed on the same list as being blessed).",
  "Luke 6:27": "But I say unto you which hear, Love your enemies, do good to them which hate you, (the instruction is not to tolerate enemies but to actively do them good)",
  "Luke 6:31": "And as ye would that men should do to you, do ye also to them likewise (a single sentence turns every complaint about how you've been treated into an instruction).",
  "Luke 6:35": "But love ye your enemies, and do good, and lend, hoping for nothing again; and your reward shall be great, and ye shall be the children of the Highest: for he is kind unto the unthankful and to the evil (the reasoning behind loving an enemy is that God already does).",
  "Luke 6:36": "Be ye therefore merciful, as your Father also is merciful (the standard given is not human decency -- it's the Father's own character).",
  "Luke 6:45": "A good man out of the good treasure of his heart bringeth forth that which is good; and an evil man out of the evil treasure of his heart bringeth forth that which is evil: for of the abundance of the heart his mouth speaketh (what a person says under pressure reveals what was already stored up before the pressure came).",
  "Luke 6:48": "He is like a man which built an house, and digged deep, and laid the foundation on a rock: and when the flood arose, the stream beat vehemently upon that house, and could not shake it: for it was founded upon a rock (the difference between the two houses in this parable was never visible until the storm came).",

  "Luke 7:7": "Wherefore neither thought I myself worthy to come unto thee: but say in a word, and my servant shall be healed (a Roman officer trusts the power of Christ's word more than most of the men of Israel who had grown up hearing it).",
  "Luke 7:9": "When Jesus heard these things, he marvelled at him, and turned him about, and said unto the people that followed him, I say unto you, I have not found so great faith, no, not in Israel (the greatest faith in the whole chapter belongs to a man outside the covenant, not inside it).",
  "Luke 7:13": "And when the Lord saw her, he had compassion on her, and said unto her, Weep not (He speaks before she has asked Him for anything).",
  "Luke 7:14": "And he came and touched the bier: and they that bare him stood still. And he said, Young man, I say unto thee, Arise (He interrupts a funeral procession and simply speaks to the dead man as though he could hear).",
  "Luke 7:16": "And there came a fear on all: and they glorified God, saying, That a great prophet is risen up among us; and, That God hath visited his people (the crowd's language is bigger than they know -- they call it a visitation, and they are right).",
  "Luke 7:22": "Then Jesus answering said unto them, Go your way, and tell John what things ye have seen and heard; how that the blind see, the lame walk, the lepers are cleansed, the deaf hear, the dead are raised, to the poor the gospel is preached (He answers a doubting prophet's question with a list of facts, not an argument).",
  "Luke 7:28": "For I say unto you, Among those that are born of women there is not a greater prophet than John the Baptist: but he that is least in the kingdom of God is greater than he (the highest praise John ever receives is followed immediately by a sentence that puts something else even higher).",
  "Luke 7:38": "And stood at his feet behind him weeping, and began to wash his feet with tears, and did wipe them with the hairs of her head, and kissed his feet, and anointed them with the ointment (she gives Him what the host of the house never thought to offer).",
  "Luke 7:47": "Wherefore I say unto thee, Her sins, which are many, are forgiven; for she loved much: but to whom little is forgiven, the same loveth little (the size of her love in this story is offered as evidence of how much she already knew she'd been forgiven).",
  "Luke 7:50": "And he said to the woman, Thy faith hath saved thee; go in peace (the same two words He will later say to another woman, healed of a twelve-year sickness).",

  "Luke 8:2": "And certain women, which had been healed of evil spirits and infirmities, Mary called Magdalene, out of whom went seven devils, (the woman who will be first to see Him risen is introduced here simply as someone He once delivered)",
  "Luke 8:15": "But that on the good ground are they, which in an honest and good heart, having heard the word, keep it, and bring forth fruit with patience (the difference in this soil is not excitement at the start but patience over the long haul).",
  "Luke 8:21": "And he answered and said unto them, My mother and my brethren are these which hear the word of God, and do it (He redraws the boundaries of His own family in front of the family standing outside).",
  "Luke 8:24": "And they came to him, and awoke him, saying, Master, master, we perish. Then he arose, and rebuked the wind and the raging of the water: and they ceased, and there was a calm (He speaks to a storm the same way He speaks to a demon -- as something that has to obey Him).",
  "Luke 8:25": "And he said unto them, Where is your faith? And they being afraid wondered, saying one to another, What manner of man is this! for he commandeth even the winds and water, and they obey him (the disciples pass from one kind of fear -- of the storm -- straight into another -- of the Man who just silenced it).",
  "Luke 8:35": "Then they went out to see what was done; and came to Jesus, and found the man, out of whom the devils were departed, sitting at the feet of Jesus, clothed, and in his right mind: and they were afraid (the man everyone in the region had learned to fear is now the calmest person in the story).",
  "Luke 8:39": "Return to thine own house, and shew how great things God hath done unto thee. And he went his way, and published throughout the whole city how great things Jesus had done unto him (the one man Jesus sends to go and talk about it is the one man everyone already knew had been impossible to help).",
  "Luke 8:44": "Came behind him, and touched the border of his garment: and immediately her issue of blood stanched (twelve years of sickness ends the instant she makes contact, before He has even turned around).",
  "Luke 8:48": "And he said unto her, Daughter, be of good comfort: thy faith hath made thee whole; go in peace (she came expecting to go unnoticed and leaves having been called by a family name).",
  "Luke 8:50": "But when Jesus heard it, he answered him, saying, Fear not: believe only, and she shall be made whole (He answers news of a death with an instruction to keep believing, not to brace for the worst).",
  "Luke 8:54": "And he put them all out, and took her by the hand, and called, saying, Maid, arise (the same hands that touched a leper and stopped a hemorrhage now take hold of a dead child's hand).",

  "Luke 9:6": "And they departed, and went through the towns, preaching the gospel, and healing every where (a small crew of learners becomes a traveling gospel team the same day they're commissioned).",
  "Luke 9:16": "Then he took the five loaves and the two fishes, and looking up to heaven, he blessed them, and brake, and gave to the disciples to set before the multitude (He looks up before He breaks anything -- the miracle starts with where His eyes go, not His hands).",
  "Luke 9:17": "And they did eat, and were all filled: and there was taken up of fragments that remained to them twelve baskets (more food is left over after the meal than He started with before it).",
  "Luke 9:20": "He said unto them, But whom say ye that I am? Peter answering said, The Christ of God (the question every crowd's opinion had been dodging finally gets asked directly, to the men closest to Him).",
  "Luke 9:22": "Saying, The Son of man must suffer many things, and be rejected of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be slain, and be raised the third day (He predicts His own death and resurrection in the same breath, with the same certainty).",
  "Luke 9:23": "And he said to them all, If any man will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross daily, and follow me (the cross in this sentence is not a single moment but a daily one).",
  "Luke 9:24": "For whosoever will save his life shall lose it: but whosoever will lose his life for my sake, the same shall save it (the math of the kingdom runs backward from the math everyone already knows).",
  "Luke 9:29": "And as he prayed, the fashion of his countenance was altered, and his raiment was white and glistering (glory breaks through while He is praying, not while He is performing).",
  "Luke 9:35": "And there came a voice out of the cloud, saying, This is my beloved Son: hear him (Moses and Elijah are standing right there, and the voice from heaven still narrows everything down to one Man).",
  "Luke 9:58": "And Jesus said unto him, Foxes have holes, and birds of the air have nests; but the Son of man hath not where to lay his head (the Man who made every fox's den has nowhere of His own to sleep).",
  "Luke 9:62": "And Jesus said unto him, No man, having put his hand to the plough, and looking back, is fit for the kingdom of God (a straight furrow requires eyes forward -- the same is true here).",

  "Luke 10:2": "Therefore said he unto them, The harvest truly is great, but the labourers are few: pray ye therefore the Lord of the harvest, that he would send forth labourers into his harvest (the shortage He points out is workers, not harvest).",
  "Luke 10:9": "And heal the sick that are therein, and say unto them, The kingdom of God is come nigh unto you (the sentence they're told to say is present tense -- come nigh, not coming someday).",
  "Luke 10:17": "And the seventy returned again with joy, saying, Lord, even the devils are subject unto us through thy name (they come back most excited about the one thing He immediately redirects their attention away from).",
  "Luke 10:18": "And he said unto them, I beheld Satan as lightning fall from heaven (He watched something these seventy men have only just now started to share in).",
  "Luke 10:20": "Notwithstanding in this rejoice not, that the spirits are subject unto you; but rather rejoice, because your names are written in heaven (He points them toward the quieter miracle over the flashier one).",
  "Luke 10:21": "In that hour Jesus rejoiced in spirit, and said, I thank thee, O Father, Lord of heaven and earth, that thou hast hid these things from the wise and prudent, and hast revealed them unto babes: even so, Father; for so it seemed good in thy sight (one of the few times any Gospel records Him rejoicing, and it's over who gets to understand Him).",
  "Luke 10:30": "And Jesus answering said, A certain man went down from Jerusalem to Jericho, and fell among thieves, which stripped him of his raiment, and wounded him, and departed, leaving him half dead (the story never names the wounded man -- he could be anyone, which is the point).",
  "Luke 10:33": "But a certain Samaritan, as he journeyed, came where he was: and when he saw him, he had compassion on him, (the man who stops to help is the one the original audience would have expected to walk past)",
  "Luke 10:39": "And she had a sister called Mary, which also sat at Jesus' feet, and heard his word (she takes the posture usually reserved for a student learning at the feet of a Rabbi).",
  "Luke 10:42": "But one thing is needful: and Mary hath chosen that good part, which shall not be taken away from her (of everything competing for attention in that house, He names exactly one thing as necessary).",

  "Luke 11:1": "And it came to pass, that, as he was praying in a certain place, when he ceased, one of his disciples said unto him, Lord, teach us to pray, as John also taught his disciples (out of everything they watched Him do, this is the one thing the disciples specifically ask Him to teach).",
  "Luke 11:2": "And he said unto them, When ye pray, say, Our Father which art in heaven, Hallowed be thy name. Thy kingdom come. Thy will be done, as in heaven, so in earth (the model prayer opens by addressing God as Father, before it asks Him for anything).",
  "Luke 11:9": "And I say unto you, Ask, and it shall be given you; seek, and ye shall find; knock, and it shall be opened unto you (three verbs, three promises, none of them conditioned on getting the wording right).",
  "Luke 11:13": "If ye then, being evil, know how to give good gifts unto your children: how much more shall your heavenly Father give the Holy Spirit to them that ask him (He argues from the lesser to the greater -- if flawed parents can get this right, how much more the Father)?",
  "Luke 11:23": "He that is not with me is against me: and he that gathereth not with me scattereth (no third position is described in this sentence).",
  "Luke 11:28": "But he said, Yea rather, blessed are they that hear the word of God, and keep it (He turns a compliment about His mother into a blessing anyone listening can share).",
  "Luke 11:33": "No man, when he hath lighted a candle, putteth it in a secret place, neither under a bushel, but on a candlestick, that they which come in may see the light (light hidden on purpose defeats the reason it was lit).",
  "Luke 11:39": "And the Lord said unto him, Now do ye Pharisees make clean the outside of the cup and the platter; but your inward part is full of ravening and wickedness (invited to dinner, He answers a question about handwashing by describing the inside of His host).",
  "Luke 11:42": "But woe unto you, Pharisees! for ye tithe mint and rue and all manner of herbs, and pass over judgment and the love of God: these ought ye to have done, and not to leave the other undone (meticulous about the smallest details while missing the largest ones).",

  "Luke 12:15": "And he said unto them, Take heed, and beware of covetousness: for a man's life consisteth not in the abundance of the things which he possesseth (He answers a request to settle an inheritance dispute by naming the deeper problem behind it).",
  "Luke 12:20": "But God said unto him, Thou fool, this night thy soul shall be required of thee: then whose shall those things be, which thou hast provided (a man plans for years he will not live to see)?",
  "Luke 12:31": "But rather seek ye the kingdom of God; and all these things shall be added unto you (the things people worry about most are treated here as the leftovers, not the goal).",
  "Luke 12:32": "Fear not, little flock; for it is your Father's good pleasure to give you the kingdom (the smallest possible group -- a little flock -- is given the largest possible gift).",
  "Luke 12:34": "For where your treasure is, there will your heart be also (He doesn't tell them to change their hearts directly -- He tells them where to move their treasure).",
  "Luke 12:37": "Blessed are those servants, whom the lord when he cometh shall find watching: verily I say unto you, that he shall gird himself, and make them to sit down to meat, and will come forth and serve them (the master returns and serves the servants who were found waiting for him).",
  "Luke 12:40": "Be ye therefore ready also: for the Son of man cometh at an hour when ye think not (the timing is left unknown on purpose, so that readiness can never become a formality).",
  "Luke 12:47": "And that servant, which knew his lord's will, and prepared not himself, neither did according to his will, shall be beaten with many stripes (the parable measures consequences in degrees -- knowledge carries more weight than ignorance, not less).",
  "Luke 12:48": "But he that knew not, and did commit things worthy of stripes, shall be beaten with few stripes. For unto whomsoever much is given, of him shall be much required: and to whom men have committed much, of him they will ask the more (the principle stated plainly here -- more given, more required -- is the same rule that later shapes how every steward's work gets evaluated).",
  "Luke 12:49": "I am come to send fire on the earth; and what will I, if it be already kindled (He describes His own mission in terms of something meant to burn)?",
  "Luke 12:50": "But I have a baptism to be baptized with; and how am I straitened till it be accomplished (He is speaking of the Cross, still weeks away, and it already weighs on Him)!",

  "Luke 13:3": "I tell you, Nay: but, except ye repent, ye shall all likewise perish (He refuses to rank a tragedy by whose sin caused it, and turns the question back on everyone listening instead).",
  "Luke 13:12": "And when Jesus saw her, he called her to him, and said unto her, Woman, thou art loosed from thine infirmity (eighteen years of being bent over end with four words).",
  "Luke 13:16": "And ought not this woman, being a daughter of Abraham, whom Satan hath bound, lo, these eighteen years, be loosed from this bond on the sabbath day (He answers a complaint about timing by naming what the timing actually cost her)?",
  "Luke 13:19": "It is like a grain of mustard seed, which a man took, and cast into his garden; and it grew, and waxed a great tree; and the fowls of the air lodged in the branches of it (the kingdom's beginning is described as the smallest thing in the garden).",
  "Luke 13:24": "Strive to enter in at the strait gate: for many, I say unto you, will seek to enter in, and shall not be able (the door was never locked -- what fails is trying to get through it by any means other than the one he is about to describe).",
  "Luke 13:25": "When once the master of the house is risen up, and hath shut to the door, and ye begin to stand without, and to knock at the door, saying, Lord, Lord, open unto us; and he shall answer and say unto you, I know you not whence ye are (the ones locked out had been close enough to knock -- nearness was never the same thing as belonging).",
  "Luke 13:26": "Then shall ye begin to say, We have eaten and drunk in thy presence, and thou hast taught in our streets (their appeal is proximity and familiarity -- exactly the two things that turn out not to be the point).",
  "Luke 13:27": "But he shall say, I tell you, I know you not whence ye are; depart from me, all ye workers of iniquity (the same two words end this exchange that ended the one two verses earlier -- the missing thing was never activity, it was relationship).",
  "Luke 13:28": "There shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth, when ye shall see Abraham, and Isaac, and Jacob, and all the prophets, in the kingdom of God, and you yourselves thrust out (the grief pictured here is the sight of the door from the outside, watching others already seated within).",
  "Luke 13:32": "And he said unto them, Go ye, and tell that fox, Behold, I cast out devils, and I do cures to day and to morrow, and the third day I shall be perfected (a king threatens His life and He answers with His own schedule, unchanged).",
  "Luke 13:34": "O Jerusalem, Jerusalem, which killest the prophets, and stonest them that are sent unto thee; how often would I have gathered thy children together, as a hen doth gather her brood under her wings, and ye would not (the grief in this sentence belongs to Him, not to the city that caused it)!",

  "Luke 14:11": "For whosoever exalteth himself shall be abased; and he that humbleth himself shall be exalted (the same rule He gives about dinner seating turns out to describe everything else too).",
  "Luke 14:13": "But when thou makest a feast, call the poor, the maimed, the lame, the blind (He tells a house full of the comfortable to invite the people least able to return the favor):",
  "Luke 14:26": "If any man come to me, and hate not his father, and mother, and wife, and children, and brethren, and sisters, yea, and his own life also, he cannot be my disciple (the word hate here means comparison, not cruelty -- everything else has to come second).",
  "Luke 14:27": "And whosoever doth not bear his cross, and come after me, cannot be my disciple (following Him was never presented as free of cost).",
  "Luke 14:33": "So likewise, whosoever he be of you that forsaketh not all that he hath, he cannot be my disciple (He tells the crowd to count the cost before He lets them commit to Him).",
  "Luke 14:34": "Salt is good: but if the salt have lost his savour, wherewith shall it be seasoned? (the question has no answer -- salt that has stopped being salt cannot be re-salted by anything else.)",
  "Luke 14:35": "It is neither fit for the land, nor yet for the dunghill; but men cast it out. He that hath ears to hear, let him hear (even the compost heap has no use for it -- He reaches for the most useless image available).",

  "Luke 15:4": "What man of you, having an hundred sheep, if he lose one of them, doth not leave the ninety and nine in the wilderness, and go after that which is lost, until he find it (the shepherd's math values the one over the ninety-nine, at least for the length of the search)?",
  "Luke 15:7": "I say unto you, that likewise joy shall be in heaven over one sinner that repenteth, more than over ninety and nine just persons, which need no repentance (heaven's celebration is aimed at the one who was found, not the ones who never wandered).",
  "Luke 15:13": "And not many days after the younger son gathered all together, and took his journey into a far country, and there wasted his substance with riotous living (he converts an inheritance into a season, and spends both).",
  "Luke 15:17": "And when he came to himself, he said, How many hired servants of my father's have bread enough and to spare, and I perish with hunger (the turning point in this story happens in a pig pen, not a synagogue)!",
  "Luke 15:20": "And he arose, and came to his father. But when he was yet a great way off, his father saw him, and had compassion, and ran, and fell on his neck, and kissed him (the father is watching for him before the son has said a single word of his rehearsed speech).",
  "Luke 15:22": "But the father said to his servants, Bring forth the best robe, and put it on him; and put a ring on his hand, and shoes on his feet (he is dressed like a son again before he has done anything to earn it back).",
  "Luke 15:24": "For this my son was dead, and is alive again; he was lost, and is found. And they began to be merry (the father names him son in the same breath he explains why there's a celebration).",
  "Luke 15:31": "And he said unto him, Son, thou art ever with me, and all that I have is thine (the father leaves his own party to plead with the son who never left, using the same tenderness he showed the one who did).",

  "Luke 16:10": "He that is faithful in that which is least is faithful also in much: and he that is unjust in the least is unjust also in much (character shows up first in the small, unwatched decisions).",
  "Luke 16:13": "No servant can serve two masters: for either he will hate the one, and love the other; or else he will hold to the one, and despise the other. Ye cannot serve God and mammon (He doesn't say it's hard to serve both -- He says it's impossible).",
  "Luke 16:15": "And he said unto them, Ye are they which justify yourselves before men; but God knoweth your hearts: for that which is highly esteemed among men is abomination in the sight of God (He answers the Pharisees' scorn by naming exactly what God sees when He looks past their reputation).",
  "Luke 16:16": "The law and the prophets were until John: since that time the kingdom of God is preached, and every man presseth into it (He marks the hinge point of redemptive history in one sentence -- the law and the prophets on one side, the Kingdom preached on the other, with John standing at the seam).",
  "Luke 16:17": "And it is easier for heaven and earth to pass, than one tittle of the law to fail (the claim being made here is about His own faithfulness to the law, not the reader's -- the very next verse shows Him upholding a piece of it the Pharisees had quietly abandoned).",
  "Luke 16:18": "Whosoever putteth away his wife, and marrieth another, committeth adultery: and whosoever marrieth her that is put away from her husband committeth adultery (He picks the one issue the Pharisees had rewritten to their own convenience, and states the original terms back to them without adjustment).",
  "Luke 16:19": "There was a certain rich man, which was clothed in purple and fine linen, and fared sumptuously every day (the rich man in this story is never even given a name).",
  "Luke 16:20": "And there was a certain beggar named Lazarus, which was laid at his gate, full of sores, (the beggar is named and the rich man isn't -- Luke reverses which one the story bothers to remember)",
  "Luke 16:25": "But Abraham said, Son, remember that thou in thy lifetime receivedst thy good things, and likewise Lazarus evil things: but now he is comforted, and thou art tormented (the reversal in this story is total, and it happens the moment both men die).",
  "Luke 16:31": "And he said unto him, If they hear not Moses and the prophets, neither will they be persuaded, though one rose from the dead (a warning that a resurrection is not always enough to convince someone who has already decided not to listen).",

  "Luke 17:2": "It were better for him that a millstone were hanged about his neck, and he cast into the sea, than that he should offend one of these little ones (He measures the seriousness of causing a child to stumble against the worst death anyone in that room could imagine).",
  "Luke 17:4": "And if he trespass against thee seven times in a day, and seven times in a day turn again to thee, saying, I repent; thou shalt forgive him (the number in this verse isn't a ceiling -- it's a way of saying there isn't one).",
  "Luke 17:10": "So likewise ye, when ye shall have done all those things which are commanded you, say, We are unprofitable servants: we have done that which was our duty to do (even total obedience is not described here as something that puts God in anyone's debt).",
  "Luke 17:13": "And they lifted up their voices, and said, Jesus, Master, have mercy on us (ten men with the same disease cry out with the same two words).",
  "Luke 17:14": "And when he saw them, he said unto them, Go shew yourselves unto the priests. And it came to pass, that, as they went, they were cleansed (the healing happens on the road, somewhere between the instruction and the destination -- while they were still walking in faith toward what He'd told them to do).",
  "Luke 17:16": "And fell down on his face at his feet, giving him thanks: and he was a Samaritan (the one who comes back is the man in the group of ten with the least claim on a Jewish healer's gratitude).",
  "Luke 17:17": "And Jesus answering said, Were there not ten cleansed? but where are the nine (the question hangs in the air without an answer -- Luke never tells us)?",
  "Luke 17:19": "And he said unto him, Arise, go thy way: thy faith hath made thee whole (the other nine were also healed -- only this one is told he's been made whole).",
  "Luke 17:21": "Neither shall they say, Lo here! or, lo there! for, behold, the kingdom of God is within you (He answers a question about location with an answer that isn't a location at all).",
  "Luke 17:34": "I tell you, in that night there shall be two men in one bed; the one shall be taken, and the other shall be left (two people, side by side, in identical circumstances, with entirely different outcomes).",

  "Luke 18:1": "And he spake a parable unto them to this end, that men ought always to pray, and not to faint (the point of the story is stated before the story itself is told).",
  "Luke 18:7": "And shall not God avenge his own elect, which cry day and night unto him, though he bear long with them (He compares Himself to an unjust judge only to argue from the lesser to the greater -- if even a bad judge can be worn down, how much more a good Father)?",
  "Luke 18:12": "I fast twice in the week, I give tithes of all that I possess (his prayer reads like a résumé, itemized and dated, addressed to a God he assumes needs reminding).",
  "Luke 18:13": "And the publican, standing afar off, would not lift up so much as his eyes unto heaven, but smote upon his breast, saying, God be merciful to me a sinner (he brings nothing into the temple that day but the truth about himself).",
  "Luke 18:14": "I tell you, this man went down to his house justified rather than the other: for every one that exalteth himself shall be abased; and he that humbleth himself shall be exalted (the man who listed no accomplishments leaves more right with God than the man who listed several).",
  "Luke 18:16": "But Jesus called them unto him, and said, Suffer little children to come unto me, and forbid them not: for of such is the kingdom of God (the disciples try to guard His time and He corrects them by naming who the kingdom actually belongs to).",
  "Luke 18:24": "And when Jesus saw that he was very sorrowful, he said, How hardly shall they that have riches enter into the kingdom of God! (the shock in the room is not the statement itself -- it's that the crowd had always assumed wealth was the evidence of God's favor, not an obstacle to it).",
  "Luke 18:25": "For it is easier for a camel to go through a needle's eye, than for a rich man to enter into the kingdom of God (riches themselves are not named as the problem two verses earlier -- the dependence on them is; this is the picture of what that dependence looks like from the outside).",
  "Luke 18:27": "And he said, The things which are impossible with men are possible with God (He answers a crowd's despair over who can be saved with a statement about what God, not man, is able to do).",
  "Luke 18:31": "Then he took unto him the twelve, and said unto them, Behold, we go up to Jerusalem, and all things that are written by the prophets concerning the Son of man shall be accomplished (He walks toward Jerusalem knowing exactly what the prophets already said would happen there).",
  "Luke 18:33": "And they shall scourge him, and put him to death: and the third day he shall rise again (He states His own resurrection with the same plainness He states His own execution).",
  "Luke 18:38": "And he cried, saying, Jesus, thou Son of David, have mercy on me (a blind beggar identifies Him correctly by a title the sighted crowd around him never uses).",
  "Luke 18:42": "And Jesus said unto him, Receive thy sight: thy faith hath saved thee (the same word covers his eyes and his soul in a single sentence).",

  "Luke 19:5": "And when Jesus came to the place, he looked up, and saw him, and said unto him, Zaccheus, make haste, and come down; for to day I must abide at thy house (out of an entire crowd, He looks up into one tree and calls one man by name).",
  "Luke 19:8": "And Zaccheus stood, and said unto the Lord; Behold, Lord, the half of my goods I give to the poor; and if I have taken any thing from any man by false accusation, I restore him fourfold (a man notorious for taking now can't stop giving back).",
  "Luke 19:9": "And Jesus said unto him, This day is salvation come to this house, forsomuch as he also is a son of Abraham (salvation arrives at Zacchaeus's house the same afternoon Jesus does).",
  "Luke 19:10": "For the Son of man is come to seek and to save that which was lost (He states His entire mission in a single sentence, standing in the house of the man the whole town had given up on).",
  "Luke 19:17": "And he said unto him, Well, thou good servant: because thou hast been faithful in a very little, have thou authority over ten cities (the smallest faithfulness in this story is answered with the largest reward).",
  "Luke 19:38": "Saying, Blessed be the King that cometh in the name of the Lord: peace in heaven, and glory in the highest (the crowd calls Him King on the same road that leads, within days, to a cross).",
  "Luke 19:40": "And he answered and said unto them, I tell you that, if these should hold their peace, the stones would immediately cry out (He tells the Pharisees that His praise will happen with or without human voices to carry it).",
  "Luke 19:41": "And when he was come near, he beheld the city, and wept over it, (He weeps over Jerusalem in the very same procession where the crowd is celebrating Him)",
  "Luke 19:46": "Saying unto them, It is written, My house is the house of prayer: but ye have made it a den of thieves (the one place in the Gospels where His anger becomes physical is aimed at what had been done inside His Father's house).",

  "Luke 20:17": "And he beheld them, and said, What is this then that is written, The stone which the builders rejected, the same is become the head of the corner (He quotes a psalm about Himself back to the very builders who are about to reject Him)?",
  "Luke 20:25": "And he said unto them, Render therefore unto Cesar the things which be Cesar's, and unto God the things which be God's (a trap built with two questions is answered with one sentence that closes both jaws on nothing).",
  "Luke 20:36": "Neither can they die any more: for they are equal unto the angels; and are the children of God, being the children of the resurrection (He answers a hypothetical about seven husbands by describing a life the question never accounted for).",
  "Luke 20:38": "For he is not a God of the dead, but of the living: for all live unto him (He argues for the resurrection from a verb tense -- God is, not God was, the God of the patriarchs).",
  "Luke 20:46": "Beware of the scribes, which desire to walk in long robes, and love greetings in the markets, and the highest seats in the synagogues, and the chief rooms at feasts (He warns the crowd about the very men they had been trained to imitate).",

  "Luke 21:3": "And he said, Of a truth I say unto you, that this poor widow hath cast in more than they all (He measures the gift by what it cost her, not by what it totaled).",
  "Luke 21:9": "But when ye shall hear of wars and commotions, be not terrified: for these things must first come to pass; but the end is not by and by (He tells them what's coming specifically so that when it comes, it won't be mistaken for the end).",
  "Luke 21:18": "But there shall not an hair of your head perish (spoken in the same breath as a warning that some of them will be put to death -- the promise reaches further than the danger does).",
  "Luke 21:19": "In your patience possess ye your souls (endurance, not escape, is the promise He gives them for what's ahead).",
  "Luke 21:27": "And then shall they see the Son of man coming in a cloud with power and great glory (the same title He used for Himself all through this Gospel is the title used to describe His return).",
  "Luke 21:28": "And when these things begin to come to pass, then look up, and lift up your heads; for your redemption draweth nigh (the instruction for the worst news in the chapter is to look up, not down).",
  "Luke 21:33": "Heaven and earth shall pass away: but my words shall not pass away (He measures His own words against the durability of the universe, and gives His words the win).",
  "Luke 21:36": "Watch ye therefore, and pray always, that ye may be accounted worthy to escape all these things that shall come to pass, and to stand before the Son of man (the posture He asks for is readiness, not prediction).",

  "Luke 22:15": "And he said unto them, With desire I have desired to eat this passover with you before I suffer (He names His own coming suffering out loud at the start of the meal, before anyone else at the table does).",
  "Luke 22:19": "And he took bread, and gave thanks, and brake it, and gave unto them, saying, This is my body which is given for you: this do in remembrance of me (He hands them the meaning of His own death before it has happened, so they'll recognize it once it has).",
  "Luke 22:20": "Likewise also the cup after supper, saying, This cup is the new testament in my blood, which is shed for you (a covenant is announced over a cup of wine, hours before it is paid for at a cross).",
  "Luke 22:26": "But ye shall not be so: but he that is greatest among you, let him be as the younger; and he that is chief, as he that doth serve (He redefines greatness at the very table where they had just been arguing about who had the most of it).",
  "Luke 22:32": "But I have prayed for thee, that thy faith fail not: and when thou art converted, strengthen thy brethren (He tells Peter what's coming and prays for him before Peter has any idea he'll need it).",
  "Luke 22:42": "Saying, Father, if thou be willing, remove this cup from me: nevertheless not my will, but thine, be done (the prayer that decided everything was prayed by the one Man with the standing to refuse who chose not to).",
  "Luke 22:44": "And being in an agony he prayed more earnestly: and his sweat was as it were great drops of blood falling down to the ground (His body registers what's coming before a single soldier has touched Him).",
  "Luke 22:51": "And Jesus answered and said, Suffer ye thus far. And he touched his ear, and healed him (in the middle of His own arrest, He stops to heal the man arresting Him).",
  "Luke 22:61": "And the Lord turned, and looked upon Peter. And Peter remembered the word of the Lord, how he had said unto him, Before the cock crow, thou shalt deny me thrice (no rebuke is recorded here -- only a look, and it's enough to break him).",
  "Luke 22:70": "Then said they all, Art thou then the Son of God? And he said unto them, Ye say that I am (asked directly, under threat of death, He does not soften the claim).",

  "Luke 23:34": "Then said Jesus, Father, forgive them; for they know not what they do. And they parted his raiment, and cast lots (He asks forgiveness for the men driving the nails while they are still driving them).",
  "Luke 23:41": "And we indeed justly; for we receive the due reward of our deeds: but this man hath done nothing amiss (a dying criminal states the innocence of the Man dying next to him more plainly than the court that convicted Him did).",
  "Luke 23:42": "And he said unto Jesus, Lord, remember me when thou comest into thy kingdom (his entire request for eternity is nine words, spoken with nothing left to offer but the request itself).",
  "Luke 23:43": "And Jesus said unto him, Verily I say unto thee, To day shalt thou be with me in paradise (a man who will die within hours is promised paradise before the day is even over).",
  "Luke 23:46": "And when Jesus had cried with a loud voice, he said, Father, into thy hands I commend my spirit: and having said thus, he gave up the ghost (His last recorded words are not a cry of defeat but an act of trust, spoken on purpose).",
  "Luke 23:47": "Now when the centurion saw what was done, he glorified God, saying, Certainly this was a righteous man (a Roman soldier reaches the verdict the Jewish court never did).",

  "Luke 24:6": "He is not here, but is risen: remember how he spake unto you when he was yet in Galilee, (four words undo everything the previous day accomplished -- he is not here)",
  "Luke 24:15": "And it came to pass, that, while they communed together and reasoned, Jesus himself drew near, and went with them (He joins two grieving disciples on the road before either of them recognizes who's walking beside them).",
  "Luke 24:16": "But their eyes were holden that they should not know him (the risen Christ walks for miles next to two of His own followers, unrecognized).",
  "Luke 24:27": "And beginning at Moses and all the prophets, he expounded unto them in all the scriptures the things concerning himself (He teaches His own resurrection out of the Old Testament before He ever tells them plainly who He is).",
  "Luke 24:31": "And their eyes were opened, and they knew him; and he vanished out of their sight (recognition and disappearance happen in the same instant, at the breaking of a loaf of bread).",
  "Luke 24:32": "And they said one to another, Did not our heart burn within us, while he talked with us by the way, and while he opened to us the scriptures (they felt something true before they had words for what it was)?",
  "Luke 24:39": "Behold my hands and my feet, that it is I myself: handle me, and see; for a spirit hath not flesh and bones, as ye see me have (He offers His own body as evidence, not just His own words).",
  "Luke 24:47": "And that repentance and remission of sins should be preached in his name among all nations, beginning at Jerusalem (the message is sent out first to the very city that had just crucified Him).",
  "Luke 24:52": "And they worshipped him, and returned to Jerusalem with great joy: (the Gospel that opened with an old priest struck silent by an angel closes with a room full of ordinary people worshipping out loud)",
  "John 1:1": "In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God (three claims stacked into one sentence before a single event of the story has happened).",
  "John 1:3": "All things were made by him; and without him was not any thing made that was made (nothing on the list of everything that exists came into being apart from him).",
  "John 1:4": "In him was life; and the life was the light of men (life and light are not two separate gifts here, they are the same gift seen from two angles).",
  "John 1:11": "He came unto his own, and his own received him not (the Creator arrives among his own creation and is turned away at the door).",
  "John 1:12": "But as many as received him, to them gave he power to become the sons of God, even to them that believe on his name (sonship here is something given upon belief, not something owed by birth):",
  "John 1:14": "And the Word was made flesh, and dwelt among us, (and we beheld his glory, the glory as of the only begotten of the Father,) full of grace and truth (the eternal Word takes on a body and moves into the neighborhood).",
  "John 1:17": "For the law was given by Moses, but grace and truth came by Jesus Christ (Moses delivered a standard; Jesus delivered a person).",
  "John 1:29": "The next day John seeth Jesus coming unto him, and saith, Behold the Lamb of God, which taketh away the sin of the world (John names what Jesus came to do before Jesus has done any of it).",
  "John 1:41": "He first findeth his own brother Simon, and saith unto him, We have found the Messias, which is, being interpreted, the Christ (the very first thing Andrew does after meeting Jesus is go get his brother).",
  "John 1:47": "Jesus saw Nathanael coming to him, and saith of him, Behold an Israelite indeed, in whom is no guile (Jesus reads a stranger's character before the man has spoken a single word to him)!",
  "John 1:51": "And he saith unto him, Verily, verily, I say unto you, Hereafter ye shall see heaven open, and the angels of God ascending and descending upon the Son of man (Jacob once dreamed of a ladder reaching heaven; Nathanael is told he is standing in front of the reality).",
  "John 2:4": "Jesus saith unto her, Woman, what have I to do with thee? mine hour is not yet come (he answers his mother by pointing to a timing that answers to his Father, not to her).",
  "John 2:11": "This beginning of miracles did Jesus in Cana of Galilee, and manifested forth his glory; and his disciples believed on him (turning water into wine is the first public sign of who he actually is).",
  "John 2:16": "And said unto them that sold doves, Take these things hence; make not my Father\u2019s house an house of merchandise (he calls the building his Father's house in the middle of clearing the merchants out of it).",
  "John 2:19": "Jesus answered and said unto them, Destroy this temple, and in three days I will raise it up (he answers a demand for proof with a prediction about his own death and resurrection, though no one in the room understands that yet).",
  "John 2:21": "But he spake of the temple of his body (John explains the riddle only after the fact, years later).",
  "John 2:24": "But Jesus did not commit himself unto them, because he knew all men (he does not trust a crowd's excitement just because it is aimed at him),",
  "John 2:25": "And needed not that any should testify of man: for he knew what was in man (no one has to tell him what a person is really like).",
  "John 3:2": "The same came to Jesus by night (a member of the ruling council comes to question him after dark, where no one will see him doing it), and said unto him, Rabbi, we know that thou art a teacher come from God: for no man can do these miracles that thou doest, except God be with him.",
  "John 3:3": "Jesus answered and said unto him, Verily, verily, I say unto thee, Except a man be born again, he cannot see the kingdom of God (he answers a question about credentials with a claim about birth).",
  "John 3:6": "That which is born of the flesh is flesh; and that which is born of the Spirit is spirit (two different kinds of birth produce two different kinds of life, and one cannot substitute for the other).",
  "John 3:8": "The wind bloweth where it listeth, and thou hearest the sound thereof, but canst not tell whence it cometh, and whither it goeth: so is every one that is born of the Spirit (he compares the new birth to wind -- unseen in itself, known only by its effect).",
  "John 3:14": "And as Moses lifted up the serpent in the wilderness, even so must the Son of man be lifted up (a bronze snake raised on a pole in the wilderness becomes, centuries later, the picture he uses for his own crucifixion):",
  "John 3:15": "That whosoever believeth in him should not perish, but have eternal life (the offer excludes no one by name).",
  "John 3:16": "For God so loved the world, that he gave his only begotten Son (the reason comes before the method -- love first, then the giving of the Son), that whosoever believeth in him should not perish, but have everlasting life.",
  "John 3:17": "For God sent not his Son into the world to condemn the world; but that the world through him might be saved (his first mission to earth was rescue, not judgment).",
  "John 3:18": "He that believeth on him is not condemned (the verdict is already rendered, one way or the other, before the story even continues): but he that believeth not is condemned already, because he hath not believed in the name of the only begotten Son of God.",
  "John 3:19": "And this is the condemnation, that light is come into the world, and men loved darkness rather than light, because their deeds were evil (the world doesn't reject the light for lack of evidence, it prefers the dark).",
  "John 3:30": "He must increase, but I must decrease (John describes his own career in four words).",
  "John 3:36": "He that believeth on the Son hath everlasting life: and he that believeth not the Son shall not see life; but the wrath of God abideth on him (the verse measures faith not by feeling but by whether wrath still rests on a person).",
  "John 4:6": "Now Jacob\u2019s well was there. Jesus therefore, being wearied with his journey, sat thus on the well: and it was about the sixth hour (the Creator of the universe sits down by a well, tired from walking).",
  "John 4:7": "There cometh a woman of Samaria to draw water: Jesus saith unto her, Give me to drink (he opens the conversation by asking her for something, not by offering her something).",
  "John 4:9": "Then saith the woman of Samaria unto him, How is it that thou, being a Jew, askest drink of me, which am a woman of Samaria? for the Jews have no dealings with the Samaritans (she is more surprised that he is talking to her at all than by anything he has said).",
  "John 4:10": "Jesus answered and said unto her, If thou knewest the gift of God, and who it is that saith to thee, Give me to drink; thou wouldest have asked of him, and he would have given thee living water (he offers to trade a request for water into a gift she has not asked for yet).",
  "John 4:14": "But whosoever drinketh of the water that I shall give him shall never thirst; but the water that I shall give him shall be in him a well of water springing up into everlasting life (what he gives does not need refilling, because the source moves with the person who drinks it).",
  "John 4:18": "For thou hast had five husbands; and he whom thou now hast is not thy husband: in that saidst thou truly (he confirms what she admitted and then tells her the rest of what she did not).",
  "John 4:22": "Ye worship ye know not what: we know what we worship: for salvation is of the Jews (he settles the theological dispute in a single clause before moving past it).",
  "John 4:24": "God is a Spirit: and they that worship him must worship him in spirit and in truth (worship here is defined by two things that neither location nor ritual can provide).",
  "John 4:26": "Jesus saith unto her, I that speak unto thee am he (he tells a Samaritan woman at a well what he withheld, in plainer terms, from a ruler of the Sanhedrin).",
  "John 4:29": "Come, see a man, which told me all things that ever I did (the very thing that exposed her past becomes the thing she runs to tell the whole town): is not this the Christ?",
  "John 4:34": "Jesus saith unto them, My meat is to do the will of him that sent me, and to finish his work (he names hunger and calls it something else entirely).",
  "John 4:35": "Say not ye, There are yet four months, and then cometh harvest? behold, I say unto you, Lift up your eyes, and look on the fields; for they are white already to harvest (he tells them the harvest is not coming later, it is standing in front of them right now).",
  "John 4:39": "And many of the Samaritans of that city believed on him for the saying of the woman, which testified, He told me all that ever I did (a whole town starts believing because of one woman's testimony about her own life).",
  "John 4:48": "Then said Jesus unto him, Except ye see signs and wonders, ye will not believe (he names the weakness in the man's faith before answering his request).",
  "John 4:50": "Jesus saith unto him, Go thy way; thy son liveth (the healing happens the moment the words are spoken, miles away from where the boy actually lies). And the man believed the word that Jesus had spoken unto him, and he went his way.",
  "John 4:53": "So the father knew that it was at the same hour, in the which Jesus said unto him, Thy son liveth: and himself believed, and his whole house (the timestamp confirms it for him, and the whole household becomes believers over one word spoken at a distance).",
  "John 5:6": "When Jesus saw him lie, and knew that he had been now a long time in that case, he saith unto him, Wilt thou be made whole (he asks a man who has been sick for thirty-eight years a question that sounds almost too simple)?",
  "John 5:8": "Jesus saith unto him, Rise, take up thy bed, and walk (one sentence does what thirty-eight years of waiting by the pool never did).",
  "John 5:17": "But Jesus answered them, My Father worketh hitherto, and I work (he answers a Sabbath accusation by placing his own work alongside his Father's, without qualification).",
  "John 5:18": "Therefore the Jews sought the more to kill him, because he not only had broken the sabbath, but said also that God was his Father, making himself equal with God (his opponents understand the claim correctly -- that is exactly why they want him dead).",
  "John 5:19": "Then answered Jesus and said unto them, Verily, verily, I say unto you, The Son can do nothing of himself, but what he seeth the Father do: for what things soever he doeth, these also doeth the Son likewise (he describes a unity of action so complete that nothing the Son does originates apart from the Father).",
  "John 5:21": "For as the Father raiseth up the dead, and quickeneth them; even so the Son quickeneth whom he will (he claims the power to raise the dead as his own, not as something borrowed).",
  "John 5:24": "Verily, verily, I say unto you, He that heareth my word, and believeth on him that sent me, hath everlasting life, and shall not come into condemnation; but is passed from death unto life (the verse describes eternal life as something already received, not something still pending).",
  "John 5:25": "Verily, verily, I say unto you, The hour is coming, and now is, when the dead shall hear the voice of the Son of God: and they that hear shall live (he speaks of the dead hearing his voice while he is still standing in front of living men).",
  "John 5:28": "Marvel not at this: for the hour is coming, in the which all that are in the graves shall hear his voice (every grave, without exception, is included in what he says is coming),",
  "John 5:30": "I can of mine own self do nothing: as I hear, I judge: and my judgment is just; because I seek not mine own will, but the will of the Father which hath sent me (he grounds his own judgment in obedience, not independence).",
  "John 5:36": "But I have greater witness than that of John: for the works which the Father hath given me to finish, the same works that I do, bear witness of me, that the Father hath sent me (the proof he offers is not a claim about himself, it is the work itself).",
  "John 6:9": "There is a lad here, which hath five barley loaves, and two small fishes: but what are they among so many (a boy's lunch is the entire supply on hand when the miracle begins)?",
  "John 6:11": "And Jesus took the loaves; and when he had given thanks (he thanks God for the food before there is enough of it to feed anyone), he distributed to the disciples, and the disciples to them that were set down; and likewise of the fishes as much as they would.",
  "John 6:12": "When they were filled, he said unto his disciples, Gather up the fragments that remain, that nothing be lost (he multiplies bread by the basketful and still tells them to pick up every piece of it).",
  "John 6:20": "But he saith unto them, It is I; be not afraid (the words he uses to calm the men in the boat are the same words God once used to name himself to Moses).",
  "John 6:26": "Jesus answered them and said, Verily, verily, I say unto you, Ye seek me, not because ye saw the miracles, but because ye did eat of the loaves, and were filled (he answers the crowd's enthusiasm by naming the actual reason for it).",
  "John 6:27": "Labour not for the meat which perisheth, but for that meat which endureth unto everlasting life, which the Son of man shall give unto you: for him hath God the Father sealed (he tells a hungry crowd to want more than bread).",
  "John 6:35": "And Jesus said unto them, I am the bread of life: he that cometh to me shall never hunger; and he that believeth on me shall never thirst (he answers both hunger and thirst with the same claim about himself).",
  "John 6:37": "All that the Father giveth me shall come to me; and him that cometh to me I will in no wise cast out (the promise has no exceptions listed, and none implied).",
  "John 6:38": "For I came down from heaven, not to do mine own will, but the will of him that sent me (he describes his entire mission as obedience, not personal agenda).",
  "John 6:39": "And this is the Father\u2019s will which hath sent me, that of all which he hath given me I should lose nothing (he states the outcome of the Father's will as already decided, not still at risk), but should raise it up again at the last day.",
  "John 6:40": "And this is the will of him that sent me, that every one which seeth the Son, and believeth on him, may have everlasting life (everlasting life is given to seeing and believing, not to any further condition): and I will raise him up at the last day.",
  "John 6:44": "No man can come to me, except the Father which hath sent me draw him (he places the first move toward himself in God's hands, not the sinner's): and I will raise him up at the last day.",
  "John 6:47": "Verily, verily, I say unto you, He that believeth on me hath everlasting life (the verse states possession in the present tense, not a hope for later).",
  "John 6:51": "I am the living bread which came down from heaven: if any man eat of this bread, he shall live for ever: and the bread that I will give is my flesh, which I will give for the life of the world (the bread he is describing is his own body, offered before it has been taken from him).",
  "John 6:63": "It is the spirit that quickeneth; the flesh profiteth nothing (he clarifies his own hardest sentence before anyone can misread it literally): the words that I speak unto you, they are spirit, and they are life.",
  "John 6:66": "From that time many of his disciples went back, and walked no more with him (the crowd that wanted to crown him a chapter earlier now leaves because of what he actually said).",
  "John 6:68": "Then Simon Peter answered him, Lord, to whom shall we go? thou hast the words of eternal life (Peter does not defend the hard teaching, he just states he has nowhere else to go).",
  "John 7:6": "Then Jesus said unto them, My time is not yet come: but your time is alway ready (he draws a line between his own timing, set by the Father, and theirs, set by nothing in particular).",
  "John 7:16": "Jesus answered them, and said, My doctrine is not mine, but his that sent me (he denies personal authorship of everything he teaches).",
  "John 7:17": "If any man will do his will, he shall know of the doctrine, whether it be of God, or whether I speak of myself (he offers a test for his own words that depends on the hearer's willingness, not just their intellect).",
  "John 7:24": "Judge not according to the appearance, but judge righteous judgment (he tells them to judge by the whole of the standard, not by whichever piece is convenient).",
  "John 7:28": "Then cried Jesus in the temple as he taught, saying, Ye both know me, and ye know whence I am: and I am not come of myself, but he that sent me is true (he answers a crowd that thinks it already knows him by pointing past himself to the one who sent him), whom ye know not.",
  "John 7:37": "In the last day, that great day of the feast, Jesus stood and cried, saying, If any man thirst, let him come unto me, and drink (he stands up in the middle of a festival and issues an open invitation with no qualifications attached).",
  "John 7:38": "He that believeth on me, as the scripture hath said, out of his belly shall flow rivers of living water (what flows out of the believer is not a trickle but a river).",
  "John 7:46": "The officers answered, Never man spake like this man (the men sent to arrest him come back with a compliment instead of a prisoner).",
  "John 7:51": "Doth our law judge any man, before it hear him, and know what he doeth (a member of the council is the one voice in the room asking for due process)?",
  "John 8:11": "She said, No man, Lord. And Jesus said unto her, Neither do I condemn thee (he lets her go free without a single condition attached to the sentence): go, and sin no more.",
  "John 8:12": "Then spake Jesus again unto them, saying, I am the light of the world: he that followeth me shall not walk in darkness, but shall have the light of life (he claims for himself the very thing the festival lamps in the temple court were only symbols of).",
  "John 8:24": "I said therefore unto you, that ye shall die in your sins: for if ye believe not that I am he, ye shall die in your sins (he names the one thing standing between them and that outcome as belief, not behavior).",
  "John 8:29": "And he that sent me is with me: the Father hath not left me alone; for I do always those things that please him (he can say what no other person ever could -- that he has never once failed to please the Father).",
  "John 8:32": "And ye shall know the truth, and the truth shall make you free (freedom here is the result of knowing something, not achieving something).",
  "John 8:34": "Jesus answered them, Verily, verily, I say unto you, Whosoever committeth sin is the servant of sin (he names slavery as the actual condition sin produces, whatever freedom it claims to offer).",
  "John 8:36": "If the Son therefore shall make you free, ye shall be free indeed (he does not offer partial freedom; the freedom he gives has no leftover chains).",
  "John 8:44": "Ye are of your father the devil, and the lusts of your father ye will do. He was a murderer from the beginning, and abode not in the truth, because there is no truth in him. When he speaketh a lie, he speaketh of his own: for he is a liar, and the father of it (he traces the very first lie back to its source in a single sentence).",
  "John 8:51": "Verily, verily, I say unto you, If a man keep my saying, he shall never see death (he makes a claim about death itself that no teacher before him had dared make).",
  "John 8:56": "Your father Abraham rejoiced to see my day: and he saw it, and was glad (a man who lived two thousand years earlier is described as rejoicing over a day he would not live to see in the flesh).",
  "John 8:58": "Jesus said unto them, Verily, verily, I say unto you, Before Abraham was, I am (he answers a question about his age by reaching for God's own name for himself).",
  "John 9:3": "Jesus answered, Neither hath this man sinned, nor his parents: but that the works of God should be made manifest in him (he refuses to treat the man's blindness as a scoreboard for anyone's sin).",
  "John 9:5": "As long as I am in the world, I am the light of the world (he repeats a claim he already made once, this time right before healing a man born in total darkness).",
  "John 9:11": "He answered and said, A man that is called Jesus made clay, and anointed mine eyes, and said unto me, Go to the pool of Siloam, and wash: and I went and washed, and I received sight (a man born blind describes his own healing in five plain words).",
  "John 9:25": "He answered and said, Whether he be a sinner or no, I know not: one thing I know, that, whereas I was blind, now I see (he does not argue theology with the men interrogating him, he just states what happened to his eyes).",
  "John 9:33": "If this man were not of God, he could do nothing (the healed beggar out-reasons the religious court currently questioning him).",
  "John 9:35": "Jesus heard that they had cast him out; and when he had found him, he said unto him, Dost thou believe on the Son of God (Jesus goes looking for the man the religious leaders just threw out)?",
  "John 9:38": "And he said, Lord, I believe. And he worshipped him (the same day he is cast out of the synagogue, he ends up worshipping the one who healed him).",
  "John 9:39": "And Jesus said, For judgment I am come into this world, that they which see not might see; and that they which see might be made blind (he describes his own arrival as a dividing line, not a universal cure).",
  "John 10:3": "To him the porter openeth; and the sheep hear his voice: and he calleth his own sheep by name (the shepherd does not just own the flock, he knows each one individually by name), and leadeth them out.",
  "John 10:9": "I am the door: by me if any man enter in, he shall be saved (he states the way in with no alternative entrance mentioned), and shall go in and out, and find pasture.",
  "John 10:10": "The thief cometh not, but for to steal, and to kill, and to destroy: I am come that they might have life, and that they might have it more abundantly (he contrasts his own purpose against a thief's in the very same sentence).",
  "John 10:11": "I am the good shepherd: the good shepherd giveth his life for the sheep (he defines what makes the shepherd good before anything has happened yet to prove it).",
  "John 10:14": "I am the good shepherd, and know my sheep, and am known of mine (the knowing runs in both directions -- he knows them, and they know him).",
  "John 10:16": "And other sheep I have, which are not of this fold: them also I must bring, and they shall hear my voice; and there shall be one fold, and one shepherd (he already sees the two flocks becoming one before either group has met the other).",
  "John 10:17": "Therefore doth my Father love me, because I lay down my life, that I might take it again (he speaks of laying down his life and taking it back up as though both parts were equally certain).",
  "John 10:18": "No man taketh it from me, but I lay it down of myself (no one on the cross takes his life from him -- he tells them plainly that he is giving it). I have power to lay it down, and I have power to take it again. This commandment have I received of my Father.",
  "John 10:27": "My sheep hear my voice, and I know them (knowing his sheep is not dependent on their knowing him first), and they follow me:",
  "John 10:28": "And I give unto them eternal life; and they shall never perish, neither shall any man pluck them out of my hand (the promise addresses every hand that might try, and rules out all of them).",
  "John 10:29": "My Father, which gave them me, is greater than all; and no man is able to pluck them out of my Father\u2019s hand (he backs his own promise with his Father's, so the same guarantee is made twice).",
  "John 10:30": "I and my Father are one (three words claim the very unity his accusers are about to pick up stones over).",
  "John 11:3": "Therefore his sisters sent unto him, saying, Lord, behold, he whom thou lovest is sick (the sisters do not ask for anything directly, they just tell him who is sick).",
  "John 11:4": "When Jesus heard that, he said, This sickness is not unto death, but for the glory of God, that the Son of God might be glorified thereby (he names the outcome of this illness before it has even run its course).",
  "John 11:6": "When he had heard therefore that he was sick, he abode two days still in the same place where he was (he delays on purpose, even knowing the man he loves is dying).",
  "John 11:14": "Then said Jesus unto them plainly, Lazarus is dead (he drops the euphemism and says plainly what he had only hinted at before).",
  "John 11:15": "And I am glad for your sakes that I was not there, to the intent ye may believe (he names his own absence at the death as something that will help their faith, not hurt it); nevertheless let us go unto him.",
  "John 11:21": "Then said Martha unto Jesus, Lord, if thou hadst been here, my brother had not died (her opening words to him are a statement of what did not happen, not a request for what still could).",
  "John 11:25": "Jesus said unto her, I am the resurrection, and the life: he that believeth in me, though he were dead, yet shall he live (he does not point Martha to a doctrine about resurrection, he tells her that resurrection is standing in front of her):",
  "John 11:26": "And whosoever liveth and believeth in me shall never die (he expands the promise to cover the living as well as the dead in the very next sentence). Believest thou this?",
  "John 11:35": "Jesus wept (the shortest verse in the Bible is also one of the plainest facts about who he is).",
  "John 11:40": "Jesus saith unto her, Said I not unto thee, that, if thou wouldest believe, thou shouldest see the glory of God (he ties her ability to see anything to whether she believes first)?",
  "John 11:41": "Then they took away the stone from the place where the dead was laid. And Jesus lifted up his eyes, and said, Father, I thank thee that thou hast heard me (he thanks the Father before the miracle happens, not after).",
  "John 11:43": "And when he thus had spoken, he cried with a loud voice, Lazarus, come forth (he calls a specific name into an open grave, not a general command to the dead).",
  "John 11:44": "And he that was dead came forth, bound hand and foot with graveclothes: and his face was bound about with a napkin. Jesus saith unto them, Loose him, and let him go (the miracle raises him; ordinary hands still have to unwrap him).",
  "John 11:50": "Nor consider that it is expedient for us, that one man should die for the people, and that the whole nation perish not (the high priest means it as politics, and ends up saying something truer than he knows).",
  "John 11:52": "And not for that nation only, but that also he should gather together in one the children of God that were scattered abroad (the death being plotted in this room will end up gathering the very people this room fears).",
  "John 12:3": "Then took Mary a pound of ointment of spikenard, very costly, and anointed the feet of Jesus, and wiped his feet with her hair: and the house was filled with the odour of the ointment (one act of extravagant devotion fills the entire house with its scent).",
  "John 12:7": "Then said Jesus, Let her alone: against the day of my burying hath she kept this (he reads her gift as preparation for his own death before anyone else in the room does).",
  "John 12:8": "For the poor always ye have with you; but me ye have not always (he tells them plainly that his presence with them has a deadline).",
  "John 12:13": "Took branches of palm trees, and went forth to meet him, and cried, Hosanna: Blessed is the King of Israel that cometh in the name of the Lord (the crowd shouts the right words for the wrong reasons, days before some of the same voices call for his death).",
  "John 12:24": "Verily, verily, I say unto you, Except a corn of wheat fall into the ground and die, it abideth alone: but if it die, it bringeth forth much fruit (he describes his own coming death using the most ordinary image available -- a single seed).",
  "John 12:25": "He that loveth his life shall lose it; and he that hateth his life in this world shall keep it unto life eternal (he states the paradox flatly: holding on loses it, letting go keeps it).",
  "John 12:26": "If any man serve me, let him follow me; and where I am, there shall also my servant be: if any man serve me, him will my Father honour (the honor he promises comes from the Father, not from the crowd).",
  "John 12:27": "Now is my soul troubled; and what shall I say? Father, save me from this hour: but for this cause came I unto this hour (he names the temptation to ask for a way out, and answers it in the same breath).",
  "John 12:32": "And I, if I be lifted up from the earth, will draw all men unto me (he describes his own execution as the very thing that will pull people toward him, not away).",
  "John 12:35": "Then Jesus said unto them, Yet a little while is the light with you. Walk while ye have the light, lest darkness come upon you (he tells them the light has a closing time, and urges them to use it while it is still open): for he that walketh in darkness knoweth not whither he goeth.",
  "John 12:44": "Jesus cried and said, He that believeth on me, believeth not on me, but on him that sent me (he redirects every claim of belief in himself back to the one who sent him).",
  "John 12:46": "I am come a light into the world, that whosoever believeth on me should not abide in darkness (he names himself as the one exit from the dark, not one option among several).",
  "John 12:47": "And if any man hear my words, and believe not, I judge him not: for I came not to judge the world, but to save the world (he draws a sharp line between his first coming and the judgment still to come).",
  "John 13:1": "Now before the feast of the passover, when Jesus knew that his hour was come that he should depart out of this world unto the Father, having loved his own which were in the world, he loved them unto the end (the last thing said about his love for them, before the betrayal even starts, is that it did not run out).",
  "John 13:4": "He riseth from supper, and laid aside his garments; and took a towel, and girded himself (the one with all authority given into his hands picks up the towel a servant would carry).",
  "John 13:5": "After that he poureth water into a bason, and began to wash the disciples\u2019 feet, and to wipe them with the towel wherewith he was girded (he washes the feet of the very men who will scatter within hours).",
  "John 13:8": "Peter saith unto him, Thou shalt never wash my feet. Jesus answered him, If I wash thee not, thou hast no part with me (he ties Peter's refusal of the towel to something far bigger than foot-washing).",
  "John 13:14": "If I then, your Lord and Master, have washed your feet; ye also ought to wash one another\u2019s feet (he turns his own action into the example, not just the moment).",
  "John 13:15": "For I have given you an example, that ye should do as I have done to you (he wants imitation, not admiration).",
  "John 13:21": "When Jesus had thus said, he was troubled in spirit, and testified, and said, Verily, verily, I say unto you, that one of you shall betray me (he says it plainly, at the same table, without naming the man yet).",
  "John 13:27": "And after the sop Satan entered into him. Then said Jesus unto him, That thou doest, do quickly (he does not stop the betrayal, he tells the betrayer to get on with it).",
  "John 13:34": "A new commandment I give unto you, That ye love one another; as I have loved you, that ye also love one another (the new commandment is not a new rule, it is his own love turned into the standard).",
  "John 13:35": "By this shall all men know that ye are my disciples, if ye have love one to another (he names the evidence that will identify his disciples to the watching world, and it is not doctrine).",
  "John 13:38": "Jesus answered him, Wilt thou lay down thy life for my sake? Verily, verily, I say unto thee, The cock shall not crow, till thou hast denied me thrice (he answers Peter's boast of loyalty with an exact prediction of how it will fail).",
  "John 14:1": "Let not your heart be troubled: ye believe in God, believe also in me (he tells them to trust him the same way they already trust God).",
  "John 14:2": "In my Father\u2019s house are many mansions: if it were not so, I would have told you. I go to prepare a place for you (he describes his departure as construction, not abandonment).",
  "John 14:3": "And if I go and prepare a place for you, I will come again, and receive you unto myself; that where I am, there ye may be also (the point of preparing the place is so they end up where he is).",
  "John 14:6": "Jesus saith unto him, I am the way, the truth, and the life: no man cometh unto the Father, but by me (he does not offer himself as one road among several, he says he is the only one).",
  "John 14:9": "Jesus saith unto him, Have I been so long time with you, and yet hast thou not known me, Philip? he that hath seen me hath seen the Father (he answers a request to see God by pointing to himself, standing in the room); and how sayest thou then, Shew us the Father?",
  "John 14:13": "And whatsoever ye shall ask in my name, that will I do, that the Father may be glorified in the Son (he ties the purpose of answered prayer to the Father's glory, not the one asking).",
  "John 14:16": "And I will pray the Father, and he shall give you another Comforter, that he may abide with you for ever (the Helper he promises is not temporary, unlike every other help they had known);",
  "John 14:18": "I will not leave you comfortless: I will come to you (he refuses to call his departure abandonment).",
  "John 14:20": "At that day ye shall know that I am in my Father, and ye in me, and I in you (he describes a mutual indwelling that runs in both directions at once).",
  "John 14:23": "Jesus answered and said unto him, If a man love me, he will keep my words: and my Father will love him, and we will come unto him, and make our abode with him (he promises not a visit but a permanent home).",
  "John 14:27": "Peace I leave with you, my peace I give unto you: not as the world giveth, give I unto you (he draws a hard line between the peace he offers and every other kind on the market). Let not your heart be troubled, neither let it be afraid.",
  "John 15:1": "I am the true vine (he claims the title outright instead of letting the image speak for itself), and my Father is the husbandman.",
  "John 15:2": "Every branch in me that beareth not fruit he taketh away: and every branch that beareth fruit, he purgeth it, that it may bring forth more fruit (fruit follows one thing only -- whether a person keeps the Cross as the object of their faith; that is what separates the branch that gets pruned from the branch that gets removed).",
  "John 15:4": "Abide in me, and I in you. As the branch cannot bear fruit of itself, except it abide in the vine (the branch does not manufacture its own fruit, it only carries what the vine supplies); no more can ye, except ye abide in me.",
  "John 15:5": "I am the vine, ye are the branches: He that abideth in me, and I in him, the same bringeth forth much fruit: for without me ye can do nothing (he does not say little, he says nothing).",
  "John 15:6": "If a man abide not in me, he is cast forth as a branch, and is withered; and men gather them, and cast them into the fire, and they are burned (to abide here means continuing faith in the Cross -- the withering and burning follow its absence, not its presence).",
  "John 15:8": "Herein is my Father glorified, that ye bear much fruit (he ties the Father's glory to fruit grown through the branches, not the vine acting alone); so shall ye be my disciples.",
  "John 15:9": "As the Father hath loved me, so have I loved you: continue ye in my love (he invites them to stay in a love that already exists, not to earn one that does not).",
  "John 15:12": "This is my commandment, That ye love one another, as I have loved you (the standard for loving one another is set by how he loved them, not by feeling).",
  "John 15:13": "Greater love hath no man than this, that a man lay down his life for his friends (he defines the greatest possible love before he demonstrates it himself).",
  "John 15:15": "Henceforth I call you not servants; for the servant knoweth not what his lord doeth: but I have called you friends (he changes their title before he changes anything about their circumstances); for all things that I have heard of my Father I have made known unto you.",
  "John 15:16": "Ye have not chosen me, but I have chosen you (he corrects the assumption before anyone has the chance to make it), and ordained you, that ye should go and bring forth fruit, and that your fruit should remain: that whatsoever ye shall ask of the Father in my name, he may give it you.",
  "John 15:26": "But when the Comforter is come, whom I will send unto you from the Father, even the Spirit of truth, which proceedeth from the Father, he shall testify of me (he defines the Spirit's coming mission as pointing to himself, not away from him):",
  "John 16:7": "Nevertheless I tell you the truth; It is expedient for you that I go away: for if I go not away, the Comforter will not come unto you; but if I depart, I will send him unto you (he tells them his own departure is the condition for the Spirit's arrival, not an unrelated loss).",
  "John 16:8": "And when he is come, he will reprove the world of sin, and of righteousness, and of judgment (he names three things the Spirit will do in the world, and none of them is comfort):",
  "John 16:13": "Howbeit when he, the Spirit of truth, is come, he will guide you into all truth (the promise is comprehensive -- all truth, not a portion of it): for he shall not speak of himself; but whatsoever he shall hear, that shall he speak: and he will shew you things to come.",
  "John 16:20": "Verily, verily, I say unto you, That ye shall weep and lament, but the world shall rejoice: and ye shall be sorrowful, but your sorrow shall be turned into joy (he does not promise to remove the sorrow, he promises to turn it into something else).",
  "John 16:22": "And ye now therefore have sorrow: but I will see you again, and your heart shall rejoice, and your joy no man taketh from you (he describes a joy specifically immune to outside interference).",
  "John 16:27": "For the Father himself loveth you (he tells them the Father's love for them is not mediated through him, it is direct), because ye have loved me, and have believed that I came out from God.",
  "John 16:33": "These things I have spoken unto you, that in me ye might have peace. In the world ye shall have tribulation: but be of good cheer; I have overcome the world (he tells them to take courage based on a victory he states as already won, not one still pending).",
  "John 17:3": "And this is life eternal, that they might know thee the only true God, and Jesus Christ, whom thou hast sent (he defines eternal life as knowing two persons, not agreeing to a set of facts).",
  "John 17:4": "I have glorified thee on the earth: I have finished the work which thou gavest me to do (he speaks of the cross as already finished, hours before it happens).",
  "John 17:11": "And now I am no more in the world, but these are in the world, and I come to thee. Holy Father, keep through thine own name those whom thou hast given me, that they may be one, as we are (he asks for a unity among his followers modeled on his own unity with the Father).",
  "John 17:15": "I pray not that thou shouldest take them out of the world, but that thou shouldest keep them from the evil (his prayer is not for their removal from the world, it is for their protection inside it).",
  "John 17:17": "Sanctify them through thy truth: thy word is truth (he does not say the word contains truth, he says it is truth).",
  "John 17:20": "Neither pray I for these alone, but for them also which shall believe on me through their word (his prayer reaches forward to people who have not believed yet);",
  "John 17:21": "That they all may be one; as thou, Father, art in me, and I in thee, that they also may be one in us: that the world may believe that thou hast sent me (he ties the unity of his followers to the world's ability to believe the message at all).",
  "John 17:24": "Father, I will that they also, whom thou hast given me, be with me where I am; that they may behold my glory, which thou hast given me: for thou lovedst me before the foundation of the world (he grounds his own identity in a love that predates creation itself).",
  "John 18:4": "Jesus therefore, knowing all things that should come upon him (he walks toward his own arrest with full knowledge of what it will cost, not caught by surprise), went forth, and said unto them, Whom seek ye?",
  "John 18:5": "They answered him, Jesus of Nazareth. Jesus saith unto them, I am he (he answers with the same two words God once used to name himself to Moses). And Judas also, which betrayed him, stood with them.",
  "John 18:6": "As soon then as he had said unto them, I am he, they went backward, and fell to the ground (an armed crowd sent to arrest one unarmed man ends up on the ground before he lifts a hand).",
  "John 18:9": "That the saying might be fulfilled, which he spake, Of them which thou gavest me have I lost none (in the middle of his own arrest, he is still accounting for the safety of the men standing next to him).",
  "John 18:11": "Then said Jesus unto Peter, Put up thy sword into the sheath: the cup which my Father hath given me, shall I not drink it (he refuses the sword offered on his behalf and names the cup he intends to drink instead)?",
  "John 18:20": "Jesus answered him, I spake openly to the world; I ever taught in the synagogue, and in the temple, whither the Jews always resort; and in secret have I said nothing (he answers a secret trial by pointing out that nothing he ever taught was said in private).",
  "John 18:23": "Jesus answered him, If I have spoken evil, bear witness of the evil: but if well, why smitest thou me (he responds to being struck with a question, not a threat)?",
  "John 18:27": "Peter then denied again: and immediately the cock crew (the sign Jesus predicted arrives the moment Peter finishes denying him a third time).",
  "John 18:37": "Pilate therefore said unto him, Art thou a king then? Jesus answered, Thou sayest that I am a king. To this end was I born, and for this cause came I into the world, that I should bear witness unto the truth (asked directly whether he is a king, he answers by naming what he came to testify to). Every one that is of the truth heareth my voice.",
  "John 18:38": "Pilate saith unto him, What is truth? And when he had said this, he went out again unto the Jews, and saith unto them, I find in him no fault at all (the man about to sentence him to death is also the man who states, on the record, that he is innocent).",
  "John 19:6": "When the chief priests therefore and officers saw him, they cried out, saying, Crucify him, crucify him. Pilate saith unto them, Take ye him, and crucify him: for I find no fault in him (Pilate repeats his verdict of innocence in the same breath he hands Jesus over to be crucified).",
  "John 19:11": "Jesus answered, Thou couldest have no power at all against me, except it were given thee from above (he tells the man holding power over his life that even that power is on loan): therefore he that delivered me unto thee hath the greater sin.",
  "John 19:17": "And he bearing his cross went forth (the one who spoke the universe into being now carries the instrument of his own execution) into a place called the place of a skull, which is called in the Hebrew Golgotha:",
  "John 19:19": "And Pilate wrote a title, and put it on the cross. And the writing was, JESUS OF NAZARETH THE KING OF THE JEWS (a sign meant as mockery ends up posting the truth over his head in three languages).",
  "John 19:24": "They said therefore among themselves, Let us not rend it, but cast lots for it, whose it shall be: that the scripture might be fulfilled, which saith, They parted my raiment among them, and for my vesture they did cast lots (soldiers gambling for clothing at the foot of the cross are, without knowing it, fulfilling a psalm written centuries earlier). These things therefore the soldiers did.",
  "John 19:26": "When Jesus therefore saw his mother, and the disciple standing by, whom he loved, he saith unto his mother, Woman, behold thy son (dying on a cross, he still arranges care for his mother before he arranges anything else)!",
  "John 19:28": "After this, Jesus knowing that all things were now accomplished, that the scripture might be fulfilled, saith, I thirst (even his physical suffering lines up with what was written about him long before he was born).",
  "John 19:30": "When Jesus therefore had received the vinegar, he said, It is finished (two words announce a completed transaction, not an interrupted one): and he bowed his head, and gave up the ghost.",
  "John 19:33": "But when they came to Jesus, and saw that he was dead already, they brake not his legs (the detail that seems incidental turns out to fulfill a specific, centuries-old prediction):",
  "John 19:38": "And after this Joseph of Arimathea, being a disciple of Jesus, but secretly for fear of the Jews, besought Pilate that he might take away the body of Jesus (a secret follower makes his first public move only after Jesus is already dead): and Pilate gave him leave. He came therefore, and took the body of Jesus.",
  "John 20:8": "Then went in also that other disciple, which came first to the sepulchre, and he saw, and believed (an empty tomb and folded graveclothes are enough evidence for John before he has even seen Jesus himself).",
  "John 20:15": "Jesus saith unto her, Woman, why weepest thou? whom seekest thou? She, supposing him to be the gardener (the first person to speak with the risen Christ does not recognize him at all), saith unto him, Sir, if thou have borne him hence, tell me where thou hast laid him, and I will take him away.",
  "John 20:16": "Jesus saith unto her, Mary (one word, her own name spoken in his voice, is what finally tells her who she is talking to). She turned herself, and saith unto him, Rabboni; which is to say, Master.",
  "John 20:17": "Jesus saith unto her, Touch me not; for I am not yet ascended to my Father: but go to my brethren, and say unto them, I ascend unto my Father, and your Father; and to my God, and your God (he links her relationship to God with his own, using the same words for both).",
  "John 20:19": "Then the same day at evening, being the first day of the week, when the doors were shut where the disciples were assembled for fear of the Jews, came Jesus and stood in the midst (locked doors are not treated as an obstacle), and saith unto them, Peace be unto you.",
  "John 20:20": "And when he had so said, he shewed unto them his hands and his side. Then were the disciples glad, when they saw the Lord (the fear that had them locked in a room turns to joy the moment he is standing in front of them).",
  "John 20:22": "And when he had said this, he breathed on them, and saith unto them, Receive ye the Holy Ghost (the same breath that once formed Adam is now given to the men he is sending out):",
  "John 20:25": "The other disciples therefore said unto him, We have seen the Lord. But he said unto them, Except I shall see in his hands the print of the nails, and put my finger into the print of the nails, and thrust my hand into his side, I will not believe (he sets a condition for his own faith, and Jesus will meet it exactly, without rebuking him for asking).",
  "John 20:28": "And Thomas answered and said unto him, My Lord and my God (the most doubtful of the twelve ends up giving Jesus the highest title anyone in this Gospel gives him).",
  "John 20:29": "Jesus saith unto him, Thomas, because thou hast seen me, thou hast believed: blessed are they that have not seen, and yet have believed (he pronounces a blessing that reaches every reader of this book who will never see him in the flesh).",
  "John 20:31": "But these are written, that ye might believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God (John states outright why he wrote any of this down at all); and that believing ye might have life through his name.",
  "John 21:6": "And he said unto them, Cast the net on the right side of the ship, and ye shall find (the same instruction that produced nothing all night produces more fish than the net can hold, the moment he gives it). They cast therefore, and now they were not able to draw it for the multitude of fishes.",
  "John 21:7": "Therefore that disciple whom Jesus loved saith unto Peter, It is the Lord (John recognizes him first, and Peter is in the water before the sentence is finished). Now when Simon Peter heard that it was the Lord, he girt his fisher\u2019s coat unto him, (for he was naked,) and did cast himself into the sea.",
  "John 21:12": "Jesus saith unto them, Come and dine (the resurrected Lord's first recorded act on the shore is feeding breakfast to tired fishermen). And none of the disciples durst ask him, Who art thou? knowing that it was the Lord.",
  "John 21:15": "So when they had dined, Jesus saith to Simon Peter, Simon, son of Jonas, lovest thou me more than these? He saith unto him, Yea, Lord; thou knowest that I love thee. He saith unto him, Feed my lambs (he answers Peter's declaration of love with an assignment, not a reassurance).",
  "John 21:17": "He saith unto him the third time, Simon, son of Jonas, lovest thou me? Peter was grieved because he said unto him the third time, Lovest thou me? And he said unto him, Lord, thou knowest all things (Peter stops trying to prove his love and appeals instead to what Jesus already knows); thou knowest that I love thee. Jesus saith unto him, Feed my sheep.",
  "John 21:19": "This spake he, signifying by what death he should glorify God. And when he had spoken this, he saith unto him, Follow me (the same two words that called Peter at the very beginning are the words that restore him at the very end).",
  "John 21:22": "Jesus saith unto him, If I will that he tarry till I come, what is that to thee? follow thou me (he redirects Peter's curiosity about someone else's future back to Peter's own calling).",
  "Acts 1:3": "To whom also he shewed himself alive after his passion by many infallible proofs, being seen of them forty days (forty days of appearances, not one, leave no room for the resurrection to be mistaken for a rumor), and speaking of the things pertaining to the kingdom of God:",
  "Acts 1:5": "For John truly baptized with water; but ye shall be baptized with the Holy Ghost not many days hence (a promise with a countdown attached, not an open-ended someday).",
  "Acts 1:8": "But ye shall receive power, after that the Holy Ghost is come upon you: and ye shall be witnesses unto me both in Jerusalem, and in all Judea, and in Samaria, and unto the uttermost part of the earth (the mission expands in circles outward, starting from exactly where they are standing).",
  "Acts 1:9": "And when he had spoken these things, while they beheld, he was taken up (his last words are followed immediately by his last visible act, both in front of witnesses); and a cloud received him out of their sight.",
  "Acts 1:11": "Which also said, Ye men of Galilee, why stand ye gazing up into heaven? this same Jesus, which is taken up from you into heaven, shall so come in like manner as ye have seen him go into heaven (the promised return is described as matching the departure, the same body, the same way).",
  "Acts 1:14": "These all continued with one accord in prayer and supplication, with the women, and Mary the mother of Jesus, and with his brethren (the brothers who once doubted him are now praying in the same room).",
  "Acts 2:1": "And when the day of Pentecost was fully come, they were all with one accord in one place (the waiting Jesus commanded in Jerusalem ends on the exact day a Jewish feast already had a name for).",
  "Acts 2:4": "And they were all filled with the Holy Ghost, and began to speak with other tongues, as the Spirit gave them utterance (the words are not theirs to plan, only theirs to speak).",
  "Acts 2:17": "And it shall come to pass in the last days, saith God, I will pour out of my Spirit upon all flesh: and your sons and your daughters shall prophesy, and your young men shall see visions, and your old men shall dream dreams (a promise with no exceptions carved out for age, gender, or status).",
  "Acts 2:21": "And it shall come to pass, that whosoever shall call on the name of the Lord shall be saved (the sermon's first mention of salvation names no qualification but calling).",
  "Acts 2:23": "Him, being delivered by the determinate counsel and foreknowledge of God, ye have taken, and by wicked hands have crucified and slain (Peter holds both truths without softening either one: God planned it, and they are guilty of it).",
  "Acts 2:24": "Whom God hath raised up, having loosed the pains of death: because it was not possible that he should be holden of it (death could not keep what it had no legal claim on).",
  "Acts 2:32": "This Jesus hath God raised up, whereof we all are witnesses (Peter does not argue the resurrection, he simply names the witnesses standing in the crowd).",
  "Acts 2:36": "Therefore let all the house of Israel know assuredly, that God hath made that same Jesus, whom ye have crucified, both Lord and Christ (the very man they executed is given the same sentence as their guilt).",
  "Acts 2:38": "Then Peter said unto them, Repent, and be baptized every one of you in the name of Jesus Christ for the remission of sins, and ye shall receive the gift of the Holy Ghost (baptism follows the repentance that has already changed the heart, it does not produce it).",
  "Acts 2:41": "Then they that gladly received his word were baptized: and the same day there were added unto them about three thousand souls (the church's first day of existence is also its largest single day of growth).",
  "Acts 2:47": "Praising God, and having favour with all the people. And the Lord added to the church daily such as should be saved (the growth here is stated plainly as the Lord's own doing, not the product of a program).",
  "Acts 3:6": "Then Peter said, Silver and gold have I none; but such as I have give I thee: In the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth rise up and walk (he gives away what he actually has instead of apologizing for what he doesn't).",
  "Acts 3:12": "And when Peter saw it, he answered unto the people, Ye men of Israel, why marvel ye at this? or why look ye so earnestly on us, as though by our own power or holiness we had made this man to walk? (he redirects the crowd's amazement before it can land on the wrong two men).",
  "Acts 3:13": "The God of Abraham, and of Isaac, and of Jacob, the God of our fathers, hath glorified his Son Jesus; whom ye delivered up, and denied him in the presence of Pilate, when he was determined to let him go (Peter names the very moment the crowd could have let the story end differently).",
  "Acts 3:15": "And killed the Prince of life, whom God hath raised from the dead; whereof we are witnesses (the title and the accusation sit in the same sentence on purpose).",
  "Acts 3:16": "And his name through faith in his name hath made this man strong, whom ye see and know: yea, the faith which is by him hath given him this perfect soundness in the presence of you all (the healing is not partial, and it happened in front of the very people questioning it).",
  "Acts 3:19": "Repent ye therefore, and be converted, that your sins may be blotted out, when the times of refreshing shall come from the presence of the Lord (the same crowd just accused of killing Christ is offered, in the next breath, forgiveness by him).",
  "Acts 3:23": "And it shall come to pass, that every soul, which will not hear that prophet, shall be destroyed from among the people (the warning is aimed at a crowd that has not yet believed, not at a believer's standing -- the whole sermon is still building toward the same offer of verse nineteen).",
  "Acts 3:26": "Unto you first God, having raised up his Son Jesus, sent him to bless you, in turning away every one of you from his iniquities (the offer goes first to the very city that crucified him).",
  "Acts 4:10": "Be it known unto you all, and to all the people of Israel, that by the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth, whom ye crucified, whom God raised from the dead, even by him doth this man stand here before you whole (Peter answers a question about power by naming the one they killed and God raised).",
  "Acts 4:12": "Neither is there salvation in any other: for there is none other name under heaven given among men, whereby we must be saved (stated to the very council that has the authority to kill him for saying it).",
  "Acts 4:13": "Now when they saw the boldness of Peter and John, and perceived that they were unlearned and ignorant men, they marvelled; and they took knowledge of them, that they had been with Jesus (the council cannot explain the boldness, so they trace it back to whose company these men had kept).",
  "Acts 4:19": "But Peter and John answered and said unto them, Whether it be right in the sight of God to hearken unto you more than unto God, judge ye (they hand the council its own verdict to render).",
  "Acts 4:20": "For we cannot but speak the things which we have seen and heard (not a boast of courage, just a plain statement of what eyewitnesses find themselves unable to stop doing).",
  "Acts 4:31": "And when they had prayed, the place was shaken where they were assembled together; and they were all filled with the Holy Ghost, and they spake the word of God with boldness (they ask for boldness, and the room itself answers first).",
  "Acts 4:33": "And with great power gave the apostles witness of the resurrection of the Lord Jesus: and great grace was upon them all (the resurrection is what they preach, and grace is what marks them while they preach it).",
  "Acts 5:3": "But Peter said, Ananias, why hath Satan filled thine heart to lie to the Holy Ghost, and to keep back part of the price of the land? (the lie was aimed at impressing the church, but Peter names who it actually reached).",
  "Acts 5:4": "Whiles it remained, was it not thine own? and after it was sold, was it not in thine own power? why hast thou conceived this thing in thine heart? thou hast not lied unto men, but unto God (no one had demanded the money; the sin was never the giving, it was the pretending).",
  "Acts 5:5": "And Ananias hearing these words fell down, and gave up the ghost: and great fear came on all them that heard these things (the church's first recorded internal sin meets the swiftest recorded judgment).",
  "Acts 5:15": "Insomuch that they brought forth the sick into the streets, and laid them on beds and couches, that at the least the shadow of Peter passing by might overshadow some of them (their faith reaches for a shadow because they cannot always reach the man).",
  "Acts 5:19": "But the angel of the Lord by night opened the prison doors, and brought them forth, and said (the prison that held them overnight cannot hold them past it).",
  "Acts 5:29": "Then Peter and the other apostles answered and said, We ought to obey God rather than men (given to the very council that had already ordered them jailed once).",
  "Acts 5:31": "Him hath God exalted with his right hand to be a Prince and a Saviour, for to give repentance to Israel, and forgiveness of sins (both repentance and forgiveness are described here as things he gives, not things Israel produces on its own).",
  "Acts 5:41": "And they departed from the presence of the council, rejoicing that they were counted worthy to suffer shame for his name (a beating meant to silence them becomes something they leave rejoicing over).",
  "Acts 6:3": "Wherefore, brethren, look ye out among you seven men of honest report, full of the Holy Ghost and wisdom, whom we may appoint over this business (the qualification for waiting tables is set at the same height as the qualification for preaching).",
  "Acts 6:5": "And the saying pleased the whole multitude: and they chose Stephen, a man full of faith and of the Holy Ghost, and Philip, and Prochorus, and Nicanor, and Timon, and Parmenas, and Nicolas a proselyte of Antioch (the very first deacon named on this list is a man about to die for his faith without ever holding the title of apostle).",
  "Acts 6:7": "And the word of God increased; and the number of the disciples multiplied in Jerusalem greatly; and a great company of the priests were obedient to the faith (the growth is credited to the word, and it reaches even into the ranks of the men who run the temple).",
  "Acts 6:8": "And Stephen, full of faith and power, did great wonders and miracles among the people (a man appointed to serve tables is doing the same works Peter and John were doing).",
  "Acts 6:10": "And they were not able to resist the wisdom and the spirit by which he spake (his opponents cannot win the argument, so the next chapter shows them changing tactics).",
  "Acts 6:15": "And all that sat in the council, looking stedfastly on him, saw his face as it had been the face of an angel (the men about to condemn him are the ones who see it first).",
  "Acts 7:9": "And the patriarchs, moved with envy, sold Joseph into Egypt: but God was with him (the brothers' worst act becomes the very road God uses to save them later).",
  "Acts 7:22": "And Moses was learned in all the wisdom of the Egyptians, and was mighty in words and in deeds (forty years of Egyptian training are not wasted, they are simply not yet the point).",
  "Acts 7:25": "For he supposed his brethren would have understood how that God by his hand would deliver them: but they understood not (Moses moves forty years too early, on his own initiative instead of God's).",
  "Acts 7:35": "This Moses whom they refused, saying, Who made thee a ruler and a judge? the same did God send to be a ruler and a deliverer by the hand of the angel which appeared to him in the bush (Stephen draws a straight line from the Moses they once rejected to the Christ they have just rejected).",
  "Acts 7:37": "This is that Moses, which said unto the children of Israel, A prophet shall the Lord your God raise up unto you of your brethren, like unto me; him shall ye hear (Stephen quotes Moses naming his own greater successor to a council that has just refused to hear him).",
  "Acts 7:51": "Ye stiffnecked and uncircumcised in heart and ears, ye do always resist the Holy Ghost: as your fathers did, so do ye (a defendant on trial for his life turns and puts his judges on trial instead).",
  "Acts 7:55": "But he, being full of the Holy Ghost, looked up stedfastly into heaven, and saw the glory of God, and Jesus standing on the right hand of God (everywhere else in the New Testament Jesus is seated; here, at the death of the first martyr, he is on his feet).",
  "Acts 7:58": "And cast him out of the city, and stoned him: and the witnesses laid down their clothes at a young man's feet, whose name was Saul (Luke names the coat-holder because the rest of this book belongs to him).",
  "Acts 7:59": "And they stoned Stephen, calling upon God, and saying, Lord Jesus, receive my spirit (his last request is prayed straight to Jesus, the same prayer Jesus once prayed to the Father).",
  "Acts 7:60": "And he kneeled down, and cried with a loud voice, Lord, lay not this sin to their charge. And when he had said this, he fell asleep (he dies asking forgiveness for the men killing him, in words that echo the cross).",
  "Acts 8:1": "And Saul was consenting unto his death. And at that time there was a great persecution against the church which was at Jerusalem; and they were all scattered abroad throughout the regions of Judea and Samaria, except the apostles (the persecution meant to stamp the church out becomes the very thing that spreads it beyond Jerusalem).",
  "Acts 8:4": "Therefore they that were scattered abroad went every where preaching the word (refugees, not missionaries, end up carrying the message).",
  "Acts 8:13": "Then Simon himself believed also: and when he was baptized, he continued with Philip, and wondered, beholding the miracles and signs which were done (his faith is genuine enough to be baptized, and shallow enough to still be dazzled by the show).",
  "Acts 8:20": "But Peter said unto him, Thy money perish with thee, because thou hast thought that the gift of God may be purchased with money (Peter's answer is as sharp as the offer was wrong).",
  "Acts 8:22": "Repent therefore of this thy wickedness, and pray God, if perhaps the thought of thine heart may be forgiven thee (even this sin, named this bluntly, is met with a door still open).",
  "Acts 8:27": "And he arose and went: and, behold, a man of Ethiopia, an eunuch of great authority under Candace queen of the Ethiopians, who had the charge of all her treasure, and had come to Jerusalem for to worship (he traveled hundreds of miles to worship, and God sends one man to meet him on the road home),",
  "Acts 8:31": "And he said, How can I, except some man should guide me? And he desired Philip that he would come up and sit with him (he admits what he doesn't understand instead of pretending).",
  "Acts 8:35": "Then Philip opened his mouth, and began at the same scripture, and preached unto him Jesus (Philip starts from the exact verse the man was already reading, not somewhere else).",
  "Acts 8:36": "And as they went on their way, they came unto a certain water: and the eunuch said, See, here is water; what doth hinder me to be baptized? (a question asked by a man who already seems to know the answer is nothing).",
  "Acts 8:39": "And when they were come up out of the water, the Spirit of the Lord caught away Philip, that the eunuch saw him no more: and he went on his way rejoicing (the missionary vanishes, but the joy stays).",
  "Acts 9:4": "And he fell to the earth, and heard a voice saying unto him, Saul, Saul, why persecutest thou me? (Jesus does not ask why Saul persecutes the church, he asks why Saul persecutes him).",
  "Acts 9:5": "And he said, Who art thou, Lord? And the Lord said, I am Jesus whom thou persecutest: it is hard for thee to kick against the pricks (the name Saul hated most is the one answer he gets).",
  "Acts 9:6": "And he trembling and astonished said, Lord, what wilt thou have me to do? (the man who came to arrest believers is, in this very sentence, converted).",
  "Acts 9:15": "But the Lord said unto him, Go thy way: for he is a chosen vessel unto me, to bear my name before the Gentiles, and kings, and the children of Israel (Ananias objects on the strength of the man's record; the Lord answers with the man's future instead).",
  "Acts 9:17": "And Ananias went his way, and entered into the house; and putting his hands on him said, Brother Saul (the very first word Ananias speaks to the church's chief persecutor is a word for family).",
  "Acts 9:20": "And straightway he preached Christ in the synagogues, that he is the Son of God (the same synagogues holding letters authorizing his arrests now hear him preach the opposite).",
  "Acts 9:22": "But Saul increased the more in strength, and confounded the Jews which dwelt at Damascus, proving that this is very Christ (his old training in the scriptures, once aimed against Christ, is now aimed for him).",
  "Acts 9:34": "And Peter said unto him, Aeneas, Jesus Christ maketh thee whole: arise, and make thy bed. And he arose immediately (Peter names whose power did this before the man is even off the mat).",
  "Acts 9:40": "But Peter put them all forth, and kneeled down, and prayed; and turning him to the body said, Tabitha, arise. And she opened her eyes: and when she saw Peter, she sat up (the same two words Jesus once spoke over a dead girl, Peter now speaks over a dead woman).",
  "Acts 10:4": "And when he looked on him, he was afraid, and said, What is it, Lord? And he said unto him, Thy prayers and thine alms are come up for a memorial before God (a Gentile's prayers reach heaven before he has ever heard the gospel preached to him).",
  "Acts 10:15": "And the voice spake unto him again the second time, What God hath cleansed, that call not thou common (the vision is framed as being about food, but Peter will spend the rest of this chapter learning it was never really about food).",
  "Acts 10:28": "And he said unto them, Ye know how that it is an unlawful thing for a man that is a Jew to keep company, or come unto one of another nation; but God hath shewed me that I should not call any man common or unclean (Peter states his old rule and its new exception in the same sentence).",
  "Acts 10:34": "Then Peter opened his mouth, and said, Of a truth I perceive that God is no respecter of persons (a lifetime of assumptions is undone by one vision and one visit):",
  "Acts 10:38": "How God anointed Jesus of Nazareth with the Holy Ghost and with power: who went about doing good, and healing all that were oppressed of the devil; for God was with him (Peter summarizes three years of ministry in a single sentence: he went about doing good).",
  "Acts 10:40": "Him God raised up the third day, and shewed him openly (Peter's sermon, like every apostolic sermon in this book, builds to the same place).",
  "Acts 10:43": "To him give all the prophets witness, that through his name whosoever believeth in him shall receive remission of sins (the offer, spoken here for the first time to Gentiles, has exactly the same terms it had for Jews).",
  "Acts 10:44": "While Peter yet spake these words, the Holy Ghost fell on all them which heard the word (the Spirit falls before Peter even finishes the sermon, and before anyone has been baptized in water).",
  "Acts 10:47": "Can any man forbid water, that these should not be baptized, which have received the Holy Ghost as well as we? (Peter asks it as though the answer were already obvious, because by then it was).",
  "Acts 11:14": "Who shall tell thee words, whereby thou and all thy house shall be saved (Cornelius already prays and already gives alms, and still needs someone to come tell him words).",
  "Acts 11:17": "Forasmuch then as God gave them the like gift as he did unto us, who believed on the Lord Jesus Christ; what was I, that I could withstand God? (Peter's whole defense comes down to one question: who was he to argue with what God had already done).",
  "Acts 11:18": "When they heard these things, they held their peace, and glorified God, saying, Then hath God also to the Gentiles granted repentance unto life (the objection dissolves the moment the evidence is heard in full).",
  "Acts 11:21": "And the hand of the Lord was with them: and a great number believed, and turned unto the Lord (the first preaching to Gentiles at Antioch happens without any apostle present to authorize it).",
  "Acts 11:23": "Who, when he came, and had seen the grace of God, was glad, and exhorted them all, that with purpose of heart they would cleave unto the Lord (Barnabas does not test their theology first, he simply sees grace and is glad).",
  "Acts 11:26": "And when he had found him, he brought him unto Antioch. And it came to pass, that a whole year they assembled themselves with the church, and taught much people. And the disciples were called Christians first in Antioch (a name given by outsiders as a label sticks, and the church keeps it).",
  "Acts 12:5": "Peter therefore was kept in prison: but prayer was made without ceasing of the church unto God for him (the church has no army and no political leverage, only this).",
  "Acts 12:7": "And, behold, the angel of the Lord came upon him, and a light shined in the prison: and he smote Peter on the side, and raised him up, saying, Arise up quickly. And his chains fell off from his hands (the chains open before the doors do).",
  "Acts 12:9": "And he went out, and followed him; and wist not that it was true which was done by the angel; but thought he saw a vision (he is being rescued and still doesn't believe it).",
  "Acts 12:11": "And when Peter was come to himself, he said, Now I know of a surety, that the Lord hath sent his angel (the realization comes only after he is already free, not before).",
  "Acts 12:15": "And they said unto her, Thou art mad. But she constantly affirmed that it was even so. Then said they, It is his angel (the whole prayer meeting is more willing to believe in a ghost than in the answer to its own prayer).",
  "Acts 12:23": "And immediately the angel of the Lord smote him, because he gave not God the glory: and he was eaten of worms, and gave up the ghost (the king who let a crowd call him a god does not outlive the acclaim by long).",
  "Acts 12:24": "But the word of God grew and multiplied (a king dies in the very same chapter that ends with the word of God simply continuing to spread).",
  "Acts 13:2": "As they ministered to the Lord, and fasted, the Holy Ghost said, Separate me Barnabas and Saul for the work whereunto I have called them (the first missionary call in this book interrupts a prayer meeting, not a planning meeting).",
  "Acts 13:9": "Then Saul, (who also is called Paul,) filled with the Holy Ghost, set his eyes on him (the text quietly changes his name in the very sentence where it shows him stepping into his calling).",
  "Acts 13:11": "And now, behold, the hand of the Lord is upon thee, and thou shalt be blind, not seeing the sun for a season (the same kind of light that once knocked Paul to the ground is now the judgment Paul pronounces on someone else).",
  "Acts 13:26": "Men and brethren, children of the stock of Abraham, and whosoever among you feareth God, to you is the word of this salvation sent (Paul includes the Gentile God-fearers in his very first sentence, without hedging).",
  "Acts 13:30": "But God raised him from the dead (Paul's entire sermon turns on this one short verse).",
  "Acts 13:39": "And by him all that believe are justified from all things, from which ye could not be justified by the law of Moses (Paul names exactly what the law never could do, in the same city where the law was still read every Sabbath).",
  "Acts 13:48": "And when the Gentiles heard this, they were glad, and glorified the word of the Lord: and as many as were ordained to eternal life believed (the joy comes first, from people who never expected to be included at all).",
  "Acts 14:3": "Long time therefore abode they speaking boldly in the Lord, which gave testimony unto the word of his grace, and granted signs and wonders to be done by their hands (the message is grace, and the signs back it up rather than replace it).",
  "Acts 14:10": "Said with a loud voice, Stand upright on thy feet. And he leaped and walked (the healing is immediate and complete, with nothing gradual in between).",
  "Acts 14:15": "And saying, Sirs, why do ye these things? We also are men of like passions with you (offered worship as a god, Paul's first move is to insist on being ordinary).",
  "Acts 14:20": "Howbeit, as the disciples stood round about him, he rose up, and came into the city: and the next day he departed with Barnabas to Derbe (a crowd that tried to worship him days earlier now leaves him for dead, and he simply gets up).",
  "Acts 14:22": "Confirming the souls of the disciples, and exhorting them to continue in the faith, and that we must through much tribulation enter into the kingdom of God (Paul goes back to the very cities that stoned him, to strengthen the people still living there).",
  "Acts 15:1": "And certain men which came down from Judea taught the brethren, and said, Except ye be circumcised after the manner of Moses, ye cannot be saved (the first heresy the church has to formally answer is an addition to the cross, not a denial of it).",
  "Acts 15:9": "And put no difference between us and them, purifying their hearts by faith (Peter states there are not two classes of believer, only one kind of purifying, and it is by faith).",
  "Acts 15:11": "But we believe that through the grace of the Lord Jesus Christ we shall be saved, even as they (the apostle to the Jews states it is grace, not law, that saves Jew and Gentile alike, in the same sentence).",
  "Acts 15:19": "Wherefore my sentence is, that we trouble not them, which from among the Gentiles are turned to God (James's ruling protects new believers from a burden nobody in the room had managed to carry either).",
  "Acts 15:28": "For it seemed good to the Holy Ghost, and to us, to lay upon you no greater burden than these necessary things (the council's letter credits the Holy Spirit before it credits itself).",
  "Acts 16:14": "And a certain woman named Lydia, a seller of purple, of the city of Thyatira, which worshipped God, heard us: whose heart the Lord opened, that she attended unto the things which were spoken of Paul (her conversion is described as something God does to her heart before it is described as anything she decides).",
  "Acts 16:25": "And at midnight Paul and Silas prayed, and sang praises unto God: and the prisoners heard them (they are singing before the earthquake, not after it).",
  "Acts 16:26": "And suddenly there was a great earthquake, so that the foundations of the prison were shaken: and immediately all the doors were opened, and every one's bands were loosed (a prison built to hold two singing men cannot survive the song).",
  "Acts 16:28": "But Paul cried with a loud voice, saying, Do thyself no harm: for we are all here (a free man's first words are spent saving the life of the man who locked him up).",
  "Acts 16:31": "And they said, Believe on the Lord Jesus Christ, and thou shalt be saved, and thy house (the jailer asks the same question three thousand people asked at Pentecost, and gets an answer just as short).",
  "Acts 16:33": "And he took them the same hour of the night, and washed their stripes; and was baptized, he and all his, straightway (before he tends to his own house, he tends first to the wounds he caused).",
  "Acts 17:3": "Opening and alleging, that Christ must needs have suffered, and risen again from the dead; and that this Jesus, whom I preach unto you, is Christ (Paul builds his whole case for the Messiah out of the very death and resurrection the crowd would expect to disqualify one).",
  "Acts 17:11": "These were more noble than those in Thessalonica, in that they received the word with all readiness of mind, and searched the scriptures daily, whether those things were so (their nobility is measured not by rank, but by how carefully they checked what they were told).",
  "Acts 17:24": "God that made the world and all things therein, seeing that he is Lord of heaven and earth, dwelleth not in temples made with hands (Paul stands in a city full of temples and opens by saying God does not live in any of them).",
  "Acts 17:26": "And hath made of one blood all nations of men for to dwell on all the face of the earth (spoken to a crowd that prided itself on being the one civilized race among barbarians).",
  "Acts 17:28": "For in him we live, and move, and have our being; as certain also of your own poets have said, For we are also his offspring (Paul quotes their own writers back to them instead of only quoting a scripture they had never read).",
  "Acts 17:31": "Because he hath appointed a day, in the which he will judge the world in righteousness by that man whom he hath ordained; whereof he hath given assurance unto all men, in that he hath raised him from the dead (the proof offered for a coming judgment is not a threat, it is an empty tomb).",
  "Acts 17:32": "And when they heard of the resurrection of the dead, some mocked: and others said, We will hear thee again of this matter (the same sermon produces mockery in one hearer and a second invitation in another).",
  "Acts 18:6": "he shook his raiment, and said unto them, Your blood be upon your own heads; I am clean: from henceforth I will go unto the Gentiles (he does not stop preaching, he simply stops waiting on this particular audience).",
  "Acts 18:10": "For I am with thee, and no man shall set on thee to hurt thee: for I have much people in this city (the Lord names a harvest in Corinth before Paul has seen any of it).",
  "Acts 18:26": "And he began to speak boldly in the synagogue: whom when Aquila and Priscilla had heard, they took him unto them, and expounded unto him the way of God more perfectly (a tentmaker and his wife quietly correct one of the most eloquent preachers in Ephesus, and he lets them).",
  "Acts 19:2": "He said unto them, Have ye received the Holy Ghost since ye believed? And they said unto him, We have not so much as heard whether there be any Holy Ghost (Paul can tell within a few sentences that something is missing).",
  "Acts 19:6": "And when Paul had laid his hands upon them, the Holy Ghost came on them; and they spake with tongues, and prophesied (the same evidence that marked Pentecost marks this small group in Ephesus as well).",
  "Acts 19:12": "So that from his body were brought unto the sick handkerchiefs or aprons, and the diseases departed from them, and the evil spirits went out of them (it is not the cloth doing the healing, it is what the cloth had touched).",
  "Acts 19:15": "And the evil spirit answered and said, Jesus I know, and Paul I know; but who are ye? (borrowing a name they had only watched someone else use does not work the same way for them).",
  "Acts 19:20": "So mightily grew the word of God and prevailed (the riot in this chapter is still two chapters away; right now, the word is simply winning).",
  "Acts 20:10": "And Paul went down, and fell on him, and embracing him said, Trouble not yourselves; for his life is in him (a boy who fell asleep during a long sermon and fell to his death is alive again before the sermon resumes).",
  "Acts 20:24": "But none of these things move me, neither count I my life dear unto myself, so that I might finish my course with joy (he is not indifferent to danger, he has simply already decided what matters more).",
  "Acts 20:28": "Take heed therefore unto yourselves, and to all the flock, over the which the Holy Ghost hath made you overseers, to feed the church of God, which he hath purchased with his own blood (the price paid for the church sets the standard for how carefully it should be shepherded).",
  "Acts 20:35": "I have shewed you all things, how that so labouring ye ought to support the weak, and to remember the words of the Lord Jesus, how he said, It is more blessed to give than to receive (Paul closes his farewell with a saying of Jesus that appears nowhere in the four Gospels).",
  "Acts 21:13": "Then Paul answered, What mean ye to weep and to break mine heart? for I am ready not to be bound only, but also to die at Jerusalem for the name of the Lord Jesus (their tears are not what will change his mind).",
  "Acts 21:14": "And when he would not be persuaded, we ceased, saying, The will of the Lord be done (even the friends trying to stop him end up surrendering to the same will he is obeying).",
  "Acts 21:39": "But Paul said, I am a man which am a Jew of Tarsus, a city in Cilicia, a citizen of no mean city (moments after being beaten by a mob, he answers a soldier's question with calm, exact facts).",
  "Acts 22:8": "And I answered, Who art thou, Lord? And he said unto me, I am Jesus of Nazareth, whom thou persecutest (twenty-five years later, telling the story to a hostile crowd, Paul still uses the exact name Jesus used to identify himself).",
  "Acts 22:16": "And now why tarriest thou? arise, and be baptized, and wash away thy sins, calling on the name of the Lord (the washing is described as already accomplished by the calling, with baptism simply putting it into action).",
  "Acts 22:21": "And he said unto me, Depart: for I will send thee far hence unto the Gentiles (the same crowd that once sent him after Christians is the reason he is now sent toward everyone else).",
  "Acts 23:1": "And Paul, earnestly beholding the council, said, Men and brethren, I have lived in all good conscience before God until this day (he opens his defense before the very court that once used him to persecute the church).",
  "Acts 23:6": "he cried out in the council, Men and brethren, I am a Pharisee, the son of a Pharisee: of the hope and resurrection of the dead I am called in question (Paul reframes the entire trial around the one doctrine his accusers cannot agree on among themselves).",
  "Acts 23:11": "And the night following the Lord stood by him, and said, Be of good cheer, Paul: for as thou hast testified of me in Jerusalem, so must thou bear witness also at Rome (the same night a plot to kill him is being sworn, Jesus tells him exactly where this is actually headed).",
  "Acts 24:16": "And herein do I exercise myself, to have always a conscience void of offence toward God, and toward men (on trial for his life, Paul states his daily discipline as plainly as any other habit).",
  "Acts 24:25": "And as he reasoned of righteousness, temperance, and judgment to come, Felix trembled, and answered, Go thy way for this time (the man with the power to release Paul is the one left shaking, and he sends the preacher away instead of dealing with what he heard).",
  "Acts 25:11": "For if I be an offender, or have committed any thing worthy of death, I refuse not to die: but if there be none of these things whereof these accuse me, no man may deliver me unto them. I appeal unto Cesar (he is not trying to escape justice, he is refusing an unjust one).",
  "Acts 25:19": "But had certain questions against him of their own superstition, and of one Jesus, which was dead, whom Paul affirmed to be alive (a Roman governor summarizes two years of legal proceedings in a single sentence, and lands on the one fact that actually matters).",
  "Acts 26:8": "Why should it be thought a thing incredible with you, that God should raise the dead? (Paul turns his own defense into a question the whole room has to answer for itself).",
  "Acts 26:15": "And I said, Who art thou, Lord? And he said, I am Jesus whom thou persecutest (Paul repeats this exchange in front of a king the same way he told it to a Damascus congregation decades earlier, unchanged).",
  "Acts 26:18": "To open their eyes, and to turn them from darkness to light, and from the power of Satan unto God, that they may receive forgiveness of sins, and inheritance among them which are sanctified by faith that is in me (this is Paul's own commission, quoted word for word to Agrippa, not a summary of it).",
  "Acts 26:23": "That Christ should suffer, and that he should be the first that should rise from the dead, and should shew light unto the people, and to the Gentiles (Paul tells a Jewish king that the gospel was never meant for Jews exclusively, even from its first prediction).",
  "Acts 26:28": "Then Agrippa said unto Paul, Almost thou persuadest me to be a Christian (a king in chains of his own making answers a prisoner's sermon with the word almost).",
  "Acts 26:29": "And Paul said, I would to God, that not only thou, but also all that hear me this day, were both almost, and altogether such as I am, except these bonds (the prisoner is the only truly free man in the room, and he says so).",
  "Acts 27:22": "And now I exhort you to be of good cheer: for there shall be no loss of any man's life among you, but of the ship (in the middle of a storm that has taken away all hope, Paul is the one calm voice on board).",
  "Acts 27:25": "Wherefore, sirs, be of good cheer: for I believe God, that it shall be even as it was told me (his confidence rests on a promise heard the night before, not on anything visible outside the ship).",
  "Acts 27:31": "Paul said to the centurion and to the soldiers, Except these abide in the ship, ye cannot be saved (the promise that no one would die does not cancel out the need to still do what keeps them alive).",
  "Acts 27:35": "And when he had thus spoken, he took bread, and gave thanks to God in presence of them all: and when he had broken it, he began to eat (a prisoner leads two hundred and seventy-five frightened strangers in the one thing that steadies him, giving thanks).",
  "Acts 28:5": "And he shook off the beast into the fire, and felt no harm (a shipwreck and a snakebite in the same day fail to do what two years of trials in Jerusalem and Caesarea could not do either).",
  "Acts 28:15": "whom when Paul saw, he thanked God, and took courage (the sight of other believers, more than anything Rome itself has to offer, is what lifts him).",
  "Acts 28:28": "Be it known therefore unto you, that the salvation of God is sent unto the Gentiles, and that they will hear it (the book that opened with the gospel confined to Jerusalem closes with Paul stating it now belongs to everyone who will listen).",
  "Acts 28:31": "Preaching the kingdom of God, and teaching those things which concern the Lord Jesus Christ, with all confidence, no man forbidding him (the book ends not with Paul's death, but with an open door and a message no chain ever managed to stop).",
  "Romans 1:1": "Paul, a servant of Jesus Christ, called to be an apostle, separated unto the gospel of God (he names himself a slave before he names himself an apostle),",
  "Romans 1:4": "And declared to be the Son of God with power, according to the spirit of holiness, by the resurrection from the dead (the claim rests on an empty tomb, not an argument):",
  "Romans 1:16": "For I am not ashamed of the gospel of Christ (a flat denial, stated once and left alone): for it is the power of God unto salvation to every one that believeth; to the Jew first, and also to the Greek.",
  "Romans 1:17": "For therein is the righteousness of God revealed from faith to faith: as it is written, The just shall live by faith (the whole letter is compressed into this one line before fifteen chapters unpack it).",
  "Romans 1:18": "For the wrath of God is revealed from heaven against all ungodliness and unrighteousness of men, who hold the truth in unrighteousness (the letter names the problem two verses after naming the cure);",
  "Romans 1:20": "For the invisible things of him from the creation of the world are clearly seen, being understood by the things that are made, even his eternal power and Godhead; so that they are without excuse (creation is called as a witness before a single prophet has spoken):",
  "Romans 1:21": "Because that, when they knew God, they glorified him not as God, neither were thankful (the collapse begins with a refusal to say thank you, not with an act); but became vain in their imaginations, and their foolish heart was darkened.",
  "Romans 1:25": "Who changed the truth of God into a lie, and worshipped and served the creature more than the Creator, who is blessed for ever. Amen (worship never disappears, it only gets redirected toward something smaller than the one it was made for).",
  "Romans 2:1": "Therefore thou art inexcusable, O man, whosoever thou art that judgest: for wherein thou judgest another, thou condemnest thyself (the judge is found guilty of the exact charge on his own docket); for thou that judgest doest the same things.",
  "Romans 2:4": "Or despisest thou the riches of his goodness and forbearance and longsuffering; not knowing that the goodness of God leadeth thee to repentance (patience is named here as a kindness doing work, not an absence of concern)?",
  "Romans 2:7": "To them who by patient continuance in well doing seek for glory and honour and immortality, eternal life (Paul states what law-keeping would require if it could actually deliver Heaven -- an argument he spends the next two chapters proving no one can meet).",
  "Romans 2:8": "But unto them that are contentious, and do not obey the truth, but obey unrighteousness, indignation and wrath (the standard cuts both ways -- the same logic that could reward perfect obedience condemns its absence),",
  "Romans 2:9": "Tribulation and anguish, upon every soul of man that doeth evil, of the Jew first, and also of the Gentile (the sentence names the Jew before the Gentile -- covenant privilege raises accountability here, it doesn't lower it);",
  "Romans 2:10": "But glory, honour, and peace, to every man that worketh good, to the Jew first, and also to the Gentile (the same ordering repeats for reward as for judgment -- Paul is stating a principle that runs both directions, not playing favorites):",
  "Romans 2:11": "For there is no respect of persons with God (stated once, flatly, and left there).",
  "Romans 2:15": "Which shew the work of the law written in their hearts, their conscience also bearing witness (moral knowledge is described as present before any Bible ever reaches a person), and their thoughts the mean while accusing or else excusing one another;)",
  "Romans 2:21": "Thou therefore which teachest another, teachest thou not thyself? thou that preachest a man should not steal, dost thou steal (the sermon is turned back on the preacher)?",
  "Romans 2:23": "Thou that makest thy boast of the law, through breaking the law dishonourest thou God (the very thing being boasted in is the thing being broken)?",
  "Romans 2:25": "For circumcision verily profiteth, if thou keep the law: but if thou be a breaker of the law, thy circumcision is made uncircumcision (the ritual is only ever as good as what stands behind it).",
  "Romans 2:29": "But he is a Jew, which is one inwardly; and circumcision is that of the heart, in the spirit, and not in the letter; whose praise is not of men, but of God (a definition of the true Jew that has nothing to do with genealogy).",
  "Romans 3:9": "What then? are we better than they? No, in no wise: for we have before proved both Jews and Gentiles, that they are all under sin (every escape route is closed before the door out is offered);",
  "Romans 3:10": "As it is written, There is none righteous, no, not one (an indictment that allows no exceptions):",
  "Romans 3:20": "Therefore by the deeds of the law there shall no flesh be justified in his sight: for by the law is the knowledge of sin (the law gets credit for diagnosis, never for cure).",
  "Romans 3:21": "But now the righteousness of God without the law is manifested, being witnessed by the law and the prophets (something new arrives that the old system had been pointing toward all along);",
  "Romans 3:22": "Even the righteousness of God which is by faith of Jesus Christ unto all and upon all them that believe: for there is no difference (one line erases every distinction the chapter had just spent nine verses drawing):",
  "Romans 3:23": "For all have sinned, and come short of the glory of God (the most quoted verse in the chapter is also its shortest sentence);",
  "Romans 3:24": "Being justified freely by his grace through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus (freely is the one word that changes everything before it):",
  "Romans 3:25": "Whom God hath set forth to be a propitiation through faith in his blood, to declare his righteousness for the remission of sins that are past, through the forbearance of God (a debt from centuries earlier is settled retroactively at one point in history);",
  "Romans 3:27": "Where is boasting then? It is excluded (not lowered, removed entirely). By what law? of works? Nay: but by the law of faith.",
  "Romans 3:28": "Therefore we conclude that a man is justified by faith without the deeds of the law (the conclusion three chapters of argument have been building toward, stated in one line).",
  "Romans 4:3": "For what saith the scripture? Abraham believed God, and it was counted unto him for righteousness (a transaction recorded centuries before the law existed to complicate it).",
  "Romans 4:4": "Now to him that worketh is the reward not reckoned of grace, but of debt (wages and gifts are named as two things that cannot be the same transaction).",
  "Romans 4:5": "But to him that worketh not, but believeth on him that justifieth the ungodly, his faith is counted for righteousness (the object of justification is named plainly as the ungodly, not the almost-good).",
  "Romans 4:11": "And he received the sign of circumcision, a seal of the righteousness of the faith which he had yet being uncircumcised (the sign arrives after the fact it symbolizes, not before): that he might be the father of all them that believe, though they be not circumcised; that righteousness might be imputed unto them also:",
  "Romans 4:13": "For the promise, that he should be the heir of the world, was not to Abraham, or to his seed, through the law, but through the righteousness of faith (the inheritance was never running through a system that arrived four hundred years later).",
  "Romans 4:15": "Because the law worketh wrath: for where no law is, there is no transgression (a law can expose a debt it has no power to forgive).",
  "Romans 4:16": "Therefore it is of faith, that it might be by grace (the two words are chained together so tightly that one cannot function without the other); to the end the promise might be sure to all the seed; not to that only which is of the law, but to that also which is of the faith of Abraham; who is the father of us all,",
  "Romans 4:20": "He staggered not at the promise of God through unbelief; but was strong in faith, giving glory to God (an old man's confidence is credited entirely to what he believed, not to what he could still do);",
  "Romans 4:25": "Who was delivered for our offences, and was raised again for our justification (one death paid a debt, one resurrection confirmed the receipt).",
  "Romans 5:1": "Therefore being justified by faith, we have peace with God through our Lord Jesus Christ (the fighting is declared over as an accomplished fact, not a hoped-for outcome):",
  "Romans 5:6": "For when we were yet without strength, in due time Christ died for the ungodly (he did not wait for anyone to become strong enough to deserve the timing).",
  "Romans 5:8": "But God commendeth his love toward us, in that, while we were yet sinners, Christ died for us (the proof of the love is timed to the worst possible moment to offer it).",
  "Romans 5:9": "Much more then, being now justified by his blood, we shall be saved from wrath through him (an argument from the harder thing already finished to the easier thing still to come).",
  "Romans 5:10": "For if, when we were enemies, we were reconciled to God by the death of his Son, much more, being reconciled, we shall be saved by his life (reconciliation is described as the harder half of the transaction, already behind us).",
  "Romans 5:12": "Wherefore, as by one man sin entered into the world, and death by sin; and so death passed upon all men, for that all have sinned (one man's act is described with a reach spanning every person born after him):",
  "Romans 5:15": "But not as the offence, so also is the free gift (the two are described as unequal in scale, not equal and opposite). For if through the offence of one many be dead, much more the grace of God, and the gift by grace, which is by one man, Jesus Christ, hath abounded unto many.",
  "Romans 5:20": "Moreover the law entered, that the offence might abound. But where sin abounded, grace did much more abound (whatever measure sin reaches, grace is described reaching further):",
  "Romans 5:21": "That as sin hath reigned unto death, even so might grace reign through righteousness unto eternal life by Jesus Christ our Lord (the chapter ends by naming the same throne, occupied now by a different ruler).",
  "Romans 6:2": "God forbid. How shall we, that are dead to sin, live any longer therein (grace is defended not by lowering the bar but by naming what has already died)?",
  "Romans 6:4": "Therefore we are buried with him by baptism into death: that like as Christ was raised up from the dead by the glory of the Father, even so we also should walk in newness of life (the old life is described as buried, not merely improved).",
  "Romans 6:6": "Knowing this, that our old man is crucified with him, that the body of sin might be destroyed, that henceforth we should not serve sin (the word used is crucified, the same word used for what happened to him).",
  "Romans 6:9": "Knowing that Christ being raised from the dead dieth no more; death hath no more dominion over him (a completed work needs nothing repeated).",
  "Romans 6:14": "For sin shall not have dominion over you: for ye are not under the law, but under grace (dominion is broken by a change of location, not a change of effort).",
  "Romans 6:17": "But God be thanked, that ye were the servants of sin, but ye have obeyed from the heart that form of doctrine which was delivered you (thanks is given for a past-tense condition, already left behind).",
  "Romans 6:22": "But now being made free from sin, and become servants to God, ye have your fruit unto holiness, and the end everlasting life (freedom results in a new servitude, not in no servitude at all).",
  "Romans 6:23": "For the wages of sin is death; but the gift of God is eternal life through Jesus Christ our Lord (one side of the sentence is earned, the other side is given, and the verse insists on keeping them separate).",
  "Romans 7:4": "Wherefore, my brethren, ye also are become dead to the law by the body of Christ; that ye should be married to another, even to him who is raised from the dead, that we should bring forth fruit unto God (the analogy shifts the entire question from behavior to marriage).",
  "Romans 7:6": "But now we are delivered from the law, that being dead wherein we were held; that we should serve in newness of spirit, and not in the oldness of the letter (deliverance is described in the past tense, as something already accomplished).",
  "Romans 7:7": "What shall we say then? Is the law sin? God forbid (the law is defended as a mirror, never blamed as the disease). Nay, I had not known sin, but by the law: for I had not known lust, except the law had said, Thou shalt not covet.",
  "Romans 7:12": "Wherefore the law is holy, and the commandment holy, and just, and good (the case against relying on the law is never made by accusing the law itself).",
  "Romans 7:18": "For I know that in me (that is, in my flesh,) dwelleth no good thing: for to will is present with me (willpower is named as present, only insufficient, not absent); but how to perform that which is good I find not.",
  "Romans 7:19": "For the good that I would I do not: but the evil which I would not, that I do (an honest description of the gap between intention and action, offered without excuse).",
  "Romans 7:24": "O wretched man that I am! who shall deliver me from the body of this death (the question itself is the turning point, the moment he stops trying to fix it himself)?",
  "Romans 7:25": "I thank God through Jesus Christ our Lord (the answer arrives in five words, immediately after the cry). So then with the mind I myself serve the law of God; but with the flesh the law of sin.",
  "Romans 8:1": "There is therefore now no condemnation to them which are in Christ Jesus, who walk not after the flesh, but after the Spirit (the verdict is announced before the chapter explains how it was reached).",
  "Romans 8:2": "For the law of the Spirit of life in Christ Jesus hath made me free from the law of sin and death (one law is described as stronger than the other, not merely its opposite).",
  "Romans 8:3": "For what the law could not do, in that it was weak through the flesh (the law's failure is named plainly before the solution is introduced), God sending his own Son in the likeness of sinful flesh, and for sin, condemned sin in the flesh:",
  "Romans 8:9": "But ye are not in the flesh, but in the Spirit, if so be that the Spirit of God dwell in you. Now if any man have not the Spirit of Christ, he is none of his (a believer is defined here by who lives inside them, not by a record of behavior).",
  "Romans 8:13": "For if ye live after the flesh, ye shall die: but if ye through the Spirit do mortify the deeds of the body, ye shall live (the death and life in view here are a defeated day versus a victorious one, not a gain or loss of salvation -- and only one of the two powers named in this verse is able to do the mortifying).",
  "Romans 8:14": "For as many as are led by the Spirit of God, they are the sons of God (identity is tied to being led, not to achieving).",
  "Romans 8:15": "For ye have not received the spirit of bondage again to fear; but ye have received the Spirit of adoption, whereby we cry, Abba, Father (the word again signals that fear was the old condition, not the permanent one).",
  "Romans 8:16": "The Spirit itself beareth witness with our spirit, that we are the children of God (an inner testimony runs alongside a believer's own awareness, confirming it rather than replacing it):",
  "Romans 8:17": "And if children, then heirs; heirs of God, and joint-heirs with Christ (the inheritance is named jointly, not separately, with the one who earned it); if so be that we suffer with him, that we may be also glorified together.",
  "Romans 8:28": "And we know that all things work together for good to them that love God, to them who are the called according to his purpose (the promise covers all things, not only the pleasant ones).",
  "Romans 8:31": "What shall we then say to these things? If God be for us, who can be against us (a question the rest of the chapter never bothers to answer, because it does not need to)?",
  "Romans 8:33": "Who shall lay any thing to the charge of God’s elect? It is God that justifieth (the only one with legal standing to accuse is also the one who acquits).",
  "Romans 8:34": "Who is he that condemneth? It is Christ that died, yea rather, that is risen again, who is even at the right hand of God, who also maketh intercession for us (the one qualified to condemn is instead named as the one interceding).",
  "Romans 8:35": "Who shall separate us from the love of Christ? shall tribulation, or distress, or persecution, or famine, or nakedness, or peril, or sword (a list of the worst things a person could suffer, offered as the test case, and none of them qualify)?",
  "Romans 8:39": "Nor height, nor depth, nor any other creature, shall be able to separate us from the love of God, which is in Christ Jesus our Lord (the list runs out of categories before it finds one that works).",
  "Romans 9:5": "Whose are the fathers, and of whom as concerning the flesh Christ came, who is over all, God blessed for ever. Amen (the sentence names the man just introduced, by his human ancestry, God).",
  "Romans 9:6": "Not as though the word of God hath taken none effect. For they are not all Israel, which are of Israel (the promise did not fail, the definition was simply narrower than the family tree):",
  "Romans 9:8": "That is, They which are the children of the flesh, these are not the children of God: but the children of the promise are counted for the seed (biology is disqualified as the deciding factor in the very next clause).",
  "Romans 9:11": "(For the children being not yet born, neither having done any good or evil, that the purpose of God according to election might stand, not of works, but of him that calleth;) (the choice is made before either twin could do anything to earn or forfeit it).",
  "Romans 9:16": "So then it is not of him that willeth, nor of him that runneth, but of God that sheweth mercy (effort and desire are both named and both set aside in the same sentence).",
  "Romans 9:20": "Nay but, O man, who art thou that repliest against God? Shall the thing formed say to him that formed it, Why hast thou made me thus (the question is answered with a question about scale)?",
  "Romans 9:32": "Wherefore? Because they sought it not by faith, but as it were by the works of the law. For they stumbled at that stumblingstone (the very thing they were running toward became what they tripped over);",
  "Romans 9:33": "As it is written, Behold, I lay in Sion a stumblingstone and rock of offence: and whosoever believeth on him shall not be ashamed (the same stone is a hazard to one and a foundation to the other, depending only on faith).",
  "Romans 10:3": "For they being ignorant of God’s righteousness, and going about to establish their own righteousness, have not submitted themselves unto the righteousness of God (the failure named here is a refusal to submit, not a lack of effort).",
  "Romans 10:4": "For Christ is the end of the law for righteousness to every one that believeth (an ending, not a continuation under new management).",
  "Romans 10:9": "That if thou shalt confess with thy mouth the Lord Jesus, and shalt believe in thine heart that God hath raised him from the dead, thou shalt be saved (two things named, nothing else added to the list).",
  "Romans 10:10": "For with the heart man believeth unto righteousness; and with the mouth confession is made unto salvation (the same event described from two directions, inward and outward).",
  "Romans 10:11": "For the scripture saith, Whosoever believeth on him shall not be ashamed (no qualifier attached to whosoever).",
  "Romans 10:13": "For whosoever shall call upon the name of the Lord shall be saved (the shortest, widest promise in the chapter).",
  "Romans 10:17": "So then faith cometh by hearing, and hearing by the word of God (a cause traced back to its source in one sentence).",
  "Romans 11:1": "I say then, Hath God cast away his people? God forbid. For I also am an Israelite, of the seed of Abraham, of the tribe of Benjamin (Paul offers himself as the counter-example before making any argument).",
  "Romans 11:6": "And if by grace, then is it no more of works: otherwise grace is no more grace (the two categories are not allowed to share space in the same sentence). But if it be of works, then is it no more grace: otherwise work is no more work.",
  "Romans 11:20": "Well; because of unbelief they were broken off, and thou standest by faith. Be not highminded, but fear (the branches removed are Israel corporately, not individual believers -- and the branch grafted in stands there by faith, the same ground Israel could have stood on).",
  "Romans 11:23": "And they also, if they abide not still in unbelief, shall be graffed in: for God is able to graff them in again (the branches removed are described as still graftable, not discarded).",
  "Romans 11:26": "And so all Israel shall be saved: as it is written, There shall come out of Sion the Deliverer, and shall turn away ungodliness from Jacob (the exile from belief is described as having an ending):",
  "Romans 11:29": "For the gifts and calling of God are without repentance (God is described as not changing his mind about a promise once made).",
  "Romans 11:32": "For God hath concluded them all in unbelief, that he might have mercy upon all (both groups are placed in the same category first, so the same mercy can reach both).",
  "Romans 11:33": "O the depth of the riches both of the wisdom and knowledge of God! how unsearchable are his judgments, and his ways past finding out! (three chapters of argument give way, without warning, to a chapter of worship)",
  "Romans 12:1": "I beseech you therefore, brethren, by the mercies of God, that ye present your bodies a living sacrifice, holy, acceptable unto God, which is your reasonable service (the appeal is built on mercy already given, not obedience still owed).",
  "Romans 12:2": "And be not conformed to this world: but be ye transformed by the renewing of your mind (transformation is located in the mind before it shows up anywhere else), that ye may prove what is that good, and acceptable, and perfect, will of God.",
  "Romans 12:3": "For I say, through the grace given unto me, to every man that is among you, not to think of himself more highly than he ought to think; but to think soberly, according as God hath dealt to every man the measure of faith (self-assessment is tied to a measure that was given, not earned).",
  "Romans 12:9": "Let love be without dissimulation (love is defined first by what it refuses to fake). Abhor that which is evil; cleave to that which is good.",
  "Romans 12:14": "Bless them which persecute you: bless, and curse not (the instruction is repeated in the negative, closing off the loophole).",
  "Romans 12:19": "Dearly beloved, avenge not yourselves, but rather give place unto wrath: for it is written, Vengeance is mine; I will repay, saith the Lord (settling the score is handed off to someone else entirely).",
  "Romans 12:21": "Be not overcome of evil, but overcome evil with good (the same verb, overcome, describes two very different outcomes).",
  "Romans 13:1": "Let every soul be subject unto the higher powers. For there is no power but of God: the powers that be are ordained of God (government is traced back to a source higher than the government itself).",
  "Romans 13:8": "Owe no man any thing, but to love one another: for he that loveth another hath fulfilled the law (one debt is named as the only one that should never be marked paid).",
  "Romans 13:10": "Love worketh no ill to his neighbour: therefore love is the fulfilling of the law (an entire list of commandments summarized in a single motive).",
  "Romans 13:12": "The night is far spent, the day is at hand: let us therefore cast off the works of darkness, and let us put on the armour of light (the appeal is framed as a matter of timing before it is framed as a matter of morality).",
  "Romans 13:14": "But put ye on the Lord Jesus Christ, and make not provision for the flesh, to fulfil the lusts thereof (the alternative to feeding old desires is not willpower, it is putting on someone else entirely).",
  "Romans 14:4": "Who art thou that judgest another man’s servant? to his own master he standeth or falleth. Yea, he shall be holden up: for God is able to make him stand (the question of standing belongs to a master who is not the one asking it).",
  "Romans 14:8": "For whether we live, we live unto the Lord; and whether we die, we die unto the Lord: whether we live therefore, or die, we are the Lord’s (ownership, not circumstance, is named as the constant).",
  "Romans 14:10": "But why dost thou judge thy brother? or why dost thou set at nought thy brother? for we shall all stand before the judgment seat of Christ (everyone in the argument is reminded they will answer for themselves, not for each other).",
  "Romans 14:15": "But if thy brother be grieved with thy meat, now walkest thou not charitably. Destroy not him with thy meat, for whom Christ died (the same person Christ died for is the one a careless freedom could wound).",
  "Romans 14:17": "For the kingdom of God is not meat and drink; but righteousness, and peace, and joy in the Holy Ghost (the argument over food is declared, from the start, to be about the wrong subject).",
  "Romans 14:23": "And he that doubteth is damned if he eat, because he eateth not of faith: for whatsoever is not of faith is sin (the faith running through this whole chapter is faith in Christ and the Cross -- acting against your own conscience, whatever the specific issue, means acting apart from that).",
  "Romans 15:1": "We then that are strong ought to bear the infirmities of the weak, and not to please ourselves (strength is redefined as a resource for someone else, not a status for oneself).",
  "Romans 15:3": "For even Christ pleased not himself; but, as it is written, The reproaches of them that reproached thee fell on me (the instruction is rooted in an example, not a rule).",
  "Romans 15:4": "For whatsoever things were written aforetime were written for our learning, that we through patience and comfort of the scriptures might have hope (the entire Old Testament is described as written with a later reader already in mind).",
  "Romans 15:7": "Wherefore receive ye one another, as Christ also received us to the glory of God (the standard for how to receive someone is set by how Christ already received the one doing the receiving).",
  "Romans 15:13": "Now the God of hope fill you with all joy and peace in believing, that ye may abound in hope, through the power of the Holy Ghost (the joy and peace named here are the product of believing, not of circumstances lining up).",
  "Romans 15:20": "Yea, so have I strived to preach the gospel, not where Christ was named, lest I should build upon another man’s foundation (Paul describes his ambition as going where the gospel had not gone, not where the credit was easiest to get):",
  "Romans 16:1": "I commend unto you Phebe our sister, which is a servant of the church which is at Cenchrea (a woman carries this letter to Rome, named first among a long list of names):",
  "Romans 16:7": "Salute Andronicus and Junia, my kinsmen, and my fellowprisoners, who are of note among the apostles, who also were in Christ before me (Paul names two people whose faith predates his own, without a trace of resentment about it).",
  "Romans 16:20": "And the God of peace shall bruise Satan under your feet shortly (the letter's last word on the enemy arrives almost in passing). The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you. Amen.",
  "Romans 16:25": "Now to him that is of power to stablish you according to my gospel, and the preaching of Jesus Christ, according to the revelation of the mystery, which was kept secret since the world began (the letter that opened by naming ‘the gospel of God’ closes by tracing that same gospel back to before the world began),",
  "Romans 16:27": "To God only wise, be glory through Jesus Christ for ever. Amen (sixteen chapters of argument end in a single line of worship). Written to the Romans from Corinthus, and sent by Phebe servant of the church at Cenchrea.",
  "1 Corinthians 1:9": "God is faithful, by whom ye were called unto the fellowship of his Son Jesus Christ our Lord (the calling and the faithfulness both rest on God, not on the one being called).",
  "1 Corinthians 1:17": "For Christ sent me not to baptize, but to preach the gospel: not with wisdom of words, lest the cross of Christ should be made of none effect (he draws a sharp line between the ritual and the message -- one could be emptied by too much attention paid to the other).",
  "1 Corinthians 1:18": "For the preaching of the cross is to them that perish foolishness; but unto us which are saved it is the power of God (the same message lands as nonsense or as power depending entirely on which side of it a person is standing).",
  "1 Corinthians 1:21": "For after that in the wisdom of God the world by wisdom knew not God, it pleased God by the foolishness of preaching to save them that believe (the method itself looks foolish -- talking -- which is exactly why the result can't be credited to human cleverness).",
  "1 Corinthians 1:23": "But we preach Christ crucified, unto the Jews a stumblingblock, and unto the Greeks foolishness (the very thing both audiences reject going in is the thing being preached, unmodified for either one);",
  "1 Corinthians 1:25": "Because the foolishness of God is wiser than men; and the weakness of God is stronger than men (he does not say God's foolishness merely rivals human wisdom -- he says it outranks it).",
  "1 Corinthians 1:27": "But God hath chosen the foolish things of the world to confound the wise; and God hath chosen the weak things of the world to confound the things which are mighty (the method matches the message -- an unimpressive cross, chosen through an unimpressive means of choosing);",
  "1 Corinthians 1:29": "That no flesh should glory in his presence (the entire selection process described in the previous verses serves this one purpose).",
  "1 Corinthians 1:30": "But of him are ye in Christ Jesus, who of God is made unto us wisdom, and righteousness, and sanctification, and redemption (four things a person might spend a lifetime trying to acquire are named here as already supplied):",
  "1 Corinthians 2:2": "For I determined not to know any thing among you, save Jesus Christ, and him crucified (he narrows an unlimited subject down to one point on purpose).",
  "1 Corinthians 2:4": "And my speech and my preaching was not with enticing words of man\u2019s wisdom, but in demonstration of the Spirit and of power (he wanted the result traceable to the message, not to the messenger's skill):",
  "1 Corinthians 2:8": "Which none of the princes of this world knew: for had they known it, they would not have crucified the Lord of glory (the men who orchestrated the crucifixion did not know they were fulfilling the very plan that would defeat them).",
  "1 Corinthians 2:9": "But as it is written, Eye hath not seen, nor ear heard, neither have entered into the heart of man, the things which God hath prepared for them that love him (three human senses are named and ruled out as the source of this knowledge, one after another).",
  "1 Corinthians 2:12": "Now we have received, not the spirit of the world, but the spirit which is of God; that we might know the things that are freely given to us of God (the knowing depends on which spirit is doing the informing, not on native intelligence).",
  "1 Corinthians 2:14": "But the natural man receiveth not the things of the Spirit of God: for they are foolishness unto him: neither can he know them, because they are spiritually discerned (this is described as an inability, not a decision -- the natural man isn't refusing to see, he lacks the organ for it).",
  "1 Corinthians 2:16": "For who hath known the mind of the Lord, that he may instruct him? But we have the mind of Christ (the question is rhetorical and expects silence -- the next sentence quietly claims what the silence just conceded).",
  "1 Corinthians 3:6": "I have planted, Apollos watered; but God gave the increase (two men get named for the labor; credit for the result goes to neither of them).",
  "1 Corinthians 3:9": "For we are labourers together with God: ye are God\u2019s husbandry, ye are God\u2019s building (the workers belong to God as much as the field and the building do).",
  "1 Corinthians 3:11": "For other foundation can no man lay than that is laid, which is Jesus Christ (the foundation is described as already laid and unchangeable, not as one option among several).",
  "1 Corinthians 3:13": "Every man\u2019s work shall be made manifest: for the day shall declare it, because it shall be revealed by fire; and the fire shall try every man\u2019s work of what sort it is (the test named here is aimed at the work, not at the worker's standing).",
  "1 Corinthians 3:16": "Know ye not that ye are the temple of God, and that the Spirit of God dwelleth in you (the question assumes something about them that they should already know)?",
  "1 Corinthians 3:21": "Therefore let no man glory in men. For all things are yours (the boundary he draws isn't smaller ownership, it's bigger -- everything belongs to them, so no single preacher needs to be claimed);",
  "1 Corinthians 4:1": "Let a man so account of us, as of the ministers of Christ, and stewards of the mysteries of God (he defines the job before anyone gets to grade the performance).",
  "1 Corinthians 4:2": "Moreover it is required in stewards, that a man be found faithful (the requirement named is faithfulness, not success).",
  "1 Corinthians 4:7": "For who maketh thee to differ from another? and what hast thou that thou didst not receive? now if thou didst receive it, why dost thou glory, as if thou hadst not received it (three questions in a row dismantle the same boast from three different directions)?",
  "1 Corinthians 4:20": "For the kingdom of God is not in word, but in power (talk and power are set against each other here as two different categories, not two degrees of the same thing).",
  "1 Corinthians 5:7": "Purge out therefore the old leaven, that ye may be a new lump, as ye are unleavened. For even Christ our passover is sacrificed for us (the instruction to clean house is grounded in a fact already true, not a status still being earned):",
  "1 Corinthians 6:19": "What? know ye not that your body is the temple of the Holy Ghost which is in you, which ye have of God, and ye are not your own (the question assumes ownership has already changed hands)?",
  "1 Corinthians 6:20": "For ye are bought with a price: therefore glorify God in your body, and in your spirit, which are God\u2019s (the instruction follows the fact stated just before it, not the other way around).",
  "1 Corinthians 7:19": "Circumcision is nothing, and uncircumcision is nothing, but the keeping of the commandments of God (the one marker the culture prized most is emptied of meaning here, and replaced with something no ritual can supply).",
  "1 Corinthians 7:23": "Ye are bought with a price; be not ye the servants of men (the same sentence that names the purchase price also states what it bought them out of).",
  "1 Corinthians 8:6": "But to us there is but one God, the Father, of whom are all things, and we in him; and one Lord Jesus Christ, by whom are all things, and we by him (everything and everyone named in this sentence is described as flowing from, or through, one of these two).",
  "1 Corinthians 8:9": "But take heed lest by any means this liberty of yours become a stumblingblock to them that are weak (freedom is not cancelled here -- it is made answerable to someone besides the person exercising it).",
  "1 Corinthians 8:12": "But when ye sin so against the brethren, and wound their weak conscience, ye sin against Christ (harm done to the least confident believer in the room is counted here as harm done directly to him).",
  "1 Corinthians 8:13": "Wherefore, if meat make my brother to offend, I will eat no flesh while the world standeth, lest I make my brother to offend (he states the strongest possible version of the principle, not the minimum he could get away with).",
  "1 Corinthians 9:19": "For though I be free from all men, yet have I made myself servant unto all, that I might gain the more (the servitude described here is voluntary, which is the only way it could also be free).",
  "1 Corinthians 9:22": "To the weak became I as weak, that I might gain the weak: I am made all things to all men, that I might by all means save some (the goal named at the end of this sentence is not agreement -- it is some being saved).",
  "1 Corinthians 9:24": "Know ye not that they which run in a race run all, but one receiveth the prize? So run, that ye may obtain (he borrows the discipline of a runner, not the odds -- in the race he actually has in mind, everyone who runs it wins).",
  "1 Corinthians 9:25": "And every man that striveth for the mastery is temperate in all things. Now they do it to obtain a corruptible crown; but we an incorruptible (the discipline required is the same in both cases; only the size of what's at stake has changed).",
  "1 Corinthians 10:6": "Now these things were our examples, to the intent we should not lust after evil things, as they also lusted (this history is preserved on purpose, as a warning, not as trivia).",
  "1 Corinthians 10:11": "Now all these things happened unto them for ensamples: and they are written for our admonition, upon whom the ends of the world are come (the same events are described here as both history and a message addressed forward across time).",
  "1 Corinthians 10:12": "Wherefore let him that thinketh he standeth take heed lest he fall (the caution here is addressed to confidence, not to standing itself).",
  "1 Corinthians 10:13": "There hath no temptation taken you but such as is common to man: but God is faithful, who will not suffer you to be tempted above that ye are able; but will with the temptation also make a way to escape, that ye may be able to bear it (Paul answers his own warning in the very next breath -- the escape is promised before the temptation even arrives).",
  "1 Corinthians 10:16": "The cup of blessing which we bless, is it not the communion of the blood of Christ? The bread which we break, is it not the communion of the body of Christ (two questions, both expecting the same answer, tie an ordinary meal to the actual event it represents)?",
  "1 Corinthians 10:31": "Whether therefore ye eat, or drink, or whatsoever ye do, do all to the glory of God (the instruction covers the two most basic human acts, and then everything else, in one sentence).",
  "1 Corinthians 11:11": "Nevertheless neither is the man without the woman, neither the woman without the man, in the Lord (whatever else this passage says, this verse insists on mutual need, not one-sided dependence).",
  "1 Corinthians 11:24": "And when he had given thanks, he brake it, and said, Take, eat: this is my body, which is broken for you: this do in remembrance of me (the bread is broken before it is given, and the breaking itself is named as part of what is being remembered).",
  "1 Corinthians 11:25": "After the same manner also he took the cup, when he had supped, saying, This cup is the new testament in my blood: this do ye, as oft as ye drink it, in remembrance of me (a covenant this large is ratified at a dinner table, not in a temple).",
  "1 Corinthians 11:26": "For as often as ye eat this bread, and drink this cup, ye do shew the Lord\u2019s death till he come (the meal points backward to the cross and forward to his return in the same sentence).",
  "1 Corinthians 11:28": "But let a man examine himself, and so let him eat of that bread, and drink of that cup (the examination named here is aimed inward, at the individual, not outward at anyone else in the room).",
  "1 Corinthians 11:29": "For he that eateth and drinketh unworthily, eateth and drinketh damnation to himself, not discerning the Lord\u2019s body (what's on trial here is whether the meal is recognized for what it is, not whether the person eating it belongs to him).",
  "1 Corinthians 12:7": "But the manifestation of the Spirit is given to every man to profit withal (the gifts are named as given to everyone, and given for everyone's benefit, not for the recipient alone).",
  "1 Corinthians 12:13": "For by one Spirit are we all baptized into one body, whether we be Jews or Gentiles, whether we be bond or free; and have been all made to drink into one Spirit (the categories that divided the ancient world are listed here and dissolved in the same sentence).",
  "1 Corinthians 12:21": "And the eye cannot say unto the hand, I have no need of thee: nor again the head to the feet, I have no need of you (even the most prominent parts of the body are told plainly that they cannot say this).",
  "1 Corinthians 12:26": "And whether one member suffer, all the members suffer with it; or one member be honoured, all the members rejoice with it (the nervous system of a human body is used here to describe what should happen emotionally in a church).",
  "1 Corinthians 12:27": "Now ye are the body of Christ, and members in particular (the metaphor running through the whole chapter is dropped here and stated as plain fact).",
  "1 Corinthians 13:1": "Though I speak with the tongues of men and of angels, and have not charity, I am become as sounding brass, or a tinkling cymbal (the greatest possible gift of speech, without love, is reduced here to noise).",
  "1 Corinthians 13:3": "And though I bestow all my goods to feed the poor, and though I give my body to be burned, and have not charity, it profiteth me nothing (he names the most extreme sacrifices imaginable and still finds a way for them to count for nothing).",
  "1 Corinthians 13:4": "Charity suffereth long, and is kind; charity envieth not; charity vaunteth not itself, is not puffed up (this is not a list of feelings -- every verb in this sentence describes something love does),",
  "1 Corinthians 13:8": "Charity never faileth: but whether there be prophecies, they shall fail; whether there be tongues, they shall cease; whether there be knowledge, it shall vanish away (every other gift discussed in this letter is given an expiration date here; love is the one exception named).",
  "1 Corinthians 13:12": "For now we see through a glass, darkly; but then face to face: now I know in part; but then shall I know even as also I am known (the shift from partial to complete is described from both directions -- what he will know, and the fact that he is already fully known).",
  "1 Corinthians 13:13": "And now abideth faith, hope, charity, these three; but the greatest of these is charity (three things are named as permanent, and then ranked).",
  "1 Corinthians 14:3": "But he that prophesieth speaketh unto men to edification, and exhortation, and comfort (the purpose test for any gift used publicly is stated here in three words).",
  "1 Corinthians 14:12": "Even so ye, forasmuch as ye are zealous of spiritual gifts, seek that ye may excel to the edifying of the church (the zeal itself is not corrected here -- only its target is redirected).",
  "1 Corinthians 14:33": "For God is not the author of confusion, but of peace, as in all churches of the saints (whatever else is happening in a service, this sentence rules out one possible source of it).",
  "1 Corinthians 14:40": "Let all things be done decently and in order (an entire chapter of specific instructions is summarized here in six words).",
  "1 Corinthians 15:3": "For I delivered unto you first of all that which I also received, how that Christ died for our sins according to the scriptures (this claim is anchored to Scripture written centuries before the event it describes);",
  "1 Corinthians 15:4": "And that he was buried, and that he rose again the third day according to the scriptures (burial and resurrection are both named as historical claims, not as symbols):",
  "1 Corinthians 15:10": "But by the grace of God I am what I am: and his grace which was bestowed upon me was not in vain; but I laboured more abundantly than they all: yet not I, but the grace of God which was with me (he credits the labor to grace, and then in the same breath takes the credit back and hands it to grace again).",
  "1 Corinthians 15:14": "And if Christ be not risen, then is our preaching vain, and your faith is also vain (he states plainly what would be true if the resurrection weren't -- and lets the weight of it sit there before verse 20 answers it).",
  "1 Corinthians 15:19": "If in this life only we have hope in Christ, we are of all men most miserable (the entire argument of this chapter is compressed into what this one sentence would mean if it were true).",
  "1 Corinthians 15:20": "But now is Christ risen from the dead, and become the firstfruits of them that slept (two words -- but now -- turn nineteen verses of hypothetical loss into something that didn't happen).",
  "1 Corinthians 15:22": "For as in Adam all die, even so in Christ shall all be made alive (one man's history is answered here by one Man's, name for name, structure for structure).",
  "1 Corinthians 15:26": "The last enemy that shall be destroyed is death (of everything that opposes the plan of God, this is named as the final one standing, and the last to fall).",
  "1 Corinthians 15:44": "It is sown a natural body; it is raised a spiritual body. There is a natural body, and there is a spiritual body (the word sown, used throughout this passage, describes something that looks like an ending and turns out to be a planting).",
  "1 Corinthians 15:52": "In a moment, in the twinkling of an eye, at the last trump: for the trumpet shall sound, and the dead shall be raised incorruptible, and we shall be changed (the most significant change any of these readers will ever undergo is measured here in the smallest unit of time available).",
  "1 Corinthians 15:55": "O death, where is thy sting? O grave, where is thy victory (the two things every funeral in history has feared are addressed directly here, and asked where their power went)?",
  "1 Corinthians 15:57": "But thanks be to God, which giveth us the victory through our Lord Jesus Christ (the entire argument of this chapter resolves into one line of thanks before the practical instruction of the next verse begins).",
  "1 Corinthians 15:58": "Therefore, my beloved brethren, be ye stedfast, unmoveable, always abounding in the work of the Lord, forasmuch as ye know that your labour is not in vain in the Lord (the word therefore ties fifty-seven verses of argument to one practical conclusion).",
  "1 Corinthians 16:13": "Watch ye, stand fast in the faith, quit you like men, be strong (four short commands close the letter's practical instructions the way a drill sergeant would, not a philosopher).",
  "1 Corinthians 16:14": "Let all your things be done with charity (after sixteen chapters covering divisions, lawsuits, food, marriage, and church order, this is offered as the one rule that would have prevented most of it).",
  "1 Corinthians 16:22": "If any man love not the Lord Jesus Christ, let him be Anathema Maran-atha (the letter's closing line pairs a curse with a hope in the same breath -- judgment for those who don't love him, and the promise that he is coming).",
  "2 Corinthians 1:4": "Who comforteth us in all our tribulation, that we may be able to comfort them which are in any trouble, by the comfort wherewith we ourselves are comforted of God (the comfort he receives is explicitly described as material for comforting someone else, not something kept).",
  "2 Corinthians 1:9": "But we had the sentence of death in ourselves, that we should not trust in ourselves, but in God which raiseth the dead (the crisis did exactly what it was meant to do -- moved his trust off himself and onto God).",
  "2 Corinthians 1:20": "For all the promises of God in him are yea, and in him Amen, unto the glory of God by us (every promise God ever made is said to find its yes in one place -- not a list, a person).",
  "2 Corinthians 1:22": "Who hath also sealed us, and given the earnest of the Spirit in our hearts (the earnest named here is a down payment, not a loan -- it guarantees what's still coming; it isn't owed back).",
  "2 Corinthians 1:24": "Not for that we have dominion over your faith, but are helpers of your joy: for by faith ye stand (he names the one thing that keeps a person standing, and it isn't him).",
  "2 Corinthians 2:14": "Now thanks be unto God, which always causeth us to triumph in Christ, and maketh manifest the savour of his knowledge by us in every place (the triumph described here is constant, not occasional -- always, not sometimes).",
  "2 Corinthians 2:16": "To the one we are the savour of death unto death; and to the other the savour of life unto life. And who is sufficient for these things? (The same message produces opposite results in two different people -- the difference was never in the message.)",
  "2 Corinthians 3:5": "Not that we are sufficient of ourselves to think any thing as of ourselves; but our sufficiency is of God (competence is relocated in one sentence -- from a man who has none, to a source who has all of it).",
  "2 Corinthians 3:6": "Who also hath made us able ministers of the new testament; not of the letter, but of the spirit: for the letter killeth, but the spirit giveth life (one covenant is named as killing, the other as giving life -- the letter was never able to do what the Spirit does).",
  "2 Corinthians 3:18": "But we all, with open face beholding as in a glass the glory of the Lord, are changed into the same image from glory to glory, even as by the Spirit of the Lord (the change described here has no finish line named in the sentence -- glory to glory, not glory to done).",
  "2 Corinthians 4:6": "For God, who commanded the light to shine out of darkness, hath shined in our hearts, to give the light of the knowledge of the glory of God in the face of Jesus Christ (the same voice that commanded light on the first day is described doing it again, this time inside a person).",
  "2 Corinthians 4:7": "But we have this treasure in earthen vessels, that the excellency of the power may be of God, and not of us (the container is named as ordinary on purpose, so no one mistakes the clay for the treasure it's carrying).",
  "2 Corinthians 4:16": "For which cause we faint not; but though our outward man perish, yet the inward man is renewed day by day (two different clocks are described running on the same body -- one winding down, one being renewed daily).",
  "2 Corinthians 4:17": "For our light affliction, which is but for a moment, worketh for us a far more exceeding and eternal weight of glory (he calls the same suffering light and momentary, not because it is small, but because of what it's being weighed against);",
  "2 Corinthians 5:1": "For we know that if our earthly house of this tabernacle were dissolved, we have a building of God, an house not made with hands, eternal in the heavens (the next home is described as already standing, not still under construction).",
  "2 Corinthians 5:7": "(For we walk by faith, not by sight:) (The shortest verse in the chapter states the rule the whole chapter has been running on.)",
  "2 Corinthians 5:15": "And that he died for all, that they which live should not henceforth live unto themselves, but unto him which died for them, and rose again (the death is named first, and the new direction of the living follows it -- not the other way around).",
  "2 Corinthians 5:17": "Therefore if any man be in Christ, he is a new creature: old things are passed away; behold, all things are become new (nothing here is described as improved -- old things pass away, new things arrive; it's a replacement, not a renovation).",
  "2 Corinthians 5:19": "To wit, that God was in Christ, reconciling the world unto himself, not imputing their trespasses unto them; and hath committed unto us the word of reconciliation (the reconciling is placed entirely on God's side of the ledger -- their trespasses are described as not being counted, not as being balanced).",
  "2 Corinthians 5:21": "For he hath made him to be sin for us, who knew no sin; that we might be made the righteousness of God in him (an exchange is described in one sentence -- his sinlessness for their sin, running in only one direction).",
  "2 Corinthians 6:2": "(For he saith, I have heard thee in a time accepted, and in the day of salvation have I succoured thee: behold, now is the accepted time; behold, now is the day of salvation.) (The appointed day is announced twice in one breath as already here, not still on its way.)",
  "2 Corinthians 6:10": "As sorrowful, yet alway rejoicing; as poor, yet making many rich; as having nothing, and yet possessing all things (every phrase in this verse pairs an appearance with a fact, and the fact wins every time).",
  "2 Corinthians 6:14": "Be ye not unequally yoked together with unbelievers: for what fellowship hath righteousness with unrighteousness? and what communion hath light with darkness? (The image is agricultural -- two animals of unequal strength pulling one plow, wearing each other out instead of working.)",
  "2 Corinthians 6:16": "And what agreement hath the temple of God with idols? for ye are the temple of the living God (the question assumes an answer no one in the room would actually give out loud); as God hath said, I will dwell in them, and walk in them; and I will be their God, and they shall be my people.",
  "2 Corinthians 6:17": "Wherefore come out from among them, and be ye separate, saith the Lord (separation from the world is commanded here, not isolation from it), and touch not the unclean thing (unholy contact with a polluted world is what's in view); and I will receive you (walking clean in this world is made possible only by continued faith in the Cross, which gives the Holy Spirit room to work),",
  "2 Corinthians 6:18": "And will be a Father unto you, and ye shall be my sons and daughters, saith the Lord Almighty (the Hebrew behind 'Almighty' is Shaddai, built on the word for a mother's breast -- the title itself pictures nourishment drawn from the Lord, not from the world).",
  "2 Corinthians 7:1": "Having therefore these promises, dearly beloved, let us cleanse ourselves from all filthiness of the flesh and spirit, perfecting holiness in the fear of God (the cleansing is described as something they do in response to promises already given, not something that earns them).",
  "2 Corinthians 7:10": "For godly sorrow worketh repentance to salvation not to be repented of: but the sorrow of the world worketh death (the same word, sorrow, is used for two different roads -- one arrives at life, the other at death, and the difference is which way it faces).",
  "2 Corinthians 8:9": "For ye know the grace of our Lord Jesus Christ, that, though he was rich, yet for your sakes he became poor, that ye through his poverty might be rich (the math is named plainly -- his riches became poverty so their poverty could become riches).",
  "2 Corinthians 8:12": "For if there be first a willing mind, it is accepted according to that a man hath, and not according to that he hath not (the standard named here is willingness, not the size of the gift).",
  "2 Corinthians 9:6": "But this I say, He which soweth sparingly shall reap also sparingly; and he which soweth bountifully shall reap also bountifully (the harvest described here follows the planting in kind, not just in size).",
  "2 Corinthians 9:7": "Every man according as he purposeth in his heart, so let him give; not grudgingly, or of necessity: for God loveth a cheerful giver (the Greek word behind cheerful is closer to hilarious -- reluctant giving and cheerful giving are named as two different things entirely).",
  "2 Corinthians 9:8": "And God is able to make all grace abound toward you; that ye, always having all sufficiency in all things, may abound to every good work (sufficiency is promised in every direction the sentence can reach -- all grace, all things, every good work):",
  "2 Corinthians 9:15": "Thanks be unto God for his unspeakable gift (the chapter spends fifteen verses on human giving and ends by naming a gift no one in the room gave).",
  "2 Corinthians 10:4": "(For the weapons of our warfare are not carnal, but mighty through God to the pulling down of strong holds;) (The weapons are described by what they aren't first -- not carnal -- before anything is said about what they are.)",
  "2 Corinthians 10:5": "Casting down imaginations, and every high thing that exalteth itself against the knowledge of God, and bringing into captivity every thought to the obedience of Christ (the target named is not behavior, it's thought -- a captivity that happens further upstream than action);",
  "2 Corinthians 10:12": "For we dare not make ourselves of the number, or compare ourselves with some that commend themselves: but they measuring themselves by themselves, and comparing themselves among themselves, are not wise (he refuses to join a contest where the players are also the judges).",
  "2 Corinthians 10:17": "But he that glorieth, let him glory in the Lord (one word decides where the glory lands, and he names the only place it's allowed to).",
  "2 Corinthians 10:18": "For not he that commendeth himself is approved, but whom the Lord commendeth (approval is described as coming from exactly one source, and self-recommendation isn't it).",
  "2 Corinthians 11:3": "But I fear, lest by any means, as the serpent beguiled Eve through his subtilty, so your minds should be corrupted from the simplicity that is in Christ (the danger named is not false doctrine arriving loudly, but simplicity being quietly complicated).",
  "2 Corinthians 11:14": "And no marvel; for Satan himself is transformed into an angel of light (the warning names disguise as the actual danger -- not an obvious enemy, a convincing one).",
  "2 Corinthians 11:29": "Who is weak, and I am not weak? who is offended, and I burn not? (He claims every weakness in every church he's responsible for as his own.)",
  "2 Corinthians 11:30": "If I must needs glory, I will glory of the things which concern mine infirmities (he chooses to boast in the one category of things most people spend their whole lives hiding).",
  "2 Corinthians 12:9": "And he said unto me, My grace is sufficient for thee: for my strength is made perfect in weakness. Most gladly therefore will I rather glory in my infirmities, that the power of Christ may rest upon me (the answer he receives is not the removal he asked for, but a strength that operates on exactly the ground he didn't want).",
  "2 Corinthians 12:10": "Therefore I take pleasure in infirmities, in reproaches, in necessities, in persecutions, in distresses for Christ's sake: for when I am weak, then am I strong (he states the paradox as settled fact, not as a puzzle still being worked out -- weak, therefore strong).",
  "2 Corinthians 13:4": "For though he was crucified through weakness, yet he liveth by the power of God. For we also are weak in him, but we shall live with him by the power of God toward you (the same power that raised him from a death he actually died is named as available to a church he hasn't even visited yet).",
  "2 Corinthians 13:5": "Examine yourselves, whether ye be in the faith; prove your own selves. Know ye not your own selves, how that Jesus Christ is in you, except ye be reprobates? (The examination he calls for is aimed at one question -- whether Christ is in you -- not a checklist of behavior.)",
  "2 Corinthians 13:8": "For we can do nothing against the truth, but for the truth (he draws the line around what he's willing to bend for anyone -- nothing, against the truth, whatever the cost).",
  "2 Corinthians 13:14": "The grace of the Lord Jesus Christ, and the love of God, and the communion of the Holy Ghost, be with you all. Amen (the letter closes by naming three persons in one sentence, each doing something different for the same reader). The second epistle to the Corinthians was written from Philippi, a city of Macedonia, by Titus and Lucas.",
  "Galatians 1:1": "Paul, an apostle, (not of men, neither by man, but by Jesus Christ, and God the Father, who raised him from the dead;) (he skips every human source and anchors his authority in the resurrection itself).",
  "Galatians 1:4": "Who gave himself for our sins, that he might deliver us from this present evil world, according to the will of God and our Father (the giving was self-initiated -- nothing external forced the hand that gave itself):",
  "Galatians 1:6": "I marvel that ye are so soon removed from him that called you into the grace of Christ unto another gospel (the letter opens with astonishment instead of a greeting -- Paul skips his usual thanksgiving entirely):",
  "Galatians 1:7": "Which is not another; but there be some that trouble you, and would pervert the gospel of Christ (he corrects his own wording mid-sentence -- there is no second gospel to switch to, only a distortion of the one that already exists).",
  "Galatians 1:8": "But though we, or an angel from heaven, preach any other gospel unto you than that which we have preached unto you, let him be accursed (Paul puts himself inside his own warning first, then raises the bar to an angel -- the standard applies to the message, regardless of who carries it).",
  "Galatians 1:9": "As we said before, so say I now again, If any man preach any other gospel unto you than that ye have received, let him be accursed (the repetition is deliberate -- a settled position stated twice, not a line thrown out once in anger).",
  "Galatians 1:10": "For do I now persuade men, or God? or do I seek to please men? for if I yet pleased men, I should not be the servant of Christ (he names the one test that would disqualify him from the title he has just claimed).",
  "Galatians 1:12": "For I neither received it of man, neither was I taught it, but by the revelation of Jesus Christ (no classroom, no human teacher stands between him and the source).",
  "Galatians 1:15": "But when it pleased God, who separated me from my mother's womb, and called me by his grace (the calling is dated before his birth and credited to grace, before he had done anything to earn or resist it),",
  "Galatians 1:20": "Now the things which I write unto you, behold, before God, I lie not (he puts his own account under oath before writing another word).",
  "Galatians 1:24": "And they glorified God in me (the same man they once feared becomes, without changing subject, the reason they praise God).",
  "Galatians 2:2": "And I went up by revelation, and communicated unto them that gospel which I preach among the Gentiles, but privately to them which were of reputation, lest by any means I should run, or had run, in vain (the whole future of his ministry among the Gentiles is put on the table in a private meeting before it is ever tested in public).",
  "Galatians 2:4": "And that because of false brethren unawares brought in, who came in privily to spy out our liberty which we have in Christ Jesus, that they might bring us into bondage (freedom and bondage are named as the only two options on the table -- there is no third, milder position).",
  "Galatians 2:6": "But of these who seemed to be somewhat, (whatsoever they were, it maketh no matter to me: God accepteth no man's person:) for they who seemed to be somewhat in conference added nothing to me (the most respected men in the room are said to have added nothing -- the message he carries was already complete before he sat down with them):",
  "Galatians 2:9": "And when James, Cephas, and John, who seemed to be pillars, perceived the grace that was given unto me, they gave to me and Barnabas the right hands of fellowship (the leaders of the Jerusalem church put their handshake behind a mission field they will never personally work); that we should go unto the heathen, and they unto the circumcision.",
  "Galatians 2:11": "But when Peter was come to Antioch, I withstood him to the face, because he was to be blamed (rank does not exempt Peter from correction, and it does not stop Paul from giving it).",
  "Galatians 2:14": "But when I saw that they walked not uprightly according to the truth of the gospel, I said unto Peter before them all, If thou, being a Jew, livest after the manner of Gentiles, and not as do the Jews, why compellest thou the Gentiles to live as do the Jews? (the rebuke is delivered in front of the whole church Peter was trying to impress).",
  "Galatians 2:16": "Knowing that a man is not justified by the works of the law, but by the faith of Jesus Christ, even we have believed in Jesus Christ, that we might be justified by the faith of Christ, and not by the works of the law: for by the works of the law shall no flesh be justified (the sentence names the same conclusion three times in three different ways, as if one statement of it would not be enough).",
  "Galatians 2:17": "But if, while we seek to be justified by Christ, we ourselves also are found sinners, is therefore Christ the minister of sin? God forbid (a failure in the Believer is never allowed to become an accusation against Christ).",
  "Galatians 2:19": "For I through the law am dead to the law, that I might live unto God (the law is the very thing that pronounces him dead to the law -- its own verdict removes it from the case).",
  "Galatians 2:20": "I am crucified with Christ: nevertheless I live; yet not I, but Christ liveth in me: and the life which I now live in the flesh I live by the faith of the Son of God, who loved me, and gave himself for me (the sentence changes subjects mid-stream -- it starts with Paul dying and ends with someone else living).",
  "Galatians 2:21": "I do not frustrate the grace of God: for if righteousness come by the law, then Christ is dead in vain (he states the stakes as plainly as they can be stated -- either the law can do it, or the cross was not necessary; not both).",
  "Galatians 3:1": "O foolish Galatians, who hath bewitched you, that ye should not obey the truth, before whose eyes Jesus Christ hath been evidently set forth, crucified among you? (he preached the cross with enough clarity that hearing it was like watching it happen).",
  "Galatians 3:2": "This only would I learn of you, Received ye the Spirit by the works of the law, or by the hearing of faith? (their own experience is the evidence he calls to the stand, not a new argument).",
  "Galatians 3:6": "Even as Abraham believed God, and it was accounted to him for righteousness (the account is credited to belief, generations before there was a law to keep).",
  "Galatians 3:8": "And the scripture, foreseeing that God would justify the heathen through faith, preached before the gospel unto Abraham, saying, In thee shall all nations be blessed (the gospel is described as older than the record most people think it started with).",
  "Galatians 3:10": "For as many as are of the works of the law are under the curse: for it is written, Cursed is every one that continueth not in all things which are written in the book of the law to do them (the standard named here is total -- not most things, all things, continually).",
  "Galatians 3:11": "But that no man is justified by the law in the sight of God, it is evident: for, The just shall live by faith (the verse he quotes to prove his point is taken from the law's own prophets).",
  "Galatians 3:13": "Christ hath redeemed us from the curse of the law, being made a curse for us: for it is written, Cursed is every one that hangeth on a tree (the curse the law pronounced finds a place to land that is not the one who broke it).",
  "Galatians 3:16": "Now to Abraham and his seed were the promises made. He saith not, And to seeds, as of many; but as of one, And to thy seed, which is Christ (the argument turns on a single letter -- singular instead of plural).",
  "Galatians 3:19": "Wherefore then serveth the law? It was added because of transgressions, till the seed should come to whom the promise was made; and it was ordained by angels in the hand of a mediator (the law is given an expiration date in the same sentence that explains why it was given at all).",
  "Galatians 3:22": "But the scripture hath concluded all under sin, that the promise by faith of Jesus Christ might be given to them that believe (the very verdict that shuts every door turns out to be what clears the way to the one door left open).",
  "Galatians 3:24": "Wherefore the law was our schoolmaster to bring us unto Christ, that we might be justified by faith (the law's whole job description is written here as transportation, not destination).",
  "Galatians 3:26": "For ye are all the children of God by faith in Christ Jesus (sonship belongs to the reader as much as to Abraham, and on the same terms).",
  "Galatians 3:28": "There is neither Jew nor Greek, there is neither bond nor free, there is neither male nor female: for ye are all one in Christ Jesus (every line society draws to separate people is named here and then erased in the same sentence).",
  "Galatians 3:29": "And if ye be Christ's, then are ye Abraham's seed, and heirs according to the promise (the inheritance reaches a Gentile reader through a single word -- if).",
  "Galatians 4:4": "But when the fulness of the time was come, God sent forth his Son, made of a woman, made under the law (the sender acts first -- nothing in the verse describes anyone asking him to).",
  "Galatians 4:5": "To redeem them that were under the law, that we might receive the adoption of sons (the redemption and the adoption are named in the same sentence -- one leads immediately into the other).",
  "Galatians 4:6": "And because ye are sons, God hath sent forth the Spirit of his Son into your hearts, crying, Abba, Father (the sonship comes first in the sentence -- the Spirit is sent because of it, not in order to earn it).",
  "Galatians 4:7": "Wherefore thou art no more a servant, but a son; and if a son, then an heir of God through Christ (the whole verse moves through three titles without pausing -- servant, son, heir).",
  "Galatians 4:9": "But now, after that ye have known God, or rather are known of God, how turn ye again to the weak and beggarly elements, whereunto ye desire again to be in bondage? (he corrects his own sentence mid-thought -- their knowing God matters less than the fact that God already knew them first).",
  "Galatians 4:19": "My little children, of whom I travail in birth again until Christ be formed in you (he uses the language of labor for people he has already led to faith once).",
  "Galatians 4:28": "Now we, brethren, as Isaac was, are the children of promise (the reader's identity is compared to a birth that took decades of waiting and no ordinary explanation).",
  "Galatians 4:30": "Nevertheless what saith the scripture? Cast out the bondwoman and her son: for the son of the bondwoman shall not be heir with the son of the freewoman (two systems are pictured as unable to share one inheritance).",
  "Galatians 4:31": "So then, brethren, we are not children of the bondwoman, but of the free (the identity assigned here is stated as settled fact, not as an aspiration).",
  "Galatians 5:1": "Stand fast therefore in the liberty wherewith Christ hath made us free, and be not entangled again with the yoke of bondage (the freedom already belongs to the reader -- the instruction is to keep standing in it, not to go acquire it).",
  "Galatians 5:4": "Christ is become of no effect unto you, whosoever of you are justified by the law; ye are fallen from grace (law and grace cannot both be the thing being trusted at once -- reaching for one is already letting go of the other).",
  "Galatians 5:6": "For in Jesus Christ neither circumcision availeth any thing, nor uncircumcision; but faith which worketh by love (the entire debate over the ritual is set aside in favor of a different question -- what is the faith doing).",
  "Galatians 5:9": "A little leaven leaveneth the whole lump (the danger named is proportion -- it does not take much of the wrong ingredient to change the whole loaf).",
  "Galatians 5:11": "And I, brethren, if I yet preach circumcision, why do I yet suffer persecution? then is the offence of the cross ceased (he offers his own ongoing persecution as proof of what he is actually still preaching).",
  "Galatians 5:13": "For, brethren, ye have been called unto liberty; only use not liberty for an occasion to the flesh, but by love serve one another (the same word, liberty, opens the sentence and is then immediately fenced from one specific misuse).",
  "Galatians 5:14": "For all the law is fulfilled in one word, even in this; Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself (an entire law code is compressed into a single command, not expanded into a longer one).",
  "Galatians 5:17": "For the flesh lusteth against the Spirit, and the Spirit against the flesh: and these are contrary the one to the other: so that ye cannot do the things that ye would (the conflict described here is not occasional -- it is structural, built into the sentence as a permanent standoff).",
  "Galatians 5:18": "But if ye be led of the Spirit, ye are not under the law (being led and being under law are described as mutually exclusive states, not as two rules a person keeps at once).",
  "Galatians 5:22": "But the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, longsuffering, gentleness, goodness, faith (the list is called fruit, singular, not fruits -- one thing with many features, not nine separate achievements).",
  "Galatians 5:24": "And they that are Christ's have crucified the flesh with the affections and lusts (the verb is already past tense -- this is described as something done, not something still being attempted).",
  "Galatians 5:25": "If we live in the Spirit, let us also walk in the Spirit (the verse separates having life from living it out -- one is a gift already received, the other still has to be practiced).",
  "Galatians 6:2": "Bear ye one another's burdens, and so fulfil the law of Christ (the law he names here is fulfilled by carrying weight for someone else, not by carrying it alone).",
  "Galatians 6:7": "Be not deceived; God is not mocked: for whatsoever a man soweth, that shall he also reap (this is stated as a fixed law of the field, not a threat -- what goes into the ground decides what comes up).",
  "Galatians 6:8": "For he that soweth to his flesh shall of the flesh reap corruption; but he that soweth to the Spirit shall of the Spirit reap life everlasting (two different fields are pictured side by side, and each one is shown producing only its own crop).",
  "Galatians 6:9": "And let us not be weary in well doing: for in due season we shall reap, if we faint not (the harvest date belongs to the season, not to the one doing the sowing).",
  "Galatians 6:11": "Ye see how large a letter I have written unto you with mine own hand (Paul takes the pen from his scribe for this one -- the size of his own handwriting becomes part of the message).",
  "Galatians 6:14": "But God forbid that I should glory, save in the cross of our Lord Jesus Christ, by whom the world is crucified unto me, and I unto the world (the cross is named as cutting both directions -- it separates him from the world exactly as it separates the world from him).",
  "Galatians 6:15": "For in Christ Jesus neither circumcision availeth any thing, nor uncircumcision, but a new creature (he closes the whole argument by naming what actually counts, after spending five chapters naming what does not).",
  "Galatians 6:17": "From henceforth let no man trouble me: for I bear in my body the marks of the Lord Jesus (he offers his own scars as the only credential he still cares to present).",
  "Ephesians 1:4": "According as he hath chosen us in him before the foundation of the world, that we should be holy and without blame before him in love (the choosing named here is about the purpose set before creation, not a scorecard kept during it):",
  "Ephesians 1:5": "Having predestinated us unto the adoption of children by Jesus Christ to himself, according to the good pleasure of his will (what was predestined is the way a person becomes a child, not a lottery over who qualifies),",
  "Ephesians 1:7": "In whom we have redemption through his blood, the forgiveness of sins, according to the riches of his grace (redemption and forgiveness are named in the same breath, priced by the same blood);",
  "Ephesians 1:11": "In whom also we have obtained an inheritance, being predestinated according to the purpose of him who worketh all things after the counsel of his own will (an inheritance obtained, not earned, decided by a will that answers to nothing outside itself):",
  "Ephesians 1:13": "In whom ye also trusted, after that ye heard the word of truth, the gospel of your salvation: in whom also after that ye believed, ye were sealed with that holy Spirit of promise (the sealing follows hearing and believing -- the order in the sentence is never reversed),",
  "Ephesians 1:18": "The eyes of your understanding being enlightened; that ye may know what is the hope of his calling, and what the riches of the glory of his inheritance in the saints (the inheritance named here belongs to God, and the saints are what he inherited),",
  "Ephesians 1:19": "And what is the exceeding greatness of his power to us-ward who believe, according to the working of his mighty power (the power available to a believer is not left undefined -- the next verse measures it against something specific),",
  "Ephesians 1:20": "Which he wrought in Christ, when he raised him from the dead, and set him at his own right hand in the heavenly places (the measuring stick for that power is the power that emptied a tomb),",
  "Ephesians 1:22": "And hath put all things under his feet, and gave him to be the head over all things to the church (the same authority placed under his feet is the authority given over the church),",
  "Ephesians 2:1": "And you hath he quickened, who were dead in trespasses and sins; (the word chosen for the starting condition is death, not sickness or weakness)",
  "Ephesians 2:4": "But God, who is rich in mercy, for his great love wherewith he loved us (the sentence names the cause before it names the result -- love comes first on the page, the same as it did in fact),",
  "Ephesians 2:5": "Even when we were dead in sins, hath quickened us together with Christ, (by grace ye are saved;) (the parenthesis interrupts the sentence to say the one thing that could not wait until it finished),",
  "Ephesians 2:6": "And hath raised us up together, and made us sit together in heavenly places in Christ Jesus (the seat described is not a future promise -- both verbs are already past tense):",
  "Ephesians 2:8": "For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God (three phrases in a row, and each one rules out a different way a person might try to take credit):",
  "Ephesians 2:9": "Not of works, lest any man should boast (the reason works are excluded is stated plainly here -- not because they are evil, but because they would hand someone something to boast about).",
  "Ephesians 2:10": "For we are his workmanship, created in Christ Jesus unto good works, which God hath before ordained that we should walk in them (good works are placed after salvation in this sentence, never before it -- the order carries the whole argument).",
  "Ephesians 2:13": "But now in Christ Jesus ye who sometimes were far off are made nigh by the blood of Christ (distance and nearness are both measured from the same point -- the blood, not the effort of whoever is traveling).",
  "Ephesians 2:14": "For he is our peace, who hath made both one, and hath broken down the middle wall of partition between us (the wall is described as broken down, not renegotiated or made easier to see through);",
  "Ephesians 2:18": "For through him we both have access by one Spirit unto the Father (one access, one Spirit, and the word both makes sure no second route gets implied).",
  "Ephesians 2:19": "Now therefore ye are no more strangers and foreigners, but fellowcitizens with the saints, and of the household of God (citizenship and household membership are both named here -- neither one describes a visitor);",
  "Ephesians 2:20": "And are built upon the foundation of the apostles and prophets, Jesus Christ himself being the chief corner stone (a foundation only holds together because of what sits at the corner, not because of everything stacked on top of it);",
  "Ephesians 3:1": "For this cause I Paul, the prisoner of Jesus Christ for you Gentiles, (he names his jailer as Christ, not Rome, before he has said a word about the actual circumstances)",
  "Ephesians 3:6": "That the Gentiles should be fellowheirs, and of the same body, and partakers of his promise in Christ by the gospel (three separate words for belonging, stacked on top of each other, as if one alone would not have made the point):",
  "Ephesians 3:8": "Unto me, who am less than the least of all saints, is this grace given, that I should preach among the Gentiles the unsearchable riches of Christ (he calls himself the least of all saints in the same sentence where he is handed the largest assignment in the letter);",
  "Ephesians 3:12": "In whom we have boldness and access with confidence by the faith of him (boldness and access are named as a pair -- neither one shows up in this sentence alone).",
  "Ephesians 3:16": "That he would grant you, according to the riches of his glory, to be strengthened with might by his Spirit in the inner man (the strengthening in view happens in the one part of a person nobody else can see);",
  "Ephesians 3:17": "That Christ may dwell in your hearts by faith; that ye, being rooted and grounded in love (rooted and grounded name two different kinds of stability -- one below ground, one built on top of it),",
  "Ephesians 3:19": "And to know the love of Christ, which passeth knowledge, that ye might be filled with all the fulness of God (he prays for something to be known that he has just called unknowable, and never treats that as a contradiction).",
  "Ephesians 3:20": "Now unto him that is able to do exceeding abundantly above all that we ask or think, according to the power that worketh in us (the ceiling named here is not what we ask for -- it is what we cannot even think to ask),",
  "Ephesians 4:3": "Endeavouring to keep the unity of the Spirit in the bond of peace (unity is described as something to keep, which means it already exists and was never something to build from nothing).",
  "Ephesians 4:6": "One God and Father of all, who is above all, and through all, and in you all (three different prepositions describe three different relationships to the same one God, not three gods).",
  "Ephesians 4:7": "But unto every one of us is given grace according to the measure of the gift of Christ (the measure is not identical from person to person, but the source it gets measured from is).",
  "Ephesians 4:11": "And he gave some, apostles; and some, prophets; and some, evangelists; and some, pastors and teachers (one gift divided into several roles, rather than the same role handed to everyone);",
  "Ephesians 4:13": "Till we all come in the unity of the faith, and of the knowledge of the Son of God, unto a perfect man, unto the measure of the stature of the fulness of Christ (maturity is described here as a shared destination, not a private achievement):",
  "Ephesians 4:15": "But speaking the truth in love, may grow up into him in all things, which is the head, even Christ (truth and love are named together on purpose -- either one without the other stalls the growth being described):",
  "Ephesians 4:22": "That ye put off concerning the former conversation the old man, which is corrupt according to the deceitful lusts (the corruption described is a process still in motion, not a state that already finished);",
  "Ephesians 4:23": "And be renewed in the spirit of your mind (the renewing described is continuous, not a single event already completed);",
  "Ephesians 4:24": "And that ye put on the new man, which after God is created in righteousness and true holiness (putting off and putting on are pictured as one motion, not two separate projects scheduled for two separate days).",
  "Ephesians 4:26": "Be ye angry, and sin not: let not the sun go down upon your wrath (anger itself is never forbidden here -- only the sunset is given as a deadline):",
  "Ephesians 4:29": "Let no corrupt communication proceed out of your mouth, but that which is good to the use of edifying, that it may minister grace unto the hearers (speech is measured here by what it builds in the listener, not by what it releases in the speaker).",
  "Ephesians 4:30": "And grieve not the holy Spirit of God, whereby ye are sealed unto the day of redemption (the seal and the grief are named in the same sentence -- one describes permanence, the other describes what it feels like to ignore him anyway).",
  "Ephesians 4:32": "And be ye kind one to another, tenderhearted, forgiving one another, even as God for Christ’s sake hath forgiven you (the standard set for forgiving others is not ordinary decency, it is the forgiveness already received).",
  "Ephesians 5:2": "And walk in love, as Christ also hath loved us, and hath given himself for us an offering and a sacrifice to God for a sweetsmelling savour (the instruction to walk in love is grounded in an example already finished, not a standard invented for the occasion).",
  "Ephesians 5:6": "Let no man deceive you with vain words: for because of these things cometh the wrath of God upon the children of disobedience (the same phrase, children of disobedience, named what every believer used to be two chapters earlier -- not a status anyone can slide back into by accident).",
  "Ephesians 5:8": "For ye were sometimes darkness, but now are ye light in the Lord: walk as children of light (the verse does not say you were in darkness -- it says you were darkness, and now something has actually changed what you are):",
  "Ephesians 5:14": "Wherefore he saith, Awake thou that sleepest, and arise from the dead, and Christ shall give thee light (the command to wake up is followed at once by the promise of light, as though the waking and the light arrive together).",
  "Ephesians 5:18": "And be not drunk with wine, wherein is excess; but be filled with the Spirit (one kind of being filled is set against another, as if the real question was never about the wine);",
  "Ephesians 5:21": "Submitting yourselves one to another in the fear of God (the submission described runs in every direction at once, not down a single chain of command).",
  "Ephesians 5:25": "Husbands, love your wives, even as Christ also loved the church, and gave himself for it (the standard given to husbands is not affection, it is a love that already gave something up);",
  "Ephesians 5:31": "For this cause shall a man leave his father and mother, and shall be joined unto his wife, and they two shall be one flesh (leaving one household and joining another is the picture chosen, not simply adding a relationship alongside the old one).",
  "Ephesians 5:32": "This is a great mystery: but I speak concerning Christ and the church (he has been describing a marriage for thirty verses and only says now what it was actually about).",
  "Ephesians 6:2": "Honour thy father and mother; (which is the first commandment with promise;) (Paul stops mid-sentence to point out that this is the first command in the list with a reward attached to it).",
  "Ephesians 6:4": "And, ye fathers, provoke not your children to wrath: but bring them up in the nurture and admonition of the Lord (the negative instruction and the positive one are given in the same breath -- neither is complete without the other).",
  "Ephesians 6:5": "Servants, be obedient to them that are your masters according to the flesh, with fear and trembling, in singleness of your heart, as unto Christ (the phrase according to the flesh quietly limits how far that authority actually reaches);",
  "Ephesians 6:6": "Not with eyeservice, as menpleasers; but as the servants of Christ, doing the will of God from the heart (the difference described is not in the work being done, it is in who is believed to be watching);",
  "Ephesians 6:8": "Knowing that whatsoever good thing any man doeth, the same shall he receive of the Lord, whether he be bond or free (the reward described answers to the work done, not to the social rank of the one who did it).",
  "Ephesians 6:10": "Finally, my brethren, be strong in the Lord, and in the power of his might (the strength named here is not the reader's own -- it is borrowed twice over, in him, and by his might).",
  "Ephesians 6:12": "For we wrestle not against flesh and blood, but against principalities, against powers, against the rulers of the darkness of this world, against spiritual wickedness in high places (the opponent named in this verse is never the person standing in front of you).",
  "Ephesians 6:13": "Wherefore take unto you the whole armour of God, that ye may be able to withstand in the evil day, and having done all, to stand (the goal named at the end of the verse is not to take new ground, only to still be standing on the ground already held).",
  "Ephesians 6:17": "And take the helmet of salvation, and the sword of the Spirit, which is the word of God (every other piece named in this chapter is defensive -- this is the only one built to be used against something):",
  "Ephesians 6:18": "Praying always with all prayer and supplication in the Spirit, and watching thereunto with all perseverance and supplication for all saints (the armour list ends, and the very next thing named is prayer, as if it were simply one more piece of the same equipment);",
  "Philippians 1:1": "Paul and Timotheus, the servants of Jesus Christ, to all the saints in Christ Jesus which are at Philippi, with the bishops and deacons (the word translated servants describes ownership, not employment -- love is named as what binds them, not duty):",
  "Philippians 1:6": "Being confident of this very thing, that he which hath begun a good work in you will perform it until the day of Jesus Christ (the one who started the work is also the one credited with finishing it -- the verse never hands that job to the reader):",
  "Philippians 1:9": "And this I pray, that your love may abound yet more and more in knowledge and in all judgment (love is not left here to run on sentiment alone -- it is paired with knowledge and discernment, as though love without either would miss its target);",
  "Philippians 1:11": "Being filled with the fruits of righteousness, which are by Jesus Christ, unto the glory and praise of God (the fruit is named as coming through Christ, and the glory it produces is aimed back at God, not at whoever grew it).",
  "Philippians 1:19": "For I know that this shall turn to my salvation through your prayer, and the supply of the Spirit of Jesus Christ (the word translated salvation here carries the sense of deliverance -- Paul is expecting release from prison, not writing about eternity),",
  "Philippians 1:20": "According to my earnest expectation and my hope, that in nothing I shall be ashamed, but that with all boldness, as always, so now also Christ shall be magnified in my body, whether it be by life, or by death (the outcome he actually names has nothing to do with which way his trial goes -- only with whether Christ is magnified either way).",
  "Philippians 1:21": "For to me to live is Christ, and to die is gain (he names living and dying as two different kinds of gain, not a good option set against a tragic one).",
  "Philippians 1:23": "For I am in a strait betwixt two, having a desire to depart, and to be with Christ; which is far better (the two things pulling at him are not life and death -- they are staying with people he loves and going to be with the one he loves most):",
  "Philippians 1:27": "Only let your conversation be as it becometh the gospel of Christ (the word translated conversation covers the whole of a life, not just what is said out loud):",
  "Philippians 1:28": "And in nothing terrified by your adversaries: which is to them an evident token of perdition, but to you of salvation, and that of God (the same event reads as one sign to your enemies and the opposite sign to you, and Paul says both readings are correct).",
  "Philippians 1:29": "For unto you it is given in the behalf of Christ, not only to believe on him, but also to suffer for his sake (belief and suffering are named here as a matched pair, both handed to the reader as a gift, not just the first one);",
  "Philippians 2:3": "Let nothing be done through strife or vainglory; but in lowliness of mind let each esteem other better than themselves (the standard set here is not modesty about yourself -- it is genuine estimation of someone else).",
  "Philippians 2:6": "Who, being in the form of God, thought it not robbery to be equal with God (equality with God is described here as something he already possessed, not something he reached for):",
  "Philippians 2:7": "But made himself of no reputation, and took upon him the form of a servant, and was made in the likeness of men (the emptying described is voluntary -- nothing external forced the trade):",
  "Philippians 2:8": "And being found in fashion as a man, he humbled himself, and became obedient unto death, even the death of the cross (the sentence does not stop at death -- it specifies which death, as if the method mattered as much as the fact).",
  "Philippians 2:9": "Wherefore God also hath highly exalted him, and given him a name which is above every name (the exaltation follows the humbling in the very next verse, with no gap left between them):",
  "Philippians 2:10": "That at the name of Jesus every knee should bow, of things in heaven, and things in earth, and things under the earth (the verse accounts for every possible location a knee could be found in, and leaves none of them exempt);",
  "Philippians 2:12": "Wherefore, my beloved, as ye have always obeyed, not as in my presence only, but now much more in my absence, work out your own salvation with fear and trembling (the growth in view here is maturity, not the earning of a standing that was already given).",
  "Philippians 2:13": "For it is God which worketh in you both to will and to do of his good pleasure (both the desire and the follow-through are credited to the same source in this sentence).",
  "Philippians 2:15": "That ye may be blameless and harmless, the sons of God, without rebuke, in the midst of a crooked and perverse nation, among whom ye shine as lights in the world (the light is not something added to the darkness around it -- it only shows up as light because of the contrast);",
  "Philippians 2:16": "Holding forth the word of life; that I may rejoice in the day of Christ, that I have not run in vain, neither laboured in vain (he names the same fear twice, in running and in labouring, as though one image alone would not cover it).",
  "Philippians 2:21": "For all seek their own, not the things which are Jesus Christ’s (the complaint is not that these men are wicked -- it is that their interests and Christ’s have quietly stopped overlapping).",
  "Philippians 2:27": "For indeed he was sick nigh unto death: but God had mercy on him; and not on him only, but on me also, lest I should have sorrow upon sorrow (the mercy shown to one man is described as sparing a second man a grief he never even had to feel).",
  "Philippians 3:2": "Beware of dogs, beware of evil workers, beware of the concision (the same root word for circumcision gets used twice in this passage, once for the true thing and once for its counterfeit).",
  "Philippians 3:3": "For we are the circumcision, which worship God in the spirit, and rejoice in Christ Jesus, and have no confidence in the flesh (the title his opponents were fighting over is handed instead to the people who never brought it up).",
  "Philippians 3:7": "But what things were gain to me, those I counted loss for Christ (the same items get relabeled within one sentence -- what used to be entered as profit is now filed under loss).",
  "Philippians 3:8": "Yea doubtless, and I count all things but loss for the excellency of the knowledge of Christ Jesus my Lord: for whom I have suffered the loss of all things, and do count them but dung, that I may win Christ,",
  "Philippians 3:9": "And be found in him, not having mine own righteousness, which is of the law, but that which is through the faith of Christ, the righteousness which is of God by faith (two different righteousnesses are named and set aside in the same breath -- his own, and the law’s -- before a third one is finally named as the one he actually wants):",
  "Philippians 3:12": "Not as though I had already attained, either were already perfect: but I follow after, if that I may apprehend that for which also I am apprehended of Christ Jesus (Paul denies having arrived, in the very letter where he is being held up as the example to follow).",
  "Philippians 3:13": "Brethren, I count not myself to have apprehended: but this one thing I do, forgetting those things which are behind, and reaching forth unto those things which are before,",
  "Philippians 3:14": "I press toward the mark for the prize of the high calling of God in Christ Jesus (the goal is described as something he is still moving toward, not something already reached).",
  "Philippians 3:18": "(For many walk, of whom I have told you often, and now tell you even weeping, that they are the enemies of the cross of Christ: (the people he weeps over here are named as enemies of the cross, not stragglers who wandered a little from it)",
  "Philippians 3:19": "Whose end is destruction, whose God is their belly, and whose glory is in their shame, who mind earthly things.) (three things are listed as what these people actually worship -- their appetite, their shame, and the earth, in that order.)",
  "Philippians 3:20": "For our conversation is in heaven; from whence also we look for the Saviour, the Lord Jesus Christ (the citizenship named here is present tense, not a future arrangement still being processed):",
  "Philippians 3:21": "Who shall change our vile body, that it may be fashioned like unto his glorious body, according to the working whereby he is able even to subdue all things unto himself (the same power that subdues all things is the power used to remake one body).",
  "Philippians 4:4": "Rejoice in the Lord alway: and again I say, Rejoice (the command is repeated once, immediately, as if Paul expected the first instance to be doubted).",
  "Philippians 4:6": "Be careful for nothing; but in every thing by prayer and supplication with thanksgiving let your requests be made known unto God (nothing is the size of concern this verse rules out, and everything is the size of concern it hands to prayer instead).",
  "Philippians 4:7": "And the peace of God, which passeth all understanding, shall keep your hearts and minds through Christ Jesus (the peace described here is not the absence of a problem -- it is a guard posted around the heart while the problem is still there).",
  "Philippians 4:8": "Finally, brethren, whatsoever things are true, whatsoever things are honest, whatsoever things are just, whatsoever things are pure, whatsoever things are lovely, whatsoever things are of good report; if there be any virtue, and if there be any praise, think on these things (the list names eight different categories, and every one of them describes a thought the reader is free to choose).",
  "Philippians 4:11": "Not that I speak in respect of want: for I have learned, in whatsoever state I am, therewith to be content (the word learned marks this as something Paul acquired over time, not a temperament he happened to be born with).",
  "Philippians 4:12": "I know both how to be abased, and I know how to abound: every where and in all things I am instructed both to be full and to be hungry, both to abound and to suffer need (both directions get named as things he had to be taught -- plenty is not assumed to come naturally either).",
  "Philippians 4:13": "I can do all things through Christ which strengtheneth me (the strength named in this verse is borrowed, not built -- the sentence never claims an ability that stands apart from its source).",
  "Philippians 4:17": "Not because I desire a gift: but I desire fruit that may abound to your account (Paul separates what he wants from what he is willing to receive, and names only the first one as his actual motive).",
  "Philippians 4:19": "But my God shall supply all your need according to his riches in glory by Christ Jesus (the supply promised is measured against God’s riches, not against the size of the need itself).",
  "Colossians 1:2": "To the saints and faithful brethren in Christ which are at Colosse: Grace be unto you, and peace, from God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ (peace here is named as something that travels with grace, not something worked up separately).",
  "Colossians 1:6": "Which is come unto you, as it is in all the world; and bringeth forth fruit, as it doth also in you, since the day ye heard of it, and knew the grace of God in truth (the same gospel is described producing the same fruit everywhere it lands, Colosse included):",
  "Colossians 1:9": "For this cause we also, since the day we heard it, do not cease to pray for you, and to desire that ye might be filled with the knowledge of his will in all wisdom and spiritual understanding (the request named is for his will specifically, not a general upgrade in wisdom);",
  "Colossians 1:10": "That ye might walk worthy of the Lord unto all pleasing, being fruitful in every good work, and increasing in the knowledge of God (the fruit and the growth are described flowing out of the walk, not producing it);",
  "Colossians 1:12": "Giving thanks unto the Father, which hath made us meet to be partakers of the inheritance of the saints in light (the qualifying is stated as something already done to the reader, not a bar still being cleared):",
  "Colossians 1:13": "Who hath delivered us from the power of darkness, and hath translated us into the kingdom of his dear Son (both verbs are past tense -- a transfer already completed, not one still underway):",
  "Colossians 1:14": "In whom we have redemption through his blood, even the forgiveness of sins (the sentence defines its own key term in the very next clause, so no ambiguity is left about what price was paid):",
  "Colossians 1:15": "Who is the image of the invisible God, the firstborn of every creature (firstborn here names rank, not birth order -- everything that exists came after him, not before):",
  "Colossians 1:16": "For by him were all things created, that are in heaven, and that are in earth, visible and invisible, whether they be thrones, or dominions, or principalities, or powers: all things were created by him, and for him (creation is described as both starting with him and existing for his sake, not merely passing through his hands):",
  "Colossians 1:17": "And he is before all things, and by him all things consist (the universe is held together by the same person who made it, not left to run on its own once built).",
  "Colossians 1:18": "And he is the head of the body, the church: who is the beginning, the firstborn from the dead; that in all things he might have the preeminence (the resurrection is named as making him first in rank, not merely first in time).",
  "Colossians 1:20": "And, having made peace through the blood of his cross, by him to reconcile all things unto himself; by him, I say, whether they be things in earth, or things in heaven (the peace and the reconciliation are both traced back to the same single event).",
  "Colossians 1:21": "And you, that were sometime alienated and enemies in your mind by wicked works, yet now hath he reconciled (the distance described here was never physical -- it was in the mind, and it closes the same way it opened)",
  "Colossians 1:22": "In the body of his flesh through death, to present you holy and unblameable and unreproveable in his sight (three different words are stacked up here, as if one alone could not describe how thoroughly the verdict changed):",
  "Colossians 1:24": "Who now rejoice in my sufferings for you, and fill up that which is behind of the afflictions of Christ in my flesh for his body’s sake, which is the church (Paul names joy as his actual response to a kind of suffering most people would only tolerate):",
  "Colossians 1:27": "To whom God would make known what is the riches of the glory of this mystery among the Gentiles; which is Christ in you, the hope of glory (the mystery kept hidden for ages turns out to be a location -- Christ, inside the reader):",
  "Colossians 1:28": "Whom we preach, warning every man, and teaching every man in all wisdom; that we may present every man perfect in Christ Jesus (the goal named is maturity in someone else, not converts counted):",
  "Colossians 1:29": "Whereunto I also labour, striving according to his working, which worketh in me mightily (Paul describes his own effort as powered by a working that is not his).",
  "Colossians 2:2": "That their hearts might be comforted, being knit together in love, and unto all riches of the full assurance of understanding, to the acknowledgement of the mystery of God, and of the Father, and of Christ (the unity named here is the means, not the goal -- assurance is what it is supposed to produce);",
  "Colossians 2:3": "In whom are hid all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge (the treasures are not scattered for the finding -- they are all deposited in one place).",
  "Colossians 2:6": "As ye have therefore received Christ Jesus the Lord, so walk ye in him (the walk is instructed to match the beginning -- by the same means the reader came in):",
  "Colossians 2:7": "Rooted and built up in him, and stablished in the faith, as ye have been taught, abounding therein with thanksgiving (three different images -- roots, a building, a foundation -- are used for the same idea, as if one metaphor could not hold it).",
  "Colossians 2:8": "Beware lest any man spoil you through philosophy and vain deceit, after the tradition of men, after the rudiments of the world, and not after Christ (the warning names three different sources for the danger, and only one alternative).",
  "Colossians 2:9": "For in him dwelleth all the fulness of the Godhead bodily (the fullness is not distributed or partial -- it is described as entirely present in a body).",
  "Colossians 2:10": "And ye are complete in him, which is the head of all principality and power (completeness is stated as a fact about the reader’s position, not a project still underway):",
  "Colossians 2:11": "In whom also ye are circumcised with the circumcision made without hands, in putting off the body of the sins of the flesh by the circumcision of Christ (the cutting away described here was never performed by human hands):",
  "Colossians 2:12": "Buried with him in baptism, wherein also ye are risen with him through the faith of the operation of God, who hath raised him from the dead (burial and rising are named together, in the same sentence, with no gap left between them).",
  "Colossians 2:13": "And you, being dead in your sins and the uncircumcision of your flesh, hath he quickened together with him, having forgiven you all trespasses (the forgiveness is not held back until life is proven -- it comes bundled with the being made alive);",
  "Colossians 2:14": "Blotting out the handwriting of ordinances that was against us, which was contrary to us, and took it out of the way, nailing it to his cross (the record that stood against the reader is described as nailed up in the same place the reader’s guilt was dealt with);",
  "Colossians 2:15": "And having spoiled principalities and powers, he made a shew of them openly, triumphing over them in it (the defeat described is staged in public, not settled quietly).",
  "Colossians 2:16": "Let no man therefore judge you in meat, or in drink, or in respect of an holyday, or of the new moon, or of the sabbath days (the list covers four different categories of religious rule, and rules out judgment on all of them at once):",
  "Colossians 2:17": "Which are a shadow of things to come; but the body is of Christ (the shadow and the substance are named side by side, and only one of them is called real).",
  "Colossians 2:20": "Wherefore if ye be dead with Christ from the rudiments of the world, why, as though living in the world, are ye subject to ordinances (the question points out a contradiction -- submitting again to rules for a life the reader is said to have already left),",
  "Colossians 3:1": "If ye then be risen with Christ, seek those things which are above, where Christ sitteth on the right hand of God (the seeking commanded here follows an already-accomplished rising, not the other way around).",
  "Colossians 3:3": "For ye are dead, and your life is hid with Christ in God (the life described is not missing -- it is hidden, kept in the safest place available).",
  "Colossians 3:4": "When Christ, who is our life, shall appear, then shall ye also appear with him in glory (the reader’s appearing is tied directly to his -- one event, not two).",
  "Colossians 3:5": "Mortify therefore your members which are upon the earth; fornication, uncleanness, inordinate affection, evil concupiscence, and covetousness, which is idolatry (five things are named, and the last one is given a category no one would have guessed on their own):",
  "Colossians 3:6": "For which things’ sake the wrath of God cometh on the children of disobedience (the same condition was already named in chapter one as the reader’s own past -- alienated, enemies in mind by wicked works -- not a future threat):",
  "Colossians 3:7": "In the which ye also walked some time, when ye lived in them (the same list just given is now described in the past tense, as a place the reader used to live).",
  "Colossians 3:9": "Lie not one to another, seeing that ye have put off the old man with his deeds (the lying singled out here is aimed at yourself as much as anyone else -- the old man’s habits die hardest in private);",
  "Colossians 3:10": "And have put on the new man, which is renewed in knowledge after the image of him that created him (the renewal described is modeled on the original -- the same pattern used at creation):",
  "Colossians 3:11": "Where there is neither Greek nor Jew, circumcision nor uncircumcision, Barbarian, Scythian, bond nor free: but Christ is all, and in all (the list empties out every category that used to matter, and fills the space with one thing instead).",
  "Colossians 3:12": "Put on therefore, as the elect of God, holy and beloved, bowels of mercies, kindness, humbleness of mind, meekness, longsuffering (the list of what to put on begins with who the reader already is, not what they still need to become);",
  "Colossians 3:13": "Forbearing one another, and forgiving one another, if any man have a quarrel against any: even as Christ forgave you, so also do ye (the standard set for forgiving someone else is the forgiveness already received, not a lesser version of it).",
  "Colossians 3:14": "And above all these things put on charity, which is the bond of perfectness (charity is placed above the whole list, as the one thing holding all the others together).",
  "Colossians 3:15": "And let the peace of God rule in your hearts, to the which also ye are called in one body; and be ye thankful (the peace is told to rule, using the same word used elsewhere for a governing authority).",
  "Colossians 3:16": "Let the word of Christ dwell in you richly in all wisdom; teaching and admonishing one another in psalms and hymns and spiritual songs, singing with grace in your hearts to the Lord (the same word is described teaching the reader and then being sung back out again).",
  "Colossians 3:17": "And whatsoever ye do in word or deed, do all in the name of the Lord Jesus, giving thanks to God and the Father by him (the instruction covers everything a person could possibly say or do, with no category left outside it).",
  "Colossians 3:23": "And whatsoever ye do, do it heartily, as to the Lord, and not unto men (the audience named for the work is switched -- not the master standing in the room, but the one who is not);",
  "Colossians 3:24": "Knowing that of the Lord ye shall receive the reward of the inheritance: for ye serve the Lord Christ (an inheritance is not usually called a reward -- this verse calls it both at once).",
  "Colossians 4:2": "Continue in prayer, and watch in the same with thanksgiving (watchfulness is paired with prayer here, as though prayer alone could quietly stop without anyone noticing);",
  "Colossians 4:3": "Withal praying also for us, that God would open unto us a door of utterance, to speak the mystery of Christ, for which I am also in bonds (Paul asks for an open door while writing from a locked one):",
  "Colossians 4:5": "Walk in wisdom toward them that are without, redeeming the time (the wisdom named here is aimed specifically at outsiders, not at fellow believers).",
  "Colossians 4:6": "Let your speech be alway with grace, seasoned with salt, that ye may know how ye ought to answer every man (two different qualities are named for speech, as if grace alone could still turn bland).",
  "Colossians 4:12": "Epaphras, who is one of you, a servant of Christ, saluteth you, always labouring fervently for you in prayers, that ye may stand perfect and complete in all the will of God (his labor is described taking place entirely out of sight, in prayer alone).",
  "Colossians 4:17": "And say to Archippus, Take heed to the ministry which thou hast received in the Lord, that thou fulfil it (the reminder is delivered secondhand, through the whole church reading the letter, not privately).",
  "Colossians 4:18": "The salutation by the hand of me Paul. Remember my bonds. Grace be with you. Amen (the letter ends the way it began, with grace, but not before asking to be remembered in chains). Written from Rome to the Colossians by Tychicus and Onesimus.",
  "1 Thessalonians 1:1": "Paul, and Silvanus, and Timotheus, unto the church of the Thessalonians which is in God the Father and in the Lord Jesus Christ: Grace be unto you, and peace, from God our Father, and the Lord Jesus Christ (this letter opens with the first record of that greeting -- grace and peace -- attached to a church, not just a person).",
  "1 Thessalonians 1:3": "Remembering without ceasing your work of faith, and labour of love, and patience of hope in our Lord Jesus Christ, in the sight of God and our Father (the same three -- faith, love, hope -- that Paul later famously pairs together in a single verse to the Corinthians show up here first, already linked);",
  "1 Thessalonians 1:5": "For our gospel came not unto you in word only, but also in power, and in the Holy Ghost, and in much assurance; as ye know what manner of men we were among you for your sake (the gospel is described arriving with three things attached to the words -- power, the Spirit, and assurance -- not word alone).",
  "1 Thessalonians 1:6": "And ye became followers of us, and of the Lord, having received the word in much affliction, with joy of the Holy Ghost (the affliction and the joy are named in the same breath, without either one canceling out the other):",
  "1 Thessalonians 1:9": "For they themselves shew of us what manner of entering in we had unto you, and how ye turned to God from idols to serve the living and true God (the report already spreading about them describes something Paul never had to advertise himself);",
  "1 Thessalonians 1:10": "And to wait for his Son from heaven, whom he raised from the dead, even Jesus, which delivered us from the wrath to come (the deliverance from coming wrath is stated as already accomplished, tied to a resurrection that already happened, not a future maybe).",
  "1 Thessalonians 2:4": "But as we were allowed of God to be put in trust with the gospel, even so we speak; not as pleasing men, but God, which trieth our hearts (the audience he says he is speaking to is not the room in front of him).",
  "1 Thessalonians 2:8": "So being affectionately desirous of you, we were willing to have imparted unto you, not the gospel of God only, but also our own souls, because ye were dear unto us (what he gave them is named in two parts -- the message, and then himself).",
  "1 Thessalonians 2:9": "For ye remember, brethren, our labour and travail: for labouring night and day, because we would not be chargeable unto any of you, we preached unto you the gospel of God (the free gift he preached came at a cost he made sure never landed on them).",
  "1 Thessalonians 2:13": "For this cause also thank we God without ceasing, because, when ye received the word of God which ye heard of us, ye received it not as the word of men, but as it is in truth, the word of God, which effectually worketh also in you that believe (the word is described as active, not stored -- working in the ones who believe, not merely held by them).",
  "1 Thessalonians 2:17": "But we, brethren, being taken from you for a short time in presence, not in heart, endeavoured the more abundantly to see your face with great desire (he draws a line between two kinds of absence -- one is physical, the other never happened).",
  "1 Thessalonians 2:19": "For what is our hope, or joy, or crown of rejoicing? Are not even ye in the presence of our Lord Jesus Christ at his coming (he answers his own question about what his reward will be by naming the very people he is writing to)?",
  "1 Thessalonians 3:5": "For this cause, when I could no longer forbear, I sent to know your faith, lest by some means the tempter have tempted you, and our labour be in vain (the worry named here is for their endurance under pressure, not for their standing before God).",
  "1 Thessalonians 3:8": "For now we live, if ye stand fast in the Lord (Paul ties his own sense of being alive to their steadiness, not the other way around).",
  "1 Thessalonians 3:13": "To the end he may stablish your hearts unblameable in holiness before God, even our Father, at the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ with all his saints (the request moves from love between believers now to being found blameless at a future coming -- one is asked to produce the other).",
  "1 Thessalonians 4:1": "Furthermore then we beseech you, brethren, and exhort you by the Lord Jesus, that as ye have received of us how ye ought to walk and to please God, so ye would abound more and more (the instruction is to do more of what they are already doing, not to start over).",
  "1 Thessalonians 4:3": "For this is the will of God, even your sanctification, that ye should abstain from fornication (the will of God is named here as a person’s sanctification, not a rule kept for its own sake):",
  "1 Thessalonians 4:9": "But as touching brotherly love ye need not that I write unto you: for ye yourselves are taught of God to love one another (Paul says he does not need to teach them something God is already teaching them directly).",
  "1 Thessalonians 4:11": "And that ye study to be quiet, and to do your own business, and to work with your own hands, as we commanded you (ambition here is redirected toward quietness, the opposite of what ambition usually chases);",
  "1 Thessalonians 4:13": "But I would not have you to be ignorant, brethren, concerning them which are asleep, that ye sorrow not, even as others which have no hope (grief itself is not forbidden here -- only the kind that has nothing on the other side of it).",
  "1 Thessalonians 4:14": "For if we believe that Jesus died and rose again, even so them also which sleep in Jesus will God bring with him (the argument runs from one resurrection already accomplished to another one still to come).",
  "1 Thessalonians 4:16": "For the Lord himself shall descend from heaven with a shout, with the voice of the archangel, and with the trump of God: and the dead in Christ shall rise first (the dead are named as rising first -- ahead of the living, not behind them):",
  "1 Thessalonians 4:17": "Then we which are alive and remain shall be caught up together with them in the clouds, to meet the Lord in the air: and so shall we ever be with the Lord (the chapter that opened with instructions on how to live ends with a promise that has nothing to do with behavior at all).",
  "1 Thessalonians 4:18": "Wherefore comfort one another with these words (the entire section on the resurrection of the dead closes with one instruction -- use it to comfort, not to calculate).",
  "1 Thessalonians 5:2": "For yourselves know perfectly that the day of the Lord so cometh as a thief in the night (the comparison is to timing, not danger -- a thief’s defining trait here is that no one sees him coming).",
  "1 Thessalonians 5:4": "But ye, brethren, are not in darkness, that that day should overtake you as a thief (the same image used as a warning in the previous verse is turned around here -- what surprises one group does not surprise the other).",
  "1 Thessalonians 5:5": "Ye are all the children of light, and the children of the day: we are not of the night, nor of darkness (the identity is stated first, twice, before anything is asked of the reader).",
  "1 Thessalonians 5:8": "But let us, who are of the day, be sober, putting on the breastplate of faith and love; and for an helmet, the hope of salvation (salvation is described here as something already possessed and worn, not something being earned piece by piece).",
  "1 Thessalonians 5:9": "For God hath not appointed us to wrath, but to obtain salvation by our Lord Jesus Christ (the appointment named is not a possibility being offered, it is a destination already assigned),",
  "1 Thessalonians 5:10": "Who died for us, that, whether we wake or sleep, we should live together with him (the outcome, life together with him, is not made to depend on which condition -- awake or asleep -- the reader happens to be in).",
  "1 Thessalonians 5:14": "Now we exhort you, brethren, warn them that are unruly, comfort the feebleminded, support the weak, be patient toward all men (four different people get four different responses -- the same word, patient, is not the one applied to all of them).",
  "1 Thessalonians 5:15": "See that none render evil for evil unto any man; but ever follow that which is good, both among yourselves, and to all men (the good required here extends past the community, to everyone, with no exception named).",
  "1 Thessalonians 5:18": "In every thing give thanks: for this is the will of God in Christ Jesus concerning you (the instruction is to give thanks in everything, not for everything -- the difference matters).",
  "1 Thessalonians 5:23": "And the very God of peace sanctify you wholly; and I pray God your whole spirit and soul and body be preserved blameless unto the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ (the request covers spirit, soul, and body, named separately, so nothing about a person is left out of it).",
  "1 Thessalonians 5:24": "Faithful is he that calleth you, who also will do it (the verse that follows a request for God to finish the work ends by naming him, not the reader, as the one faithful to complete it).",
  "2 Thessalonians 1:1": "Paul, and Silvanus, and Timotheus, unto the church of the Thessalonians in God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ (the church's address given here is not a city, but a relationship):",
  "2 Thessalonians 1:2": "Grace unto you, and peace, from God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ (both names are credited as the one source, not two separate suppliers).",
  "2 Thessalonians 1:3": "We are bound to thank God always for you, brethren, as it is meet, because that your faith groweth exceedingly, and the charity of every one of you all toward each other aboundeth (two things are named growing at once -- faith reaching up, and love reaching toward each other);",
  "2 Thessalonians 1:4": "So that we ourselves glory in you in the churches of God for your patience and faith in all your persecutions and tribulations that ye endure (Paul uses them as his example when boasting to other churches, not the other way around):",
  "2 Thessalonians 1:5": "Which is a manifest token of the righteous judgment of God, that ye may be counted worthy of the kingdom of God, for which ye also suffer (the suffering is named as evidence of a coming judgment, not a payment required to reach it):",
  "2 Thessalonians 1:6": "Seeing it is a righteous thing with God to recompense tribulation to them that trouble you (the fairness argued for here runs in both directions -- comfort for the persecuted, reckoning for the persecutors);",
  "2 Thessalonians 1:7": "And to you who are troubled rest with us, when the Lord Jesus shall be revealed from heaven with his mighty angels (the rest promised is tied to a specific event, not to the trouble simply running out on its own),",
  "2 Thessalonians 1:8": "In flaming fire taking vengeance on them that know not God, and that obey not the gospel of our Lord Jesus Christ (two groups are named as one -- those who never knew God, and those who never obeyed the gospel):",
  "2 Thessalonians 1:9": "Who shall be punished with everlasting destruction from the presence of the Lord, and from the glory of his power (the punishment is described as separation from a presence, not merely from a place);",
  "2 Thessalonians 1:10": "When he shall come to be glorified in his saints, and to be admired in all them that believe (because our testimony among you was believed) in that day (Paul inserts his own reason for being included in that day right in the middle of the sentence -- they believed what he told them).",
  "2 Thessalonians 1:11": "Wherefore also we pray always for you, that our God would count you worthy of this calling, and fulfil all the good pleasure of his goodness, and the work of faith with power (the request names God as the one who fulfils and empowers, not the one being negotiated with):",
  "2 Thessalonians 1:12": "That the name of our Lord Jesus Christ may be glorified in you, and ye in him, according to the grace of our God and the Lord Jesus Christ (the glorifying runs both directions in this sentence -- his name in them, and them in him).",
  "2 Thessalonians 2:1": "Now we beseech you, brethren, by the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ, and by our gathering together unto him (two events are named side by side here as reasons for what follows, not as separate topics),",
  "2 Thessalonians 2:2": "That ye be not soon shaken in mind, or be troubled, neither by spirit, nor by word, nor by letter as from us, as that the day of Christ is at hand (he lists three ways a false report could reach them -- a claimed spirit, a claimed word, even a forged letter -- covering every channel at once).",
  "2 Thessalonians 2:3": "Let no man deceive you by any means: for that day shall not come, except there come a falling away first, and that man of sin be revealed, the son of perdition (the day in question is tied to two things happening first, not to a feeling or a rumor);",
  "2 Thessalonians 2:4": "Who opposeth and exalteth himself above all that is called God, or that is worshipped; so that he as God sitteth in the temple of God, shewing himself that he is God (three separate claims are stacked here -- opposing, exalting, and finally sitting in the place reserved for God alone).",
  "2 Thessalonians 2:5": "Remember ye not, that, when I was yet with you, I told you these things (he reminds them this was not new information -- he told them in person, before he ever wrote it down)?",
  "2 Thessalonians 2:6": "And now ye know what withholdeth that he might be revealed in his time (something is actively holding a revealing back -- this is restraint, not delay).",
  "2 Thessalonians 2:7": "For the mystery of iniquity doth already work: only he who now letteth will let, until he be taken out of the way (the restraining force is described as continuing right up until the moment it is removed, not weakening gradually before then).",
  "2 Thessalonians 2:8": "And then shall that Wicked be revealed, whom the Lord shall consume with the spirit of his mouth, and shall destroy with the brightness of his coming (the weapon named against him is not an army, but breath and brightness):",
  "2 Thessalonians 2:9": "Even him, whose coming is after the working of Satan with all power and signs and lying wonders (the power behind his arrival is named outright as Satan's, before anything else is said about him),",
  "2 Thessalonians 2:10": "And with all deceivableness of unrighteousness in them that perish; because they received not the love of the truth, that they might be saved (what condemns them is not an absence of opportunity -- the truth was offered and they simply never loved it).",
  "2 Thessalonians 2:11": "And for this cause God shall send them strong delusion, that they should believe a lie (the delusion arrives after the rejection, as a consequence of it, not as its cause):",
  "2 Thessalonians 2:12": "That they all might be damned who believed not the truth, but had pleasure in unrighteousness (the same group is described twice over -- by what they turned down and by what they chose in its place).",
  "2 Thessalonians 2:13": "But we are bound to give thanks alway to God for you, brethren beloved of the Lord, because God hath from the beginning chosen you to salvation through sanctification of the Spirit and belief of the truth (chosen from the beginning is stated as fact before anything about their own believing is mentioned):",
  "2 Thessalonians 2:14": "Whereunto he called you by our gospel, to the obtaining of the glory of our Lord Jesus Christ (the calling and the goal are named in the same sentence, with nothing in between for the reader to accomplish).",
  "2 Thessalonians 2:15": "Therefore, brethren, stand fast, and hold the traditions which ye have been taught, whether by word, or our epistle (what they are told to hold onto is named as something already given to them, not something still being worked out).",
  "2 Thessalonians 2:16": "Now our Lord Jesus Christ himself, and God, even our Father, which hath loved us, and hath given us everlasting consolation and good hope through grace (the comfort and hope named here are both described as already given, not promised for later),",
  "2 Thessalonians 2:17": "Comfort your hearts, and stablish you in every good word and work (the request moves from an inward comfort to an outward steadiness, in that order).",
  "2 Thessalonians 3:1": "Finally, brethren, pray for us, that the word of the Lord may have free course, and be glorified, even as it is with you (Paul asks them to pray using what already happened among them as the model for what he wants to happen elsewhere):",
  "2 Thessalonians 3:2": "And that we may be delivered from unreasonable and wicked men: for all men have not faith (the reason given for the danger is stated plainly -- not everyone has what Paul is asking them to pray for).",
  "2 Thessalonians 3:3": "But the Lord is faithful, who shall stablish you, and keep you from evil (the faithfulness named belongs to the Lord, right after a verse naming the men who cannot be trusted).",
  "2 Thessalonians 3:4": "And we have confidence in the Lord touching you, that ye both do and will do the things which we command you (the confidence Paul states is placed in the Lord, even though the sentence is about their obedience).",
  "2 Thessalonians 3:5": "And the Lord direct your hearts into the love of God, and into the patient waiting for Christ (the request is for direction, not arrival -- the Lord is asked to guide the hearts, not simply to fill them).",
  "2 Thessalonians 3:6": "Now we command you, brethren, in the name of our Lord Jesus Christ, that ye withdraw yourselves from every brother that walketh disorderly, and not after the tradition which he received of us (what's being withdrawn here is fellowship, not a verdict on where someone stands with God).",
  "2 Thessalonians 3:7": "For yourselves know how ye ought to follow us: for we behaved not ourselves disorderly among you (he points them back to what they already witnessed firsthand, not to a new standard);",
  "2 Thessalonians 3:8": "Neither did we eat any man’s bread for nought; but wrought with labour and travail night and day, that we might not be chargeable to any of you (this is the second time in these two letters he mentions paying his own way -- the point mattered enough to repeat):",
  "2 Thessalonians 3:9": "Not because we have not power, but to make ourselves an ensample unto you to follow us (he clarifies the reason before anyone assumes the wrong one -- not a lack of right, but a choice).",
  "2 Thessalonians 3:10": "For even when we were with you, this we commanded you, that if any would not work, neither should he eat (the command is about eating, not about belonging -- a rule for the table, not a test of standing).",
  "2 Thessalonians 3:11": "For we hear that there are some which walk among you disorderly, working not at all, but are busybodies (idleness and meddling are named together, as if one naturally produces the other).",
  "2 Thessalonians 3:12": "Now them that are such we command and exhort by our Lord Jesus Christ, that with quietness they work, and eat their own bread (the correction aimed at busybodies points toward quietness, not toward more activity).",
  "2 Thessalonians 3:13": "But ye, brethren, be not weary in well doing (the exhortation comes right after two verses about people who would not work at all, as if to guard the rest of the church from growing tired because of them).",
  "2 Thessalonians 3:14": "And if any man obey not our word by this epistle, note that man, and have no company with him, that he may be ashamed (the aim of this withdrawal is named as shame that leads him back, not a shutting of the door).",
  "2 Thessalonians 3:15": "Yet count him not as an enemy, but admonish him as a brother (the label never changes to enemy -- correction happens, but he stays brother throughout).",
  "2 Thessalonians 3:16": "Now the Lord of peace himself give you peace always by all means. The Lord be with you all (the peace requested covers every situation, by every means, with no exception carved out).",
  "2 Thessalonians 3:17": "The salutation of Paul with mine own hand, which is the token in every epistle: so I write (Paul names his own handwriting as proof against forgery, in a letter written partly because of one).",
  "2 Thessalonians 3:18": "The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you all. Amen (the letter closes exactly as it opened -- with grace). The second epistle to the Thessalonians was written from Athens.",
  "1 Timothy 1:1": "Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ by the commandment of God our Saviour, and Lord Jesus Christ, which is our hope (his authority is traced to a command, not to personal ambition);",
  "1 Timothy 1:2": "Unto Timothy, my own son in the faith: Grace, mercy, and peace, from God our Father and Jesus Christ our Lord (grace is named first, then mercy, then peace, as though one leads to the next).",
  "1 Timothy 1:3": "As I besought thee to abide still at Ephesus, when I went into Macedonia, that thou mightest charge some that they teach no other doctrine (the charge is not to add new content, but to stop content that had already been added),",
  "1 Timothy 1:4": "Neither give heed to fables and endless genealogies, which minister questions, rather than godly edifying which is in faith: so do (the alternative he names produces questions, not the edifying he says faith produces).",
  "1 Timothy 1:5": "Now the end of the commandment is charity out of a pure heart, and of a good conscience, and of faith unfeigned (three things are named as the aim of the whole command, and none of them is a rule):",
  "1 Timothy 1:6": "From which some having swerved have turned aside unto vain jangling (the picture used is missing a target, not stepping over a line);",
  "1 Timothy 1:7": "Desiring to be teachers of the law; understanding neither what they say, nor whereof they affirm (they claim expertise in the very subject the verse says they do not understand).",
  "1 Timothy 1:8": "But we know that the law is good, if a man use it lawfully (the law is defended here in the same paragraph that argues against relying on it -- the two claims are not in tension);",
  "1 Timothy 1:9": "Knowing this, that the law is not made for a righteous man, but for the lawless and disobedient, for the ungodly and for sinners, for unholy and profane, for murderers of fathers and murderers of mothers, for manslayers (the list that follows names what the law was written to restrain, not what defines the believer reading it),",
  "1 Timothy 1:10": "For whoremongers, for them that defile themselves with mankind, for menstealers, for liars, for perjured persons, and if there be any other thing that is contrary to sound doctrine (the list closes with a category broad enough to cover anything left out by name);",
  "1 Timothy 1:11": "According to the glorious gospel of the blessed God, which was committed to my trust (the gospel is called glorious before it is called anything else).",
  "1 Timothy 1:12": "And I thank Christ Jesus our Lord, who hath enabled me, for that he counted me faithful, putting me into the ministry (he is counted faithful before any record of his faithfulness could have existed);",
  "1 Timothy 1:13": "Who was before a blasphemer, and a persecutor, and injurious: but I obtained mercy, because I did it ignorantly in unbelief (three titles are listed for what he used to be, and the verse still calls what followed mercy, not luck).",
  "1 Timothy 1:14": "And the grace of our Lord was exceeding abundant with faith and love which is in Christ Jesus (grace is named as abundant before faith and love are even mentioned -- the second two are described as arriving with the first).",
  "1 Timothy 1:15": "This is a faithful saying, and worthy of all acceptation, that Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners; of whom I am chief (he places himself first in the very list he is using to prove mercy reaches everyone else).",
  "1 Timothy 1:16": "Howbeit for this cause I obtained mercy, that in me first Jesus Christ might shew forth all longsuffering, for a pattern to them which should hereafter believe on him to life everlasting (his own case is described as a pattern for everyone who comes after, not an exception made for him alone).",
  "1 Timothy 1:17": "Now unto the King eternal, immortal, invisible, the only wise God, be honour and glory for ever and ever. Amen (four descriptions are stacked up before the sentence ever reaches its verb).",
  "1 Timothy 1:18": "This charge I commit unto thee, son Timothy, according to the prophecies which went before on thee, that thou by them mightest war a good warfare (the charge is tied to something spoken over Timothy earlier, not to a decision made in this moment);",
  "1 Timothy 1:19": "Holding faith, and a good conscience; which some having put away concerning faith have made shipwreck (a wreck, not a slow drift, is the picture used for what happens when this is set aside):",
  "1 Timothy 1:20": "Of whom is Hymeneus and Alexander; whom I have delivered unto Satan, that they may learn not to blaspheme (being handed over is described as corrective, aimed at their learning something, not a final sentence).",
  "1 Timothy 2:1": "I exhort therefore, that, first of all, supplications, prayers, intercessions, and giving of thanks, be made for all men (four kinds of prayer are named before the verse ever says who they are for);",
  "1 Timothy 2:2": "For kings, and for all that are in authority; that we may lead a quiet and peaceable life in all godliness and honesty (the target of these prayers -- kings and authorities -- is named before the reason for praying is given).",
  "1 Timothy 2:4": "Who will have all men to be saved, and to come unto the knowledge of the truth (the desire named here has no exception built into the sentence).",
  "1 Timothy 2:5": "For there is one God, and one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus (the mediator is called a man in the very sentence that puts him between God and men);",
  "1 Timothy 2:6": "Who gave himself a ransom for all, to be testified in due time (the giving is described as his own act, not something done to him).",
  "1 Timothy 2:7": "Whereunto I am ordained a preacher, and an apostle, (I speak the truth in Christ, and lie not;) a teacher of the Gentiles in faith and verity (he interrupts his own sentence to deny an accusation before finishing the thought).",
  "1 Timothy 2:8": "I will therefore that men pray every where, lifting up holy hands, without wrath and doubting (the posture named for prayer is not limited by location -- everywhere is stated outright).",
  "1 Timothy 2:9": "In like manner also, that women adorn themselves in modest apparel, with shamefacedness and sobriety; not with broided hair, or gold, or pearls, or costly array (the items named here are not banned elsewhere in this letter -- what's described is a pattern of priority, not a rule);",
  "1 Timothy 2:13": "For Adam was first formed, then Eve (the order named here is given as the reason, not as incidental detail).",
  "1 Timothy 2:15": "Notwithstanding she shall be saved in childbearing, if they continue in faith and charity and holiness with sobriety (the salvation named here is tied to the promise of protection just described, and to the birth of Christ through Mary -- not to a woman's standing before God).",
  "1 Timothy 3:1": "This is a true saying, If a man desire the office of a bishop, he desireth a good work (wanting the position is called good before anything about the position itself is described).",
  "1 Timothy 3:2": "A bishop then must be blameless, the husband of one wife, vigilant, sober, of good behaviour, given to hospitality, apt to teach (the list that follows describes a present pattern of life, not a resume built once and filed away);",
  "1 Timothy 3:4": "One that ruleth well his own house, having his children in subjection with all gravity (the same word used for running a household is used two verses later for shepherding a church);",
  "1 Timothy 3:5": "(For if a man know not how to rule his own house, how shall he take care of the church of God?) (the question is left rhetorical -- the answer is treated as too obvious to need stating).",
  "1 Timothy 3:6": "Not a novice, lest being lifted up with pride he fall into the condemnation of the devil (pride is named as the specific danger, not inexperience itself).",
  "1 Timothy 3:7": "Moreover he must have a good report of them which are without; lest he fall into reproach and the snare of the devil (the concern named here reaches outside the church, to how he is seen by people who are not in it).",
  "1 Timothy 3:9": "Holding the mystery of the faith in a pure conscience (what they are asked to hold is called a mystery here, not a rulebook).",
  "1 Timothy 3:10": "And let these also first be proved; then let them use the office of a deacon, being found blameless (being proved is placed before being appointed, not the other way around).",
  "1 Timothy 3:13": "For they that have used the office of a deacon well purchase to themselves a good degree, and great boldness in the faith which is in Christ Jesus (serving well is described as producing boldness, not merely rank).",
  "1 Timothy 3:15": "But if I tarry long, that thou mayest know how thou oughtest to behave thyself in the house of God, which is the church of the living God, the pillar and ground of the truth (the church is called a pillar and a ground here -- something that holds a structure up, not something the structure holds up).",
  "1 Timothy 3:16": "And without controversy great is the mystery of godliness: God was manifest in the flesh, justified in the Spirit, seen of angels, preached unto the Gentiles, believed on in the world, received up into glory (six things are listed about Christ in a row, moving from his appearing in flesh to his return to glory, with nothing left for the reader to add).",
  "1 Timothy 4:1": "Now the Spirit speaketh expressly, that in the latter times some shall depart from the faith, giving heed to seducing spirits, and doctrines of devils (the departure described here is leaving the Cross as the means of salvation, not merely changing an opinion);",
  "1 Timothy 4:2": "Speaking lies in hypocrisy; having their conscience seared with a hot iron (the deception named is not accidental -- a seared conscience is deliberate, not confused);",
  "1 Timothy 4:3": "Forbidding to marry, and commanding to abstain from meats, which God hath created to be received with thanksgiving of them which believe and know the truth (forbidding what God created is named as the error here, not asceticism in general).",
  "1 Timothy 4:4": "For every creature of God is good, and nothing to be refused, if it be received with thanksgiving (nothing is excluded by category -- the qualifier given is how it's received, not what it is):",
  "1 Timothy 4:6": "If thou put the brethren in remembrance of these things, thou shalt be a good minister of Jesus Christ, nourished up in the words of faith and of good doctrine, whereunto thou hast attained (being a good minister is tied here to what he reminds people of, not to anything original he invents).",
  "1 Timothy 4:7": "But refuse profane and old wives’ fables, and exercise thyself rather unto godliness (bodily discipline gets one word of comparison before the next verse moves on to the discipline that matters more).",
  "1 Timothy 4:8": "For bodily exercise profiteth little: but godliness is profitable unto all things, having promise of the life that now is, and of that which is to come (godliness is credited with reaching both this life and the next, in that order).",
  "1 Timothy 4:10": "For therefore we both labour and suffer reproach, because we trust in the living God, who is the Saviour of all men, specially of those that believe (labor and reproach are named together, as if one naturally follows the other for anyone doing this work).",
  "1 Timothy 4:12": "Let no man despise thy youth; but be thou an example of the believers, in word, in conversation, in charity, in spirit, in faith, in purity (six areas are listed for the example he is to set, and age is not one of the conditions attached to any of them).",
  "1 Timothy 4:14": "Neglect not the gift that is in thee, which was given thee by prophecy, with the laying on of the hands of the presbytery (the instruction not to neglect it assumes the gift is already there, not something still to be acquired).",
  "1 Timothy 4:16": "Take heed unto thyself, and unto the doctrine; continue in them: for in doing this thou shalt both save thyself, and them that hear thee (the 'save' named here is protection from the false doctrine just described, for both Timothy and those who hear him -- not the earning of a standing already settled).",
  "1 Timothy 5:1": "Rebuke not an elder, but intreat him as a father; and the younger men as brethren (the appeal is described in family terms before any rule is given);",
  "1 Timothy 5:3": "Honour widows that are widows indeed (the honor named here is specifically for those who have no one else -- the verse narrows before it commands).",
  "1 Timothy 5:4": "But if any widow have children or nephews, let them learn first to shew piety at home, and to requite their parents: for that is good and acceptable before God (the responsibility is placed on the family first, before the church is ever mentioned).",
  "1 Timothy 5:6": "But she that liveth in pleasure is dead while she liveth (being alive and being dead are both used to describe the same woman, depending on which way she is facing).",
  "1 Timothy 5:8": "But if any provide not for his own, and specially for those of his own house, he hath denied the faith, and is worse than an infidel (the comparison drawn here is to an infidel, specifically because even an infidel is expected to do this much).",
  "1 Timothy 5:10": "Well reported of for good works; if she have brought up children, if she have lodged strangers, if she have washed the saints’ feet, if she have relieved the afflicted, if she have diligently followed every good work (five specific actions are listed as evidence, not a single reputation taken on faith).",
  "1 Timothy 5:12": "Having damnation, because they have cast off their first faith (the charge described here is about breaking a pledge already made, not a verdict on where these widows stand with God).",
  "1 Timothy 5:14": "I will therefore that the younger women marry, bear children, guide the house, give none occasion to the adversary to speak reproachfully (marriage, children, and household are named as the answer to the idleness just described in the verse before).",
  "1 Timothy 5:15": "For some are already turned aside after Satan (the turning described here is toward Satan by name, not merely away from a rule).",
  "1 Timothy 5:17": "Let the elders that rule well be counted worthy of double honour, especially they who labour in the word and doctrine (double is the word used, not single -- the ones who labor in word and doctrine are set apart from the rest of the list).",
  "1 Timothy 5:19": "Against an elder receive not an accusation, but before two or three witnesses (a rule about evidence is given here before any rule about guilt).",
  "1 Timothy 5:20": "Them that sin rebuke before all, that others also may fear (the correction is made public specifically so that others take notice, not to humiliate the one corrected).",
  "1 Timothy 5:22": "Lay hands suddenly on no man, neither be partaker of other men’s sins: keep thyself pure (purity is placed at the end of a warning about other people’s sins, as if the two are connected).",
  "1 Timothy 5:24": "Some men’s sins are open beforehand, going before to judgment; and some men they follow after (some sins are described as visible before judgment and some only at judgment -- the verse does not rank one category worse, only that they surface at different times).",
  "1 Timothy 6:1": "Let as many servants as are under the yoke count their own masters worthy of all honour, that the name of God and his doctrine be not blasphemed (the reputation at stake here is named as God's, not the servant's own).",
  "1 Timothy 6:2": "And they that have believing masters, let them not despise them, because they are brethren; but rather do them service, because they are faithful and beloved, partakers of the benefit. These things teach and exhort (being equals in Christ is given here as a reason for more service, not less).",
  "1 Timothy 6:4": "He is proud, knowing nothing, but doting about questions and strifes of words, whereof cometh envy, strife, railings, evil surmisings (a chain of results is listed, each one leading to the next, starting from a single root -- pride),",
  "1 Timothy 6:5": "Perverse disputings of men of corrupt minds, and destitute of the truth, supposing that gain is godliness: from such withdraw thyself (withdrawal is the instruction given, aimed at people the same verse calls destitute of the very truth they claim to defend).",
  "1 Timothy 6:6": "But godliness with contentment is great gain (contentment is what turns godliness into gain, according to this verse -- not the other way around).",
  "1 Timothy 6:7": "For we brought nothing into this world, and it is certain we can carry nothing out (the same fact is stated twice, once about arriving and once about leaving, so the point is impossible to miss).",
  "1 Timothy 6:10": "For the love of money is the root of all evil: which while some coveted after, they have erred from the faith, and pierced themselves through with many sorrows (the love of money is named as the root, not the money itself -- and Believers are the ones described as erring here, not outsiders).",
  "1 Timothy 6:11": "But thou, O man of God, flee these things; and follow after righteousness, godliness, faith, love, patience, meekness (the list of things to flee comes first, then the list of things to follow, in that order).",
  "1 Timothy 6:12": "Fight the good fight of faith, lay hold on eternal life, whereunto thou art also called, and hast professed a good profession before many witnesses (the fight, the taking hold, and the calling are all named as already underway, not as goals still to be started).",
  "1 Timothy 6:14": "That thou keep this commandment without spot, unrebukeable, until the appearing of our Lord Jesus Christ (the commandment is to be kept without spot until an appearing the verse does not yet describe as having happened):",
  "1 Timothy 6:16": "Who only hath immortality, dwelling in the light which no man can approach unto; whom no man hath seen, nor can see: to whom be honour and power everlasting. Amen (immortality is credited to God alone, in a sentence that goes on to say no one has seen him and no one can).",
  "1 Timothy 6:17": "Charge them that are rich in this world, that they be not highminded, nor trust in uncertain riches, but in the living God, who giveth us richly all things to enjoy (trusting in riches is named as the danger here, not having them);",
  "1 Timothy 6:18": "That they do good, that they be rich in good works, ready to distribute, willing to communicate (being rich in good works is described as the right use of riches, not merely their surrender);",
  "1 Timothy 6:19": "Laying up in store for themselves a good foundation against the time to come, that they may lay hold on eternal life (the foundation being laid is described as being laid up now, against a time still to come).",
  "1 Timothy 6:21": "Which some professing have erred concerning the faith. Grace be with thee. Amen. The first to Timothy was written from Laodicea, which is the chiefest city of Phrygia Pacatiana (the closing warning names people who professed the Faith and still erred from it -- profession alone is not treated here as the safeguard).",
  "2 Timothy 1:1": "Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ by the will of God, according to the promise of life which is in Christ Jesus (the life promised here is not described as earned, only found in Christ Jesus),",
  "2 Timothy 1:3": "I thank God, whom I serve from my forefathers with pure conscience, that without ceasing I have remembrance of thee in my prayers night and day (night and day are both named, leaving no gap in how often he prayed for Timothy);",
  "2 Timothy 1:4": "Greatly desiring to see thee, being mindful of thy tears, that I may be filled with joy (the joy he asks for here is simply seeing Timothy again, nothing more elaborate is named);",
  "2 Timothy 1:5": "When I call to remembrance the unfeigned faith that is in thee, which dwelt first in thy grandmother Lois, and thy mother Eunice; and I am persuaded that in thee also (the faith named here is traced back two generations before it is said to be in Timothy himself).",
  "2 Timothy 1:6": "Wherefore I put thee in remembrance that thou stir up the gift of God, which is in thee by the putting on of my hands (Paul says he laid hands on Timothy, not that he supplied what was not already there).",
  "2 Timothy 1:7": "For God hath not given us the spirit of fear; but of power, and of love, and of a sound mind (three things are named as what God did give, in the same breath as the one thing he did not).",
  "2 Timothy 1:8": "Be not thou therefore ashamed of the testimony of our Lord, nor of me his prisoner: but be thou partaker of the afflictions of the gospel according to the power of God (the shame in view is being ashamed of the testimony itself, not of any particular hardship);",
  "2 Timothy 1:9": "Who hath saved us, and called us with an holy calling, not according to our works, but according to his own purpose and grace, which was given us in Christ Jesus before the world began (salvation and calling are both credited to purpose and grace here, with works named and set aside in the same sentence),",
  "2 Timothy 1:10": "But is now made manifest by the appearing of our Saviour Jesus Christ, who hath abolished death, and hath brought life and immortality to light through the gospel (life and immortality are described as brought to light, not newly invented):",
  "2 Timothy 1:12": "For the which cause I also suffer these things: nevertheless I am not ashamed: for I know whom I have believed, and am persuaded that he is able to keep that which I have committed unto him against that day (the confidence stated here rests on knowing a person, not on knowing an outcome).",
  "2 Timothy 1:13": "Hold fast the form of sound words, which thou hast heard of me, in faith and love which is in Christ Jesus (the form he is told to hold onto is described as something already given to him, not something to construct).",
  "2 Timothy 1:15": "This thou knowest, that all they which are in Asia be turned away from me; of whom are Phygellus and Hermogenes (two men are named here, out of what this verse says was an entire province turning away).",
  "2 Timothy 1:16": "The Lord give mercy unto the house of Onesiphorus; for he oft refreshed me, and was not ashamed of my chain (the mercy asked for here is for a man whose house, by this wording, seems to have already lost him):",
  "2 Timothy 1:18": "The Lord grant unto him that he may find mercy of the Lord in that day: and in how many things he ministered unto me at Ephesus, thou knowest very well (the day named here refers elsewhere to a reckoning for believers, not a judgment of sin already settled at the cross).",
  "2 Timothy 2:1": "Thou therefore, my son, be strong in the grace that is in Christ Jesus (the strength commanded here has a named source -- grace -- not effort supplied by Timothy himself).",
  "2 Timothy 2:2": "And the things that thou hast heard of me among many witnesses, the same commit thou to faithful men, who shall be able to teach others also (the chain described here runs three generations deep in a single sentence -- Paul, Timothy, and the men Timothy will teach).",
  "2 Timothy 2:4": "No man that warreth entangleth himself with the affairs of this life; that he may please him who hath chosen him to be a soldier (pleasing the one who enlisted him is named as the soldier's whole object, not the war itself).",
  "2 Timothy 2:6": "The husbandman that laboureth must be first partaker of the fruits (the order given here is labor first, then the harvest -- not the other way around).",
  "2 Timothy 2:8": "Remember that Jesus Christ of the seed of David was raised from the dead according to my gospel (his human ancestry and his resurrection are named in the same sentence, side by side):",
  "2 Timothy 2:9": "Wherein I suffer trouble, as an evil doer, even unto bonds; but the word of God is not bound (the contrast drawn here is between what happened to Paul and what happened to the message -- only one of the two was chained).",
  "2 Timothy 2:10": "Therefore I endure all things for the elect’s sakes, that they may also obtain the salvation which is in Christ Jesus with eternal glory (endurance is described here as being for the sake of others, not merely Paul’s own perseverance).",
  "2 Timothy 2:11": "It is a faithful saying: For if we be dead with him, we shall also live with him (the two halves of this sentence are presented as one event, not two separate steps):",
  "2 Timothy 2:12": "If we suffer, we shall also reign with him: if we deny him, he also will deny us (both instances of denial in this line trace back to the same thing -- what happened at the Cross):",
  "2 Timothy 2:13": "If we believe not, yet he abideth faithful: he cannot deny himself (this ties his faithfulness to who he is, not to how steady our belief happens to be).",
  "2 Timothy 2:14": "Of these things put them in remembrance, charging them before the Lord that they strive not about words to no profit, but to the subverting of the hearers (arguing over words is named here for exactly what it produces -- nothing gained, hearers unsettled).",
  "2 Timothy 2:15": "Study to shew thyself approved unto God, a workman that needeth not to be ashamed, rightly dividing the word of truth (the approval named here comes from God, not from an audience).",
  "2 Timothy 2:16": "But shun profane and vain babblings: for they will increase unto more ungodliness (the trajectory described here only runs one direction -- toward more, not less).",
  "2 Timothy 2:17": "And their word will eat as doth a canker: of whom is Hymeneus and Philetus (the warning gets attached to two actual names, not left as a vague possibility);",
  "2 Timothy 2:18": "Who concerning the truth have erred, saying that the resurrection is past already; and overthrow the faith of some (this names one specific, checkable claim, not a general drift into doubt).",
  "2 Timothy 2:19": "Nevertheless the foundation of God standeth sure, having this seal, The Lord knoweth them that are his. And, Let every one that nameth the name of Christ depart from iniquity (the foundation is called sure in the very verse that has just described people making shipwreck of their faith).",
  "2 Timothy 2:20": "But in a great house there are not only vessels of gold and of silver, but also of wood and of earth; and some to honour, and some to dishonour (four kinds of vessels are named in one house, not one standard applied to all).",
  "2 Timothy 2:21": "If a man therefore purge himself from these, he shall be a vessel unto honour, sanctified, and meet for the master’s use, and prepared unto every good work (the purging described here is the doorway into usefulness, not a separate transaction from it).",
  "2 Timothy 2:22": "Flee also youthful lusts: but follow righteousness, faith, charity, peace, with them that call on the Lord out of a pure heart (fleeing one thing and following four others are given as a single instruction, not two separate options).",
  "2 Timothy 2:25": "In meekness instructing those that oppose themselves; if God peradventure will give them repentance to the acknowledging of the truth (the repentance in view here is named as something God gives, not something the opponent produces on his own);",
  "2 Timothy 2:26": "And that they may recover themselves out of the snare of the devil, who are taken captive by him at his will (captivity is described here as something a person is recovered out of, not a permanent classification).",
  "2 Timothy 3:1": "This know also, that in the last days perilous times shall come (the times are called dangerous before anything about them is described).",
  "2 Timothy 3:4": "Traitors, heady, highminded, lovers of pleasures more than lovers of God (the comparison at the end of this list is stated as a preference, not a total absence -- pleasure is loved more, not instead);",
  "2 Timothy 3:5": "Having a form of godliness, but denying the power thereof: from such turn away (the form is not the problem named here -- what's denied is the power, and that's what the instruction responds to).",
  "2 Timothy 3:8": "Now as Jannes and Jambres withstood Moses, so do these also resist the truth: men of corrupt minds, reprobate concerning the faith (a pattern from Israel's past is used here to describe people in Paul's present, centuries apart).",
  "2 Timothy 3:9": "But they shall proceed no further: for their folly shall be manifest unto all men, as theirs also was (a limit is placed on how far this error is allowed to go, not an open-ended threat).",
  "2 Timothy 3:11": "Persecutions, afflictions, which came unto me at Antioch, at Iconium, at Lystra; what persecutions I endured: but out of them all the Lord delivered me (three cities are named, and one outcome covers all three -- deliverance, not survival by chance).",
  "2 Timothy 3:12": "Yea, and all that will live godly in Christ Jesus shall suffer persecution (the persecution described here is not the exception to godly living, it's named as what follows it).",
  "2 Timothy 3:13": "But evil men and seducers shall wax worse and worse, deceiving, and being deceived (the deceiver and the deceived are named as the same category here, not two separate groups).",
  "2 Timothy 3:14": "But continue thou in the things which thou hast learned and hast been assured of, knowing of whom thou hast learned them (the instruction to continue is tied to who taught it, not only to what was taught);",
  "2 Timothy 3:15": "And that from a child thou hast known the holy scriptures, which are able to make thee wise unto salvation through faith which is in Christ Jesus (the scriptures are credited with making one wise unto salvation, and the means named for that wisdom is faith, not the reading itself).",
  "2 Timothy 3:16": "All scripture is given by inspiration of God, and is profitable for doctrine, for reproof, for correction, for instruction in righteousness (four uses are listed for the same scripture, covering what to believe and how to live by it):",
  "2 Timothy 3:17": "That the man of God may be perfect, throughly furnished unto all good works (the furnishing described here is said to reach every good work, not merely some of them).",
  "2 Timothy 4:1": "I charge thee therefore before God, and the Lord Jesus Christ, who shall judge the quick and the dead at his appearing and his kingdom (the charge that follows is placed under the weight of a coming judgment, not merely good advice);",
  "2 Timothy 4:2": "Preach the word; be instant in season, out of season; reprove, rebuke, exhort with all longsuffering and doctrine (readiness is commanded for both convenient and inconvenient moments, with no third option named).",
  "2 Timothy 4:3": "For the time will come when they will not endure sound doctrine; but after their own lusts shall they heap to themselves teachers, having itching ears (the itch described here is in the hearer, and the teachers are simply the ones who agree to scratch it);",
  "2 Timothy 4:4": "And they shall turn away their ears from the truth, and shall be turned unto fables (the same verb, turn, describes both the leaving and the arriving).",
  "2 Timothy 4:5": "But watch thou in all things, endure afflictions, do the work of an evangelist, make full proof of thy ministry (four instructions are given in a row, and none of them is optional to the others).",
  "2 Timothy 4:6": "For I am now ready to be offered, and the time of my departure is at hand (Paul names his own death using the language of a drink offering poured out, not a defeat).",
  "2 Timothy 4:7": "I have fought a good fight, I have finished my course, I have kept the faith (all three verbs are past tense here -- the fight, the course, and the faith are spoken of as already settled):",
  "2 Timothy 4:8": "Henceforth there is laid up for me a crown of righteousness, which the Lord, the righteous judge, shall give me at that day: and not to me only, but unto all them also that love his appearing (the crown named here is not reserved for Paul alone -- the same sentence extends it to everyone who shares that love).",
  "2 Timothy 4:10": "For Demas hath forsaken me, having loved this present world, and is departed unto Thessalonica; Crescens to Galatia, Titus unto Dalmatia (only one of these three departures is described with a reason attached -- loving this present world).",
  "2 Timothy 4:16": "At my first answer no man stood with me, but all men forsook me: I pray God that it may not be laid to their charge (Paul's response to being abandoned is a request for mercy on the ones who abandoned him).",
  "2 Timothy 4:17": "Notwithstanding the Lord stood with me, and strengthened me; that by me the preaching might be fully known, and that all the Gentiles might hear: and I was delivered out of the mouth of the lion (the desertion of the verse before this one and the presence described here stand side by side, back-to-back).",
  "2 Timothy 4:18": "And the Lord shall deliver me from every evil work, and will preserve me unto his heavenly kingdom: to whom be glory for ever and ever. Amen (the deliverance named in this verse is not deliverance from death -- Paul has already said his departure is at hand).",
  "2 Timothy 4:22": "The Lord Jesus Christ be with thy spirit. Grace be with you. Amen. The second epistle unto Timotheus, ordained the first bishop of the church of the Ephesians, was written from Rome, when Paul was brought before Nero the second time (the last words of Paul's last letter are addressed to one man's spirit, and then widen to everyone reading it).",
  "Titus 1:1": "Paul, a servant of God, and an apostle of Jesus Christ, according to the faith of God’s elect, and the acknowledging of the truth which is after godliness (servant comes before apostle in his own introduction -- the title carrying authority is listed second, not first);",
  "Titus 1:4": "To Titus, mine own son after the common faith: Grace, mercy, and peace, from God the Father and the Lord Jesus Christ our Saviour (mercy is placed between grace and peace, as if one could not be reached without passing through the other).",
  "Titus 1:6": "If any be blameless, the husband of one wife, having faithful children not accused of riot or unruly (the children in view are already believers -- their conduct is offered as evidence, not as the deciding factor).",
  "Titus 1:7": "For a bishop must be blameless, as the steward of God; not selfwilled, not soon angry, not given to wine, no striker, not given to filthy lucre (the list names what a steward avoids before it ever names what he does);",
  "Titus 1:9": "Holding fast the faithful word as he hath been taught, that he may be able by sound doctrine both to exhort and to convince the gainsayers (the same word does both jobs here -- it builds up the ones listening and answers the ones arguing).",
  "Titus 1:10": "For there are many unruly and vain talkers and deceivers, specially they of the circumcision (this group is named specifically -- those trying to reattach the law to a message that had already moved past it):",
  "Titus 1:11": "Whose mouths must be stopped, who subvert whole houses, teaching things which they ought not, for filthy lucre’s sake (the motive named for the false teaching is not conviction, it is money).",
  "Titus 1:13": "This witness is true. Wherefore rebuke them sharply, that they may be sound in the faith (the goal named for the sharp rebuke is soundness, not just silence);",
  "Titus 1:15": "Unto the pure all things are pure: but unto them that are defiled and unbelieving is nothing pure; but even their mind and conscience is defiled (the defilement described here has moved from the outside in -- it now sits in the mind doing the judging).",
  "Titus 1:16": "They profess that they know God; but in works they deny him, being abominable, and disobedient, and unto every good work reprobate (the profession and the works are placed side by side so the contradiction between them cannot be missed).",
  "Titus 2:5": "To be discreet, chaste, keepers at home, good, obedient to their own husbands, that the word of God be not blasphemed (the reputation of the message itself is named as what is riding on this).",
  "Titus 2:10": "Not purloining, but shewing all good fidelity; that they may adorn the doctrine of God our Saviour in all things (the doctrine is described here as something a life can dress up or embarrass, depending on how it’s lived).",
  "Titus 2:11": "For the grace of God that bringeth salvation hath appeared to all men,",
  "Titus 2:12": "Teaching us that, denying ungodliness and worldly lusts, we should live soberly, righteously, and godly, in this present world (grace is described as the thing doing the teaching here, not the law);",
  "Titus 2:13": "Looking for that blessed hope, and the glorious appearing of the great God and our Saviour Jesus Christ (the hope in view is named as a person arriving, not an idea being fulfilled);",
  "Titus 2:14": "Who gave himself for us, that he might redeem us from all iniquity, and purify unto himself a peculiar people, zealous of good works (the good works show up at the end of this sentence, after the redeeming and purifying are already finished).",
  "Titus 3:3": "For we ourselves also were sometimes foolish, disobedient, deceived, serving divers lusts and pleasures, living in malice and envy, hateful, and hating one another (Paul places himself inside this list along with everyone else -- the word is we, not they).",
  "Titus 3:4": "But after that the kindness and love of God our Saviour toward man appeared,",
  "Titus 3:5": "Not by works of righteousness which we have done, but according to his mercy he saved us, by the washing of regeneration, and renewing of the Holy Ghost (the sentence rules out one explanation before it gives the real one);",
  "Titus 3:7": "That being justified by his grace, we should be made heirs according to the hope of eternal life (heirs receive by relationship, not by wages).",
  "Titus 3:8": "This is a faithful saying, and these things I will that thou affirm constantly, that they which have believed in God might be careful to maintain good works. These things are good and profitable unto men (the order in this sentence is believing first, maintaining second -- the second follows the first, not the other way around).",
  "Titus 3:9": "But avoid foolish questions, and genealogies, and contentions, and strivings about the law; for they are unprofitable and vain (four things are listed together here, and the one thread running through all of them is that none is the Cross).",
  "Titus 3:10": "A man that is an heretick after the first and second admonition reject (two warnings are given before anything else happens -- the door is not shut on the first offense);",
  "Titus 3:11": "Knowing that he that is such is subverted, and sinneth, being condemned of himself (the condemnation described here is self-inflicted, not imposed from outside).",
  "Titus 3:14": "And let ours also learn to maintain good works for necessary uses, that they be not unfruitful (the good works in view here have an address -- necessary uses, not abstract virtue).",
  "Philemon 1:1": "Paul, a prisoner of Jesus Christ, and Timothy our brother, unto Philemon our dearly beloved, and fellowlabourer, (he names his imprisonment as belonging to Christ, not to the empire that actually locked the door),",
  "Philemon 1:5": "Hearing of thy love and faith, which thou hast toward the Lord Jesus, and toward all saints; (the same two things, love and faith, are named as running in two directions at once -- toward the Lord and toward everyone else)",
  "Philemon 1:6": "That the communication of thy faith may become effectual by the acknowledging of every good thing which is in you in Christ Jesus. (faith that is never shared is described here as faith that has not yet become what it is for.)",
  "Philemon 1:7": "For we have great joy and consolation in thy love, because the bowels of the saints are refreshed by thee, brother. (Paul's own strength is described as running partly on reports of someone else's kindness).",
  "Philemon 1:9": "Yet for love's sake I rather beseech thee, being such an one as Paul the aged, and now also a prisoner of Jesus Christ. (he has the standing to command and chooses to ask instead).",
  "Philemon 1:10": "I beseech thee for my son Onesimus, whom I have begotten in my bonds: (the whole letter turns out to be about one runaway slave, and his name does not appear until verse ten).",
  "Philemon 1:11": "Which in time past was to thee unprofitable, but now profitable to thee and to me: (the same man is described before and after, and only Christ accounts for the difference).",
  "Philemon 1:12": "Whom I have sent again: thou therefore receive him, that is, mine own bowels: (Paul sends back a runaway slave and calls him his own heart in the same sentence).",
  "Philemon 1:15": "For perhaps he therefore departed for a season, that thou shouldest receive him for ever; (the running away and the return are folded into a single purpose, stated only after the fact).",
  "Philemon 1:16": "Not now as a servant, but above a servant, a brother beloved, specially to me, but how much more unto thee, both in the flesh, and in the Lord? (the same man is asked to be received under two titles at once -- servant in one sense, brother in the other).",
  "Philemon 1:17": "If thou count me therefore a partner, receive him as myself. (Paul asks that the runaway slave be welcomed exactly as Paul himself would be).",
  "Philemon 1:18": "If he hath wronged thee, or oweth thee ought, put that on mine account; (whatever is owed gets moved onto Paul's own ledger before Philemon even answers)",
  "Philemon 1:19": "I Paul have written it with mine own hand, I will repay it: albeit I do not say to thee how thou owest unto me even thine own self besides. (Paul offers to settle one debt while quietly naming a far larger one Philemon already owes him).",
  "Philemon 1:21": "Having confidence in thy obedience I wrote unto thee, knowing that thou wilt also do more than I say. (he asks for one thing and states in advance that he expects more than he asked for).",
  "Philemon 1:22": "But withal prepare me also a lodging: for I trust that through your prayers I shall be given unto you. (he asks for a guest room while writing from a cell).",
  "Philemon 1:25": "The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with your spirit. Amen. Written from Rome to Philemon, by Onesimus a servant. (the letter is delivered by the same hand it was written to rescue).",
  "Hebrews 1:2": "Hath in these last days spoken unto us by his Son, whom he hath appointed heir of all things, by whom also he made the worlds (the same person is named both heir of everything and maker of everything in one sentence);",
  "Hebrews 1:3": "Who being the brightness of his glory, and the express image of his person, and upholding all things by the word of his power, when he had by himself purged our sins, sat down on the right hand of the Majesty on high (the sentence moves from who he is straight into what he did, and ends with him seated -- the work described as already finished);",
  "Hebrews 1:4": "Being made so much better than the angels, as he hath by inheritance obtained a more excellent name than they (the name is described as inherited, not earned by comparison).",
  "Hebrews 1:6": "And again, when he bringeth in the firstbegotten into the world, he saith, And let all the angels of God worship him (the command to worship is given to angels, not to men -- the highest created beings are told to bow to him).",
  "Hebrews 1:8": "But unto the Son he saith, Thy throne, O God, is for ever and ever: a sceptre of righteousness is the sceptre of thy kingdom (God the Father addresses the Son as God here -- the title is not implied, it is spoken).",
  "Hebrews 1:9": "Thou hast loved righteousness, and hated iniquity; therefore God, even thy God, hath anointed thee with the oil of gladness above thy fellows (the anointing is described as a response to a settled character, not the cause of it).",
  "Hebrews 1:10": "And, Thou, Lord, in the beginning hast laid the foundation of the earth; and the heavens are the works of thine hands (the one this letter is about is named as the one who built the world it opens in):",
  "Hebrews 1:12": "And as a vesture shalt thou fold them up, and they shall be changed: but thou art the same, and thy years shall not fail (everything else in the sentence changes -- he is the one exception named).",
  "Hebrews 1:14": "Are they not all ministering spirits, sent forth to minister for them who shall be heirs of salvation (angels are described here as serving people, not the other way around)?",
  "Hebrews 2:1": "Therefore we ought to give the more earnest heed to the things which we have heard, lest at any time we should let them slip (the image used is something slipping loose, not something being taken away).",
  "Hebrews 2:9": "But we see Jesus, who was made a little lower than the angels for the suffering of death, crowned with glory and honour; that he by the grace of God should taste death for every man (the lowering and the crowning are both named in the same sentence, in that order).",
  "Hebrews 2:10": "For it became him, for whom are all things, and by whom are all things, in bringing many sons unto glory, to make the captain of their salvation perfect through sufferings (the leader is described as being made complete by the same suffering he leads others through).",
  "Hebrews 2:11": "For both he that sanctifieth and they who are sanctified are all of one: for which cause he is not ashamed to call them brethren (the word used for the connection is not servant or subject, it is brethren),",
  "Hebrews 2:14": "Forasmuch then as the children are partakers of flesh and blood, he also himself likewise took part of the same; that through death he might destroy him that had the power of death, that is, the devil (he takes on the same nature in order to meet death on its own ground);",
  "Hebrews 2:15": "And deliver them who through fear of death were all their lifetime subject to bondage (the bondage named here is not death itself, it is the fear of it).",
  "Hebrews 2:17": "Wherefore in all things it behoved him to be made like unto his brethren, that he might be a merciful and faithful high priest in things pertaining to God, to make reconciliation for the sins of the people (being made like his brethren is stated as the reason he could represent them, not an obstacle to it).",
  "Hebrews 2:18": "For in that he himself hath suffered being tempted, he is able to succour them that are tempted (his ability to help traces back to his own experience, not just his authority).",
  "Hebrews 3:1": "Wherefore, holy brethren, partakers of the heavenly calling, consider the Apostle and High Priest of our profession, Christ Jesus (two titles are given to the same person in one verse -- sent one and representing one);",
  "Hebrews 3:3": "For this man was counted worthy of more glory than Moses, inasmuch as he who hath builded the house hath more honour than the house (the comparison is not between two houses, it is between a house and its builder).",
  "Hebrews 3:6": "But Christ as a son over his own house; whose house are we, if we hold fast the confidence and the rejoicing of the hope firm unto the end (the shift from servant to son marks the difference between the two households this chapter is comparing).",
  "Hebrews 3:12": "Take heed, brethren, lest there be in any of you an evil heart of unbelief, in departing from the living God (the thing named as the actual danger is unbelief, not any particular action).",
  "Hebrews 3:13": "But exhort one another daily, while it is called To day; lest any of you be hardened through the deceitfulness of sin (the exhortation is prescribed daily, not occasionally -- deception is described as working slowly enough to need a daily answer).",
  "Hebrews 3:19": "So we see that they could not enter in because of unbelief (the reason given for their exclusion from Canaan is named once, plainly, and left there).",
  "Hebrews 4:2": "For unto us was the gospel preached, as well as unto them: but the word preached did not profit them, not being mixed with faith in them that heard it (the same message reached two different groups with two different results, and the difference named is not the message itself).",
  "Hebrews 4:3": "For we which have believed do enter into rest, as he said, As I have sworn in my wrath, if they shall enter into my rest: although the works were finished from the foundation of the world (the entry point named here is believing, and the finished work it rests on predates the readers by all of history).",
  "Hebrews 4:9": "There remaineth therefore a rest to the people of God (the rest named here already has an owner attached to it).",
  "Hebrews 4:10": "For he that is entered into his rest, he also hath ceased from his own works, as God did from his (the resting described mirrors a pattern God himself already set).",
  "Hebrews 4:12": "For the word of God is quick, and powerful, and sharper than any twoedged sword, piercing even to the dividing asunder of soul and spirit, and of the joints and marrow, and is a discerner of the thoughts and intents of the heart (four different images are stacked up here to describe one thing reaching one place).",
  "Hebrews 4:13": "Neither is there any creature that is not manifest in his sight: but all things are naked and opened unto the eyes of him with whom we have to do (nothing in the sentence is left hidden by the time it ends).",
  "Hebrews 4:14": "Seeing then that we have a great high priest, that is passed into the heavens, Jesus the Son of God, let us hold fast our profession (the instruction that follows is grounded in who has already gone ahead, not in effort alone).",
  "Hebrews 4:15": "For we have not an high priest which cannot be touched with the feeling of our infirmities; but was in all points tempted like as we are, yet without sin (the sentence names two things about him at once -- he faced everything we face, and none of it changed the outcome).",
  "Hebrews 4:16": "Let us therefore come boldly unto the throne of grace, that we may obtain mercy, and find grace to help in time of need (the approach described here is bold, not cautious -- the throne is named a source of grace before it is named a seat of judgment).",
  "Hebrews 5:7": "Who in the days of his flesh, when he had offered up prayers and supplications with strong crying and tears unto him that was able to save him from death, and was heard in that he feared (the one strong enough to save the whole world is shown here praying with tears);",
  "Hebrews 5:8": "Though he were a Son, yet learned he obedience by the things which he suffered (sonship did not exempt him from the things that taught him obedience);",
  "Hebrews 5:9": "And being made perfect, he became the author of eternal salvation unto all them that obey him (the salvation named here has an author, not merely a source);",
  "Hebrews 5:12": "For when for the time ye ought to be teachers, ye have need that one teach you again which be the first principles of the oracles of God; and are become such as have need of milk, and not of strong meat (the complaint is not that they never learned -- it is that they stopped where they started).",
  "Hebrews 5:14": "But strong meat belongeth to them that are of full age, even those who by reason of use have their senses exercised to discern both good and evil (the discernment described here comes from practice, not instruction alone).",
  "Hebrews 6:1": "Therefore leaving the principles of the doctrine of Christ, let us go on unto perfection; not laying again the foundation of repentance from dead works, and of faith toward God (the instruction is to build further, not to keep re-laying the same foundation),",
  "Hebrews 6:4": "For it is impossible for those who were once enlightened, and have tasted of the heavenly gift, and were made partakers of the Holy Ghost (four things are named in a row here, each describing something genuinely received, not merely observed),",
  "Hebrews 6:5": "And have tasted the good word of God, and the powers of the world to come (the description keeps piling up real experience, not surface familiarity),",
  "Hebrews 6:7": "For the earth which drinketh in the rain that cometh oft upon it, and bringeth forth herbs meet for them by whom it is dressed, receiveth blessing from God (the picture used is ordinary and agricultural -- ground doing exactly what ground is supposed to do):",
  "Hebrews 6:9": "But, beloved, we are persuaded better things of you, and things that accompany salvation, though we thus speak (Paul follows his own warning immediately with confidence that his readers will not need it).",
  "Hebrews 6:10": "For God is not unrighteous to forget your work and labour of love, which ye have shewed toward his name, in that ye have ministered to the saints, and do minister (their labor is named specifically as something God will not forget).",
  "Hebrews 6:17": "Wherein God, willing more abundantly to shew unto the heirs of promise the immutability of his counsel, confirmed it by an oath (the extra step God takes here is not for his own benefit -- it is named as being for ours):",
  "Hebrews 6:18": "That by two immutable things, in which it was impossible for God to lie, we might have a strong consolation, who have fled for refuge to lay hold upon the hope set before us (two things that cannot change are given so the hope resting on them does not have to either):",
  "Hebrews 6:19": "Which hope we have as an anchor of the soul, both sure and stedfast, and which entereth into that within the veil (the anchor pictured here does not just hold, it reaches somewhere);",
  "Hebrews 6:20": "Whither the forerunner is for us entered, even Jesus, made an high priest for ever after the order of Melchisedec (he is named forerunner -- someone who has already gone where the rest are still headed).",
  "Hebrews 7:1": "For this Melchisedec, king of Salem, priest of the most high God, who met Abraham returning from the slaughter of the kings, and blessed him (a king and a priest meet a returning warrior, and it is the king who blesses the warrior, not the other way around);",
  "Hebrews 7:2": "To whom also Abraham gave a tenth part of all; first being by interpretation King of righteousness, and after that also King of Salem, which is, King of peace (both of his titles are given here as translations, not just names);",
  "Hebrews 7:3": "Without father, without mother, without descent, having neither beginning of days, nor end of life; but made like unto the Son of God; abideth a priest continually (the absence of a recorded genealogy is treated here as meaningful, not accidental).",
  "Hebrews 7:7": "And without all contradiction the less is blessed of the better (the direction of the blessing is what settles which of the two is greater).",
  "Hebrews 7:11": "If therefore perfection were by the Levitical priesthood, (for under it the people received the law,) what further need was there that another priest should rise after the order of Melchisedec, and not be called after the order of Aaron (the very fact that another priesthood shows up in Scripture is treated as proof the first one had not finished the job)?",
  "Hebrews 7:16": "Who is made, not after the law of a carnal commandment, but after the power of an endless life (his qualification is not a family record, it is a life that does not end).",
  "Hebrews 7:18": "For there is verily a disannulling of the commandment going before for the weakness and unprofitableness thereof (weakness and unprofitableness are the two reasons given for the old commandment's cancellation).",
  "Hebrews 7:19": "For the law made nothing perfect, but the bringing in of a better hope did; by the which we draw nigh unto God (something new is credited with opening a door the old system never opened).",
  "Hebrews 7:22": "By so much was Jesus made a surety of a better testament (the word used here is the language of a guarantee, not a suggestion).",
  "Hebrews 7:24": "But this man, because he continueth ever, hath an unchangeable priesthood (his priesthood is unchangeable for the simple reason that he is).",
  "Hebrews 7:25": "Wherefore he is able also to save them to the uttermost that come unto God by him, seeing he ever liveth to make intercession for them (the extent of the saving named here -- to the uttermost -- is matched by the fact that he never stops interceding).",
  "Hebrews 7:26": "For such an high priest became us, who is holy, harmless, undefiled, separate from sinners, and made higher than the heavens (four different words describe the same purity from four different angles);",
  "Hebrews 7:27": "Who needeth not daily, as those high priests, to offer up sacrifice, first for his own sins, and then for the people\u2019s: for this he did once, when he offered up himself (the number of times is the entire argument of this verse -- once, instead of daily).",
  "Hebrews 8:1": "Now of the things which we have spoken this is the sum: We have such an high priest, who is set on the right hand of the throne of the Majesty in the heavens (he is described as seated, which in this letter is always shorthand for finished);",
  "Hebrews 8:6": "But now hath he obtained a more excellent ministry, by how much also he is the mediator of a better covenant, which was established upon better promises (three separate comparisons are made in a single sentence -- ministry, covenant, and promises, all called better).",
  "Hebrews 8:7": "For if that first covenant had been faultless, then should no place have been sought for the second (the existence of a second covenant is treated as proof the first one had a flaw).",
  "Hebrews 8:8": "For finding fault with them, he saith, Behold, the days come, saith the Lord, when I will make a new covenant with the house of Israel and with the house of Judah (the fault named belongs to the people, but the new covenant is offered anyway):",
  "Hebrews 8:10": "For this is the covenant that I will make with the house of Israel after those days, saith the Lord; I will put my laws into their mind, and write them in their hearts: and I will be to them a God, and they shall be to me a people (the law is relocated in this promise -- from a stone tablet to the heart):",
  "Hebrews 8:12": "For I will be merciful to their unrighteousness, and their sins and their iniquities will I remember no more (forgetting is the specific word used here, not merely forgiving).",
  "Hebrews 8:13": "In that he saith, A new covenant, he hath made the first old. Now that which decayeth and waxeth old is ready to vanish away (something only gets called old once something newer exists).",
  "Hebrews 9:12": "Neither by the blood of goats and calves, but by his own blood he entered in once into the holy place, having obtained eternal redemption for us (the entry described here happens once, and the word eternal is attached to what it obtained).",
  "Hebrews 9:14": "How much more shall the blood of Christ, who through the eternal Spirit offered himself without spot to God, purge your conscience from dead works to serve the living God (the purpose named for this purging is not relief from guilt alone, it is the ability to serve)?",
  "Hebrews 9:15": "And for this cause he is the mediator of the new testament, that by means of death, for the redemption of the transgressions that were under the first testament, they which are called might receive the promise of eternal inheritance (one death is described here as reaching backward to cover transgressions that happened before it).",
  "Hebrews 9:22": "And almost all things are by the law purged with blood; and without shedding of blood is no remission (the rule stated in this verse has no exception offered).",
  "Hebrews 9:24": "For Christ is not entered into the holy places made with hands, which are the figures of the true; but into heaven itself, now to appear in the presence of God for us (the location named here is not a replica, it is the original):",
  "Hebrews 9:26": "For then must he often have suffered since the foundation of the world: but now once in the end of the world hath he appeared to put away sin by the sacrifice of himself (put away is the word chosen here, not merely covered).",
  "Hebrews 9:27": "And as it is appointed unto men once to die, but after this the judgment (the same word, once, links this verse to the one that follows it about Christ):",
  "Hebrews 9:28": "So Christ was once offered to bear the sins of many; and unto them that look for him shall he appear the second time without sin unto salvation (his first appearing dealt with sin, so his second does not have to).",
  "Hebrews 10:1": "For the law having a shadow of good things to come, and not the very image of the things, can never with those sacrifices which they offered year by year continually make the comers thereunto perfect (a shadow and the thing that casts it are named here as two different objects).",
  "Hebrews 10:4": "For it is not possible that the blood of bulls and of goats should take away sins (the word chosen is impossible, not merely insufficient).",
  "Hebrews 10:10": "By the which will we are sanctified through the offering of the body of Jesus Christ once for all (the offering is named once, and the sanctifying it produces is not described as needing repeating).",
  "Hebrews 10:12": "But this man, after he had offered one sacrifice for sins for ever, sat down on the right hand of God (sitting down is the detail that separates his priesthood from every one before it);",
  "Hebrews 10:14": "For by one offering he hath perfected for ever them that are sanctified (one offering is credited here with a result that lasts forever).",
  "Hebrews 10:17": "And their sins and iniquities will I remember no more (the promise made here is about memory, not merely record).",
  "Hebrews 10:19": "Having therefore, brethren, boldness to enter into the holiest by the blood of Jesus (the word chosen for the approach is boldness, not permission),",
  "Hebrews 10:20": "By a new and living way, which he hath consecrated for us, through the veil, that is to say, his flesh (the way in is identified here with his own body);",
  "Hebrews 10:22": "Let us draw near with a true heart in full assurance of faith, having our hearts sprinkled from an evil conscience, and our bodies washed with pure water (the cleansing named here covers both what is inside and what is visible).",
  "Hebrews 10:23": "Let us hold fast the profession of our faith without wavering; (for he is faithful that promised;) (the reason given to hold fast is not our own strength, it is his reliability).",
  "Hebrews 10:24": "And let us consider one another to provoke unto love and to good works (provoke is used deliberately here for something positive):",
  "Hebrews 10:26": "For if we sin wilfully after that we have received the knowledge of the truth, there remaineth no more sacrifice for sins (the sacrifice named as the only one is stated as singular -- there is not a second one waiting),",
  "Hebrews 10:27": "But a certain fearful looking for of judgment and fiery indignation, which shall devour the adversaries (the word adversaries names exactly who this is aimed at).",
  "Hebrews 10:28": "He that despised Moses\u2019 law died without mercy under two or three witnesses (the requirement of witnesses named here shows this was never carried out on a single accusation):",
  "Hebrews 10:30": "For we know him that hath said, Vengeance belongeth unto me, I will recompense, saith the Lord. And again, The Lord shall judge his people (judgment is claimed here as belonging to one person alone).",
  "Hebrews 10:34": "For ye had compassion of me in my bonds, and took joyfully the spoiling of your goods, knowing in yourselves that ye have in heaven a better and an enduring substance (the loss of earthly goods is answered here with a substance described as enduring).",
  "Hebrews 10:35": "Cast not away therefore your confidence, which hath great recompence of reward (the word used for what is at stake here is reward, not standing).",
  "Hebrews 10:36": "For ye have need of patience, that, after ye have done the will of God, ye might receive the promise (patience is named as the one thing still required, not something new to earn).",
  "Hebrews 10:37": "For yet a little while, and he that shall come will come, and will not tarry (the coming described here is treated as a certainty of timing, not merely of fact).",
  "Hebrews 10:38": "Now the just shall live by faith: but if any man draw back, my soul shall have no pleasure in him (the reaction named here is grief, not rejection).",
  "Hebrews 11:1": "Now faith is the substance of things hoped for, the evidence of things not seen (the two words used here, substance and evidence, both describe something solid, not a feeling).",
  "Hebrews 11:3": "Through faith we understand that the worlds were framed by the word of God, so that things which are seen were not made of things which do appear (the things that are seen are traced back here to a source that was not visible either).",
  "Hebrews 11:4": "By faith Abel offered unto God a more excellent sacrifice than Cain, by which he obtained witness that he was righteous, God testifying of his gifts: and by it he being dead yet speaketh (the report Abel received is described as still speaking, long after Abel himself stopped).",
  "Hebrews 11:5": "By faith Enoch was translated that he should not see death; and was not found, because God had translated him: for before his translation he had this testimony, that he pleased God (the one thing named about Enoch before his translation is that he pleased God, not that he impressed anyone else).",
  "Hebrews 11:6": "But without faith it is impossible to please him: for he that cometh to God must believe that he is, and that he is a rewarder of them that diligently seek him (seeking God is paired here with believing he exists, as if one is incomplete without the other).",
  "Hebrews 11:7": "By faith Noah, being warned of God of things not seen as yet, moved with fear, prepared an ark to the saving of his house; by the which he condemned the world, and became heir of the righteousness which is by faith (Noah's obedience is described as condemning the world simply by being obeyed, not by being announced).",
  "Hebrews 11:8": "By faith Abraham, when he was called to go out into a place which he should after receive for an inheritance, obeyed; and he went out, not knowing whither he went (he obeyed a call to a place he could not have named yet).",
  "Hebrews 11:10": "For he looked for a city which hath foundations, whose builder and maker is God (the city he was looking for is described by its foundation, which is the one thing his tents never had).",
  "Hebrews 11:11": "Through faith also Sara herself received strength to conceive seed, and was delivered of a child when she was past age, because she judged him faithful who had promised (the strength named here belongs to someone who was already past the age it usually requires).",
  "Hebrews 11:13": "These all died in faith, not having received the promises, but having seen them afar off, and were persuaded of them, and embraced them, and confessed that they were strangers and pilgrims on the earth (these all died still holding promises they never saw arrive).",
  "Hebrews 11:16": "But now they desire a better country, that is, an heavenly: wherefore God is not ashamed to be called their God: for he hath prepared for them a city (wherefore is the hinge of this verse -- God is not ashamed of them because of what they desired, not what they achieved).",
  "Hebrews 11:17": "By faith Abraham, when he was tried, offered up Isaac: and he that had received the promises offered up his only begotten son (Abraham is described offering up the son the promise itself depended on),",
  "Hebrews 11:19": "Accounting that God was able to raise him up, even from the dead; from whence also he received him in a figure (the reasoning credited to Abraham here goes as far as resurrection, not just rescue).",
  "Hebrews 11:24": "By faith Moses, when he was come to years, refused to be called the son of Pharaoh\u2019s daughter (refused is the verb chosen for a title most men spent their lives trying to reach);",
  "Hebrews 11:25": "Choosing rather to suffer affliction with the people of God, than to enjoy the pleasures of sin for a season (the choice is stated as deliberate -- affliction, chosen over pleasure, not affliction that simply happened to him);",
  "Hebrews 11:26": "Esteeming the reproach of Christ greater riches than the treasures in Egypt: for he had respect unto the recompence of the reward (the reproach he chose is called greater riches than the treasure he walked away from).",
  "Hebrews 11:27": "By faith he forsook Egypt, not fearing the wrath of the king: for he endured, as seeing him who is invisible (the thing that gave him endurance is named as seeing someone invisible, not seeing a plan).",
  "Hebrews 11:33": "Who through faith subdued kingdoms, wrought righteousness, obtained promises, stopped the mouths of lions (the list moves through kingdoms, promises, and lions without slowing down for any one of them),",
  "Hebrews 11:35": "Women received their dead raised to life again: and others were tortured, not accepting deliverance; that they might obtain a better resurrection (the same verse holds two endings side by side -- some were raised to life, others refused release, for the same reason):",
  "Hebrews 11:39": "And these all, having obtained a good report through faith, received not the promise (having obtained a good report through faith, they still did not receive the thing promised):",
  "Hebrews 11:40": "God having provided some better thing for us, that they without us should not be made perfect (the reason given for the delay is not their failure, it is a plan that included someone else).",
  "Hebrews 12:1": "Wherefore seeing we also are compassed about with so great a cloud of witnesses, let us lay aside every weight, and the sin which doth so easily beset us, and let us run with patience the race that is set before us (the cloud of witnesses named here is placed before the runner even starts, not cheering from the finish line),",
  "Hebrews 12:2": "Looking unto Jesus the author and finisher of our faith; who for the joy that was set before him endured the cross, despising the shame, and is set down at the right hand of the throne of God (the joy set before him is named as the reason he endured the cross, not a reward that came after he forgot about it).",
  "Hebrews 12:3": "For consider him that endured such contradiction of sinners against himself, lest ye be wearied and faint in your minds (the instruction is to consider him, not to consider the difficulty).",
  "Hebrews 12:4": "Ye have not yet resisted unto blood, striving against sin (the comparison drawn here is to bloodshed, which puts every lesser struggle in its place).",
  "Hebrews 12:5": "And ye have forgotten the exhortation which speaketh unto you as unto children, My son, despise not thou the chastening of the Lord, nor faint when thou art rebuked of him (the exhortation being forgotten here is one that calls the reader a child, not a stranger):",
  "Hebrews 12:6": "For whom the Lord loveth he chasteneth, and scourgeth every son whom he receiveth (chastening is presented here as the evidence of love, not the absence of it).",
  "Hebrews 12:7": "If ye endure chastening, God dealeth with you as with sons; for what son is he whom the father chasteneth not (the question asked here assumes an answer everyone already knows about ordinary fathers and sons)?",
  "Hebrews 12:9": "Furthermore we have had fathers of our flesh which corrected us, and we gave them reverence: shall we not much rather be in subjection unto the Father of spirits, and live (the fathers of our flesh are compared here to a Father of spirits, and the second comparison is called the greater one)?",
  "Hebrews 12:10": "For they verily for a few days chastened us after their own pleasure; but he for our profit, that we might be partakers of his holiness (the difference named between the two kinds of correction is not the pain, it is the purpose).",
  "Hebrews 12:11": "Now no chastening for the present seemeth to be joyous, but grievous: nevertheless afterward it yieldeth the peaceable fruit of righteousness unto them which are exercised thereby (the fruit named here is described as coming afterward, not during).",
  "Hebrews 12:12": "Wherefore lift up the hands which hang down, and the feeble knees (the hands and knees named here are addressed directly, as if discouragement were physical);",
  "Hebrews 12:13": "And make straight paths for your feet, lest that which is lame be turned out of the way; but let it rather be healed (the path is called straight before it is called easy).",
  "Hebrews 12:14": "Follow peace with all men, and holiness, without which no man shall see the Lord (holiness is paired here with peace as something to be pursued, not merely preferred):",
  "Hebrews 12:16": "Lest there be any fornicator, or profane person, as Esau, who for one morsel of meat sold his birthright (the phrase used of Esau is that he was in the family, not that he ceased to be).",
  "Hebrews 12:17": "For ye know how that afterward, when he would have inherited the blessing, he was rejected: for he found no place of repentance, though he sought it carefully with tears (what he sought with tears was the blessing, not the repentance that would have secured it).",
  "Hebrews 12:18": "For ye are not come unto the mount that might be touched, and that burned with fire, nor unto blackness, and darkness, and tempest (the mountain described here could be touched, which turns out to be the whole problem with it),",
  "Hebrews 12:22": "But ye are come unto mount Sion, and unto the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem, and to an innumerable company of angels (the city named here is not approached, it is already arrived at),",
  "Hebrews 12:23": "To the general assembly and church of the firstborn, which are written in heaven, and to God the Judge of all, and to the spirits of just men made perfect (the same verse names a gathering, a Judge, and spirits made perfect, all in one place),",
  "Hebrews 12:24": "And to Jesus the mediator of the new covenant, and to the blood of sprinkling, that speaketh better things than that of Abel (the blood named here is said to speak better things, comparing one voice to another).",
  "Hebrews 12:28": "Wherefore we receiving a kingdom which cannot be moved, let us have grace, whereby we may serve God acceptably with reverence and godly fear (the kingdom described here cannot be moved, which is the quality named as the reason for gratitude):",
  "Hebrews 12:29": "For our God is a consuming fire (the image used for God here is fire, and the verse gives it no qualifier).",
  "Hebrews 12:25": "See that ye refuse not him that speaketh. For if they escaped not who refused him that spake on earth, much more shall not we escape, if we turn away from him that speaketh from heaven (the comparison drawn here is between refusing a voice on earth and refusing the same voice from heaven, not between two different offenses):",
  "Hebrews 13:2": "Be not forgetful to entertain strangers: for thereby some have entertained angels unawares (the stranger entertained unknowingly is named here as a possible angel, which changes how the verse reads on a second pass).",
  "Hebrews 13:3": "Remember them that are in bonds, as bound with them; and them which suffer adversity, as being yourselves also in the body (the instruction is to remember prisoners as bound with them, not merely to feel sorry for them from a distance).",
  "Hebrews 13:4": "Marriage is honourable in all, and the bed undefiled: but whoremongers and adulterers God will judge (the bed is called undefiled here, which is not a word this letter uses loosely).",
  "Hebrews 13:5": "Let your conversation be without covetousness; and be content with such things as ye have: for he hath said, I will never leave thee, nor forsake thee (contentment is tied here to a promise of presence, not to the amount already in hand).",
  "Hebrews 13:6": "So that we may boldly say, The Lord is my helper, and I will not fear what man shall do unto me (the confidence stated here rests on who the helper is, not on the size of the threat).",
  "Hebrews 13:7": "Remember them which have the rule over you, who have spoken unto you the word of God: whose faith follow, considering the end of their conversation (their faith is what the reader is told to follow, and their outcome is what the reader is told to consider).",
  "Hebrews 13:8": "Jesus Christ the same yesterday, and to day, and for ever (three points in time are named in one sentence, and the subject does not change across any of them).",
  "Hebrews 13:9": "Be not carried about with divers and strange doctrines. For it is a good thing that the heart be established with grace; not with meats, which have not profited them that have been occupied therein (the heart is said to be established by grace, and the verse names nothing else that can do it).",
  "Hebrews 13:10": "We have an altar, whereof they have no right to eat which serve the tabernacle (the verse claims an altar for believers that a specific other group has no right to eat from).",
  "Hebrews 13:12": "Wherefore Jesus also, that he might sanctify the people with his own blood, suffered without the gate (the location named -- outside the gate -- is repeated from the Old Testament pattern this verse is drawing on).",
  "Hebrews 13:14": "For here have we no continuing city, but we seek one to come (the city being sought is described as not yet arrived at, even by the writer).",
  "Hebrews 13:15": "By him therefore let us offer the sacrifice of praise to God continually, that is, the fruit of our lips giving thanks to his name (praise is called a sacrifice here, offered continually rather than occasionally).",
  "Hebrews 13:16": "But to do good and to communicate forget not: for with such sacrifices God is well pleased (two things are named side by side as pleasing to God -- doing good, and sharing what one has).",
  "Hebrews 13:17": "Obey them that have the rule over you, and submit yourselves: for they watch for your souls, as they that must give account, that they may do it with joy, and not with grief: for that is unprofitable for you (the leaders are described as watching for souls, and as people who will one day give an account for that watching).",
  "Hebrews 13:20": "Now the God of peace, that brought again from the dead our Lord Jesus, that great shepherd of the sheep, through the blood of the everlasting covenant (the title used here, that great shepherd, ties this verse back to the flock language running through the whole letter),",
  "Hebrews 13:21": "Make you perfect in every good work to do his will, working in you that which is wellpleasing in his sight, through Jesus Christ; to whom be glory for ever and ever. Amen (the working credited in this verse belongs to God, even though the good work is described as ours).",
  "James 1:1": "James, a servant of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ, to the twelve tribes which are scattered abroad, greeting (he calls himself a servant, never an apostle, though he was the Lord’s own brother and a leader in Jerusalem).",
  "James 1:2": "My brethren, count it all joy when ye fall into divers temptations (the joy commanded here is aimed at the testing itself, not something felt only after it ends);",
  "James 1:4": "But let patience have her perfect work, that ye may be perfect and entire, wanting nothing (the goal named is completeness, not merely getting through the trial).",
  "James 1:5": "If any of you lack wisdom, let him ask of God, that giveth to all men liberally, and upbraideth not; and it shall be given him (the request comes with no mention of a scolding for having needed to ask in the first place).",
  "James 1:6": "But let him ask in faith, nothing wavering. For he that wavereth is like a wave of the sea driven with the wind and tossed (the image chosen is water with no direction of its own, not a person simply unsure).",
  "James 1:8": "A double minded man is unstable in all his ways (instability is named as touching every part of a life, not just the one decision in question).",
  "James 1:9": "Let the brother of low degree rejoice in that he is exalted (the exaltation named here belongs to the poor man, stated as already true, not promised for later):",
  "James 1:10": "But the rich, in that he is made low: because as the flower of the grass he shall pass away (wealth gets the same verdict a flower does -- something that looks impressive and does not last).",
  "James 1:12": "Blessed is the man that endureth temptation: for when he is tried, he shall receive the crown of life, which the Lord hath promised to them that love him (the reward named is sized well past the cost of the trial that earned it).",
  "James 1:13": "Let no man say when he is tempted, I am tempted of God: for God cannot be tempted with evil, neither tempteth he any man (the source of temptation is ruled out here before it is even named):",
  "James 1:14": "But every man is tempted, when he is drawn away of his own lust, and enticed (the pull described starts inside the person, not outside).",
  "James 1:15": "Then when lust hath conceived, it bringeth forth sin: and sin, when it is finished, bringeth forth death (three stages are laid out in sequence here, and none of them is described as sudden).",
  "James 1:17": "Every good gift and every perfect gift is from above, and cometh down from the Father of lights, with whom is no variableness, neither shadow of turning (the Giver named here is described as fixed, not merely generous).",
  "James 1:19": "Wherefore, my beloved brethren, let every man be swift to hear, slow to speak, slow to wrath (the same word, slow, is used twice, applied to speaking and to anger alike):",
  "James 1:21": "Wherefore lay apart all filthiness and superfluity of naughtiness, and receive with meekness the engrafted word, which is able to save your souls (the word is described as already planted, something to be received, not something to go find).",
  "James 1:22": "But be ye doers of the word, and not hearers only, deceiving your own selves (the deception named here is not by someone else -- it is self-inflicted).",
  "James 1:23": "For if any be a hearer of the word, and not a doer, he is like unto a man beholding his natural face in a glass (the comparison drawn is to a mirror, something that shows a true reflection but changes nothing on its own):",
  "James 1:25": "But whoso looketh into the perfect law of liberty, and continueth therein, he being not a forgetful hearer, but a doer of the work, this man shall be blessed in his deed (the blessing named here belongs to the doing, not to the looking alone).",
  "James 1:26": "If any man among you seem to be religious, and bridleth not his tongue, but deceiveth his own heart, this man’s religion is vain (the one thing named as unguarded, the tongue, is enough by itself to void everything else claimed).",
  "James 1:27": "Pure religion and undefiled before God and the Father is this, To visit the fatherless and widows in their affliction, and to keep himself unspotted from the world (the definition given names two things that have nothing to do with ceremony).",
  "James 2:1": "My brethren, have not the faith of our Lord Jesus Christ, the Lord of glory, with respect of persons (partiality is named here as incompatible with the faith just described, not a separate issue).",
  "James 2:4": "Are ye not then partial in yourselves, and are become judges of evil thoughts (the question answers itself before anyone has the chance to argue)?",
  "James 2:5": "Hearken, my beloved brethren, Hath not God chosen the poor of this world rich in faith, and heirs of the kingdom which he hath promised to them that love him (the world’s math and God’s math are shown running in opposite directions in the same sentence)?",
  "James 2:8": "If ye fulfil the royal law according to the scripture, Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself, ye do well (one commandment is singled out here and given a title, royal, that none of the others receive):",
  "James 2:9": "But if ye have respect to persons, ye commit sin, and are convinced of the law as transgressors (favoritism is not softened here into a lesser fault -- it is named sin outright).",
  "James 2:10": "For whosoever shall keep the whole law, and yet offend in one point, he is guilty of all (the math used here treats the law as one thing, not a list where partial credit is possible).",
  "James 2:12": "So speak ye, and so do, as they that shall be judged by the law of liberty (speaking and doing are named together here, as if judged by the same standard).",
  "James 2:13": "For he shall have judgment without mercy, that hath shewed no mercy; and mercy rejoiceth against judgment (the sentence ends on mercy, not on judgment, even after naming both).",
  "James 2:14": "What doth it profit, my brethren, though a man say he hath faith, and have not works? can faith save him (the question is not whether faith saves, but whether the kind of faith just described is faith at all)?",
  "James 2:17": "Even so faith, if it hath not works, is dead, being alone (the verdict given is not weak faith, but no faith -- the word used is dead).",
  "James 2:18": "Yea, a man may say, Thou hast faith, and I have works: shew me thy faith without thy works, and I will shew thee my faith by my works (one of these two claims can actually be demonstrated, and the sentence names which one).",
  "James 2:19": "Thou believest that there is one God; thou doest well: the devils also believe, and tremble (the bar being tested here is set low enough that demons clear it too).",
  "James 2:20": "But wilt thou know, O vain man, that faith without works is dead (the same claim from verse seventeen is repeated here, aimed now directly at the reader)?",
  "James 2:21": "Was not Abraham our father justified by works, when he had offered Isaac his son upon the altar (the example chosen is a father raising a knife over his own son, not a minor errand)?",
  "James 2:22": "Seest thou how faith wrought with his works, and by works was faith made perfect (faith and works are described here as working together, not as two competing claims)?",
  "James 2:23": "And the scripture was fulfilled which saith, Abraham believed God, and it was imputed unto him for righteousness: and he was called the Friend of God (the title given him, Friend of God, follows a statement about what he believed, not what he built).",
  "James 2:24": "Ye see then how that by works a man is justified, and not by faith only (the justified in view here is a claim proven true in front of others, not the transaction Paul describes before God).",
  "James 2:25": "Likewise also was not Rahab the harlot justified by works, when she had received the messengers, and had sent them out another way (the second example chosen is about as far from Abraham’s social standing as the Bible could reach)?",
  "James 2:26": "For as the body without the spirit is dead, so faith without works is dead also (the comparison closes the chapter with the same word used earlier, dead, now attached to a body instead of a claim).",
  "James 3:1": "My brethren, be not many masters, knowing that we shall receive the greater condemnation (the stricter standard he names here is applied to those who teach, not to those who listen).",
  "James 3:2": "For in many things we offend all. If any man offend not in word, the same is a perfect man, and able also to bridle the whole body (the test proposed here, control of speech, is named as harder than controlling anything else).",
  "James 3:5": "Even so the tongue is a little member, and boasteth great things. Behold, how great a matter a little fire kindleth (size and effect are shown here working in opposite directions)!",
  "James 3:6": "And the tongue is a fire, a world of iniquity: so is the tongue among our members, that it defileth the whole body, and setteth on fire the course of nature; and it is set on fire of hell (the tongue is traced back here to its source, named plainly as hell, not merely bad habit).",
  "James 3:8": "But the tongue can no man tame; it is an unruly evil, full of deadly poison (the claim made is not that taming is hard, but that no man can do it).",
  "James 3:9": "Therewith bless we God, even the Father; and therewith curse we men, which are made after the similitude of God (the same mouth is shown doing both in the same verse, with nothing separating the two acts but a breath).",
  "James 3:10": "Out of the same mouth proceedeth blessing and cursing. My brethren, these things ought not so to be (the inconsistency named here is stated as a fact, then immediately judged).",
  "James 3:13": "Who is a wise man and endued with knowledge among you? let him shew out of a good conversation his works with meekness of wisdom (wisdom is measured here by conduct, not by the claim to possess it).",
  "James 3:14": "But if ye have bitter envying and strife in your hearts, glory not, and lie not against the truth (boasting in this condition is named here as a lie, not an exaggeration).",
  "James 3:15": "This wisdom descendeth not from above, but is earthly, sensual, devilish (three words are stacked up here to describe one source, leaving no room to call it merely misguided).",
  "James 3:17": "But the wisdom that is from above is first pure, then peaceable, gentle, and easy to be intreated, full of mercy and good fruits, without partiality, and without hypocrisy (purity is named first in this list, with everything else following from it).",
  "James 3:18": "And the fruit of righteousness is sown in peace of them that make peace (the harvest described here belongs to the ones who made the peace, not merely those who enjoyed it).",
  "James 4:1": "From whence come wars and fightings among you? come they not hence, even of your lusts that war in your members (the fighting described here is traced to a battle already underway inside the person before it reaches anyone else)?",
  "James 4:2": "Ye lust, and have not: ye kill, and desire to have, and cannot obtain: ye fight and war, yet ye have not, because ye ask not (the reason given for going without, at the end of the list, is simply that nothing was asked for).",
  "James 4:3": "Ye ask, and receive not, because ye ask amiss, that ye may consume it upon your lusts (asking is not the problem named here -- what the asking was for is).",
  "James 4:4": "Ye adulterers and adulteresses, know ye not that the friendship of the world is enmity with God? whosoever therefore will be a friend of the world is the enemy of God (the word chosen for this friendship is enmity, not merely distraction).",
  "James 4:6": "But he giveth more grace. Wherefore he saith, God resisteth the proud, but giveth grace unto the humble (the same grace is shown here meeting two different postures with two different responses).",
  "James 4:7": "Submit yourselves therefore to God. Resist the devil, and he will flee from you (the order given here matters -- submission comes before resistance, not after).",
  "James 4:8": "Draw nigh to God, and he will draw nigh to you. Cleanse your hands, ye sinners; and purify your hearts, ye double minded (the nearness offered here moves in both directions at once).",
  "James 4:10": "Humble yourselves in the sight of the Lord, and he shall lift you up (the lifting up named here is done by someone other than the one who humbled himself).",
  "James 4:11": "Speak not evil one of another, brethren. He that speaketh evil of his brother, and judgeth his brother, speaketh evil of the law, and judgeth the law: but if thou judge the law, thou art not a doer of the law, but a judge (the one judging the law is shown here trading places with the one who wrote it).",
  "James 4:12": "There is one lawgiver, who is able to save and to destroy: who art thou that judgest another (the question closing this verse is aimed at the size of the person asking it, not the case itself)?",
  "James 4:14": "Whereas ye know not what shall be on the morrow. For what is your life? It is even a vapour, that appeareth for a little time, and then vanisheth away (the image chosen for a life is something already fading even while it is still visible).",
  "James 4:17": "Therefore to him that knoweth to do good, and doeth it not, to him it is sin (the sin named here requires no bad act at all -- only the absence of a good one already known).",
  "James 5:1": "Go to now, ye rich men, weep and howl for your miseries that shall come upon you (the weeping commanded here is not for what has already happened, but for what is still coming).",
  "James 5:3": "Your gold and silver is cankered; and the rust of them shall be a witness against you, and shall eat your flesh as it were fire. Ye have heaped treasure together for the last days (the very metal used to represent lasting wealth is shown here decaying like anything else).",
  "James 5:4": "Behold, the hire of the labourers who have reaped down your fields, which is of you kept back by fraud, crieth: and the cries of them which have reaped are entered into the ears of the Lord of sabaoth (the wages withheld are described as crying out on their own, without anyone speaking on their behalf).",
  "James 5:7": "Be patient therefore, brethren, unto the coming of the Lord. Behold, the husbandman waiteth for the precious fruit of the earth, and hath long patience for it, until he receive the early and latter rain (the patience commanded here is compared to a farmer’s, who works long before he sees any result).",
  "James 5:8": "Be ye also patient; stablish your hearts: for the coming of the Lord draweth nigh (the coming named here is described as near, given as the reason for the patience just commanded).",
  "James 5:9": "Grudge not one against another, brethren, lest ye be condemned: behold, the judge standeth before the door (the judge described here is already positioned at the door, not still on the way).",
  "James 5:11": "Behold, we count them happy which endure. Ye have heard of the patience of Job, and have seen the end of the Lord; that the Lord is very pitiful, and of tender mercy (the example named here is remembered for how the story ended, not merely for how long the trial lasted).",
  "James 5:12": "But above all things, my brethren, swear not, neither by heaven, neither by the earth, neither by any other oath: but let your yea be yea; and your nay, nay; lest ye fall into condemnation (the standard set here asks for a word plain enough to need no oath propping it up).",
  "James 5:13": "Is any among you afflicted? let him pray. Is any merry? let him sing psalms (two very different states are given the same instruction here, addressed to God either way).",
  "James 5:14": "Is any sick among you? let him call for the elders of the church; and let them pray over him, anointing him with oil in the name of the Lord (the sick person is told here to call for others, not simply to pray alone):",
  "James 5:15": "And the prayer of faith shall save the sick, and the Lord shall raise him up; and if he have committed sins, they shall be forgiven him (healing and forgiveness are named together here, without either one made to depend on the other).",
  "James 5:16": "Confess your faults one to another, and pray one for another, that ye may be healed. The effectual fervent prayer of a righteous man availeth much (the confession commanded here happens between people before the healing that follows it).",
  "James 5:17": "Elias was a man subject to like passions as we are, and he prayed earnestly that it might not rain: and it rained not on the earth by the space of three years and six months (the prophet who stopped the rain is described here first as someone with the same weaknesses as anyone reading this).",
  "James 5:19": "Brethren, if any of you do err from the truth, and one convert him (the one who wanders here is still called brethren, not renamed before he is even found);",
  "1 Peter 1:1": "Peter, an apostle of Jesus Christ, to the strangers scattered throughout Pontus, Galatia, Cappadocia, Asia, and Bithynia, (the letter is addressed to people scattered across five Roman provinces, none of whom Peter had personally met)",
  "1 Peter 1:2": "Elect according to the foreknowledge of God the Father, through sanctification of the Spirit, unto obedience and sprinkling of the blood of Jesus Christ: Grace unto you, and peace, be multiplied (all three persons of the Trinity are named in a single sentence of greeting, each doing something different).",
  "1 Peter 1:3": "Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, which according to his abundant mercy hath begotten us again unto a lively hope by the resurrection of Jesus Christ from the dead, (the hope is called living because it is tied to a resurrection that already happened, not one still hoped for)",
  "1 Peter 1:4": "To an inheritance incorruptible, and undefiled, and that fadeth not away, reserved in heaven for you, (three different words rule out three different ways an inheritance usually gets lost -- decay, corruption, and time)",
  "1 Peter 1:5": "Who are kept by the power of God through faith unto salvation ready to be revealed in the last time (the verb kept names God’s power as the one doing the keeping, not the believer’s own grip).",
  "1 Peter 1:6": "Wherein ye greatly rejoice, though now for a season, if need be, ye are in heaviness through manifold temptations (joy and grief are named as running at the same time here, not taking turns):",
  "1 Peter 1:7": "That the trial of your faith, being much more precious than of gold that perisheth, though it be tried with fire, might be found unto praise and honour and glory at the appearing of Jesus Christ (even gold, the most valuable thing Peter can reach for, is used only as the lesser comparison):",
  "1 Peter 1:8": "Whom having not seen, ye love; in whom, though now ye see him not, yet believing, ye rejoice with joy unspeakable and full of glory (love and joy are both described running toward someone these readers have never physically seen):",
  "1 Peter 1:10": "Of which salvation the prophets have enquired and searched diligently, who prophesied of the grace that should come unto you (the prophets are described searching their own writings, trying to understand what they themselves had been given to say):",
  "1 Peter 1:11": "Searching what, or what manner of time the Spirit of Christ which was in them did signify, when it testified beforehand the sufferings of Christ, and the glory that should follow (suffering and glory are named in that order, the same order the Gospels themselves follow).",
  "1 Peter 1:12": "Unto whom it was revealed, that not unto themselves, but unto us they did minister the things, which are now reported unto you by them that have preached the gospel unto you with the Holy Ghost sent down from heaven; which things the angels desire to look into (angels are described wanting to understand something they have never personally needed -- redemption).",
  "1 Peter 1:13": "Wherefore gird up the loins of your mind, be sober, and hope to the end for the grace that is to be brought unto you at the revelation of Jesus Christ (the image is of a runner tucking in loose robes before a race, applied here to how a mind gets ready);",
  "1 Peter 1:15": "But as he which hath called you is holy, so be ye holy in all manner of conversation (the standard named here is not a rule to follow but a person to resemble);",
  "1 Peter 1:17": "And if ye call on the Father, who without respect of persons judgeth according to every man’s work, pass the time of your sojourning here in fear (the fear named here is reverence before an impartial judge, not anxiety about being disqualified):",
  "1 Peter 1:18": "Forasmuch as ye know that ye were not redeemed with corruptible things, as silver and gold, from your vain conversation received by tradition from your fathers (silver and gold are named as the two most valuable things Peter could reach for, and then set aside as insufficient);",
  "1 Peter 1:19": "But with the precious blood of Christ, as of a lamb without blemish and without spot (the description matches the requirement for every Old Testament sacrifice, applied here to a Person instead of an animal):",
  "1 Peter 1:20": "Who verily was foreordained before the foundation of the world, but was manifest in these last times for you, (the plan is dated before creation itself, and the carrying out of it is dated to a moment in ordinary history)",
  "1 Peter 1:22": "Seeing ye have purified your souls in obeying the truth through the Spirit unto unfeigned love of the brethren, see that ye love one another with a pure heart fervently (the love commanded here is qualified twice in one sentence -- unfeigned, and fervent):",
  "1 Peter 1:23": "Being born again, not of corruptible seed, but of incorruptible, by the word of God, which liveth and abideth for ever (birth is used as the picture on purpose -- something new starting, not something old improving).",
  "1 Peter 1:25": "But the word of the Lord endureth for ever. And this is the word which by the gospel is preached unto you (the contrast built in the verse before this one lasts exactly one sentence -- grass that withers, then a word that does not).",
  "1 Peter 2:1": "Wherefore laying aside all malice, and all guile, and hypocrisies, and envies, and all evil speakings, (five things are named and set aside in a single breath)",
  "1 Peter 2:2": "As newborn babes, desire the sincere milk of the word, that ye may grow thereby (the image assumes an appetite already there, not one that has to be talked into existing):",
  "1 Peter 2:4": "To whom coming, as unto a living stone, disallowed indeed of men, but chosen of God, and precious, (the same stone receives two opposite verdicts in one sentence -- rejected by men, chosen by God)",
  "1 Peter 2:5": "Ye also, as lively stones, are built up a spiritual house, an holy priesthood, to offer up spiritual sacrifices, acceptable to God by Jesus Christ (every reader becomes part of the same building material just named for Christ in the verse before).",
  "1 Peter 2:7": "Unto you therefore which believe he is precious: but unto them which be disobedient, the stone which the builders disallowed, the same is made the head of the corner, (the same stone is described in two different sentences, and the difference between them was never the stone)",
  "1 Peter 2:9": "But ye are a chosen generation, a royal priesthood, an holy nation, a peculiar people; that ye should shew forth the praises of him who hath called you out of darkness into his marvellous light (four titles are stacked in a row here, and every one of them once belonged to Israel alone):",
  "1 Peter 2:10": "Which in time past were not a people, but are now the people of God: which had not obtained mercy, but now have obtained mercy (the same sentence structure repeats twice in this verse, once for status and once for mercy).",
  "1 Peter 2:12": "Having your conversation honest among the Gentiles: that, whereas they speak against you as evildoers, they may by your good works, which they shall behold, glorify God in the day of visitation (the same watching crowd that starts out accusing is the one expected to end up glorifying God).",
  "1 Peter 2:15": "For so is the will of God, that with well doing ye may put to silence the ignorance of foolish men (the strategy named here for answering foolish talk is conduct, not argument):",
  "1 Peter 2:16": "As free, and not using your liberty for a cloke of maliciousness, but as the servants of God (freedom and service are named in the same sentence as the same thing, not as opposites).",
  "1 Peter 2:17": "Honour all men. Love the brotherhood. Fear God. Honour the king (four commands in four short sentences, each one aimed at a different relationship).",
  "1 Peter 2:18": "Servants, be subject to your masters with all fear; not only to the good and gentle, but also to the froward (the instruction covers both kinds of masters in the same sentence, on purpose).",
  "1 Peter 2:20": "For what glory is it, if, when ye be buffeted for your faults, ye shall take it patiently? but if, when ye do well, and suffer for it, ye take it patiently, this is acceptable with God (the same patience earns no credit in the first half of this verse and full credit in the second).",
  "1 Peter 2:21": "For even hereunto were ye called: because Christ also suffered for us, leaving us an example, that ye should follow his steps (suffering itself is named as the calling here, not something that interrupts it):",
  "1 Peter 2:23": "Who, when he was reviled, reviled not again; when he suffered, he threatened not; but committed himself to him that judgeth righteously (the response named in each half of this verse is silence, not retaliation):",
  "1 Peter 2:24": "Who his own self bare our sins in his own body on the tree, that we, being dead to sins, should live unto righteousness: by whose stripes ye were healed (a death and a healing are named as happening in the same body, at the same time).",
  "1 Peter 2:25": "For ye were as sheep going astray; but are now returned unto the Shepherd and Bishop of your souls (the wandering is described as the old condition, already finished, not the present one).",
  "1 Peter 3:3": "Whose adorning let it not be that outward adorning of plaiting the hair, and of wearing of gold, or of putting on of apparel (three specific items are named here, not outward appearance in general);",
  "1 Peter 3:4": "But let it be the hidden man of the heart, in that which is not corruptible, even the ornament of a meek and quiet spirit, which is in the sight of God of great price (the word incorruptible is used here for a personality trait, the same word used elsewhere for the inheritance itself).",
  "1 Peter 3:6": "Even as Sara obeyed Abraham, calling him lord: whose daughters ye are, as long as ye do well, and are not afraid with any amazement (Sara is named as the example, and the fear ruled out at the end of the verse is fear itself, not disobedience).",
  "1 Peter 3:7": "Likewise, ye husbands, dwell with them according to knowledge, giving honour unto the wife, as unto the weaker vessel, and as being heirs together of the grace of life; that your prayers be not hindered (a husband’s prayer life is tied directly here to how he treats his wife).",
  "1 Peter 3:8": "Finally, be ye all of one mind, having compassion one of another, love as brethren, be pitiful, be courteous (five qualities are named back to back, none of them optional):",
  "1 Peter 3:9": "Not rendering evil for evil, or railing for railing: but contrariwise blessing; knowing that ye are thereunto called, that ye should inherit a blessing (the same word, blessing, appears twice in this verse -- once as an action, once as an inheritance).",
  "1 Peter 3:12": "For the eyes of the Lord are over the righteous, and his ears are open unto their prayers: but the face of the Lord is against them that do evil (two senses, sight and hearing, are both described turned toward the righteous in the same verse).",
  "1 Peter 3:13": "And who is he that will harm you, if ye be followers of that which is good (the question is asked as though the answer is obvious, and it is meant to be)?",
  "1 Peter 3:14": "But and if ye suffer for righteousness’ sake, happy are ye: and be not afraid of their terror, neither be troubled (happiness and suffering are named in the same sentence without contradiction);",
  "1 Peter 3:15": "But sanctify the Lord God in your hearts: and be ready always to give an answer to every man that asketh you a reason of the hope that is in you with meekness and fear (the readiness described here is aimed at a question, not a debate):",
  "1 Peter 3:16": "Having a good conscience; that, whereas they speak evil of you, as of evildoers, they may be ashamed that falsely accuse your good conversation in Christ (the shame predicted here belongs to the accuser, not the accused).",
  "1 Peter 3:18": "For Christ also hath once suffered for sins, the just for the unjust, that he might bring us to God, being put to death in the flesh, but quickened by the Spirit (the word once rules out any repeat performance -- this suffering happened a single time):",
  "1 Peter 3:20": "Which sometime were disobedient, when once the longsuffering of God waited in the days of Noah, while the ark was a preparing, wherein few, that is, eight souls were saved by water (eight people are named as the entire surviving population of the world at that time).",
  "1 Peter 3:21": "The like figure whereunto even baptism doth also now save us (not the putting away of the filth of the flesh, but the answer of a good conscience toward God,) by the resurrection of Jesus Christ (the verse names exactly what the water does not do, in the middle of explaining what it actually symbolizes):",
  "1 Peter 4:1": "Forasmuch then as Christ hath suffered for us in the flesh, arm yourselves likewise with the same mind: for he that hath suffered in the flesh hath ceased from sin (the weapon named here for the fight against sin is a mindset borrowed from what Christ already did);",
  "1 Peter 4:2": "That he no longer should live the rest of his time in the flesh to the lusts of men, but to the will of God (the phrase the rest of his time treats everything after conversion as one remaining stretch, aimed at a single direction).",
  "1 Peter 4:4": "Wherein they think it strange that ye run not with them to the same excess of riot, speaking evil of you (the criticism described here comes from people confused by an absence, not by anything actually done):",
  "1 Peter 4:8": "And above all things have fervent charity among yourselves: for charity shall cover the multitude of sins (the word used for what love does here is cover, not excuse).",
  "1 Peter 4:10": "As every man hath received the gift, even so minister the same one to another, as good stewards of the manifold grace of God (the word every rules out anyone claiming they were left without something to give).",
  "1 Peter 4:11": "If any man speak, let him speak as the oracles of God; if any man minister, let him do it as of the ability which God giveth: that God in all things may be glorified through Jesus Christ, to whom be praise and dominion for ever and ever. Amen (speaking and serving are named side by side in this verse, and both are traced back to the same source).",
  "1 Peter 4:12": "Beloved, think it not strange concerning the fiery trial which is to try you, as though some strange thing happened unto you (the word strange is used twice in one verse and denied both times):",
  "1 Peter 4:13": "But rejoice, inasmuch as ye are partakers of Christ’s sufferings; that, when his glory shall be revealed, ye may be glad also with exceeding joy (two different kinds of gladness are named here, one for now and one for later).",
  "1 Peter 4:14": "If ye be reproached for the name of Christ, happy are ye; for the spirit of glory and of God resteth upon you: on their part he is evil spoken of, but on your part he is glorified (the same Spirit gets two opposite receptions in the same sentence, depending on who is speaking).",
  "1 Peter 4:16": "Yet if any man suffer as a Christian, let him not be ashamed; but let him glorify God on this behalf (the word suffer appears in the verse before this one for the wrong reasons and in this one for the right one).",
  "1 Peter 4:17": "For the time is come that judgment must begin at the house of God: and if it first begin at us, what shall the end be of them that obey not the gospel of God (the household named first for judgment is the one that already believes, not the one that does not)?",
  "1 Peter 4:18": "And if the righteous scarcely be saved, where shall the ungodly and the sinner appear (the question is left open here on purpose, with no answer supplied)?",
  "1 Peter 4:19": "Wherefore let them that suffer according to the will of God commit the keeping of their souls to him in well doing, as unto a faithful Creator (the title used for God here, Creator, is not the one most naturally reached for in a verse about suffering).",
  "1 Peter 5:1": "The elders which are among you I exhort, who am also an elder, and a witness of the sufferings of Christ, and also a partaker of the glory that shall be revealed (Peter appeals here to the same office he is writing to, rather than pulling rank as an apostle):",
  "1 Peter 5:2": "Feed the flock of God which is among you, taking the oversight thereof, not by constraint, but willingly; not for filthy lucre, but of a ready mind (two wrong motives are named and ruled out before the right one is ever mentioned);",
  "1 Peter 5:3": "Neither as being lords over God’s heritage, but being ensamples to the flock (the model of leadership given here is imitation, not command).",
  "1 Peter 5:4": "And when the chief Shepherd shall appear, ye shall receive a crown of glory that fadeth not away (the title chief Shepherd places every human pastor addressed in this letter under someone else’s authority).",
  "1 Peter 5:5": "Likewise, ye younger, submit yourselves unto the elder. Yea, all of you be subject one to another, and be clothed with humility: for God resisteth the proud, and giveth grace to the humble (the command to submit widens by the end of the verse to include everyone, not just the younger group it names first).",
  "1 Peter 5:6": "Humble yourselves therefore under the mighty hand of God, that he may exalt you in due time (the exalting is promised here, but the timing is left entirely up to someone else):",
  "1 Peter 5:7": "Casting all your care upon him; for he careth for you (the same word for care appears twice in this verse, once for what gets handed over and once for who is holding it).",
  "1 Peter 5:8": "Be sober, be vigilant; because your adversary the devil, as a roaring lion, walketh about, seeking whom he may devour (the lion is described roaring and walking, not attacking yet -- the danger named here is what comes next):",
  "1 Peter 5:9": "Whom resist stedfast in the faith, knowing that the same afflictions are accomplished in your brethren that are in the world (the readers are reminded here that they are not the only ones facing this).",
  "1 Peter 5:10": "But the God of all grace, who hath called us unto his eternal glory by Christ Jesus, after that ye have suffered a while, make you perfect, stablish, strengthen, settle you (four different verbs describe what God does after the suffering, not instead of it).",
  "1 Peter 5:12": "By Silvanus, a faithful brother unto you, as I suppose, I have written briefly, exhorting, and testifying that this is the true grace of God wherein ye stand (Peter calls his own letter brief, despite covering suffering, submission, holiness, and the end of all things).",
  "2 Peter 1:1": "Simon Peter, a servant and an apostle of Jesus Christ, to them that have obtained like precious faith with us through the righteousness of God and our Saviour Jesus Christ (he names servant before apostle, and states plainly that the faith he shares with his readers is not a lesser version of his own):",
  "2 Peter 1:2": "Grace and peace be multiplied unto you through the knowledge of God, and of Jesus our Lord, (the multiplying in view here runs through knowing someone, not performing something)",
  "2 Peter 1:3": "According as his divine power hath given unto us all things that pertain unto life and godliness, through the knowledge of him that hath called us to glory and virtue (everything needed is described as already given, not still being supplied):",
  "2 Peter 1:4": "Whereby are given unto us exceeding great and precious promises: that by these ye might be partakers of the divine nature, having escaped the corruption that is in the world through lust. (The promises are named as the means, and partaking of God's own nature as the result.)",
  "2 Peter 1:5": "And beside this, giving all diligence, add to your faith virtue; and to virtue knowledge; (the list that follows builds one item on top of the last, not side by side)",
  "2 Peter 1:6": "And to knowledge temperance; and to temperance patience; and to patience godliness; (the chain keeps extending, each quality supplying the next one down the line)",
  "2 Peter 1:7": "And to godliness brotherly kindness; and to brotherly kindness charity. (the list ends where Paul's own lists usually end -- with love.)",
  "2 Peter 1:8": "For if these things be in you, and abound, they make you that ye shall neither be barren nor unfruitful in the knowledge of our Lord Jesus Christ. (fruitfulness here is tied to how much these qualities are present, not merely whether they are.)",
  "2 Peter 1:9": "But he that lacketh these things is blind, and cannot see afar off, and hath forgotten that he was purged from his old sins. (the blindness described here is toward what has already happened to him, not toward something he still needs.)",
  "2 Peter 1:11": "For so an entrance shall be ministered unto you abundantly into the everlasting kingdom of our Lord and Saviour Jesus Christ (the word used for entrance is abundant, not merely sufficient).",
  "2 Peter 1:12": "Wherefore I will not be negligent to put you always in remembrance of these things, though ye know them, and be established in the present truth. (he repeats what they already know, on purpose, rather than assuming it no longer needs saying.)",
  "2 Peter 1:14": "Knowing that shortly I must put off this my tabernacle, even as our Lord Jesus Christ hath shewed me. (he names his own death in advance, and calls the body a tent he is about to leave, not a home.)",
  "2 Peter 1:16": "For we have not followed cunningly devised fables, when we made known unto you the power and coming of our Lord Jesus Christ, but were eyewitnesses of his majesty. (he answers the charge of inventing a story with the plain claim that he was there.)",
  "2 Peter 1:17": "For he received from God the Father honour and glory, when there came such a voice to him from the excellent glory, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased. (the same words spoken once at the Jordan are recorded here a second time, from a different mountain.)",
  "2 Peter 1:19": "We have also a more sure word of prophecy; whereunto ye do well that ye take heed, as unto a light that shineth in a dark place, until the day dawn, and the day star arise in your hearts (Scripture is called more sure than the very experience Peter just described witnessing firsthand):",
  "2 Peter 1:20": "Knowing this first, that no prophecy of the scripture is of any private interpretation. (no single reader is given authority to decide alone what a prophecy means.)",
  "2 Peter 1:21": "For the prophecy came not in old time by the will of man: but holy men of God spake as they were moved by the Holy Ghost. (the writers are named as moved, not as the ones deciding what to write.)",
  "2 Peter 2:1": "But there were false prophets also among the people, even as there shall be false teachers among you, who privily shall bring in damnable heresies, even denying the Lord that bought them, and bring upon themselves swift destruction. (the pattern is named as old, not new -- false teachers among Israel then, false teachers among the church now.)",
  "2 Peter 2:2": "And many shall follow their pernicious ways; by reason of whom the way of truth shall be evil spoken of. (the actual number who follow is named as many, not few.)",
  "2 Peter 2:3": "And through covetousness shall they with feigned words make merchandise of you: whose judgment now of a long time lingereth not, and their damnation slumbereth not. (the delay some might read as absence is described here as anything but idle.)",
  "2 Peter 2:4": "For if God spared not the angels that sinned, but cast them down to hell, and delivered them into chains of darkness, to be reserved unto judgment; (even angels are named as not exempt from this pattern)",
  "2 Peter 2:5": "And spared not the old world, but saved Noah the eighth person, a preacher of righteousness, bringing in the flood upon the world of the ungodly; (Noah is named a preacher first, and a survivor second.)",
  "2 Peter 2:7": "And delivered just Lot, vexed with the filthy conversation of the wicked: (the word used for Lot here is just, despite everything the rest of his story shows)",
  "2 Peter 2:9": "The Lord knoweth how to deliver the godly out of temptations, and to reserve the unjust unto the day of judgment to be punished (the same verse pictures deliverance for one group and a reserved judgment for the other, back to back):",
  "2 Peter 2:12": "But these, as natural brute beasts, made to be taken and destroyed, speak evil of the things that they understand not; and shall utterly perish in their own corruption; (the ridicule these men offer is aimed at something the text says they never actually understood.)",
  "2 Peter 2:13": "And shall receive the reward of unrighteousness, as they that count it pleasure to riot in the day time. Spots they are and blemishes, sporting themselves with their own deceivings while they feast with you; (the deception named here is aimed first at themselves, before it ever reaches anyone else.)",
  "2 Peter 2:15": "Which have forsaken the right way, and are gone astray, following the way of Balaam the son of Bosor, who loved the wages of unrighteousness; (the object of Balaam's love is named plainly -- the wages, not the work.)",
  "2 Peter 2:16": "But was rebuked for his iniquity: the dumb ass speaking with man’s voice forbad the madness of the prophet. (the rebuke in this story comes from the animal, not from any other prophet in the room.)",
  "2 Peter 2:17": "These are wells without water, clouds that are carried with a tempest; to whom the mist of darkness is reserved for ever. (both images name something that promises what it cannot deliver -- water, or rain.)",
  "2 Peter 2:18": "For when they speak great swelling words of vanity, they allure through the lusts of the flesh, through much wantonness, those that were clean escaped from them who live in error. (the targets named here are people who had already gotten out, not people who never left.)",
  "2 Peter 2:19": "While they promise them liberty, they themselves are the servants of corruption: for of whom a man is overcome, of the same is he brought in bondage. (the very thing they promise -- liberty -- is named as the one thing they do not have to give.)",
  "2 Peter 3:1": "This second epistle, beloved, I now write unto you; in both which I stir up your pure minds by way of remembrance (he calls this his second letter to the same readers, tying the two together on purpose):",
  "2 Peter 3:3": "Knowing this first, that there shall come in the last days scoffers, walking after their own lusts, (the scoffing described here is named as a sign to expect, not a surprise to fear)",
  "2 Peter 3:4": "And saying, Where is the promise of his coming? for since the fathers fell asleep, all things continue as they were from the beginning of the creation. (their argument rests entirely on nothing having changed yet.)",
  "2 Peter 3:5": "For this they willingly are ignorant of, that by the word of God the heavens were of old, and the earth standing out of the water and in the water (the ignorance named here is willing, not honest):",
  "2 Peter 3:6": "Whereby the world that then was, being overflowed with water, perished (the same word that once made the world is shown here to be able to unmake it):",
  "2 Peter 3:7": "But the heavens and the earth, which are now, by the same word are kept in store, reserved unto fire against the day of judgment and perdition of ungodly men. (the present world is described as being kept, on purpose, for a specific day still ahead.)",
  "2 Peter 3:8": "But, beloved, be not ignorant of this one thing, that one day is with the Lord as a thousand years, and a thousand years as one day. (the measurement being corrected here is not God's patience, but the reader's sense of scale.)",
  "2 Peter 3:9": "The Lord is not slack concerning his promise, as some men count slackness; but is longsuffering to us-ward, not willing that any should perish, but that all should come to repentance. (the delay some read as failure is named here as patience aimed at more people getting in.)",
  "2 Peter 3:10": "But the day of the Lord will come as a thief in the night; in the which the heavens shall pass away with a great noise, and the elements shall melt with fervent heat, the earth also and the works that are therein shall be burned up. (the same suddenness named earlier as a comfort becomes a warning here.)",
  "2 Peter 3:11": "Seeing then that all these things shall be dissolved, what manner of persons ought ye to be in all holy conversation and godliness, (the question that follows this fact is about conduct, not about calculation)",
  "2 Peter 3:13": "Nevertheless we, according to his promise, look for new heavens and a new earth, wherein dwelleth righteousness. (what is being waited for is not the absence of the old world, but the presence of righteousness in the new one.)",
  "2 Peter 3:14": "Wherefore, beloved, seeing that ye look for such things, be diligent that ye may be found of him in peace, without spot, and blameless. (the diligence named here is aimed at a future finding, not a present fear.)",
  "2 Peter 3:15": "And account that the longsuffering of our Lord is salvation; even as our beloved brother Paul also according to the wisdom given unto him hath written unto you; (Peter names Paul brother and credits his letters to wisdom given, not wisdom claimed.)",
  "2 Peter 3:16": "As also in all his epistles, speaking in them of these things; in which are some things hard to be understood, which they that are unlearned and unstable wrest, as they do also the other scriptures, unto their own destruction. (Peter puts Paul's letters in the same category as scripture, in the same sentence he admits some of it is hard.)",
  "2 Peter 3:17": "Ye therefore, beloved, seeing ye know these things before, beware lest ye also, being led away with the error of the wicked, fall from your own stedfastness. (the fall named here is a loss of steadiness, not a change of destination.)",
  "2 Peter 3:18": "But grow in grace, and in the knowledge of our Lord and Saviour Jesus Christ. To him be glory both now and for ever. Amen. (the letter closes on the same word it kept returning to throughout -- grace.)",
  "1 John 1:1": "That which was from the beginning, which we have heard, which we have seen with our eyes, which we have looked upon, and our hands have handled, of the Word of life (four different senses are named in a row -- hearing, seeing, looking, touching -- before John ever gets to his main verb);",
  "1 John 1:2": "(For the life was manifested, and we have seen it, and bear witness, and shew unto you that eternal life, which was with the Father, and was manifested unto us;) (the same event, manifested, is stated twice inside one sentence, framing everything in between.)",
  "1 John 1:3": "That which we have seen and heard declare we unto you, that ye also may have fellowship with us: and truly our fellowship is with the Father, and with his Son Jesus Christ. (the fellowship offered here runs two directions at once -- with the writer and with God.)",
  "1 John 1:5": "This then is the message which we have heard of him, and declare unto you, that God is light, and in him is no darkness at all. (the second half of the sentence rules out any exception before anyone can think to look for one).",
  "1 John 1:6": "If we say that we have fellowship with him, and walk in darkness, we lie, and do not the truth: (the claim and the conduct are set directly against each other, and the conduct is what settles which one is real)",
  "1 John 1:7": "But if we walk in the light, as he is in the light, we have fellowship one with another, and the blood of Jesus Christ his Son cleanseth us from all sin. (the cleansing named here runs continuously, in the present tense, not as a one-time transaction already filed away).",
  "1 John 1:8": "If we say that we have no sin, we deceive ourselves, and the truth is not in us. (the sin nature is what's being denied here, not any single act -- and the self-deception is named before the dishonesty).",
  "1 John 1:9": "If we confess our sins, he is faithful and just to forgive us our sins, and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness. (the word all appears twice in this sentence, and neither time does it come with an exception attached).",
  "1 John 1:10": "If we say that we have not sinned, we make him a liar, and his word is not in us. (claiming sinlessness is described here as calling God a liar, which is a much larger problem than the sin being denied).",
  "1 John 2:1": "My little children, these things write I unto you, that ye sin not. And if any man sin, we have an advocate with the Father, Jesus Christ the righteous: (the verse plans for both outcomes in the same breath -- the goal stated first, and the provision made in case the goal isn't met).",
  "1 John 2:2": "And he is the propitiation for our sins: and not for ours only, but also for the sins of the whole world. (the scope named here is deliberately wider than the audience actually reading the letter).",
  "1 John 2:3": "And hereby we do know that we know him, if we keep his commandments. (the knowing in view is proven by keeping, not replaced by it).",
  "1 John 2:4": "He that saith, I know him, and keepeth not his commandments, is a liar, and the truth is not in him. (the mismatch between claim and conduct is called a lie, not a lapse).",
  "1 John 2:5": "But whoso keepeth his word, in him verily is the love of God perfected: hereby know we that we are in him. (the perfecting named here belongs to God's love, not to the person doing the keeping).",
  "1 John 2:8": "Again, a new commandment I write unto you, which thing is true in him and in you: because the darkness is past, and the true light now shineth. (the same commandment is called both old and new in successive verses -- true in Christ first, and true in the reader only because of that).",
  "1 John 2:9": "He that saith he is in the light, and hateth his brother, is in darkness even until now. (the claim to be in the light is answered directly by the hatred that contradicts it).",
  "1 John 2:11": "But he that hateth his brother is in darkness, and walketh in darkness, and knoweth not whither he goeth, because that darkness hath blinded his eyes. (the word darkness appears three times in one verse, describing a location, a direction, and a blindness, all from the same cause).",
  "1 John 2:12": "I write unto you, little children, because your sins are forgiven you for his name’s sake. (the forgiveness is credited to his name, not to anything the reader contributed).",
  "1 John 2:14": "I have written unto you, fathers, because ye have known him that is from the beginning. I have written unto you, young men, because ye are strong, and the word of God abideth in you, and ye have overcome the wicked one. (three different groups get three different reasons, and none of the reasons is a command to try harder).",
  "1 John 2:15": "Love not the world, neither the things that are in the world. If any man love the world, the love of the Father is not in him. (the two loves named here are described as occupying the same space, so one has to be absent for the other to be present).",
  "1 John 2:17": "And the world passeth away, and the lust thereof: but he that doeth the will of God abideth for ever. (one side of this sentence is described as temporary, and the other side is the only side given a future).",
  "1 John 2:19": "They went out from us, but they were not of us; for if they had been of us, they would no doubt have continued with us: but they went out, that they might be made manifest that they were not all of us. (the departure is treated as evidence of what was already true, not as the cause of a new condition).",
  "1 John 2:22": "Who is a liar but he that denieth that Jesus is the Christ? He is antichrist, that denieth the Father and the Son. (denying the Son is treated here as the same act as denying the Father, not a lesser version of it).",
  "1 John 2:24": "Let that therefore abide in you, which ye have heard from the beginning. If that which ye have heard from the beginning shall remain in you, ye also shall continue in the Son, and in the Father. (what's asked to remain here is the original message, not an improved one).",
  "1 John 2:25": "And this is the promise that he hath promised us, even eternal life. (the promise gets one word to itself, with nothing attached to qualify it).",
  "1 John 2:27": "But the anointing which ye have received of him abideth in you, and ye need not that any man teach you: but as the same anointing teacheth you of all things, and is truth, and is no lie, and even as it hath taught you, ye shall abide in him. (the anointing is described as already given and already abiding, not as something still being sought).",
  "1 John 2:28": "And now, little children, abide in him; that, when he shall appear, we may have confidence, and not be ashamed before him at his coming. (the shame in view here belongs to his coming, not to whether the reader belongs to him).",
  "1 John 2:29": "If ye know that he is righteous, ye know that every one that doeth righteousness is born of him. (the doing named here is evidence of the birth, not the cause of it).",
  "1 John 3:1": "Behold, what manner of love the Father hath bestowed upon us, that we should be called the sons of God: therefore the world knoweth us not, because it knew him not. (the title given here is bestowed, not earned, and the world's blindness to it is explained by an older blindness).",
  "1 John 3:2": "Beloved, now are we the sons of God, and it doth not yet appear what we shall be: but we know that, when he shall appear, we shall be like him; for we shall see him as he is. (sonship is stated as a present fact in the same sentence that admits the future form of it hasn't been seen yet).",
  "1 John 3:3": "And every man that hath this hope in him purifieth himself, even as he is pure. (the purifying described here follows the hope, it doesn't produce it).",
  "1 John 3:5": "And ye know that he was manifested to take away our sins; and in him is no sin. (the two halves of this verse name what he came to remove and what was never in him to begin with).",
  "1 John 3:6": "Whosoever abideth in him sinneth not: whosoever sinneth hath not seen him, neither known him. (the sinning in view here is the habitual kind John names elsewhere as walking in darkness, not the isolated act he has already told the reader to confess).",
  "1 John 3:8": "He that committeth sin is of the devil; for the devil sinneth from the beginning. For this purpose the Son of God was manifested, that he might destroy the works of the devil. (the last sentence gives the reason for the entire Incarnation in a single clause).",
  "1 John 3:9": "Whosoever is born of God doth not commit sin; for his seed remaineth in him: and he cannot sin, because he is born of God. (this is the same practicing-sin John already distinguished from an isolated act -- a seed remaining describes a settled condition, not a single failure).",
  "1 John 3:10": "In this the children of God are manifest, and the children of the devil: whosoever doeth not righteousness is not of God, neither he that loveth not his brother. (two separate tests are given in the same verse, and both are about what's actually done, not what's claimed).",
  "1 John 3:12": "Not as Cain, who was of that wicked one, and slew his brother. And wherefore slew he him? Because his own works were evil, and his brother’s righteous. (the murder is explained here as the result of the works, not the other way around).",
  "1 John 3:14": "We know that we have passed from death unto life, because we love the brethren. He that loveth not his brother abideth in death. (the same word, abideth, that elsewhere describes a settled relationship with God is used here to describe the opposite state).",
  "1 John 3:15": "Whosoever hateth his brother is a murderer: and ye know that no murderer hath eternal life abiding in him. (the diagnosis given here is about what's abiding, or not abiding, over time, not about a single moment of anger).",
  "1 John 3:17": "But whoso hath this world’s good, and seeth his brother have need, and shutteth up his bowels of compassion from him, how dwelleth the love of God in him? (the question is left as a question, with the answer assumed rather than stated).",
  "1 John 3:18": "My little children, let us not love in word, neither in tongue; but in deed and in truth. (two pairs are set against each other -- word and tongue on one side, deed and truth on the other).",
  "1 John 3:20": "For if our heart condemn us, God is greater than our heart, and knoweth all things. (God's knowledge is named as larger than the accusation, not as a second witness for the prosecution).",
  "1 John 3:21": "Beloved, if our heart condemn us not, then have we confidence toward God. (the confidence named here is the absence of an accusation, not the presence of a perfect record).",
  "1 John 3:23": "And this is his commandment, That we should believe on the name of his Son Jesus Christ, and love one another, as he gave us commandment. (the commandment named in the singular turns out to have two parts, believing and loving, given together).",
  "1 John 3:24": "And he that keepeth his commandments dwelleth in him, and he in him. And hereby we know that he abideth in us, by the Spirit which he hath given us. (the proof offered for this dwelling is not a feeling, but a Person already given).",
  "1 John 4:1": "Beloved, believe not every spirit, but try the spirits whether they are of God: because many false prophets are gone out into the world. (the command is not to distrust everything, but to test before accepting).",
  "1 John 4:2": "Hereby know ye the Spirit of God: Every spirit that confesseth that Jesus Christ is come in the flesh is of God: (the single test given here is about the Incarnation, not a checklist of doctrines).",
  "1 John 4:3": "And every spirit that confesseth not that Jesus Christ is come in the flesh is not of God: and this is that spirit of antichrist, whereof ye have heard that it should come; and even now already is it in the world. (the spirit John's readers were told to expect in the future is named here as already present).",
  "1 John 4:4": "Ye are of God, little children, and have overcome them: because greater is he that is in you, than he that is in the world. (the overcoming is credited to a comparison of size, not a contest of effort).",
  "1 John 4:7": "Beloved, let us love one another: for love is of God; and every one that loveth is born of God, and knoweth God. (loving is named here as evidence of the birth, appearing in the same breath as knowing God).",
  "1 John 4:8": "He that loveth not knoweth not God; for God is love. (the reason given is not a rule to follow, but a description of what God actually is).",
  "1 John 4:9": "In this was manifested the love of God toward us, because that God sent his only begotten Son into the world, that we might live through him. (the love is not described in the abstract -- it's identified with one specific action).",
  "1 John 4:10": "Herein is love, not that we loved God, but that he loved us, and sent his Son to be the propitiation for our sins. (the direction of the love in this sentence runs one way before it can run the other).",
  "1 John 4:12": "No man hath seen God at any time. If we love one another, God dwelleth in us, and his love is perfected in us. (an invisible God is made visible in this verse by a very visible act).",
  "1 John 4:15": "Whosoever shall confess that Jesus is the Son of God, God dwelleth in him, and he in God. (the dwelling described here goes in both directions at once).",
  "1 John 4:16": "And we have known and believed the love that God hath to us. God is love; and he that dwelleth in love dwelleth in God, and God in him. (knowing and believing are named together, as if one without the other would be incomplete).",
  "1 John 4:17": "Herein is our love made perfect, that we may have boldness in the day of judgment: because as he is, so are we in this world. (the boldness in view belongs to a coming day, but the reason given for it is a present-tense fact).",
  "1 John 4:18": "There is no fear in love; but perfect love casteth out fear: because fear hath torment. He that feareth is not made perfect in love. (fear and love are described as unable to occupy the same space, the same way light and darkness were earlier in the letter).",
  "1 John 4:19": "We love him, because he first loved us. (the order given here matters -- his love is stated before ours, and ours is made to depend on it).",
  "1 John 4:20": "If a man say, I love God, and hateth his brother, he is a liar: for he that loveth not his brother whom he hath seen, how can he love God whom he hath not seen? (the argument moves from the visible to the invisible, not the other way around).",
  "1 John 5:1": "Whosoever believeth that Jesus is the Christ is born of God: and every one that loveth him that begat loveth him also that is begotten of him. (the same logic runs through the whole verse -- love for the Father is shown to include love for everyone else the Father has fathered).",
  "1 John 5:3": "For this is the love of God, that we keep his commandments: and his commandments are not grievous. (the second half answers an objection the first half doesn't even raise).",
  "1 John 5:4": "For whatsoever is born of God overcometh the world: and this is the victory that overcometh the world, even our faith. (the victory named at the end of this verse is not a future hope -- it's identified with something the reader already has).",
  "1 John 5:5": "Who is he that overcometh the world, but he that believeth that Jesus is the Son of God? (the question is framed so there's only one possible answer).",
  "1 John 5:6": "This is he that came by water and blood, even Jesus Christ; not by water only, but by water and blood. And it is the Spirit that beareth witness, because the Spirit is truth. (two different things are named as necessary, not just one).",
  "1 John 5:9": "If we receive the witness of men, the witness of God is greater: for this is the witness of God which he hath testified of his Son. (the comparison runs from the lesser case to the greater one).",
  "1 John 5:10": "He that believeth on the Son of God hath the witness in himself: he that believeth not God hath made him a liar; because he believeth not the record that God gave of his Son. (the same verb, believeth, decides which side of this verse a person lands on).",
  "1 John 5:11": "And this is the record, that God hath given to us eternal life, and this life is in his Son. (the record named here is a fact already given, not a promise still pending).",
  "1 John 5:12": "He that hath the Son hath life; and he that hath not the Son of God hath not life. (the verse is built entirely on one verb, hath, repeated on both sides of the same sentence).",
  "1 John 5:13": "These things have I written unto you that believe on the name of the Son of God; that ye may know that ye have eternal life, and that ye may believe on the name of the Son of God. (the stated purpose of the whole letter is knowing, not wondering).",
  "1 John 5:16": "If any man see his brother sin a sin which is not unto death, he shall ask, and he shall give him life for them that sin not unto death. There is a sin unto death: I do not say that he shall pray for it. (the letter draws a real distinction between two categories here without naming exactly where the line falls).",
  "1 John 5:17": "All unrighteousness is sin: and there is a sin not unto death. (the sentence closes on the category that does not end in death, not the one that does).",
  "1 John 5:18": "We know that whosoever is born of God sinneth not; but he that is begotten of God keepeth himself, and that wicked one toucheth him not. (the keeping named in this verse is done by the one begotten of God, not left to the reader alone).",
  "1 John 5:19": "And we know that we are of God, and the whole world lieth in wickedness. (the contrast drawn here is total, with no middle ground offered).",
  "1 John 5:20": "And we know that the Son of God is come, and hath given us an understanding, that we may know him that is true, and we are in him that is true, even in his Son Jesus Christ. This is the true God, and eternal life. (the letter's last major statement closes by naming Christ as both the true God and eternal life in the same sentence).",
  "1 John 5:21": "Little children, keep yourselves from idols. Amen. (the letter that opened with the Word made flesh closes with a warning about anything that would replace him).",
  "2 John 1:1": "The elder unto the elect lady and her children, whom I love in the truth; and not I only, but also all they that have known the truth; (he signs as elder, not apostle -- the same man, but a different title for a smaller audience).",
  "2 John 1:2": "For the truth's sake, which dwelleth in us, and shall be with us for ever. (the thing named as permanent here is truth itself, not the relationship built on it).",
  "2 John 1:3": "Grace be with you, mercy, and peace, from God the Father, and from the Lord Jesus Christ, the Son of the Father, in truth and love. (three gifts are named together here, and all three are traced back to the same source).",
  "2 John 1:4": "I rejoiced greatly that I found of thy children walking in truth, as we have received a commandment from the Father. (their walk is described as evidence found, not a report taken on faith).",
  "2 John 1:5": "And now I beseech thee, lady, not as though I wrote a new commandment unto thee, but that which we had from the beginning, that we love one another. (he is careful to say this instruction is old, not something he is introducing).",
  "2 John 1:6": "And this is love, that we walk after his commandments. This is the commandment, That, as ye have heard from the beginning, ye should walk in it. (love and obedience are named here as the same motion, not two separate things).",
  "2 John 1:7": "For many deceivers are entered into the world, who confess not that Jesus Christ is come in the flesh. This is a deceiver and an antichrist. (the test named for spotting deception is one question about the Incarnation, not a list).",
  "2 John 1:8": "Look to yourselves, that we lose not those things which we have wrought, but that we receive a full reward. (what is at stake here is named as reward, not standing).",
  "2 John 1:10": "If there come any unto you, and bring not this doctrine, receive him not into your house, neither bid him God speed: (the instruction concerns hospitality, not anyone's salvation).",
  "2 John 1:11": "For he that biddeth him God speed is partaker of his evil deeds. (offering encouragement is treated here as participation, not neutrality).",
  "2 John 1:12": "Having many things to write unto you, I would not write with paper and ink: but I trust to come unto you, and speak face to face, that our joy may be full. (he saves the fuller conversation for presence, not for the page).",
  "2 John 1:13": "The children of thy elect sister greet thee. Amen. (the letter closes by widening the family it began with, one more relative added at the very end).",
  "3 John 1:1": "The elder unto the wellbeloved Gaius, whom I love in the truth. (he signs with the same title he used in the letter before this one).",
  "3 John 1:2": "Beloved, I wish above all things that thou mayest prosper and be in health, even as thy soul prospereth. (three kinds of prosperity are named in one sentence -- financial, physical, and spiritual -- and the last one sets the pace for the other two).",
  "3 John 1:3": "For I rejoiced greatly, when the brethren came and testified of the truth that is in thee, even as thou walkest in the truth. (the report reaching him is not secondhand opinion, it is testimony from people who saw it firsthand).",
  "3 John 1:4": "I have no greater joy than to hear that my children walk in truth. (of every possible report he could receive, he names this one as the one that outranks all the others).",
  "3 John 1:5": "Beloved, thou doest faithfully whatsoever thou doest to the brethren, and to strangers; (the kindness extends past people he knows to people he has never met)",
  "3 John 1:6": "Which have borne witness of thy charity before the church: whom if thou bring forward on their journey after a godly sort, thou shalt do well: (funding a traveling preacher's journey is named here as doing well, not merely being generous)",
  "3 John 1:7": "Because that for his name's sake they went forth, taking nothing of the Gentiles. (they refused support from the very people they were reaching, so the report of their motives would stay clean).",
  "3 John 1:8": "We therefore ought to receive such, that we might be fellowhelpers to the truth. (giving support to a preacher is described as sharing directly in the work, not just funding it from a distance).",
  "3 John 1:9": "I wrote unto the church: but Diotrephes, who loveth to have the preeminence among them, receiveth us not. (the man is named outright, not left as an anonymous problem).",
  "3 John 1:10": "Wherefore, if I come, I will remember his deeds which he doeth, prating against us with malicious words: and not content therewith, neither doth he himself receive the brethren, and forbiddeth them that would, and casteth them out of the church. (three separate offenses are listed, each one worse than refusing hospitality alone).",
  "3 John 1:11": "Beloved, follow not that which is evil, but that which is good. He that doeth good is of God: but he that doeth evil hath not seen God. (the sentence leaves no room for a third category between the two).",
  "3 John 1:12": "Demetrius hath good report of all men, and of the truth itself: yea, and we also bear record; and ye know that our record is true. (three separate witnesses are stacked behind the same man's character).",
  "3 John 1:14": "But I trust I shall shortly see thee, and we shall speak face to face. Peace be to thee. Our friends salute thee. Greet the friends by name. (the letter closes by asking for names to be used, not a general greeting passed along).",
  "Jude 1:1": "Jude, the servant of Jesus Christ, and brother of James, to them that are sanctified by God the Father, and preserved in Jesus Christ, and called: (the letter opens by naming who is doing the keeping -- the Father preserving, not the reader holding on).",
  "Jude 1:2": "Mercy unto you, and peace, and love, be multiplied. (three things are wished, and all three are asked to multiply, not merely to arrive).",
  "Jude 1:3": "Beloved, when I gave all diligence to write unto you of the common salvation, it was needful for me to write unto you, and exhort you that ye should earnestly contend for the faith which was once delivered unto the saints. (he had planned a different letter and switched course -- the urgency of what follows changed his mind mid-thought).",
  "Jude 1:4": "For there are certain men crept in unawares, who were before of old ordained to this condemnation, ungodly men, turning the grace of our God into lasciviousness, and denying the only Lord God, and our Lord Jesus Christ. (grace is described here as something twisted into an excuse, not something that failed).",
  "Jude 1:5": "I will therefore put you in remembrance, though ye once knew this, how that the Lord, having saved the people out of the land of Egypt, afterward destroyed them that believed not. (the example chosen is a generation already delivered -- the danger named is unbelief afterward, not the rescue itself failing).",
  "Jude 1:6": "And the angels which kept not their first estate, but left their own habitation, he hath reserved in everlasting chains under darkness unto the judgment of the great day. (their sentence is already fixed -- what remains is the date, not the verdict).",
  "Jude 1:7": "Even as Sodom and Gomorrha, and the cities about them in like manner, giving themselves over to fornication, and going after strange flesh, are set forth for an example, suffering the vengeance of eternal fire. (the word example is doing the work in this verse -- these cities are named as a warning display, not an isolated incident).",
  "Jude 1:8": "Likewise also these filthy dreamers defile the flesh, despise dominion, and speak evil of dignities. (three separate targets are named for the same disrespect -- the flesh, authority, and dignity all get the same treatment).",
  "Jude 1:9": "Yet Michael the archangel, when contending with the devil he disputed about the body of Moses, durst not bring against him a railing accusation, but said, The Lord rebuke thee. (even an archangel refuses to trade insults with Satan directly, leaving the rebuke to someone with higher authority).",
  "Jude 1:10": "But these speak evil of those things which they know not: but what they know naturally, as brute beasts, in those things they corrupt themselves. (the very things they claim to understand turn out to be what damages them most).",
  "Jude 1:11": "Woe unto them! for they have gone in the way of Cain, and ran greedily after the error of Balaam for reward, and perished in the gainsaying of Core. (three Old Testament names are stacked in one sentence, each supplying a different flavor of the same rebellion).",
  "Jude 1:12": "These are spots in your feasts of charity, when they feast with you, feeding themselves without fear: clouds they are without water, carried about of winds; trees whose fruit withereth, without fruit, twice dead, plucked up by the roots; (every image piled up in this verse promises something -- rain, fruit, shelter -- and delivers none of it)",
  "Jude 1:13": "Raging waves of the sea, foaming out their own shame; wandering stars, to whom is reserved the blackness of darkness for ever. (a wandering star is named here for failing at the one thing a star is supposed to do -- point the way).",
  "Jude 1:14": "And Enoch also, the seventh from Adam, prophesied of these, saying, Behold, the Lord cometh with ten thousands of his saints, (Jude reaches past the entire Old Testament canon to the seventh man from Adam for this quotation),",
  "Jude 1:15": "To execute judgment upon all, and to convince all that are ungodly among them of all their ungodly deeds which they have ungodly committed, and of all their hard speeches which ungodly sinners have spoken against him. (the word ungodly appears four times in a single verse -- the repetition is the point, not an accident).",
  "Jude 1:16": "These are murmurers, complainers, walking after their own lusts; and their mouth speaketh great swelling words, having men's persons in admiration because of advantage. (their flattery is named here as a business strategy, not a personality trait).",
  "Jude 1:18": "How that they told you there should be mockers in the last time, who should walk after their own ungodly lusts. (the apostles are described as having already warned about this exact scenario, not reacting to something unforeseen).",
  "Jude 1:19": "These be they who separate themselves, sensual, having not the Spirit. (the separation named here is their own doing, not something done to them).",
  "Jude 1:20": "But ye, beloved, building up yourselves on your most holy faith, praying in the Holy Ghost, (the same word used for the false teachers' corruption two verses earlier is reversed here into building),",
  "Jude 1:21": "Keep yourselves in the love of God, looking for the mercy of our Lord Jesus Christ unto eternal life. (staying within a love already given is the picture used here, not earning entry into one still withheld).",
  "Jude 1:22": "And of some have compassion, making a difference: (the instruction assumes not every case calls for the same response)",
  "Jude 1:23": "And others save with fear, pulling them out of the fire; hating even the garment spotted by the flesh. (the image is rescue from an active fire, not judgment of a finished case).",
  "Jude 1:24": "Now unto him that is able to keep you from falling, and to present you faultless before the presence of his glory with exceeding joy, (the letter that spent twenty verses on people who fell closes by naming the one who is able to keep anyone from doing the same),",
  "Jude 1:25": "To the only wise God our Saviour, be glory and majesty, dominion and power, both now and ever. Amen. (the benediction assigns every kind of praise available to the same single recipient).",
  "Revelation 1:1": "The Revelation of Jesus Christ, which God gave unto him, to shew unto his servants things which must shortly come to pass; and he sent and signified it by his angel unto his servant John: (the revelation belongs to Christ himself, with John serving only as the one who recorded what was shown him, and its scope runs from the church age forward).",
  "Revelation 1:2": "Who bare record of the word of God, and of the testimony of Jesus Christ, and of all things that he saw. (John presents himself only as a witness, faithfully recording what he was shown).",
  "Revelation 1:3": "Blessed is he that readeth, and they that hear the words of this prophecy, and keep those things which are written therein: for the time is at hand. (the blessing rests on receiving and holding to what is written, not on achieving it).",
  "Revelation 1:4": "John to the seven churches which are in Asia: Grace be unto you, and peace, from him which is, and which was, and which is to come; and from the seven Spirits which are before his throne; (these seven churches were chosen to picture the whole church age, and grace and peace are named first because both come from what Christ has already done).",
  "Revelation 1:5": "And from Jesus Christ, who is the faithful witness, and the first begotten of the dead, and the prince of the kings of the earth. Unto him that loved us, and washed us from our sins in his own blood, (the washing is accomplished by his blood alone, at the Cross, not by anything added afterward),",
  "Revelation 1:6": "And hath made us kings and priests unto God and his Father; to him be glory and dominion for ever and ever. Amen. (this standing as kings and priests is entirely his doing, made possible by the Cross).",
  "Revelation 1:7": "Behold, he cometh with clouds; and every eye shall see him, and they also which pierced him: and all kindreds of the earth shall wail because of him. Even so, Amen. (the Second Coming is the great theme of this book, and every eye will finally see who he is).",
  "Revelation 1:8": "I am Alpha and Omega, the beginning and the ending, saith the Lord, which is, and which was, and which is to come, the Almighty. (this title guarantees that everything else the book declares will in fact come to pass).",
  "Revelation 1:9": "I John, who also am your brother, and companion in tribulation, and in the kingdom and patience of Jesus Christ, was in the isle that is called Patmos, for the word of God, and for the testimony of Jesus Christ. (John's exile was for his stand for Christ, not for any wrongdoing).",
  "Revelation 1:10": "I was in the Spirit on the Lord's day, and heard behind me a great voice, as of a trumpet, (this is the same voice of the Lord that has spoken to his people across the ages),",
  "Revelation 1:11": "Saying, I am Alpha and Omega, the first and the last: and, What thou seest, write in a book, and send it unto the seven churches which are in Asia; unto Ephesus, and unto Smyrna, and unto Pergamos, and unto Thyatira, and unto Sardis, and unto Philadelphia, and unto Laodicea. (these seven churches were each handpicked by Christ himself).",
  "Revelation 1:12": "And I turned to see the voice that spake with me. And being turned, I saw seven golden candlesticks; (the candlesticks picture the seven churches)",
  "Revelation 1:13": "And in the midst of the seven candlesticks one like unto the Son of man, clothed with a garment down to the foot, and girt about the paps with a golden girdle. (the robe and girdle present Christ as King and Priest, standing in the midst of his church).",
  "Revelation 1:14": "His head and his hairs were white like wool, as white as snow; and his eyes were as a flame of fire; (white speaks of his majesty, and the eyes of fire speak of his penetrating judgment)",
  "Revelation 1:15": "And his feet like unto fine brass, as if they burned in a furnace; and his voice as the sound of many waters. (brass pictures his true humanity carried through fire, and the voice pictures overwhelming power).",
  "Revelation 1:16": "And he had in his right hand seven stars: and out of his mouth went a sharp twoedged sword: and his countenance was as the sun shineth in his strength. (the stars represent the pastors of the seven churches, held in his hand, and the sword represents the word he speaks).",
  "Revelation 1:17": "And when I saw him, I fell at his feet as dead. And he laid his right hand upon me, saying unto me, Fear not; I am the first and the last: (John's fear is met immediately with reassurance, not rebuke)",
  "Revelation 1:18": "I am he that liveth, and was dead; and, behold, I am alive for evermore, Amen; and have the keys of hell and of death. (having conquered death himself, he alone holds authority over it).",
  "Revelation 1:19": "Write the things which thou hast seen, and the things which are, and the things which shall be hereafter; (this verse outlines the whole book: Christ among the candlesticks, the present church age, and the events that follow)",
  "Revelation 1:20": "The mystery of the seven stars which thou sawest in my right hand, and the seven golden candlesticks. The seven stars are the angels of the seven churches: and the seven candlesticks which thou sawest are the seven churches. (the pastors belong to Christ, held safely in his hand, just as the churches themselves do).",
  "Revelation 2:1": "Unto the angel of the church of Ephesus write; These things saith he that holdeth the seven stars in his right hand, who walketh in the midst of the seven golden candlesticks; (Ephesus pictures the apostolic era, and Christ is shown walking among his churches, not distant from them)",
  "Revelation 2:2": "I know thy works, and thy labour, and thy patience, and how thou canst not bear them which are evil: and thou hast tried them which say they are apostles, and are not, and hast found them liars: (this church tested false teaching carefully and held to sound doctrine)",
  "Revelation 2:3": "And hast borne, and hast patience, and for my name's sake hast laboured, and hast not fainted. (their endurance for his name is commended).",
  "Revelation 2:4": "Nevertheless I have somewhat against thee, because thou hast left thy first love. (the wording is 'left,' not 'lost' -- this is a departure from resting in Christ and the Cross, not an accident).",
  "Revelation 2:5": "Remember therefore from whence thou art fallen, and repent, and do the first works; or else I will come unto thee quickly, and will remove thy candlestick out of his place, except thou repent. (what is at stake is the church's lampstand -- its corporate witness and place among the churches -- not any individual believer's standing before God; a light removed is a testimony gone dark, and the remedy is simply to return to depending on Christ).",
  "Revelation 2:6": "But this thou hast, that thou hatest the deeds of the Nicolaitans, which I also hate. (the Nicolaitans exploited the people rather than building them up, and the Lord commends this church for rejecting that).",
  "Revelation 2:7": "He that hath an ear, let him hear what the Spirit saith unto the churches; To him that overcometh will I give to eat of the tree of life, which is in the midst of the paradise of God. (the overcomer is simply the one whose faith rests in Christ and the Cross, which is what opens the door for the Spirit to work).",
  "Revelation 2:8": "And unto the angel of the church in Smyrna write; These things saith the first and the last, which was dead, and is alive; (Smyrna pictures the martyr era of the church, and Christ identifies himself here by his own death and resurrection)",
  "Revelation 2:9": "I know thy works, and tribulation, and poverty, (but thou art rich) and I know the blasphemy of them which say they are Jews, and are not, but are the synagogue of Satan. (what looked like poverty from the outside was, in God's eyes, real riches).",
  "Revelation 2:10": "Fear none of those things which thou shalt suffer: behold, the devil shall cast some of you into prison, that ye may be tried; and ye shall have tribulation ten days: be thou faithful unto death, and I will give thee a crown of life. (the crown promised here is a reward for faithfulness, held out to encourage endurance, not a threat of losing salvation).",
  "Revelation 2:11": "He that hath an ear, let him hear what the Spirit saith unto the churches; He that overcometh shall not be hurt of the second death. (the second death is the lake of fire, and this promise is entirely positive -- the overcomer need never fear it).",
  "Revelation 2:12": "And to the angel of the church in Pergamos write; These things saith he which hath the sharp sword with two edges; (Pergamos pictures the era when the church gained state favor, and Christ addresses it with the sword of his word)",
  "Revelation 2:13": "I know thy works, and where thou dwellest, even where Satan's seat is: and thou holdest fast my name, and hast not denied my faith, even in those days wherein Antipas was my faithful martyr, who was slain among you, where Satan dwelleth. (despite the hostile setting, some in this church held to Christ).",
  "Revelation 2:14": "But I have a few things against thee, because thou hast there them that hold the doctrine of Balaam, who taught Balac to cast a stumblingblock before the children of Israel, to eat things sacrificed unto idols, and to commit fornication. (this church had allowed a shift in its faith away from the Cross toward compromise, pictured here as spiritual adultery).",
  "Revelation 2:15": "So hast thou also them that hold the doctrine of the Nicolaitans, which thing I hate. (this describes an unscriptural pattern of church leadership that exploited rather than served the people).",
  "Revelation 2:16": "Repent; or else I will come unto thee quickly, and will fight against them with the sword of my mouth. (the sword here is his word, brought to bear in judgment against this compromise).",
  "Revelation 2:17": "He that hath an ear, let him hear what the Spirit saith unto the churches; To him that overcometh will I give to eat of the hidden manna, and will give him a white stone, and in the stone a new name written, which no man knoweth saving he that receiveth it. (this promises believers a new name reflecting their complete renewal).",
  "Revelation 2:18": "And unto the angel of the church in Thyatira write; These things saith the Son of God, who hath his eyes like unto a flame of fire, and his feet are like fine brass; (Thyatira pictures the era in which the church drifted into idol worship, so Christ appears here in the role of judge)",
  "Revelation 2:19": "I know thy works, and charity, and service, and faith, and thy patience, and thy works; and the last to be more than the first. (some in this church did still love the Lord genuinely, even amid its failures).",
  "Revelation 2:20": "Notwithstanding I have a few things against thee, because thou sufferest that woman Jezebel, which calleth herself a prophetess, to teach and to seduce my servants to commit fornication, and to eat things sacrificed unto idols. (the fornication in view is spiritual -- forsaking Christ and the Cross for something else -- pictured here through this false teacher).",
  "Revelation 2:21": "And I gave her space to repent of her fornication; and she repented not. (the opportunity to turn back was given and refused).",
  "Revelation 2:22": "Behold, I will cast her into a bed, and them that commit adultery with her into great tribulation, except they repent of their deeds. (the judgment here falls on a false teacher and those who deliberately follow her false doctrine, not on believers generally).",
  "Revelation 2:23": "And I will kill her children with death; and all the churches shall know that I am he which searcheth the reins and hearts: and I will give unto every one of you according to your works. (works are simply the visible evidence the Lord reads -- what a person truly trusts always shows in what they do).",
  "Revelation 2:24": "But unto you I say, and unto the rest in Thyatira, as many as have not this doctrine, and which have not known the depths of Satan, as they speak; I will put upon you none other burden. (those who refused this false teaching are given no further demand).",
  "Revelation 2:25": "But that which ye have already hold fast till I come. (the instruction is simply to keep trusting Christ until he returns).",
  "Revelation 2:26": "And he that overcometh, and keepeth my works unto the end, to him will I give power over the nations: (it is specifically his works credited to the overcomer, not the believer's own -- the reward flows from what Christ accomplished)",
  "Revelation 2:27": "And he shall rule them with a rod of iron; as the vessels of a potter shall they be broken to shivers: even as I received of my Father. (this pictures the coming kingdom age, when the redeemed will share in Christ's authority).",
  "Revelation 2:28": "And I will give him the morning star. (the morning star pictures the glory of the age still to come).",
  "Revelation 2:29": "He that hath an ear, let him hear what the Spirit saith unto the churches. (this refrain closes each letter, calling every church to listen).",
  "Revelation 3:1": "And unto the angel of the church in Sardis write; These things saith he that hath the seven Spirits of God, and the seven stars; I know thy works, that thou hast a name that thou livest, and art dead. (Sardis pictures a church with a reputation for life that had actually gone spiritually dead).",
  "Revelation 3:2": "Be watchful, and strengthen the things which remain, that are ready to die: for I have not found thy works perfect before God. (works built on a dead faith will always fall short).",
  "Revelation 3:3": "Remember therefore how thou hast received and heard, and hold fast, and repent. If therefore thou shalt not watch, I will come on thee as a thief, and thou shalt not know what hour I will come upon thee. (the call is simply to return to what was first received).",
  "Revelation 3:4": "Thou hast a few names even in Sardis which have not defiled their garments; and they shall walk with me in white: for they are worthy. (this worthiness is not self-generated -- it comes only through faith in Christ and the Cross).",
  "Revelation 3:5": "He that overcometh, the same shall be clothed in white raiment; and I will not blot out his name out of the book of life, but I will confess his name before my Father, and before his angels. (the white raiment named here is Christ's own righteousness, granted through faith in the Cross, and the whole promise culminates in Christ owning the overcomer before the Father).",
  "Revelation 3:6": "He that hath an ear, let him hear what the Spirit saith unto the churches. (the same refrain closes this letter as well).",
  "Revelation 3:7": "And to the angel of the church in Philadelphia write; These things saith he that is holy, he that is true, he that hath the key of David, he that openeth, and no man shutteth; and shutteth, and no man openeth; (Philadelphia pictures the missionary era of the church, and Christ here holds total authority over every open door)",
  "Revelation 3:8": "I know thy works: behold, I have set before thee an open door, and no man can shut it: for thou hast a little strength, and hast kept my word, and hast not denied my name. (little strength refers to their small numbers, not spiritual weakness -- this small church still carried the gospel to the world).",
  "Revelation 3:9": "Behold, I will make them of the synagogue of Satan, which say they are Jews, and are not, but do lie; behold, I will make them to come and worship before thy feet, and to know that I have loved thee. (this looks ahead to the kingdom age, when Israel will be restored and will bow before Christ).",
  "Revelation 3:10": "Because thou hast kept the word of my patience, I also will keep thee from the hour of temptation, which shall come upon all the world, to try them that dwell upon the earth. (this promises the church will be removed before the great tribulation comes upon the earth).",
  "Revelation 3:11": "Behold, I come quickly: hold that fast which thou hast, that no man take thy crown. (the crown in view is a reward for faithfulness, and the warning is that others might try to distract believers from Christ and the Cross, not that salvation itself is at risk).",
  "Revelation 3:12": "Him that overcometh will I make a pillar in the temple of my God, and he shall go no more out: and I will write upon him the name of my God, and the name of the city of my God, which is new Jerusalem, which cometh down out of heaven from my God: and I will write upon him my new name. (at the Cross Christ took on our sin; here he shares with the overcomer his own most excellent standing).",
  "Revelation 3:13": "He that hath an ear, let him hear what the Spirit saith unto the churches. (again the call is simply to listen).",
  "Revelation 3:14": "And unto the angel of the church of the Laodiceans write; These things saith the Amen, the faithful and true witness, the beginning of the creation of God; (Laodicea pictures the final, apostate era of the church, addressed by Christ as the one true and faithful witness, in contrast to this church's own unfaithfulness)",
  "Revelation 3:15": "I know thy works, that thou art neither cold nor hot: I would thou wert cold or hot. (half measures are what this church is rebuked for).",
  "Revelation 3:16": "So then because thou art lukewarm, and neither cold nor hot, I will spue thee out of my mouth. (the picture is corporate -- a lukewarm church's testimony rejected outright -- describing this specific, apostate-era congregation rather than pronouncing a verdict on any individual believer's standing before God).",
  "Revelation 3:17": "Because thou sayest, I am rich, and increased with goods, and have need of nothing; and knowest not that thou art wretched, and miserable, and poor, and blind, and naked: (material wealth had blinded this church to its actual spiritual poverty)",
  "Revelation 3:18": "I counsel thee to buy of me gold tried in the fire, that thou mayest be rich; and white raiment, that thou mayest be clothed, and that the shame of thy nakedness do not appear; and anoint thine eyes with eyesalve, that thou mayest see. (what this church actually needs cannot be bought with money -- it is already paid for in the blood of Christ).",
  "Revelation 3:19": "As many as I love, I rebuke and chasten: be zealous therefore, and repent. (this rebuke comes from love, aimed at a remnant still within this church).",
  "Revelation 3:20": "Behold, I stand at the door, and knock: if any man hear my voice, and open the door, I will come in to him, and will sup with him, and he with me. (rejected by the church as a whole, Christ now appeals directly to individuals within it).",
  "Revelation 3:21": "To him that overcometh will I grant to sit with me in my throne, even as I also overcame, and am set down with my Father in his throne. (Christ went before us as our substitute, doing what we could not do for ourselves, and now shares that victory with the overcomer).",
  "Revelation 3:22": "He that hath an ear, let him hear what the Spirit saith unto the churches. (the Spirit's appeal to come back to Christ closes each of these seven letters).",
  "Revelation 4:1": "After this I looked, and, behold, a door was opened in heaven: and the first voice which I heard was as it were of a trumpet talking with me; which said, Come up hither, and I will shew thee things which must be hereafter. (this marks the time after the churches -- after the rapture -- when John is shown what comes next).",
  "Revelation 4:2": "And immediately I was in the spirit: and, behold, a throne was set in heaven, and one sat on the throne. (John sees God the Father seated on his throne).",
  "Revelation 4:3": "And he that sat was to look upon like a jasper and a sardine stone: and there was a rainbow round about the throne, in sight like unto an emerald. (this pictures a glory beyond description).",
  "Revelation 4:4": "And round about the throne were four and twenty seats: and upon the seats I saw four and twenty elders sitting, clothed in white raiment; and they had on their heads crowns of gold. (these elders represent the redeemed, clothed in the righteousness of Christ and crowned with authority).",
  "Revelation 4:5": "And out of the throne proceeded lightnings and thunderings and voices: and there were seven lamps of fire burning before the throne, which are the seven Spirits of God. (the sevenfold lamps picture the full perfection of the Holy Spirit).",
  "Revelation 4:6": "And before the throne there was a sea of glass like unto crystal: and in the midst of the throne, and round about the throne, were four beasts full of eyes before and behind. (this pictures perfect transparency before God, along with living creatures of a kind not otherwise described in Scripture).",
  "Revelation 4:7": "And the first beast was like a lion, and the second beast like a calf, and the third beast had a face as a man, and the fourth beast was like a flying eagle. (these four living creatures remain constantly before the throne).",
  "Revelation 4:8": "And the four beasts had each of them six wings about him; and they were full of eyes within: and they rest not day and night, saying, Holy, holy, holy, Lord God Almighty, which was, and is, and is to come. (the threefold repetition calls attention to the infinite holiness of God).",
  "Revelation 4:9": "And when those beasts give glory and honour and thanks to him that sat on the throne, who liveth for ever and ever, (if these creatures give constant glory to God, how much more should the redeemed, who have known his salvation, do the same)",
  "Revelation 4:10": "The four and twenty elders fall down before him that sat on the throne, and worship him that liveth for ever and ever, and cast their crowns before the throne, saying, (the elders' worship, representing the church in heaven, pictures what the church on earth ought to be)",
  "Revelation 4:11": "Thou art worthy, O Lord, to receive glory and honour and power: for thou hast created all things, and for thy pleasure they are and were created. (this worship anticipates the day when the whole creation, ruined by sin, will be fully restored because of what Christ did at the Cross).",
  "Revelation 5:1": "And I saw in the right hand of him that sat on the throne a book written within and on the backside, sealed with seven seals. (the seven seals mark the beginning of the culminating judgments soon to be poured out on the earth).",
  "Revelation 5:2": "And I saw a strong angel proclaiming with a loud voice, Who is worthy to open the book, and to loose the seals thereof? (worthiness here means moral fitness).",
  "Revelation 5:3": "And no man in heaven, nor in earth, neither under the earth, was able to open the book, neither to look thereon. (the phrase 'no man' is worth pausing over -- there was truly no one qualified).",
  "Revelation 5:4": "And I wept much, because no man was found worthy to open and to read the book, neither to look thereon. (this book holds not only coming judgment, but also the eventual redemption of the earth, which is why its being unopened mattered so much).",
  "Revelation 5:5": "And one of the elders saith unto me, Weep not: behold, the Lion of the tribe of Juda, the Root of David, hath prevailed to open the book, and to loose the seven seals thereof. (this presents Jesus Christ, and announces that man's dilemma has already been solved).",
  "Revelation 5:6": "And I beheld, and, lo, in the midst of the throne and of the four beasts, and in the midst of the elders, stood a Lamb as it had been slain, having seven horns and seven eyes, which are the seven Spirits of God sent forth into all the earth. (the slain Lamb pictures the Cross, which alone has redeemed all things and secured for us total dominion and illumination through the Spirit).",
  "Revelation 5:7": "And he came and took the book out of the right hand of him that sat upon the throne. (all of heaven watches in awe as the Lamb steps forward to take the book).",
  "Revelation 5:8": "And when he had taken the book, the four beasts and four and twenty elders fell down before the Lamb, having every one of them harps, and golden vials full of odours, which are the prayers of saints. (the prayers of all the saints are about to be answered).",
  "Revelation 5:9": "And they sung a new song, saying, Thou art worthy to take the book, and to open the seals thereof: for thou wast slain, and hast redeemed us to God by thy blood out of every kindred, and tongue, and people, and nation; (only the redeemed can sing this song, and it credits redemption entirely to what was accomplished at the Cross)",
  "Revelation 5:10": "And hast made us unto our God kings and priests: and we shall reign on the earth. (whatever Christ is by right, the redeemed become through him, made possible by the Cross alone).",
  "Revelation 5:11": "And I beheld, and I heard the voice of many angels round about the throne and the beasts and the elders: and the number of them was ten thousand times ten thousand, and thousands of thousands; (this pictures an innumerable host beyond human comprehension)",
  "Revelation 5:12": "Saying with a loud voice, Worthy is the Lamb that was slain to receive power, and riches, and wisdom, and strength, and honour, and glory, and blessing. (through the Cross, this sevenfold blessing belongs to Christ, and through him to us as well).",
  "Revelation 5:13": "And every creature which is in heaven, and on the earth, and under the earth, and such as are in the sea, and all that are in them, heard I saying, Blessing, and honour, and glory, and power, be unto him that sitteth upon the throne, and unto the Lamb for ever and ever. (Christ's death guarantees the total, eternal defeat of Satan and the full cleansing of heaven and earth).",
  "Revelation 5:14": "And the four beasts said, Amen. And the four and twenty elders fell down and worshipped him that liveth for ever and ever. (God's eternity and the eternal results of what Christ did at the Cross are both affirmed here).",
  "Revelation 6:1": "And I saw when the Lamb opened one of the seals, and I heard, as it were the noise of thunder, one of the four beasts saying, Come and see. (this is the crucified, risen Christ opening the seals, marking the beginning of the great tribulation).",
  "Revelation 6:2": "And I saw, and behold a white horse: and he that sat on him had a bow; and a crown was given unto him: and he went forth conquering, and to conquer. (this pictures the Antichrist presenting himself as a prince of peace while preparing for war).",
  "Revelation 6:3": "And when he had opened the second seal, I heard the second beast say, Come and see. (the events unfolding on earth are first decided in heaven).",
  "Revelation 6:4": "And there went out another horse that was red: and power was given to him that sat thereon to take peace from the earth, and that they should kill one another: and there was given unto him a great sword. (this second seal pictures the wars of the great tribulation, more destructive than any period in history).",
  "Revelation 6:5": "And when he had opened the third seal, I heard the third beast say, Come and see. And I beheld, and lo a black horse; and he that sat on him had a pair of balances in his hand. (this pictures the famine that follows war).",
  "Revelation 6:6": "And I heard a voice in the midst of the four beasts say, A measure of wheat for a penny, and three measures of barley for a penny; and see thou hurt not the oil and the wine. (the sparing of the olive and the vine simply reflects that these plants need little cultivation, so their destruction is withheld).",
  "Revelation 6:7": "And when he had opened the fourth seal, I heard the voice of the fourth beast say, Come and see. (this seal introduces horror on an unprecedented scale).",
  "Revelation 6:8": "And I looked, and behold a pale horse: and his name that sat on him was Death, and Hell followed with him. And power was given unto them over the fourth part of the earth, to kill with sword, and with hunger, and with death, and with the beasts of the earth. (this pictures a widespread reign of death, likely concentrated in the Middle East).",
  "Revelation 6:9": "And when he had opened the fifth seal, I saw under the altar the souls of them that were slain for the word of God, and for the testimony which they held: (these martyrs are already in glorified bodies -- the word 'soul' here is simply used for identification)",
  "Revelation 6:10": "And they cried with a loud voice, saying, How long, O Lord, holy and true, dost thou not judge and avenge our blood on them that dwell on the earth? (this cry is less about vengeance than about longing for the whole era of sin and shame to finally end).",
  "Revelation 6:11": "And white robes were given unto every one of them; and it was said unto them, that they should rest yet for a little season, until their fellowservants also and their brethren, that should be killed as they were, should be fulfilled. (the white robes picture the righteousness of the saints).",
  "Revelation 6:12": "And I beheld when he had opened the sixth seal, and, lo, there was a great earthquake; and the sun became black as sackcloth of hair, and the moon became as blood; (the darkening is likely caused by dust filling the air from the earthquake)",
  "Revelation 6:13": "And the stars of heaven fell unto the earth, even as a fig tree casteth her untimely figs, when she is shaken of a mighty wind. (this pictures a bombardment of meteorites causing great destruction).",
  "Revelation 6:14": "And the heaven departed as a scroll when it is rolled together; and every mountain and island were moved out of their places. (this describes power of unimaginable proportions).",
  "Revelation 6:15": "And the kings of the earth, and the great men, and the rich men, and the chief captains, and the mighty men, and every bondman, and every free man, hid themselves in the dens and in the rocks of the mountains; (this is a wasted effort -- no one, from poverty to plenty, can hide from God)",
  "Revelation 6:16": "And said to the mountains and rocks, Fall on us, and hide us from the face of him that sitteth on the throne, and from the wrath of the Lamb: (the Lamb who saves today will be the judge tomorrow)",
  "Revelation 6:17": "For the great day of his wrath is come; and who shall be able to stand? (man has set himself against God since the fall, and now sees plainly how powerful God truly is).",
  "Revelation 7:1": "And after these things I saw four angels standing on the four corners of the earth, holding the four winds of the earth, that the wind should not blow on the earth, nor on the sea, nor on any tree. (judgment is held back for a short time by the sheer power of God).",
  "Revelation 7:2": "And I saw another angel ascending from the east, having the seal of the living God: and he cried with a loud voice to the four angels, to whom it was given to hurt the earth and the sea, (this angel's arrival marks the beginning of Israel's restoration)",
  "Revelation 7:3": "Saying, Hurt not the earth, neither the sea, nor the trees, till we have sealed the servants of our God in their foreheads. (this seal functions much like the blood on the doorposts at the first Passover, marking out those under God's protection).",
  "Revelation 7:4": "And I heard the number of them which were sealed: and there were sealed an hundred and forty and four thousand of all the tribes of the children of Israel. (these are the firstfruits of Israel's coming national salvation, sealed during the first half of the great tribulation).",
  "Revelation 7:5": "Of the tribe of Juda were sealed twelve thousand. Of the tribe of Reuben were sealed twelve thousand. Of the tribe of Gad were sealed twelve thousand.",
  "Revelation 7:6": "Of the tribe of Aser were sealed twelve thousand. Of the tribe of Nepthalim were sealed twelve thousand. Of the tribe of Manasses were sealed twelve thousand.",
  "Revelation 7:7": "Of the tribe of Simeon were sealed twelve thousand. Of the tribe of Levi were sealed twelve thousand. Of the tribe of Issachar were sealed twelve thousand.",
  "Revelation 7:8": "Of the tribe of Zabulon were sealed twelve thousand. Of the tribe of Joseph were sealed twelve thousand. Of the tribe of Benjamin were sealed twelve thousand. (the tribe of Dan alone is left out here, with no reason given).",
  "Revelation 7:9": "After this I beheld, and, lo, a great multitude, which no man could number, of all nations, and kindreds, and people, and tongues, stood before the throne, and before the Lamb, clothed with white robes, and palms in their hands; (these are tribulation martyrs, and the white robes show that their sin has been washed in the blood of the Lamb)",
  "Revelation 7:10": "And cried with a loud voice, saying, Salvation to our God which sitteth upon the throne, and unto the Lamb. (this again credits salvation entirely to what Jesus did at the Cross).",
  "Revelation 7:11": "And all the angels stood round about the throne, and about the elders and the four beasts, and fell before the throne on their faces, and worshipped God, (this vast worship centers on what Jesus did at the Cross in atoning for sin)",
  "Revelation 7:12": "Saying, Amen: Blessing, and glory, and wisdom, and thanksgiving, and honour, and power, and might, be unto our God for ever and ever. Amen. (the same sevenfold praise given to the Father is given here to the Son, showing both are regarded as equally worthy).",
  "Revelation 7:13": "And one of the elders answered, saying unto me, What are these which are arrayed in white robes? and whence came they? (one of the elders puts into words the question already forming in John's mind).",
  "Revelation 7:14": "And I said unto him, Sir, thou knowest. And he said to me, These are they which came out of great tribulation, and have washed their robes, and made them white in the blood of the Lamb. (they were saved by trusting Christ and what he did at the Cross, the overwhelming emphasis of this whole book).",
  "Revelation 7:15": "Therefore are they before the throne of God, and serve him day and night in his temple: and he that sitteth on the throne shall dwell among them. (the one on the throne will cast his own protecting presence over these saints).",
  "Revelation 7:16": "They shall hunger no more, neither thirst any more; neither shall the sun light on them, nor any heat. (this pictures a perfect, sorrow-free environment).",
  "Revelation 7:17": "For the Lamb which is in the midst of the throne shall feed them, and shall lead them unto living fountains of waters: and God shall wipe away all tears from their eyes. (the Lamb who saved them also sustains them -- salvation and daily life both flow from the same Cross).",
  "Revelation 8:1": "And when he had opened the seventh seal, there was silence in heaven about the space of half an hour. (this silence signals the intense expectancy of judgment about to increase).",
  "Revelation 8:2": "And I saw the seven angels which stood before God; and to them were given seven trumpets. (the seal judgments now give way to the seven trumpet judgments).",
  "Revelation 8:3": "And another angel came and stood at the altar, having a golden censer; and there was given unto him much incense, that he should offer it with the prayers of all saints upon the golden altar which was before the throne. (the incense pictures the merit of Christ's life, without which even the prayers of the saints remain incomplete).",
  "Revelation 8:4": "And the smoke of the incense, which came with the prayers of the saints, ascended up before God out of the angel's hand. (prayer offered according to God's will always rises before him mixed with the intercessory work of Christ).",
  "Revelation 8:5": "And the angel took the censer, and filled it with fire of the altar, and cast it into the earth: and there were voices, and thunderings, and lightnings, and an earthquake. (with the salvation of the Cross rejected by the earth, the judgment of the Cross now begins).",
  "Revelation 8:6": "And the seven angels which had the seven trumpets prepared themselves to sound. (judgment follows the rejection of the Cross, and these events unfold roughly midway through the tribulation).",
  "Revelation 8:7": "The first angel sounded, and there followed hail and fire mingled with blood, and they were cast upon the earth: and the third part of trees was burnt up, and all green grass was burnt up. (this judgment concentrates on the Middle East, though its effects reach further).",
  "Revelation 8:8": "And the second angel sounded, and as it were a great mountain burning with fire was cast into the sea: and the third part of the sea became blood; (this pictures a massive meteorite striking the Mediterranean)",
  "Revelation 8:9": "And the third part of the creatures which were in the sea, and had life, died; and the third part of the ships were destroyed. (the destruction here matches the scale of the meteorite strike).",
  "Revelation 8:10": "And the third angel sounded, and there fell a great star from heaven, burning as it were a lamp, and it fell upon the third part of the rivers, and upon the fountains of waters; (another meteorite is likely in view, this time striking fresh water sources)",
  "Revelation 8:11": "And the name of the star is called Wormwood: and the third part of the waters became wormwood; and many men died of the waters, because they were made bitter. (this judgment could claim hundreds of thousands, if not millions, of lives).",
  "Revelation 8:12": "And the fourth angel sounded, and the third part of the sun was smitten, and the third part of the moon, and the third part of the stars; so as the third part of them was darkened, and the day shone not for a third part of it, and the night likewise. (these judgments are literal, not symbolic, exactly as Christ himself foretold).",
  "Revelation 8:13": "And I beheld, and heard an angel flying through the midst of heaven, saying with a loud voice, Woe, woe, woe, to the inhabiters of the earth by reason of the other voices of the trumpet of the three angels, which are yet to sound! (the three woes announce that the remaining trumpet judgments will be the worst yet).",
  "Revelation 9:1": "And the fifth angel sounded, and I saw a star fall from heaven unto the earth: and to him was given the key of the bottomless pit. (the falling star is Satan himself, given the key by Christ, and this marks the first of the three woes).",
  "Revelation 9:2": "And he opened the bottomless pit; and there arose a smoke out of the pit, as the smoke of a great furnace; and the sun and the air were darkened by reason of the smoke of the pit. (this darkening will likely concentrate over the old Roman Empire territory).",
  "Revelation 9:3": "And there came out of the smoke locusts upon the earth: and unto them was given power, as the scorpions of the earth have power. (these are demon locusts, not literal insects, as the following verses make clear).",
  "Revelation 9:4": "And it was commanded them that they should not hurt the grass of the earth, neither any green thing, neither any tree; but only those men which have not the seal of God in their foreheads. (the 144,000 sealed believers are specifically protected from this torment).",
  "Revelation 9:5": "And to them it was given that they should not kill them, but that they should be tormented five months: and their torment was as the torment of a scorpion, when he striketh a man. (this torment will be real, though these creatures themselves remain invisible).",
  "Revelation 9:6": "And in those days shall men seek death, and shall not find it; and shall desire to die, and death shall flee from them. (pain severe enough to make death desirable shows just how intense this judgment will be).",
  "Revelation 9:7": "And the shapes of the locusts were like unto horses prepared unto battle; and on their heads were as it were crowns like gold, and their faces were as the faces of men. (these demon spirits, though invisible, are described here as they would actually appear).",
  "Revelation 9:8": "And they had hair as the hair of women, and their teeth were as the teeth of lions. (these beings were originally created for praise and worship, but were twisted by rebellion against God).",
  "Revelation 9:9": "And they had breastplates, as it were breastplates of iron; and the sound of their wings was as the sound of chariots of many horses running to battle. (this glimpse into the spirit world explains why merely human effort is helpless against such forces -- only Christ and the Cross answer it).",
  "Revelation 9:10": "And they had tails like unto scorpions, and there were stings in their tails: and their power was to hurt men five months. (the five-month limit shows that Satan can only do what God allows him to do).",
  "Revelation 9:11": "And they had a king over them, which is the angel of the bottomless pit, whose name in the Hebrew tongue is Abaddon, but in the Greek tongue hath his name Apollyon. (this is a powerful fallen angel who joined Lucifer's rebellion against God).",
  "Revelation 9:12": "One woe is past; and, behold, there come two woes more hereafter. (this simply marks the progress of the three woes announced earlier).",
  "Revelation 9:13": "And the sixth angel sounded, and I heard a voice from the four horns of the golden altar which is before God, (this marks the beginning of the second woe)",
  "Revelation 9:14": "Saying to the sixth angel which had the trumpet, Loose the four angels which are bound in the great river Euphrates. (these are evil angels, and the Euphrates marks the region where human rebellion against God first began).",
  "Revelation 9:15": "And the four angels were loosed, which were prepared for an hour, and a day, and a month, and a year, for to slay the third part of men. (these are executors of God's wrath, not the Righteous angels of chapter seven).",
  "Revelation 9:16": "And the number of the army of the horsemen were two hundred thousand thousand: and I heard the number of them. (this is not a human army but an army of invisible demon spirits).",
  "Revelation 9:17": "And thus I saw the horses in the vision, and them that sat on them, having breastplates of fire, and of jacinth, and brimstone: and the heads of the horses were as the heads of lions; and out of their mouths issued fire and smoke and brimstone. (this describes demon spirits riding demon horses).",
  "Revelation 9:18": "By these three was the third part of men killed, by the fire, and by the smoke, and by the brimstone, which issued out of their mouths. (the death toll from this plague could reach several hundred million).",
  "Revelation 9:19": "For their power is in their mouth, and in their tails: for their tails were like unto serpents, and had heads, and with them they do hurt. (this is why Paul said believers wrestle not against flesh and blood but against spiritual powers of darkness).",
  "Revelation 9:20": "And the rest of the men which were not killed by these plagues yet repented not of the works of their hands, that they should not worship devils, and idols of gold, and silver, and brass, and stone, and of wood: which neither can see, nor hear, nor walk: (even severe judgment does not easily move the human heart to repentance)",
  "Revelation 9:21": "Neither repented they of their murders, nor of their sorceries, nor of their fornication, nor of their thefts. (the repentance in view here is turning from creating another god, just as repentance in the church means turning from creating another sacrifice).",
  "Revelation 10:1": "And I saw another mighty angel come down from heaven, clothed with a cloud: and a rainbow was upon his head, and his face was as it were the sun, and his feet as pillars of fire: (this mighty figure is Christ himself, and his feet of fire signal that rejected mercy always brings judgment)",
  "Revelation 10:2": "And he had in his hand a little book open: and he set his right foot upon the sea, and his left foot on the earth, (the stance itself pictures Christ's coming dominion over the whole earth)",
  "Revelation 10:3": "And cried with a loud voice, as when a lion roareth: and when he had cried, seven thunders uttered their voices. (we are not told what these seven thunders said, only that they spoke).",
  "Revelation 10:4": "And when the seven thunders had uttered their voices, I was about to write: and I heard a voice from heaven saying unto me, Seal up those things which the seven thunders uttered, and write them not. (John knew the content but was forbidden to record it, so speculation about it is pointless).",
  "Revelation 10:5": "And the angel which I saw stand upon the sea and upon the earth lifted up his hand to heaven, (this gesture accompanies a solemn oath)",
  "Revelation 10:6": "And sware by him that liveth for ever and ever, who created heaven, and the things that therein are, and the earth, and the things that therein are, and the sea, and the things which are therein, that there should be time no longer: (this declares that the prayer 'thy kingdom come' is about to be answered)",
  "Revelation 10:7": "But in the days of the voice of the seventh angel, when he shall begin to sound, the mystery of God should be finished, as he hath declared to his servants the prophets. (this 'mystery' concerns why God has allowed Satan to reign over the earth this long).",
  "Revelation 10:8": "And the voice which I heard from heaven spake unto me again, and said, Go and take the little book which is open in the hand of the angel which standeth upon the sea and upon the earth. (this is the same voice that earlier sealed up the seven thunders).",
  "Revelation 10:9": "And I went unto the angel, and said unto him, Give me the little book. And he said unto me, Take it, and eat it up; and it shall make thy belly bitter, but it shall be in thy mouth sweet as honey. (eating the book pictures fully taking in its message, sweet in the receiving but bitter in the judgment it contains).",
  "Revelation 10:10": "And I took the little book out of the angel's hand, and ate it up; and it was in my mouth sweet as honey: and as soon as I had eaten it, my belly was bitter. (this signals the further judgment still to come in the second half of the tribulation).",
  "Revelation 10:11": "And he said unto me, Thou must prophesy again before many peoples, and nations, and tongues, and kings. (John's ministry of prophecy continues -- the stance over sea and earth does not yet mean full possession, which awaits the second coming).",
  "Revelation 11:1": "And there was given me a reed like unto a rod: and the angel stood, saying, Rise, and measure the temple of God, and the altar, and them that worship therein. (this points to a literal temple yet to be rebuilt in Jerusalem, foretold by Daniel centuries before Christ).",
  "Revelation 11:2": "But the court which is without the temple leave out, and measure it not; for it is given unto the Gentiles: and the holy city shall they tread under foot forty and two months. (this marks the close of what Scripture calls 'the times of the Gentiles').",
  "Revelation 11:3": "And I will give power unto my two witnesses, and they shall prophesy a thousand two hundred and threescore days, clothed in sackcloth. (the sackcloth suggests their message includes a call to repentance).",
  "Revelation 11:4": "These are the two olive trees, and the two candlesticks standing before the God of the earth. (this imagery echoes the vision given earlier to the prophet Zechariah).",
  "Revelation 11:5": "And if any man will hurt them, fire proceedeth out of their mouth, and devoureth their enemies: and if any man will hurt them, he must in this manner be killed. (this pictures divine protection, not literal fire).",
  "Revelation 11:6": "These have power to shut heaven, that it rain not in the days of their prophecy: and have power over waters to turn them to blood, and to smite the earth with all plagues, as often as they will. (this power will be exercised at their discretion throughout their ministry).",
  "Revelation 11:7": "And when they shall have finished their testimony, the beast that ascendeth out of the bottomless pit shall make war against them, and shall overcome them, and kill them. (their deaths come only after their ministry is fully completed, not before).",
  "Revelation 11:8": "And their dead bodies shall lie in the street of the great city, which spiritually is called Sodom and Egypt, where also our Lord was crucified. (this identifies Jerusalem, turned by that time into a capital of both immorality and worldliness).",
  "Revelation 11:9": "And they of the people and kindreds and tongues and nations shall see their dead bodies three days and an half, and shall not suffer their dead bodies to be put in graves. (this spectacle will likely be witnessed worldwide by television).",
  "Revelation 11:10": "And they that dwell upon the earth shall rejoice over them, and make merry, and shall send gifts one to another; because these two prophets tormented them that dwelt on the earth. (this is simply what true prophets have always tended to provoke from a hostile world).",
  "Revelation 11:11": "And after three days and an half the Spirit of life from God entered into them, and they stood upon their feet; and great fear fell upon them which saw them. (their resurrection will be an astonishing, unmistakable miracle).",
  "Revelation 11:12": "And they heard a great voice from heaven saying unto them, Come up hither. And they ascended up to heaven in a cloud; and their enemies beheld them. (this rapture of the two witnesses comes at the very close of the great tribulation).",
  "Revelation 11:13": "And the same hour was there a great earthquake, and the tenth part of the city fell, and in the earthquake were slain of men seven thousand: and the remnant were affrighted, and gave glory to the God of heaven. (there is reason in the Greek text to think some of these survivors actually turned to Christ in that moment).",
  "Revelation 11:14": "The second woe is past; and, behold, the third woe cometh quickly. (the third woe still to come is Satan's casting down to earth, described later in chapter twelve).",
  "Revelation 11:15": "And the seventh angel sounded; and there were great voices in heaven, saying, The kingdoms of this world are become the kingdoms of our Lord, and of his Christ; and he shall reign for ever and ever. (this is spoken in anticipation of what will certainly come to pass at the second coming).",
  "Revelation 11:16": "And the four and twenty elders, which sat before God on their seats, fell upon their faces, and worshipped God, (these elders represent the whole plan of God as it concerns redemption)",
  "Revelation 11:17": "Saying, We give thee thanks, O Lord God Almighty, which art, and wast, and art to come; because thou hast taken to thee thy great power, and hast reigned. (this too anticipates what God is certain to bring to pass).",
  "Revelation 11:18": "And the nations were angry, and thy wrath is come, and the time of the dead, that they should be judged, and that thou shouldest give reward unto thy servants the prophets, and to the saints, and them that fear thy name, small and great; and shouldest destroy them which destroy the earth. (the reward in view here is the judgment seat of Christ, not the great white throne judgment of the unsaved).",
  "Revelation 11:19": "And the temple of God was opened in heaven, and there was seen in his temple the ark of his testament: and there were lightnings, and voices, and thunderings, and an earthquake, and great hail. (this pictures the awesome presence, majesty, and glory of God).",
  "Revelation 12:1": "And there appeared a great wonder in heaven; a woman clothed with the sun, and the moon under her feet, and upon her head a crown of twelve stars: (this pictures national Israel, clothed in glory and restored to dominion)",
  "Revelation 12:2": "And she being with child cried, travailing in birth, and pained to be delivered. (this pictures the spiritual birth pangs of the 144,000 Jews who will come to Christ in the first half of the tribulation).",
  "Revelation 12:3": "And there appeared another wonder in heaven; and behold a great red dragon, having seven heads and ten horns, and seven crowns upon his heads. (the red dragon is Satan, and the heads picture the empires he has used across history to persecute Israel).",
  "Revelation 12:4": "And his tail drew the third part of the stars of heaven, and did cast them to the earth: and the dragon stood before the woman which was ready to be delivered, for to devour her child as soon as it was born. (this reaches back to the original rebellion of Lucifer, when a third of the angels joined him, and looks ahead to his hatred of these Jews newly come to Christ).",
  "Revelation 12:5": "And she brought forth a manchild, who was to rule all nations with a rod of iron: and her child was caught up unto God, and to his throne. (the manchild pictures the 144,000, who will be caught up to God at roughly the midpoint of the tribulation).",
  "Revelation 12:6": "And the woman fled into the wilderness, where she hath a place prepared of God, that they should feed her there a thousand two hundred and threescore days. (this place is understood to be ancient Petra, currently empty and awaiting Israel's arrival).",
  "Revelation 12:7": "And there was war in heaven: Michael and his angels fought against the dragon; and the dragon fought and his angels, (this war is instigated not by Satan but by Michael, acting at God's command)",
  "Revelation 12:8": "And prevailed not; neither was their place found any more in heaven. (Satan's defeat here anticipates his final banishment described later in this book).",
  "Revelation 12:9": "And the great dragon was cast out, that old serpent, called the Devil, and Satan, which deceiveth the whole world: he was cast out into the earth, and his angels were cast out with him. (deception is Satan's greatest weapon, and this marks the beginning of the end for him).",
  "Revelation 12:10": "And I heard a loud voice saying in heaven, Now is come salvation, and strength, and the kingdom of our God, and the power of his Christ: for the accuser of our brethren is cast down, which accused them before our God day and night. (this celebrates Christ's triumph and the end of Satan's constant accusation before God).",
  "Revelation 12:11": "And they overcame him by the blood of the Lamb, and by the word of their testimony; and they loved not their lives unto the death. (the power to overcome Satan is found exclusively in the blood of Christ and faith in that finished work).",
  "Revelation 12:12": "Therefore rejoice, ye heavens, and ye that dwell in them. Woe to the inhabiters of the earth and of the sea! for the devil is come down unto you, having great wrath, because he knoweth that he hath but a short time. (heaven rejoices at Satan's removal, even as the earth now faces his fury).",
  "Revelation 12:13": "And when the dragon saw that he was cast unto the earth, he persecuted the woman which brought forth the manchild. (this is when the Antichrist breaks his covenant with Israel and turns to attack her).",
  "Revelation 12:14": "And to the woman were given two wings of a great eagle, that she might fly into the wilderness, into her place, where she is nourished for a time, and times, and half a time, from the face of the serpent. (the Lord himself will help Israel escape to this place of refuge).",
  "Revelation 12:15": "And the serpent cast out of his mouth water as a flood after the woman, that he might cause her to be carried away of the flood. (this pictures the army of the Antichrist bent on Israel's destruction).",
  "Revelation 12:16": "And the earth helped the woman, and the earth opened her mouth, and swallowed up the flood which the dragon cast out of his mouth. (the same earthquake that helps Israel here works directly against the dragon's plan).",
  "Revelation 12:17": "And the dragon was wroth with the woman, and went to make war with the remnant of her seed, which keep the commandments of God, and have the testimony of Jesus Christ. (this remnant is made up of Jews who come to Christ after the 144,000 are caught up).",
  "Revelation 13:1": "And I stood upon the sand of the sea, and saw a beast rise up out of the sea, having seven heads and ten horns, and upon his horns ten crowns, and upon his heads the name of blasphemy. (the beast pictures the Antichrist, now empowered by Satan beyond any man before him).",
  "Revelation 13:2": "And the beast which I saw was like unto a leopard, and his feet were as the feet of a bear, and his mouth as the mouth of a lion: and the dragon gave him his power, and his seat, and great authority. (this describes a fallen angel released to aid the Antichrist, carrying traits of the ancient empires that once persecuted Israel).",
  "Revelation 13:3": "And I saw one of his heads as it were wounded to death; and his deadly wound was healed: and all the world wondered after the beast. (this wound and healing point back to a fallen angel long confined to the bottomless pit, now released to aid the Antichrist).",
  "Revelation 13:4": "And they worshipped the dragon which gave power unto the beast: and they worshipped the beast, saying, Who is like unto the beast? who is able to make war with him? (this simply shows how readily men worship raw power).",
  "Revelation 13:5": "And there was given unto him a mouth speaking great things and blasphemies; and power was given unto him to continue forty and two months. (despite his power, God alone controls the length of time he is permitted to act).",
  "Revelation 13:6": "And he opened his mouth in blasphemy against God, to blaspheme his name, and his tabernacle, and them that dwell in heaven. (this blasphemy will be broadcast worldwide through every available medium).",
  "Revelation 13:7": "And it was given unto him to make war with the saints, and to overcome them: and power was given him over all kindreds, and tongues, and nations. (this authority is limited to the territory once held by the Roman Empire, not the whole earth).",
  "Revelation 13:8": "And all that dwell upon the earth shall worship him, whose names are not written in the book of life of the Lamb slain from the foundation of the world. (names are written in the book of life only through faith in Christ and what he did at the Cross, the foundation of every true doctrine).",
  "Revelation 13:9": "If any man have an ear, let him hear. (this calls for the ability to hear spiritually, a phrase Jesus himself used often).",
  "Revelation 13:10": "He that leadeth into captivity shall go into captivity: he that killeth with the sword must be killed with the sword. Here is the patience and the faith of the saints. (this points ahead to Israel's redemption drawing near at the second coming).",
  "Revelation 13:11": "And I beheld another beast coming up out of the earth; and he had two horns like a lamb, and he spake as a dragon. (this second beast is the false prophet, deceptively lamb-like in appearance while speaking with Satan's own voice).",
  "Revelation 13:12": "And he exerciseth all the power of the first beast before him, and causeth the earth and them which dwell therein to worship the first beast, whose deadly wound was healed. (the false prophet's authority flows directly from Satan, not through the Antichrist).",
  "Revelation 13:13": "And he doeth great wonders, so that he maketh fire come down from heaven on the earth in the sight of men, (these signs will be real, performed through Satan's power, not mere trickery)",
  "Revelation 13:14": "And deceiveth them that dwell on the earth by the means of those miracles which he had power to do in the sight of the beast; saying to them that dwell on the earth, that they should make an image to the beast, which had the wound by a sword, and did live. (this image will likely be set up in the rebuilt temple in Jerusalem).",
  "Revelation 13:15": "And he had power to give life unto the image of the beast, that the image of the beast should both speak, and cause that as many as would not worship the image of the beast should be killed. (giving actual life belongs to God alone, so what is granted here is closer to imparting a spirit that allows the image to speak).",
  "Revelation 13:16": "And he causeth all, both small and great, rich and poor, free and bond, to receive a mark in their right hand, or in their foreheads: (this domain covers the old Roman Empire territory, not the entire globe)",
  "Revelation 13:17": "And that no man might buy or sell, save he that had the mark, or the name of the beast, or the number of his name. (the seduction here turns economic, after having first been religious).",
  "Revelation 13:18": "Here is wisdom. Let him that hath understanding count the number of the beast: for it is the number of a man; and his number is Six hundred threescore and six. (this is the number of a man, not a kingdom or a religion, and he will answer to God for every blasphemy he has spoken).",
  "Revelation 14:1": "And I looked, and, lo, a Lamb stood on the mount Sion, and with him an hundred forty and four thousand, having his Father's name written in their foreheads. (calling Christ 'the Lamb' here, as throughout this book, points back to the Cross as the ground of our redemption).",
  "Revelation 14:2": "And I heard a voice from heaven, as the voice of many waters, and as the voice of a great thunder: and I heard the voice of harpers harping with their harps: (this pictures worship of overwhelming intensity)",
  "Revelation 14:3": "And they sung as it were a new song before the throne, and before the four beasts, and the elders: and no man could learn that song but the hundred and forty and four thousand, which were redeemed from the earth. (they were redeemed the same way anyone is redeemed -- by trusting Christ and what he did at the Cross).",
  "Revelation 14:4": "These are they which were not defiled with women; for they are virgins. These are they which follow the Lamb whithersoever he goeth. These were redeemed from among men, being the firstfruits unto God and to the Lamb. (this pictures the 144,000 as untouched by idolatry, which Scripture treats as spiritual adultery).",
  "Revelation 14:5": "And in their mouth was found no guile: for they are without fault before the throne of God. (this describes a standing gained by faith, not by personal sinlessness).",
  "Revelation 14:6": "And I saw another angel fly in the midst of heaven, having the everlasting gospel to preach unto them that dwell on the earth, and to every nation, and kindred, and tongue, and people, (this is the same gospel of grace preached by Paul and the apostles, now proclaimed by an angel to the whole world)",
  "Revelation 14:7": "Saying with a loud voice, Fear God, and give glory to him; for the hour of his judgment is come: and worship him that made heaven, and earth, and the sea, and the fountains of waters. (this directly answers the Antichrist's counterfeit claims to creative power).",
  "Revelation 14:8": "And there followed another angel, saying, Babylon is fallen, is fallen, that great city, because she made all nations drink of the wine of the wrath of her fornication. (Babylon was the site of humanity's first organized rebellion against God, and will be the site of its last).",
  "Revelation 14:9": "And the third angel followed them, saying with a loud voice, If any man worship the beast and his image, and receive his mark in his forehead, or in his hand, (this is a warning of the most serious kind, holding every individual accountable)",
  "Revelation 14:10": "The same shall drink of the wine of the wrath of God, which is poured out without mixture into the cup of his indignation; and he shall be tormented with fire and brimstone in the presence of the holy angels, and in the presence of the Lamb: (this describes the eternal judgment awaiting all who take the mark of the beast)",
  "Revelation 14:11": "And the smoke of their torment ascendeth up for ever and ever: and they have no rest day nor night, who worship the beast and his image, and whosoever receiveth the mark of his name. (this describes a conscious, unbroken, eternal existence apart from God).",
  "Revelation 14:12": "Here is the patience of the saints: here are they that keep the commandments of God, and the faith of Jesus. (the commandments of God can only be kept as the believer's faith rests in Christ and the Cross, which then gives the Holy Spirit room to work).",
  "Revelation 14:13": "And I heard a voice from heaven saying unto me, Write, Blessed are the dead which die in the Lord from henceforth: Yea, saith the Spirit, that they may rest from their labours; and their works do follow them. (this blessing belongs to those who already died in the Lord -- their works simply accompany them, they do not earn them entry).",
  "Revelation 14:14": "And I looked, and behold a white cloud, and upon the cloud one sat like unto the Son of man, having on his head a golden crown, and in his hand a sharp sickle. (this pictures Christ himself, ready for the reaping to come at Armageddon).",
  "Revelation 14:15": "And another angel came out of the temple, crying with a loud voice to him that sat on the cloud, Thrust in thy sickle, and reap: for the time is come for thee to reap; for the harvest of the earth is ripe. (this announces that the evil of the Antichrist has reached its full measure).",
  "Revelation 14:16": "And he that sat on the cloud thrust in his sickle on the earth; and the earth was reaped. (what happens on earth in this great moment is first ordained in heaven).",
  "Revelation 14:17": "And another angel came out of the temple which is in heaven, he also having a sharp sickle. (angels will play a significant role in the coming battle of Armageddon).",
  "Revelation 14:18": "And another angel came out from the altar, which had power over fire; and cried with a loud cry to him that had the sharp sickle, saying, Thrust in thy sharp sickle, and gather the clusters of the vine of the earth; for her grapes are fully ripe. (the repetition here underscores just how significant this coming event will be).",
  "Revelation 14:19": "And the angel thrust in his sickle into the earth, and gathered the vine of the earth, and cast it into the great winepress of the wrath of God. (this pictures the nations gathering for judgment in the valley of Jehoshaphat).",
  "Revelation 14:20": "And the winepress was trodden without the city, and blood came out of the winepress, even unto the horse bridles, by the space of a thousand and six hundred furlongs. (this describes a battle of staggering scale, pressing in on Jerusalem itself).",
  "Revelation 15:1": "And I saw another sign in heaven, great and marvellous, seven angels having the seven last plagues; for in them is filled up the wrath of God. (these final plagues conclude the judgments upon the Antichrist's territory).",
  "Revelation 15:2": "And I saw as it were a sea of glass mingled with fire: and them that had gotten the victory over the beast, and over his image, and over his mark, and over the number of his name, stand on the sea of glass, having the harps of God. (this group includes those martyred by the Antichrist, now pictured in a scene of peace and triumph).",
  "Revelation 15:3": "And they sing the song of Moses the servant of God, and the song of the Lamb, saying, Great and marvellous are thy works, Lord God Almighty; just and true are thy ways, thou King of saints. (Christ is King here by virtue of the Cross, and of the faith placed in that finished work).",
  "Revelation 15:4": "Who shall not fear thee, O Lord, and glorify thy name? for thou only art holy: for all nations shall come and worship before thee; for thy judgments are made manifest. (this looks ahead to the millennial reign, when every nation will worship the Lord).",
  "Revelation 15:5": "And after that I looked, and, behold, the temple of the tabernacle of the testimony in heaven was opened: (this is the heavenly holy of holies, witnessing both to God's holiness and the justice of his government)",
  "Revelation 15:6": "And the seven angels came out of the temple, having the seven plagues, clothed in pure and white linen, and having their breasts girded with golden girdles. (their attire matches Christ's own, showing that these judgments belong to his authority).",
  "Revelation 15:7": "And one of the four beasts gave unto the seven angels seven golden vials full of the wrath of God, who liveth for ever and ever. (these vial judgments will be the most severe of all).",
  "Revelation 15:8": "And the temple was filled with smoke from the glory of God, and from his power; and no man was able to enter into the temple, till the seven plagues of the seven angels were fulfilled. (this pictures a season in which no intercession can turn aside what is about to be poured out).",
  "Revelation 16:1": "And I heard a great voice out of the temple saying to the seven angels, Go your ways, and pour out the vials of the wrath of God upon the earth. (this judgment falls specifically on the Antichrist's kingdom, not the whole earth).",
  "Revelation 16:2": "And the first went, and poured out his vial upon the earth; and there fell a noisome and grievous sore upon the men which had the mark of the beast, and upon them which worshipped his image. (this plague strikes only those within the beast's kingdom).",
  "Revelation 16:3": "And the second angel poured out his vial upon the sea; and it became as the blood of a dead man: and every living soul died in the sea. (this describes the Mediterranean turning lifeless, killing every ship's crew and every fish within it).",
  "Revelation 16:4": "And the third angel poured out his vial upon the rivers and fountains of waters; and they became blood. (this judgment is literal, not merely symbolic).",
  "Revelation 16:5": "And I heard the angel of the waters say, Thou art righteous, O Lord, which art, and wast, and shalt be, because thou hast judged thus. (everything God does is righteous, and this affirms it plainly).",
  "Revelation 16:6": "For they have shed the blood of saints and prophets, and thou hast given them blood to drink; for they are worthy. (this simply states that the judgment fits the crime).",
  "Revelation 16:7": "And I heard another out of the altar say, Even so, Lord God Almighty, true and righteous are thy judgments. (this answers the earlier cry for vengeance from those slain for their testimony).",
  "Revelation 16:8": "And the fourth angel poured out his vial upon the sun; and power was given unto him to scorch men with fire. (this adds unbearable heat on top of the earlier plagues, worsening an already severe water shortage).",
  "Revelation 16:9": "And men were scorched with great heat, and blasphemed the name of God, which hath power over these plagues: and they repented not to give him glory. (miracles rarely bring men to repentance -- repentance must come from the heart).",
  "Revelation 16:10": "And the fifth angel poured out his vial upon the seat of the beast; and his kingdom was full of darkness; and they gnawed their tongues for pain, (this concentrates on the very headquarters of the Antichrist's kingdom)",
  "Revelation 16:11": "And blasphemed the God of heaven because of their pains and their sores, and repented not of their deeds. (even the fiercest judgment fails to soften a heart set against God).",
  "Revelation 16:12": "And the sixth angel poured out his vial upon the great river Euphrates; and the water thereof was dried up, that the way of the kings of the east might be prepared. (this clears the path for armies from the east to join the coming battle of Armageddon).",
  "Revelation 16:13": "And I saw three unclean spirits like frogs come out of the mouth of the dragon, and out of the mouth of the beast, and out of the mouth of the false prophet. (this pictures demon spirits, invisible to human eyes, working to gather nations for the final battle).",
  "Revelation 16:14": "For they are the spirits of devils, working miracles, which go forth unto the kings of the earth and of the whole world, to gather them to the battle of that great day of God Almighty. (the Antichrist believes this is all his own plan, but God Almighty is orchestrating every step of it).",
  "Revelation 16:15": "Behold, I come as a thief. Blessed is he that watcheth, and keepeth his garments, lest he walk naked, and they see his shame. (this calls for constant readiness among those who remain loyal to Christ during this hour).",
  "Revelation 16:16": "And he gathered them together into a place called in the Hebrew tongue Armageddon. (this is a literal place, the Mount of Megiddo overlooking its surrounding plain).",
  "Revelation 16:17": "And the seventh angel poured out his vial into the air; and there came a great voice out of the temple of heaven, from the throne, saying, It is done. (the same voice that cried 'finished' from the Cross now cries 'finished' from the throne).",
  "Revelation 16:18": "And there were voices, and thunders, and lightnings; and there was a great earthquake, such as was not since men were upon the earth, so mighty an earthquake, and so great. (as grace was finished at the Cross, wrath is finished here).",
  "Revelation 16:19": "And the great city was divided into three parts, and the cities of the nations fell: and great Babylon came in remembrance before God, to give unto her the cup of the wine of the fierceness of his wrath. (the first organized rebellion against God began at Babylon, and it will end there as well).",
  "Revelation 16:20": "And every island fled away, and the mountains were not found. (this pictures the colossal scale of this final earthquake).",
  "Revelation 16:21": "And there fell upon men a great hail out of heaven, every stone about the weight of a talent: and men blasphemed God because of the plague of the hail; for the plague thereof was exceeding great. (those within the beast's kingdom will know this plague comes from God, yet will still refuse to turn to him).",
  "Revelation 17:1": "And there came one of the seven angels which had the seven vials, and talked with me, saying unto me, Come hither; I will shew unto thee the judgment of the great whore that sitteth upon many waters: (the great whore pictures every religion devised by man as a substitute for Christ and the Cross)",
  "Revelation 17:2": "With whom the kings of the earth have committed fornication, and the inhabitants of the earth have been made drunk with the wine of her fornication. (false religion is described here as an addiction, one of the most powerful known to man).",
  "Revelation 17:3": "So he carried me away in the spirit into the wilderness: and I saw a woman sit upon a scarlet coloured beast, full of names of blasphemy, having seven heads and ten horns. (any religion claiming a way of salvation apart from the Cross is a spiritual wilderness, however outwardly attractive it looks).",
  "Revelation 17:4": "And the woman was arrayed in purple and scarlet colour, and decked with gold and precious stones and pearls, having a golden cup in her hand full of abominations and filthiness of her fornication: (the outward wealth and allure of false religion mask what the golden cup actually holds)",
  "Revelation 17:5": "And upon her forehead was a name written, MYSTERY, BABYLON THE GREAT, THE MOTHER OF HARLOTS AND ABOMINATIONS OF THE EARTH. (whatever is not built on Christ and him crucified is labeled here by the Lord, however impressive it appears to the world).",
  "Revelation 17:6": "And I saw the woman drunken with the blood of the saints, and with the blood of the martyrs of Jesus: and when I saw her, I wondered with great admiration. (this records the long history of persecution carried out by false religion against God's people).",
  "Revelation 17:7": "And the angel said unto me, Wherefore didst thou marvel? I will tell thee the mystery of the woman, and of the beast that carrieth her, which hath the seven heads and ten horns. (the angel anticipates John's amazement and offers to explain it).",
  "Revelation 17:8": "The beast that thou sawest was, and is not; and shall ascend out of the bottomless pit, and go into perdition: and they that dwell on the earth shall wonder, whose names were not written in the book of life from the foundation of the world, when they behold the beast that was, and is not, and yet is. (this fallen angel's release from the bottomless pit will astonish the unsaved of that day, who do not realize the true source of the Antichrist's power).",
  "Revelation 17:9": "And here is the mind which hath wisdom. The seven heads are seven mountains, on which the woman sitteth. (these seven heads picture the empires that were once controlled by false religion).",
  "Revelation 17:10": "And there are seven kings: five are fallen, and one is, and the other is not yet come; and when he cometh, he must continue a short space. (five of these empires had already fallen by John's day, with Rome standing at the time and one final kingdom still to come).",
  "Revelation 17:11": "And the beast that was, and is not, even he is the eighth, and is of the seven, and goeth into perdition. (regardless of his power, eternal judgment is the destiny of this fallen angel, just as it is for every unredeemed soul).",
  "Revelation 17:12": "And the ten horns which thou sawest are ten kings, which have received no kingdom as yet; but receive power as kings one hour with the beast. (these ten nations will briefly hold power alongside the Antichrist before being destroyed at the second coming).",
  "Revelation 17:13": "These have one mind, and shall give their power and strength unto the beast. (this ten-nation alliance will unite around the Antichrist, forming the kingdom described earlier as the eighth head).",
  "Revelation 17:14": "These shall make war with the Lamb, and the Lamb shall overcome them: for he is Lord of lords, and King of kings: and they that are with him are called, and chosen, and faithful. (every saint of every age returns with Christ at the second coming, sharing in this victory).",
  "Revelation 17:15": "And he saith unto me, The waters which thou sawest, where the whore sitteth, are peoples, and multitudes, and nations, and tongues. (this simply explains the earlier symbol of 'many waters').",
  "Revelation 17:16": "And the ten horns which thou sawest upon the beast, these shall hate the whore, and shall make her desolate and naked, and shall eat her flesh, and burn her with fire. (this pictures the eventual elimination of false religion by the very powers that once used it).",
  "Revelation 17:17": "For God hath put in their hearts to fulfil his will, and to agree, and give their kingdom unto the beast, until the words of God shall be fulfilled. (these nations pursue their own agenda, yet God uses even that to accomplish his purpose).",
  "Revelation 17:18": "And the woman which thou sawest is that great city, which reigneth over the kings of the earth. (this identifies rebuilt Babylon as both a commercial and religious center under the Antichrist).",
  "Revelation 18:1": "And after these things I saw another angel come down from heaven, having great power; and the earth was lightened with his glory. (this angel's magnificence fits the significance of the coming that his message announces).",
  "Revelation 18:2": "And he cried mightily with a strong voice, saying, Babylon the great is fallen, is fallen, and is become the habitation of devils, and the hold of every foul spirit, and a cage of every unclean and hateful bird. (this pictures the city as the eventual capital of evil in all the world).",
  "Revelation 18:3": "For all nations have drunk of the wine of the wrath of her fornication, and the kings of the earth have committed fornication with her, and the merchants of the earth are waxed rich through the abundance of her delicacies. (the Antichrist will use this city to make many nations wealthy, and so win their loyalty).",
  "Revelation 18:4": "And I heard another voice from heaven, saying, Come out of her, my people, that ye be not partakers of her sins, and that ye receive not of her plagues. (believers who fail to separate from the world's system risk sharing in the same judgment as unbelievers).",
  "Revelation 18:5": "For her sins have reached unto heaven, and God hath remembered her iniquities. (the only way sin and iniquity are ever forgotten by God is through faith in what Christ did at the Cross).",
  "Revelation 18:6": "Reward her even as she rewarded you, and double unto her double according to her works: in the cup which she hath filled fill to her double. (vengeance belongs to God, not to the saints, and it will be measured out exactly as her own actions deserve).",
  "Revelation 18:7": "How much she hath glorified herself, and lived deliciously, so much torment and sorrow give her: for she saith in her heart, I sit a queen, and am no widow, and shall see no sorrow. (she calls herself a queen, while the Lord calls her the great whore, and that verdict decides her fate).",
  "Revelation 18:8": "Therefore shall her plagues come in one day, death, and mourning, and famine; and she shall be utterly burned with fire: for strong is the Lord God who judgeth her. (whatever strength the Antichrist boasts through her, the Lord's strength will prove far greater).",
  "Revelation 18:9": "And the kings of the earth, who have committed fornication and lived deliciously with her, shall bewail her, and lament for her, when they shall see the smoke of her burning, (they mourn as those who have lost their god, having placed their trust in this system rather than in Christ)",
  "Revelation 18:10": "Standing afar off for the fear of her torment, saying, Alas, alas, that great city Babylon, that mighty city! for in one hour is thy judgment come. (unlike ancient Babylon's gradual decline, this destruction will be sudden and complete).",
  "Revelation 18:11": "And the merchants of the earth shall weep and mourn over her; for no man buyeth their merchandise any more: (those who profited from this system now mourn its collapse)",
  "Revelation 18:12": "The merchandise of gold, and silver, and precious stones, and of pearls, and fine linen, and purple, and silk, and scarlet, and all thyine wood, and all manner vessels of ivory, and all manner vessels of most precious wood, and of brass, and iron, and marble,",
  "Revelation 18:13": "And cinnamon, and odours, and ointments, and frankincense, and wine, and oil, and fine flour, and wheat, and beasts, and sheep, and horses, and chariots, and slaves, and souls of men. (this list of trade goods likely reaches even into the trafficking of human lives).",
  "Revelation 18:14": "And the fruits that thy soul lusted after are departed from thee, and all things which were dainty and goodly are departed from thee, and thou shalt find them no more at all. (this exposes the folly of investing in this world's system rather than the things of God).",
  "Revelation 18:15": "The merchants of these things, which were made rich by her, shall stand afar off for the fear of her torment, weeping and wailing, (the system that made them wealthy is now ending, replaced by the coming kingdom of the Lord)",
  "Revelation 18:16": "And saying, Alas, alas, that great city, that was clothed in fine linen, and purple, and scarlet, and decked with gold, and precious stones, and pearls! (their idea of greatness is not shared by the Lord).",
  "Revelation 18:17": "For in one hour so great riches is come to nought. And every shipmaster, and all the company in ships, and sailors, and as many as trade by sea, stood afar off, (what took men many years to build, God removes in a single hour)",
  "Revelation 18:18": "And cried when they saw the smoke of her burning, saying, What city is like unto this great city! (their grief is for financial loss, with no apparent concern for their own souls).",
  "Revelation 18:19": "And they cast dust on their heads, and cried, weeping and wailing, saying, Alas, alas, that great city, wherein were made rich all that had ships in the sea by reason of her costliness! for in one hour is she made desolate. (this mourning is for lost wealth, not repentance).",
  "Revelation 18:20": "Rejoice over her, thou heaven, and ye holy apostles and prophets; for God hath avenged you on her. (while the world weeps over Babylon's fall, believers are told to rejoice).",
  "Revelation 18:21": "And a mighty angel took up a stone like a great millstone, and cast it into the sea, saying, Thus with violence shall that great city Babylon be thrown down, and shall be found no more at all. (this violent end points more to the second coming of Christ than to anything else).",
  "Revelation 18:22": "And the voice of harpers, and musicians, and of pipers, and trumpeters, shall be heard no more at all in thee; and no craftsman, of whatsoever craft he be, shall be found any more in thee; and the sound of a millstone shall be heard no more at all in thee; (every trace of this system's daily life, joyful or laboring, comes to a complete end)",
  "Revelation 18:23": "And the light of a candle shall shine no more at all in thee; and the voice of the bridegroom and of the bride shall be heard no more at all in thee: for thy merchants were the great men of the earth; for by thy sorceries were all nations deceived. (everything about this system was built on a lie, and its false light goes out for good).",
  "Revelation 18:24": "And in her was found the blood of prophets, and of saints, and of all that were slain upon the earth. (literal Babylon here symbolizes a spirit that has opposed God's work throughout history).",
  "Revelation 19:1": "And after these things I heard a great voice of much people in heaven, saying, Alleluia; Salvation, and glory, and honour, and power, unto the Lord our God: (this praise directly answers the destruction just described, and affirms that salvation belongs to God alone)",
  "Revelation 19:2": "For true and righteous are his judgments: for he hath judged the great whore, which did corrupt the earth with her fornication, and hath avenged the blood of his servants at her hand. (no false way of salvation, however attractive it appears, escapes this judgment).",
  "Revelation 19:3": "And again they said, Alleluia. And her smoke rose up for ever and ever. (this affirms that her judgment is eternal, not temporary).",
  "Revelation 19:4": "And the four and twenty elders and the four beasts fell down and worshipped God that sat on the throne, saying, Amen; Alleluia. (this praise marks the end of evil and the beginning of unbroken righteousness).",
  "Revelation 19:5": "And a voice came out of the throne, saying, Praise our God, all ye his servants, and ye that fear him, both small and great. (every true believer is called here to continual praise).",
  "Revelation 19:6": "And I heard as it were the voice of a great multitude, and as the voice of many waters, and as the voice of mighty thunderings, saying, Alleluia: for the Lord God omnipotent reigneth. (this praise proclaims that the Lord alone reigns, not Satan).",
  "Revelation 19:7": "Let us be glad and rejoice, and give honour to him: for the marriage of the Lamb is come, and his wife hath made herself ready. (the bride here is the redeemed of every age, made ready entirely through what Christ accomplished).",
  "Revelation 19:8": "And to her was granted that she should be arrayed in fine linen, clean and white: for the fine linen is the righteousness of saints. (this fine linen pictures the righteousness that comes only through the Cross).",
  "Revelation 19:9": "And he saith unto me, Write, Blessed are they which are called unto the marriage supper of the Lamb. And he saith unto me, These are the true sayings of God. (the title 'Lamb' here again ties this whole scene back to the Cross).",
  "Revelation 19:10": "And I fell at his feet to worship him. And he said unto me, See thou do it not: I am thy fellowservant, and of thy brethren that have the testimony of Jesus: worship God: for the testimony of Jesus is the spirit of prophecy. (every true word of prophecy ultimately points to Christ and what he did at the Cross).",
  "Revelation 19:11": "And I saw heaven opened, and behold a white horse; and he that sat upon him was called Faithful and True, and in righteousness he doth judge and make war. (this pictures the second coming, the single greatest moment yet to come in human history).",
  "Revelation 19:12": "His eyes were as a flame of fire, and on his head were many crowns; and he had a name written, that no man knew, but he himself. (the many crowns show that his rule extends over every realm, not just one).",
  "Revelation 19:13": "And he was clothed with a vesture dipped in blood: and his name is called The Word of God. (this blood recalls the Cross, which alone gives him the right to judge the world).",
  "Revelation 19:14": "And the armies which were in heaven followed him upon white horses, clothed in fine linen, white and clean. (these armies are every saint who has ever lived, returning with Christ at his coming).",
  "Revelation 19:15": "And out of his mouth goeth a sharp sword, that with it he should smite the nations: and he shall rule them with a rod of iron: and he treadeth the winepress of the fierceness and wrath of Almighty God. (this pictures the battle of Armageddon, where every nation allied with the Antichrist against Israel will be judged).",
  "Revelation 19:16": "And he hath on his vesture and on his thigh a name written, KING OF KINGS, AND LORD OF LORDS. (this name leaves no possible doubt as to who he actually is).",
  "Revelation 19:17": "And I saw an angel standing in the sun; and he cried with a loud voice, saying to all the fowls that fly in the midst of heaven, Come and gather yourselves together unto the supper of the great God; (this proclaims the coming scale of judgment on those who join the Antichrist)",
  "Revelation 19:18": "That ye may eat the flesh of kings, and the flesh of captains, and the flesh of mighty men, and the flesh of horses, and of them that sit on them, and the flesh of all men, both free and bond, both small and great. (God's judgment makes no distinction for those the world considers great).",
  "Revelation 19:19": "And I saw the beast, and the kings of the earth, and their armies, gathered together to make war against him that sat on the horse, and against his army. (this is the Antichrist leading the assembled nations of the world in the battle of Armageddon).",
  "Revelation 19:20": "And the beast was taken, and with him the false prophet that wrought miracles before him, with which he deceived them that had received the mark of the beast, and them that worshipped his image. These both were cast alive into a lake of fire burning with brimstone. (deception was always Satan's chief weapon, and it ends here for both the Antichrist and the false prophet).",
  "Revelation 19:21": "And the remnant were slain with the sword of him that sat upon the horse, which sword proceeded out of his mouth: and all the fowls were filled with their flesh. (whatever the Antichrist and his armies intended for Israel, this is how the conflict actually ends).",
  "Revelation 20:1": "And I saw an angel come down from heaven, having the key of the bottomless pit and a great chain in his hand. (this angel already holds the key -- it is he who hands it to Satan in the earlier vision of the fifth trumpet).",
  "Revelation 20:2": "And he laid hold on the dragon, that old serpent, which is the Devil, and Satan, and bound him a thousand years, (as dragon he shows his power, as serpent his cunning, as Devil his accusing, and as Satan his opposition to God)",
  "Revelation 20:3": "And cast him into the bottomless pit, and shut him up, and set a seal upon him, that he should deceive the nations no more, till the thousand years should be fulfilled: and after that he must be loosed a little season. (Satan will be released briefly at the end of the thousand years for one final, failed attempt to deceive the nations).",
  "Revelation 20:4": "And I saw thrones, and they sat upon them, and judgment was given unto them: and I saw the souls of them that were beheaded for the witness of Jesus, and for the word of God, and which had not worshipped the beast, neither his image, neither had received his mark upon their foreheads, or in their hands; and they lived and reigned with Christ a thousand years. (these tribulation martyrs share fully in the first resurrection and its privileges).",
  "Revelation 20:5": "But the rest of the dead lived not again until the thousand years were finished. This is the first resurrection. (the resurrection of the just and the resurrection of the unjust are separated here by a thousand years).",
  "Revelation 20:6": "Blessed and holy is he that hath part in the first resurrection: on such the second death hath no power, but they shall be priests of God and of Christ, and shall reign with him a thousand years. (everyone washed in the blood of the Lamb has no need to fear the second death).",
  "Revelation 20:7": "And when the thousand years are expired, Satan shall be loosed out of his prison, (God allows this release for a specific and valid purpose)",
  "Revelation 20:8": "And shall go out to deceive the nations which are in the four quarters of the earth, Gog and Magog, to gather them together to battle: the number of whom is as the sand of the sea. (this final deception exposes and removes all who never truly gave their hearts to Christ during the kingdom age).",
  "Revelation 20:9": "And they went up on the breadth of the earth, and compassed the camp of the saints about, and the beloved city: and fire came down from God out of heaven, and devoured them. (this last uprising against Jerusalem is put down swiftly and completely).",
  "Revelation 20:10": "And the devil that deceived them was cast into the lake of fire and brimstone, where the beast and the false prophet are, and shall be tormented day and night for ever and ever. (this marks the final and permanent end of Satan's influence anywhere in God's creation).",
  "Revelation 20:11": "And I saw a great white throne, and him that sat on it, from whose face the earth and the heaven fled away; and there was found no place for them. (the one seated here is the same Christ who is Savior today and judge at this moment).",
  "Revelation 20:12": "And I saw the dead, small and great, stand before God; and the books were opened: and another book was opened, which is the book of life: and the dead were judged out of those things which were written in the books, according to their works. (this is the resurrection and judgment of the unredeemed, judged according to a record of their own deeds).",
  "Revelation 20:13": "And the sea gave up the dead which were in it; and death and hell delivered up the dead which were in them: and they were judged every man according to their works. (every unredeemed person who has ever lived stands here -- none are exempted).",
  "Revelation 20:14": "And death and hell were cast into the lake of fire. This is the second death. (this is eternal separation from God, gathering in the wicked of every age).",
  "Revelation 20:15": "And whosoever was not found written in the book of life was cast into the lake of fire. (this includes anyone, beginning with Adam and Eve, who never turned back to God).",
  "Revelation 21:1": "And I saw a new heaven and a new earth: for the first heaven and the first earth were passed away; and there was no more sea. (passed away here means changed from one condition to another, not annihilated).",
  "Revelation 21:2": "And I John saw the holy city, new Jerusalem, coming down from God out of heaven, prepared as a bride adorned for her husband. (this pictures the eternal home God has prepared for the redeemed).",
  "Revelation 21:3": "And I heard a great voice out of heaven saying, Behold, the tabernacle of God is with men, and he will dwell with them, and they shall be his people, and God himself shall be with them, and be their God. (this fulfills what God intended for his people from the very beginning).",
  "Revelation 21:4": "And God shall wipe away all tears from their eyes; and there shall be no more death, neither sorrow, nor crying, neither shall there be any more pain: for the former things are passed away. (this addresses sin and everything that has ever resulted from it).",
  "Revelation 21:5": "And he that sat upon the throne said, Behold, I make all things new. And he said unto me, Write: for these words are true and faithful. (God himself speaks here, and everything he says he will also faithfully bring to pass).",
  "Revelation 21:6": "And he said unto me, It is done. I am Alpha and Omega, the beginning and the end. I will give unto him that is athirst of the fountain of the water of life freely. (the same declaration of 'finished' spoken at the Cross closes out all prophecy here, and this fountain is freely available to all who believe, even now).",
  "Revelation 21:7": "He that overcometh shall inherit all things; and I will be his God, and he shall be my son. (the only way to overcome is by placing faith exclusively in Christ and the Cross, and the overcomer is fully adopted as God's own son).",
  "Revelation 21:8": "But the fearful, and unbelieving, and the abominable, and murderers, and whoremongers, and sorcerers, and idolaters, and all liars, shall have their part in the lake which burneth with fire and brimstone: which is the second death. (this list of Christ-rejecters describes the destiny of the unsaved, not the standing of any believer whose trust is in Christ).",
  "Revelation 21:9": "And there came unto me one of the seven angels which had the seven vials full of the seven last plagues, and talked with me, saying, Come hither, I will shew thee the bride, the Lamb's wife. (calling her 'the Lamb's wife' ties this whole vision back to the Cross).",
  "Revelation 21:10": "And he carried me away in the spirit to a great and high mountain, and shewed me that great city, the holy Jerusalem, descending out of heaven from God, (John sees this city coming down, which is when God moves his headquarters from heaven to earth)",
  "Revelation 21:11": "Having the glory of God: and her light was like unto a stone most precious, even like a jasper stone, clear as crystal; (this radiance is simply God's own glory on display)",
  "Revelation 21:12": "And had a wall great and high, and had twelve gates, and at the gates twelve angels, and names written thereon, which are the names of the twelve tribes of the children of Israel: (every gate bearing a tribe's name shows how precious Israel remains to God's heart)",
  "Revelation 21:13": "On the east three gates; on the north three gates; on the south three gates; and on the west three gates. (this arrangement pictures perfect balance and order).",
  "Revelation 21:14": "And the wall of the city had twelve foundations, and in them the names of the twelve apostles of the Lamb. (the foundation of salvation itself was not fully given until after the Cross, hence the title 'Lamb' used here).",
  "Revelation 21:15": "And he that talked with me had a golden reed to measure the city, and the gates thereof, and the wall thereof. (this measuring reveals the completeness of everything God has purposed for his redeemed people).",
  "Revelation 21:16": "And the city lieth foursquare, and the length is as large as the breadth: and he measured the city with the reed, twelve thousand furlongs. The length and the breadth and the height of it are equal. (these dimensions are simply beyond the mind's ability to fully grasp, yet faith receives them as literal).",
  "Revelation 21:17": "And he measured the wall thereof, an hundred and forty and four cubits, according to the measure of a man, that is, of the angel. (the wall itself is purely decorative, given the size of the city it surrounds).",
  "Revelation 21:18": "And the building of the wall of it was of jasper: and the city was pure gold, like unto clear glass. (this description reaches beyond ordinary imagination, yet remains entirely literal).",
  "Revelation 21:19": "And the foundations of the wall of the city were garnished with all manner of precious stones. The first foundation was jasper; the second, sapphire; the third, a chalcedony; the fourth, an emerald; (this describes a flood of color beyond anything now imaginable)",
  "Revelation 21:20": "The fifth, sardonyx; the sixth, sardius; the seventh, chrysolyte; the eighth, beryl; the ninth, a topaz; the tenth, a chrysoprasus; the eleventh, a jacinth; the twelfth, an amethyst. (each of these twelve stones is prized for its own brilliant color).",
  "Revelation 21:21": "And the twelve gates were twelve pearls; every several gate was of one pearl: and the street of the city was pure gold, as it were transparent glass. (even the streets of this city share in its overall splendor).",
  "Revelation 21:22": "And I saw no temple therein: for the Lord God Almighty and the Lamb are the temple of it. (before the Cross a temple was necessary because God could not yet dwell directly with man; the Cross made that separation unnecessary).",
  "Revelation 21:23": "And the city had no need of the sun, neither of the moon, to shine in it: for the glory of God did lighten it, and the Lamb is the light thereof. (the title 'Lamb' again credits this whole scene to what Christ did at the Cross).",
  "Revelation 21:24": "And the nations of them which are saved shall walk in the light of it: and the kings of the earth do bring their glory and honour into it. (in that day, there will be no one left in the world who is not saved).",
  "Revelation 21:25": "And the gates of it shall not be shut at all by day: for there shall be no night there. (this speaks of the city itself; day and night will continue eternally outside it).",
  "Revelation 21:26": "And they shall bring the glory and honour of the nations into it. (this pictures a wholly righteous commerce, in every sense).",
  "Revelation 21:27": "And there shall in no wise enter into it any thing that defileth, neither whatsoever worketh abomination, or maketh a lie: but they which are written in the Lamb's book of life. (entrance belongs only to those whose faith and trust rest in what Christ did for them at the Cross).",
  "Revelation 22:1": "And he shewed me a pure river of water of life, clear as crystal, proceeding out of the throne of God and of the Lamb. (this water of life pictures the Holy Spirit, made available through what Jesus did at the Cross).",
  "Revelation 22:2": "In the midst of the street of it, and on either side of the river, was there the tree of life, which bare twelve manner of fruits, and yielded her fruit every month: and the leaves of the tree were for the healing of the nations. (this provision is for the part of the population in that age who do not yet have glorified bodies).",
  "Revelation 22:3": "And there shall be no more curse: but the throne of God and of the Lamb shall be in it; and his servants shall serve him: (the curse placed at the fall is gone here, because sin itself is gone)",
  "Revelation 22:4": "And they shall see his face; and his name shall be in their foreheads. (this pictures the closest possible relationship, and full ownership by the one who purchased them).",
  "Revelation 22:5": "And there shall be no night there; and they need no candle, neither light of the sun; for the Lord God giveth them light: and they shall reign for ever and ever. (these servants are given a share in reigning that no earthly servant has ever known).",
  "Revelation 22:6": "And he said unto me, These sayings are faithful and true: and the Lord God of the holy prophets sent his angel to shew unto his servants the things which must shortly be done. (this points ahead to the great tribulation, beginning immediately after the rapture of the church).",
  "Revelation 22:7": "Behold, I come quickly: blessed is he that keepeth the sayings of the prophecy of this book. (this is the only book in Scripture offering a preview of the future, and every believer is encouraged to study it closely).",
  "Revelation 22:8": "And I John saw these things, and heard them. And when I had heard and seen, I fell down to worship before the feet of the angel which shewed me these things. (John, it seems, makes this same mistake a second time).",
  "Revelation 22:9": "Then saith he unto me, See thou do it not: for I am thy fellowservant, and of thy brethren the prophets, and of them which keep the sayings of this book: worship God. (worship belongs to God alone, whether God the Father or God the Son).",
  "Revelation 22:10": "And he said unto me, Seal not the sayings of the prophecy of this book: for the time is at hand. (this book is meant to be understood, not hidden away as some mystery).",
  "Revelation 22:11": "He that is unjust, let him be unjust still: and he which is filthy, let him be filthy still: and he that is righteous, let him be righteous still: and he that is holy, let him be holy still. (whatever righteousness or holiness is in view here is entirely what the Cross has produced in a person's life).",
  "Revelation 22:12": "And, behold, I come quickly; and my reward is with me, to give every man according as his work shall be. (whatever a person's faith is placed in will show itself in a corresponding kind of works, but faith in the Cross alone is what God accepts).",
  "Revelation 22:13": "I am Alpha and Omega, the beginning and the end, the first and the last. (this does not mean Christ himself had a beginning -- he is the beginning and end of everything else in question).",
  "Revelation 22:14": "Blessed are they that do his commandments, that they may have the right to the tree of life, and may enter in through the gates into the city. (this right is gained only one way -- through the blood of the Lamb -- and entrance into the city comes entirely through his grace).",
  "Revelation 22:15": "For without are dogs, and sorcerers, and whoremongers, and murderers, and idolaters, and whosoever loveth and maketh a lie. (idolatry here includes placing anything on a level with God, which religion itself is often guilty of).",
  "Revelation 22:16": "I Jesus have sent mine angel to testify unto you these things in the churches. I am the root and the offspring of David, and the bright and morning star. (the title 'morning star' pictures a new beginning available to anyone who will simply look to Christ).",
  "Revelation 22:17": "And the Spirit and the bride say, Come. And let him that heareth say, Come. And let him that is athirst come. And whosoever will, let him take the water of life freely. (this invitation is open to every single person in the world -- whoever will come may freely come).",
  "Revelation 22:20": "He which testifieth these things saith, Surely I come quickly. Amen. Even so, come, Lord Jesus. (this leaves Christ's promise to return as his very last word to believers, met with the church's own answering hope).",
  "Revelation 22:21": "The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you all. Amen. (this closing benediction, echoing Paul's own words, points to Christ as the source and the Cross as the means, closing out the entire canon of Scripture on that same note)."
};
